{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates","id":1387573,"title":"WIR Pre-made Vampire Character Templates","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-05-11T12:55:17-07:00","id":1387573,"title":"WIR Pre-made Vampire Character Templates","content":"I think one of my favorite aspects of the old World of Darkness lines were the pre-made character templates found in the various Clan, Tribe, and other books. It was these things that initially drew me to the game in the first place. In 1992, I saw a copy of Clanbook: Brujah on display at my local comic and gaming store, and intrigued by the cover art, gave it a look. And inside were ten pre-made characters, giving me the desired reaction of &quot;I can play that? Cool!&quot;. I'd soon pick up it and the 2nd ed core book, and to this day, I still own almost the entire run of classic WoD books. So, with a huge amount of free time on my hands for at least another week, I've decided to go back and reexamine the various pre-made character templates from the Vampire books.<br /> And, interestingly enough, this journey does not begin with the first ever of White Wolf's signature &quot;splat books&quot;. Instead, the first pre-made character templates appeared in the first edition Vampire Players Guide in 1991. There are seven of them, one for each Clan, starting on page 18. And as I list them, you can probably spot a theme here.<ul><li>Brujah Rebel (ie Street Gang Leader)</li> <li>Gangrel Loner (ie Forest Ranger)</li> <li>Malkavian Meddler (ie Troublemaker)</li> <li>Skulking Nosferatu (ie Street Person)</li> <li>Toreador Dilettante</li> <li>Tremere Occultist</li> <li>Ventrue Manipulator (ie Tycoon)</li> </ul>If you at any time said to yourself some variation of, &quot;Wow, that is some low hanging fruit,&quot; you are not alone. These are almost literally the first thing most new players would think of if asked to come up with a character for said Clan. However, I can't really fault them for that. This is one of the very earliest Vampire sourcebooks, and these templates are at the start of the very first chapter, Character Creation (which also includes the first of White Wolf's numerous lists of merits and flaws). The intro to this chapter even describes these characters as &quot;the archetypical example&quot; of each clan. And if you are totally new to the game, it's probably fairly useful just to see what a handful of character sheets look like as a basic concept is translated into stats and dots.<br /> Another interesting thing about these seven is that none of them have had their &quot;freebie points&quot; spent, so they are unfinished, awaiting the player to spend said points and customize the archetypical character however they see fit. And each write up offers a few suggestions of ways to spend those points.<br /> The one thing every single one suggests is buying up a bunch of Willpower. At first this struck me as a little odd. All of them have WP ratings of 4 or 5, which seems pretty solid. However, WP does have a lot of uses in the game, and I would notice when I started looking at the Clanbooks that those templates had WP ratings of usually 7+. While the descriptions for each level of WP in the core book is vague to the point of unhelpful (4 is &quot;diffident&quot;?), it feels a little weird that every PC is essentially expected to be some sort of super iron-willed font of internal badassery.<br /> This brings me to my next thing. Vampire WP starts with one's Courage virtue rating, while starting Humanity is one's Conscience + Self Control. However, every single one of these has their WP rating equal so their Self-Control and their Humanity equal to their Conscience + Courage. I'm just guessing whoever wrote them up got the traits backwards and no one caught it in editing.<br /> If one is interested in issues of representation, we have a male to female ration of 4:3. The Gangrel, Malkavian, and Ventrue are women. I give credit to the Toreador being male and the Ventrue female; Typically, you'd see this the other way around. This effort to show a fairly broad range of female characters (none of whom are remotely sexualized, btw) even early during its publication may or may not have had an impact of how many female fans Vampire and later the other WoD games attracted. (I'm not a woman, so I can't really say, but I have seen some female fans mention it over the years, so there is some anecdotal evidence.) Racially speaking, the art is all black and white, without much in the way of shading, so it's hard to tell. A case could be made for the Brujah and/or the Ventrue being black or Latin, the Tremere as Latino, and the Toreador as Asian, but the art style is such that it's really impossible to guess exactly what they were going for.<br /> Ability-wise, I will mention that all of these make use of the newly introduced secondary ability skills presented in the next chapter. The inevitable result of this is a massive skill creep problem, with every single one of them trying to spread their limited number of ability dots across a giant range of abilities, many of them hyper specialized, often to the detriment of the character's overall competence.<br /> Also, every single one of them just has one dot each in their Clan's three affinity Disciplines, even if it might make more sense for them to have something else.<br /> <br /> The Brujah's equipment includes a Colt Python and a shoulder holster. I don't think I've ever seen a street gang member - &quot;punk or gangsta&quot; (yes, that is how he's described) - carry their piece in a shoulder rig. I'm also not sure why someone would blow a skill point in a single dot of Lock Picking rather than just go for a dot in Security, which would accomplish the same thing while also offering the ability to, say, hot wire a car, if you spend a couple of freebie points. Leadership and Streetwise at one dot each also seems really low for a street gang leader, even before freebies. (His Social Attributes are also tertiary, with only two dots in each.) But with Brawl and Firearms at three each and Melee at two, it's clear this character is designed mainly for combat.<br /> <br /> The Gangrel is someone who has been familiar with the wilderness since a child and became a forest ranger, &quot;fighting fires, tagging animals, and protecting the forests.&quot; She has a Remington M700 bolt action hunting rifle, and no skill in Firearms whatsoever. But she has two dots in Tracking, along with two dots in Survival, which feels redundant. Likewise a dot in Meteorology and three dots in Naturalist rather than just taking a couple of dots in Science (which is at zero). She also doesn't have a knife, which seems like a major oversight for a character who lives mostly in the woods. Which brings up the fact that there's not really any reason why this character might come to the city and interact with other vampires. But the strangest thing is the comment that this character is a forrest ranger because &quot;the Gangrel Clan has a particular affinity for wild animals.&quot; I'm not sure why that makes more sense than, say, a veterinarian, a wildlife photographer, or a zookeeper.<br /> <br /> The Malkavian ... I am not and never have been a fan of the entire concept of the Malkavians as a bunch of people who go around causing problems for everyone else, be it motivated by a need to save the fairies from banality or just for shits and giggles. However, I will at least give credit for the fact that the character's prelude blurb makes it sound like she was a member of the Future Serial Killers of America club before being committed at a young adult. And this seems to be reflected by the Humanity rating of five. But that's before you take in to account that it should actually be seven if the Conscience and Self Control had been used instead of Conscience and Courage. The list of equipment is weird and slightly useless. &quot;Straight jacket, anti psychotic drugs, hypodermic needle, crayons and coloring book.&quot; Why? I'd have at least given her a straight razor. Probably also fake credentials for getting in and out of the mental hospital that serves as one of her havens. The character is strangely familiar with Bureaucracy (two dots) and other languages, but has no grasp of Medicine or Psychology, outside of a single dot in Toxicology. And somehow knows how to use a gun (one dot in Firearms).<br /> <br /> The Nosferatu is basically a Vietnam vet turned homeless alcoholic turned vampire. (If you grew up in the 80s, this is pretty much Stock Character 101.) Overall, there's not really anything that stands out as wrong or odd about the character or his stats. Streetwise, Stealth, Survival, and Area Knowledge all at three dots each. Some might question his only having one dot in Firearms given his background, but I can easily see that skill atrophying during his time an alcoholic. The one exception is the strange single dot in Politics instead of something like Language or Medicine. Also, apparently all Nosferatu are cowards, as having low Courage is part of their modus operandi. Yes, it says that. <br /> <br /> The Toreador is interesting. The prelude describes him as someone who was a wealthy dilettante dabbling in the arts and hailed as an artist, attracting the attention of his sire, and using his sire as his muse to create art that was good enough for the sire to decide to embrace him. And then a month later, the sire denounced him as a hack and left. So he's trying to prove his sire wrong. I find this a really cool concept for a Toreador. Unfortunately, absolutely nothing else about the write up matches this. The further described concept is as a stereotypical Toreador rich layabout who flits about the local art world, mostly criticizing others. I'm not sure how this is going to prove his sire wrong. Stat wise, the character has one dot in Art, two dots in Art History, and two dots in Music. He also has three dots in Carousing and two dots in Dancing. And one dot in Chess. To borrow a phrase, there is no &quot;there&quot; there. Which is a shame, because I really liked the initial prelude idea.<br /> <br /> The Tremere is basically your bog standard occultist. Someone who had an unquenchable thirst for knowledge and esoteric lore just because. Seriously, it just comes out and says the character has no motivation. Not for his initial interest in the occult, nor for adapting the stereotypical clan roll as &quot;plotter&quot;. He is totally devoid of personality. His abilities include an Occult rating of four, which breaks the rules as this is before spending freebies. He's also missing a dot in his Skills. He's basically got some of every &quot;magic&quot; related ability in the new list of secondary abilities. Herbalism and Alchemy at three each, and Hypnotism and Meditation at one each. Effectively, the guy is a walking Occult skill and little else. His equipment includes &quot;feather, penny, wood splinter or Sire's locket.&quot; I'm pretty sure that's what's on the floor of my car.<br /> <br /> I like the Ventrue. She's your standard Type A personality, having spent her living days busting her ass to make it to the top through skill and determination. And now that she's a vampire, she's not going to settle for anything less than eventually becoming Prince. I like that. It's a character with a strong focus and goals. She's got three dots each in Leadership, Bureaucracy, and Finance, and two each in Politics and Subterfuge. A really strong foundation. She also has two dots each in Diplomacy and Economics. While I don't have much of a quibble with the first, the second seems redundant with the character's Finance ability. Not entirely sure why she has a dot in Security. I think that would've been better spent raising her Etiquette from one to two. Her equipment is fine, though I think &quot;dozens of identical business suits&quot; should be &quot;multiple stylish business suits.&quot;<br /> <br /> I'm actually a little surprised that there isn't a Caitiff one.<br /> <br /> Anyway, those are the original premade character templates. A mixed bag of good ideas, weird ideas, mundane ideas, odd stat choices, and some missed opportunities.<br /> <br /> Next, the original Clanbook Brujah.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":".  Spell checking. 05-12-2020, 03:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-12T14:02:34-07:00","id":1387803,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Brujah (Part 1)</b><br /> <br /> Setting the Wayback Machine for 1992, and the first ever White Wolf World of Darkness splat-book, the one that would set the template for everything that came after. Like I said before, this was the single book that first attracted me to Vampire and made me a White Wolf fan. I was 17 at the time, a massive metal and horror fan, with that general interest in vaguely revolutionary politics that felt rebellious and iconoclastic. And I would have totally dressed like the guy on the book's cover if I thought I was even remotely cool looking enough to have ever pulled it off. But it's easy to see how this book and its cover would have grabbed me in a way that, say, the Gangrel or Toreador books wouldn't have. And when I looked inside and found the character templates, I was at first further intrigued and eventually sold on the book and game. So let's take a look. <br /> <br /> <b>The Anarch Terrorist</b><br /> In some ways, templates are meant to be a mix of those that are archetypical and those that break expectations. And this one is clearly archetypical. Almost the default Brujah archetype for much of the early years of the game, in fact. And overall, it's not a bad one. The character's origins lie in being a foster kid shuffled between various homes and developing a natural distaste - if not outright hatred - for anything that could be described as authority. I find the idea of a would be revolutionary who is still hurt and angry at their parents for abandoning them as a child transferring that anger to a series of stand ins until eventually reaching human government in general and now the Kindred's neo-feudalism to be really interesting and full of a lot of potential for dramatic growth and tragedy. She (the character art is a female) tends to instinctively lash out at anything or anyone she perceives as trying to control her in any way, and this is well reflected with the character's Rebel demeanor masking a Fanatic nature. Ironically, she doesn't play well with others, especially ones who don't share her dislike of authority and desire for direct action, but has the ability (reflected with two dots each in Intimidation, Leadership, and Subterfuge) to guide a group in the direction she wants it to go. And props for the write up pointing this out and mentioning how uncomfortable she is with this aspect of herself. It's a fun potential angle for a player to explore if they wish. Elsewhere, the character has three dots in Politics and Firearms, and two each in Security, Stealth, Alertness, Brawl, and Dodge. Also one each in Streetwise, Drive, Melee, and Demolitions. So this is clearly a character with a lot of combat in mind. Also a dot in the Fame background, which may seem weird, but the write up mentions a certain level of notoriety among various underground anarchist and would-be revolutionary groups. Humanity is at seven, which is &quot;Normal&quot; according to the 2nd ed core book, but given the character's background and proclivity for violence, I think a case could be made for it needing to be 6 instead. Likewise, Self-Control is a four, which feels maybe a little high given the character's previously stated tendency to lash out. Equipment includes the famous Desert Eagle. If you weren't around in the 90s, you probably aren't familiar with this pistol's weird popularity with certain types of gamers and armchair gun fondlers. Its claim to fame is that it fires a .50 calibre pistol round (ie, a half inch in diameter), and so in most games, it's given heavy damage stats, making it the one people gravitate towards. However, it is a really weird pick for a professional revolutionary/terrorist, who you'd think would prefer a more ubiquitous and harder to trace/easier to replace 9mm. She also has a bunch of plastique (plastic explosives). I don't see a major problem with this, but I think it would've been more interesting if the character had a couple of dots in Science and was able to cobble together homemade explosives and incendiaries. Over all, this is a pretty solid character. <br /> <br /> <b>The College Professor </b><br /> And shifting right away to the other end of the spectrum. While the Brujah had an initial stereotype as rebellious punks, the Clanbook tried hard to balance this with the alternate archetype of intellectual philosopher. And that's where this character comes in. A dedicated teacher (the demeanor is Pedagogue) who bounced from college to college, never getting tenure because he wouldn't take the time away from teaching to actually publish anything. I admire that passion, and it fits really well with the clan. The character is far more interested in teaching - not just students, but ignorant clan-mates and other vampires - and in reforming higher education than in the usual Kindred political games. While the character has three dots in Politics, he also has four dots in Science, which would seem to be the field he mainly teaches. Also three dots in Investigation, which opens up an interesting potential angle of using the character as an armchair detective type, which I think would be fun. (The character also has one dot each in Brawl and Dodge, and two dots in Drive, which seems a little strange, but I can see this being picked up as some sort of educational trade off with a fellow Brujah student he tutored in computers, science, or politics.) The main oddity is that the character has the Instruction secondary ability, but only at two dots. Given the focus on and passion for teaching, you'd expect that to be higher. Humanity is a nine. That feels way too high for any character that isn't a pacifistic saint. That aside, this is another strong concept, though it might benefit from a few minor stat adjustments. <br /> <br /> <b>The Computer Hacker</b><br /> Welcome to the 90s. So, curiously, none of the pre-made characters in the Players Guide had any Computer knowledge whatsoever. And now a year later we have a character who pretty much revolves around it. It may be hard to imagine now, but 20 to 30+ years ago, computer users tended to be this weird intellectual subculture, frequently full of people fiercely devoted to not having other people tell them what to do and especially not what to think. So they were a natural fit for the Brujah. The character is effectively a genius computer prodigy, having five dots in Intelligence and in Computer. This is the only character in the book with five dots in any Attribute or Ability, so he stands out. While the character is socially awkward (one dot in Charisma and Appearance, but four dots in Manipulation), he's surprisingly physically adept. His Physical Attributes are secondary, with three dots each in Strength and Stamina, and he has two dots each in Drive and Firearms. While this may seem out of place at first, the write up explains that, after the Embrace, the character began to see himself like a character in a cyberpunk novel, especially when he goes out on runs with the rest of his coterie. Humanity is eight, which again feels like it's maybe a little high, but I think a case could be made for the character being a little sheltered. However, again, this is a pretty solid character with a lot of flexibility, which is pretty good for one conceived around a single ability. <br /> <br /> That's all for part one. Next will be part two of Clanbook Brujah, with the next three or four templates (depending on how much space each takes), and then the remainder and final thoughts in part three.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-13T11:03:39-07:00","id":1388093,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Brujah (part 2)</b><br /> <br /> First up, I forgot to mention that all of these templates were the work of Steve Crow, who wrote the book. And the character artwork is all by Josh Timbrook. (Timbrook also did a lot of 1st ed Werewolf work.) The artwork is all pretty good. It's got a heavy &quot;early 90s not-TSR&quot; vibe to it, that I remember from a lot of early White Wolf stuff as well as things like Nightlife, Cyberpunk 2020, and other games at the time. The character images all show a good level of personality to them, and help you &quot;get&quot; the concept pretty much immediately. Which I like.<br /> <br /> Continuing where we left off.<br /> <br /> <b>The Elder Representative</b><br /> This is, at first glance, one of those characters that feels like an outlier. Basically, he's a low level grifter and con artist who was embraced by an elder Brujah just to be its mouthpiece among younger members of the clan. Which is, in and of itself, an interesting idea. Although one would expect any elder Brujah worth her salt to be able to overawe any young punk who refused to listen to her just because of her age. But that aside, one interesting bit about the write up is that the character's backstory in many ways echoes that of the Anarch Terrorist: Abandoned at an early age, left to fend for himself on the streets, and eventually attracting the wrong (or would it be right?) attention. But where the earlier character is devoted to a form of vengeance, this one is focused on self-preservation. To the point where he's already considering ways to get rid of his sire, which would make an interesting reason for those two characters to interact. The character's big focus is on Subterfuge (four dots) and Acting (three dots), with a smattering of other social abilities (Empathy, Leadership, and Etiquette at two dots each, Intimidation at one dot). He's also good at Bureaucracy and Politics (three each). The former makes sense to me as the character has probably long learned how to game the modern system of urban administration, civil service, and office paper work; the latter I suspect is something he had drilled into him by his sire to make him more useful. This is also the first character to have spent any freebie points on Backgrounds. He's got Generation maxed out (making him 8th gen), though his Mentor is only two. I suspect this is to reflect that his sire sees him mainly as a tool and not as a prot\u00e9g\u00e9e. While this character is meant to be a weasel, and even the write up flat out says he's a lowlife, I rather like it. He's a guy just trying to survive as best he can, and has a lot of potential for growth in several different ways. I could easily see this character turning into an Anarch plotter or midlevel Camarilla player, depending on how things break in the campaign. And he feels like he's work really well in a film noir type game.<br /> <br /> <b>The European Idealist</b><br /> Another idea that was introduced in this book is that of Europe's Brujah being primarily a bunch of upper class intellectual snobs. While this may seem a little odd, given that post-WW2 Europe had no shortage of &quot;punk&quot; style subcultures, but I guess it does fit with the idea that most of the continent's Kindred are older and more conservative in who they embrace. The character is the scion of an extremely wealthy family, though not nobility, and a product of private boarding schools and all the other trappings one expects of this sort of individual. He was basically groomed and given to his sire by his family in exchange for financial aide during a period of difficulty, and I find that an interesting potential hook to have the character explore at some future point. He's been sent to the Americas to keep an eye on events and ensure that any potential upheavals here don't upset the balance of power over there. In many ways, this is a character designed to hang around and interact with the more &quot;upscale&quot; clans, especially Toreador and Ventrue. And, for that purpose, it's not a bad one. This is a socially focused character, with Social Attributes as Primary (and his Appearance is a four, which is not common to see in male characters unless they are specifically conceived as the &quot;pretty boy&quot; type), and three dots each in Leadership and Etiquette. He's also got some dots spread around in Acting, Subterfuge and Linguistics. And he's not totally incompetent in a fight, having two dots of Brawl and one each of Melee and Firearms. Given his background, I'd have focused more on Melee with fencing and swordplay, but that's just me. He's also got three dots each in Politics and Finance. I kind of wish he had a few dots moved into Investigation, but only because I like the whole &quot;upper class European armchair detective&quot; genre. And, in perfect hindsight, this is one of those cases where I really wish the revised era Academics skill had been around earlier, as this is a character who could've really benefitted from having two or three dots in that skill to represent the sort of classic education where he could cite and quote various poets, philosophers, and scholars. But anyway, this isn't a bad character, one who's very much against the expected archetype, but making a lot of sense in the context of what the book presented about how the clan is different outside of North America. (With a little tweaking, I think this template would work really well to represent a certain type of Latin American or Middle Eastern Brujah. Or, say, a Japanese one if you wanted to ignore the whole KotE thing.)<br /> <br /> <b>The Fake Rapper</b><br /> You had to be there at the time. This character is clearly meant to be a parody of early 90s pop hip hop star <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vanilla_Ice\" target=\"_blank\">Vanilla Ice</a>. He's from your cookie cutter &quot;white bread&quot; suburban background, and a conniver who figured out early on how to take advantage of other people's hard work by becoming a &quot;manager&quot; and &quot;organizer&quot;. And when rap music became the next big thing in the late 80s and early 90s, he reinvented himself as a rebel from the mean streets. Only apparently some young Brujah actually bought into his act and embraced him. Now he's stuck playing the part, a masquerade within the Masquerade. And that's actually not a bad concept for a character, especially if you are in a game set in LA and the Anarch Free States. The character is a self-absorbed ass, but it's not like that's uncommon for a vampire, and he's fairly competent at pulling off his con. He's got three dots each in Acting, Finance, and Seduction, and two each in Intimidation, Leadership, Subterfuge, Bureaucracy, and Politics. That's actually a pretty good spread for a manager/face type. And again, this is the sort of character who could end up going down a number of paths, be it embracing the roll and turning into a fairly smart Anarch leader or &quot;selling out&quot; and abandoning his current persona to be one of the suits. So, in spite of this character mainly being here to make fun of an often mocked period celebrity, it's actually a fairly solid one, which honestly surprised me when I first reread it for this. (And as an aside, I think this concept and background would work really well as a Ventrue character as well, even if it doesn't come with the hook of having to stay in a pretend roll if you change the Clan.)<br /> <br /> <b>The Rapper</b><br /> And, taking a total 180, we go from Vanilla Ice to a mash up of Ice T and Ice Cube. At its most basic, this character is what the above one pretends to be. He's actually from an inner city ghetto and been in a street gang. And like the above, his music about fighting the system and racist cops attracted the attention of some young Brujah who thought he'd be perfect for the clan. His main focus is on Streetwise (four dots), Music, and Politics (three dots each; I beginning to think there may be a joke here about all Brujah characters automatically coming with the Politics ability). Everything else is just scattered around at ones and twos. In a lot of ways, this character is almost the most archetypical or archetypical Brujah, even more so than the Anarch Terrorist. And while there's nothing inherently wrong with this character, I think he may in some ways be one of the weakest of the book. His motivation can best be described as &quot;I grew up disfranchised, so down with The Man!&quot; He's almost like a refinement of the Brujah character from the Players Guide in some ways. Which is a shame, because you'd think playing a character based on one of the guys from NWA or Body Count would be pretty awesome. As I said, this isn't a bad character, but I think it could be a little stronger in terms of examining what drives him and where he wants to go with his unlife.<br /> <br /> And that's all for this post. Next will be part three, with the last three templates, and some final thoughts. After that, <i>Clanbook: Gangrel</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-13-2020, 02:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-13T13:15:14-07:00","id":1388130,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Brujah (part 3)</b><br /> <br /> The home stretch. <br /> <br /> <b>The Rogue Cop/Vigilante</b><br /> This one may require a little bit of historical context for people under 30. The 1970s and early 80s were a rough period, especially in the inner cities. I remember an interview with one of the guys from The Ramones describing growing up in the Bronx as being like living in a war zone. And a lot of people felt like the system was more interested in coddling violent criminals than in protecting law abiding citizens. This is where films like Dirty Harry and Death Wish come from. And it's also where this character comes from. Rebels are usually Romantics, and Cynics usually Romantics who've had their hearts broken. Our character is someone who grew up idolizing police officers, only to become incredibly disillusioned with the system upon becoming one herself. We don't get a triggering incident, which is a shame, but I understand the limits imposed by how much word count one can put on a single page. At some point, the character snapped and went rogue, hunting down and executing criminals vigilante style. It's said that she's not a real cop anymore, which I think is unfortunate, as it would add multiple layers to the character and what sort of situations she might find herself in. Say, being one of the local Kindred's mole in the force, seeing to it that people look the other way at her vigilante activities in exchange for helping enforce the Masquerade. She's got all the abilities you'd expect from a maverick cop archetype. Three dots each in Dodge, Firearms, and Investigation; two each in Athletics, Brawl, Intimidation, Streetwise, Drive, Stealth, Security, and Law. However, her Humanity is 8, which feels too high for someone who has basically murdered multiple people. The write up also mentions the character having Area Knowledge, but the ability isn't listed on the character sheet. I suspect this is something that got changed at some point and never fixed in editing. There's a decent amount of material to work with here, as far as character development and future growth. Overall, not a bad character. A fairly strong &quot;meat and potatoes&quot; kind of archetype. <br /> <br /> <b>The Skinhead</b><br /> I have mixed feelings about this character. One the one hand, you do get the feeling that some people at White Wolf were taking seriously the idea that Vampire was supposed to be for mature grown ups, or at least edgy and provocative. And a character like this plays very true to the line on the very first page of the second edition core book: &quot;By becoming a monster, one learns what it is to be human.&quot; On the other hand, I wish this character was a little deeper. The write up describes him as a stereotypical Brujah with &quot;a little more development.&quot; I think what they meant was &quot;with a twist.&quot; This is a character who just sort of fell into a lifestyle of racial supremism and bigotry, growing up in a mostly white middle to upper class suburb only to fall in with the wrong crowd. And then just sort of ran with it. And is now your sort of bog standard racist stereotype. I find myself wishing more had been done with this character. If you've ever seen films like Romper Stomper and American History X (and if you haven't, I highly recommend both), there's a lot of strong storytelling potential with a character who just sort of fell into a bad lifestyle, likely as one of those people who just never fit in and was happy just to find a group that accepted them, and is now very ambivalent about their situation and want to get out. A character who is a Conformist playing at being a fanatic or visionary, for example. The character is really good at fighting: Brawl has four dots, Firearms and Melee three each, and Dodge with two. He's also got three dots in Intimidation and two in Leadership. And then a smattering of one each in things like Streetwise, Politics, Law, Linguistics (I'm presuming German, given how frequently Mein Kampf and its author get mentioned), Stealth and Subterfuge. Given how much the write up talks about him being in an intellectual leadership role for various racist groups, I'm a little surprised the character doesn't have higher rankings in Law and Politics. That or some of the secondary abilities from the Players Guide. And his Humanity is five. Yep, being a violent terrorist, serial vigilante, or manipulative con artist won't impact your Humanity, but being a racist will, I guess. I admit my reaction to some of the Humanity ratings are a result of the revised edition, which went to the trouble to actually clarify what each level meant (and at five, you're pretty much very creepy and off-putting to most people). But at the same time, I just find this kind of a cheap cop out. He's also the only one with a one in the Conscience virtue. Everyone else has two or more. Hell, the Rapper and the Rogue cop both have it at five. Like I said, this is an interesting idea for a concept, and I give credit for exploring it, at least in a shallow sort of way, but I wish more had been done with it. <br /> <br /> <b>The Streetwalker </b><br /> I think when I first saw the Clanbook almost thirty years ago (when I rode my dinosaur to the cave that sold comics), this was the one that most caught my eye. Both for the obvious reasons - I was a teenaged boy - and also because I was curious just what sort of RPG involved playing prostitutes. This is another character that was abandoned at a young age, which I admit would be an interesting bonding point if you had her, the Anarch Terrorist and the Elder Representative in the same coterie. I'm not going to mention what age the write up says the character started regularly selling herself, because it's likely to severely bother some people, and even my reaction on rereading it was &quot;that's something I could've done without this morning.&quot; She's one of those impulse embraces, picked on the spot when a Brujah attacked the other vampire who was planning to feed on her. The character is basically a user only out for herself, which is perfectly logical given her background. Her Nature is Bon Vivant, which I guess fits, but I could see a case being made for something like Survivor or Curmudgeon being a better choice. Her abilities leave a little to be desired. Streetwise is only two dots, which seems low for a character who has lived and worked on the streets all her life. Likewise, only one dot in Subterfuge, and only one dot in the Haggling secondary ability. I mean, if you are going to take the somewhat cringey step of giving a prostitute character the Haggling skill, at least make her good at it. She does have three dots in Seduction and two dots in Masquerade (the skill of passing for a living person). Also two each in Athletics, Acting, Brawl, Firearms, and Law. I can't complain a lot about these, but just personally, I think flipping her single dot of Melee with the two dots of firearms would've made more sense, as prostitutes are stereotyped as carrying bladed weapons for self defense (and her equipment includes a stiletto but no gun). Overall, I think I'd have preferred an ability focus on social manipulation and street survival. Her discipline spread has one dot in Celerity and two in Presence. I honestly would've given her Potence, as I feel it's the great equalizer between vampires who appear physically non-threatening and beefier mortals. The write up describes her has having &quot;the conscience of a pit viper&quot;, but her Conscience virtue is three, making it better than the Elder Representative, the European Idealist, the Fake Rapper, and the Skinhead. I find that wonky, even with 2nd ed rules. This is another character that isn't bad, but that I wish more had been done with. <br /> <br /> And so, that's the ten of them. Overall, I think this was a pretty strong start for the Clanbooks. Three of these - The Computer Hacker, the Elder Representative, and the Fake Rapper - I like a lot, and five are very strong and solid. I wish the Rapper had a little more motivation, and that the Skinhead and Streetwalker were better. But none of them are terrible. <br /> Representation wise, we have only three women, which is a little disappointing, especially as one of them is the Streetwalker. However, one of the other ones is the Rogue Cop, and she looks really badass in an early 90s <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cynthia_Rothrock\" target=\"_blank\">Cynthia Rothrock</a> kind of way. The Streetwalker is obviously very sexualized, but this is offset somewhat by the fact that the Fake Rapper is also. The Rapper and it appears the Anarch Terrorist are the only ones who are non-white, though I think the Fake Rapper could be interpreted that way also. I think it would've been really interesting to see what the European Idealist would've looked like if presented as female. <br /> Back in the day, I used to make lists of additional templates I thought should've been in the books. Those notes are long gone, but I remember distinctly two of the Brujah ones being an Investigative Reporter and an Outlaw Biker.<br /> Finally, when the revised books started coming out, it became clear that each Clan had a sort of theme running through it, acting as a backbone of the Clan and its archetypes. For the Brujah, that theme was Passion. They are driven by it, to the point it consumes them with Frenzy, and draw power from it via their disciplines. And looking back at the original clanbook, I can see that all of these character templates have that theme in some fashion, be it vengeance, teaching, computers, self preservation, vigilante justice, and so on. So all in all, I have to call it a success. <br /> <br /> Next, Clanbook: Gangrel.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-13T14:31:45-07:00","id":1388158,"title":"","content":"Excellent series. Thank you for all your work. Keep them coming.<br /> <br /> You're right about the <a href=\"https://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/EarlyInstallmentWeirdness\" target=\"_blank\">weirdness</a> of secondary abilities. They were to be avoided by anyone who cared about efficient point builds. Sadly, secondary abilities are no longer with us. They died a quiet, dignified death, surrounded by those they loved: the Rift discipline, catdog Gangrel, and alien parasite souleaters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11558-nosimplehiway","name":"Nosimplehiway","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11558_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1699"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-13T16:02:07-07:00","id":1388173,"title":"","content":"<b>An Interlude: The Toreador Dilettante Revisited</b><br /> <br /> Often in looking back at things in hindsight, it's easy to spot things that could have worked a lot better if done differently. And I think the original Toreador template in the Players Guide is one of those things. As I said in my overview of it, it started really strong out the gate, and then just sort of tripped and fell flat on its face. And I think I can fix that.<br /> <br /> <b>The Toreador Artist</b><br /> <br /> <i>&quot;Who are you to judge my work? Someone as empty and mundane as you could never hope to understand the passions that drive true art.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> Prelude: Coming from an upper middle class background, your parents happily indulged your interest in art. You studied all the masters, learned all the techniques, and demonstrated more than enough technical skill. But your work lacked a certain spark of inspiration. Your work got shown, and various people whose opinions mattered said you were a hot new artist. But deep inside you felt that was a lie. And then he appeared at one of your showings. His bemused dismissal of your creations angered you at first, but then you admitted you agreed with him. The two of you struck up an increasingly passionate relationship, and soon he became your muse, inspiring your to levels of creativity and vision you didn't know you even possessed. Eventually, your work gained his praise, and he said your talent deserved to live on forever. A year later, he called you a disappointment, unworthy of the gift he'd given you, and left. Devastated and enraged, you turned your pain and anger into new and darker art. You will prove him wrong, if it's the last thing you do.<br /> Concept: You are the archetypical Toreador, completely absorbed in your art as you navigate the perilous world of the Kindred. Your skills focus on art and socializing, with more survival oriented abilities playing catch up. Most of your Backgrounds are holdovers from your breathing days. While you would prefer to spend your nights locked away in your studio working, you begrudgingly take part in the rituals of feeding and Elysium. Actually getting out and doing things does seem to make you a better artist, so you begrudge your coterie less than other Kindred.<br /> Roleplaying Tips: Art is everything. Present an air of cool detachment and hipness, but inside you are a roiling cauldron of hurt and resentment that sometimes slips out as bitter sarcasm and contempt. The one thing that does excite you in a positive way is anyone who actually understands the difference - both subjectively and objectively - between good art and bad, and why the difference is important.<br /> Equipment: Basement studio full of art supplies and works in progress, a decent car, a modest wardrobe with clothes for most social occasions, a 9mm pistol you've never even fired.<br /> <br /> Nature: Autist<br /> Demeanor: Curmudgeon<br /> Clan: Toreador<br /> Generation: 13th<br /> Haven: Basement Art Studio<br /> Concept: Artist<br /> <br /> Attributes<br /> Physical: Strength: 2 Dexterity: 3 Stamina: 2<br /> Social: Charisma: 3 Manipulation 3 Appearance 4<br /> Mental: Perception: 3 Intelligence: 3 Wits: 3<br /> <br /> Abilities<br /> Talents: Acting 1 Alertness 2 Dodge 1 Empathy 2 Intimidation 1 Streetwise 1 Subterfuge 2<br /> Skills: Etiquette: 3 Firearms: 1 Music 1<br /> Knowledge: Finance 1 Investigation 1 Language: 1 Politics 1 Science 2<br /> <br /> Advantages<br /> Disciplines: Auspex 1 Celerity 1 Presence 1<br /> Backgrounds: Contacts 1 Fame 1 Resources 3<br /> Virtues: Conscience: 3 Self-Control: 4 Courage: 3<br /> <br /> Other Traits: Artistic Expression: 4 Art History: 3<br /> <br /> Humanity: 7<br /> Willpower: 6<br /> <br /> Art Notes: Young 20-something, attractive male, probably white or Asian, with the sort of look and style you'd expect of a young, hip urban artist circa 1991.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-13-2020, 07:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-13T17:52:55-07:00","id":1388200,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Gangrel (part one)</b><br /> <br /> So it took several months after Brujah for Gangrel to hit the shelves. It came out in 1993, the second year of second edition. I have vague memories of my cousin actually finding a copy of it before I did. It's cover is not as striking as Brujah's, but does evoke the idea of some sort of urban hunter/predator. The book, and its templates, was written by Brad Freeman. The artwork for the templates is again by Josh Timbrook, or as he's now credited, Joshua Gabriel Timbrook. <br /> I'll also note that this was back when the Gangrel were heavily tied to the Roma, or The Rom as the book refers to them, and we are &quot;graced&quot; with five pages of what is effectively a Fear &amp; Loathing in Las Vegas pastiche starring Gangrel Hunter S. Thompson pastiche Dr. Raoul King as he investigates The Rom in and around Toronto. Thankfully, we are not subjected to any stereotypical The Rom character templates in the back. Which I have to admit that in hindsight I find a little surprising. <br /> <br /> <b>The Archon in Training</b><br /> This guy likes to fight. And when he's not doing that, he can be found fighting, or sometimes fighting. Yeah, this isn't a very deep one. Combat is all this character revolves around. Or rather, beating the crap out of people. His entire backstory - which includes a rather poor-taste-even-for-the-time joke about domestic violence - is all about finding new and interesting excuses to beat the crap out of people. His abilities primarily revolve around beating the crap out of people (Brawl and Melee at four dots each; Firearms at three dots, I guess for when he wants to mix it up and shoot someone instead). Physical attributes are primary, while Mental ones are tertiary. He only has a single dot in Intelligence. He's moderately sociable, but one is left with the impression that this is less of a person than it is an attack dog whose owner has given it an overly long leash. Which I guess is interesting in a way. But there's not a lot of meat here. I do find myself wondering what would happen if the character came across something it couldn't beat the crap out of and had to develop a modicum of self-reflection. I will note that he's got a Humanity of six and a Conscience of three, which makes a guy who's effectively the high school bully turned wife beater turned poster boy for police brutality turned Camarilla attack dog a better person than the Brujah Skinhead. Read whatever you want into that. <br /> <br /> <b>The Babe in the Woods</b><br /> This is an odd duck. So, the Gangrel are presented as having this thing where they embrace someone and then just leave them to fend for themselves. This is such a character, taken almost to ludicrous levels. She is a plain run of the mill ordinary everywoman college student, with absolutely nothing about her that stands out, save perhaps for an interest in storytelling. Seriously, it flat out says that nothing about her stands out. Her equipment is &quot;typical pocket items or none whatsoever.&quot; Even though she's apparently still going to college and close to her family. The character does not even know or understand that she's a vampire, and it even suggests giving her a Derangement (I presume Amnesia) so that she doesn't even remember when she feeds. This would, I think, make an interesting Caitiff concept, but as a Gangrel being showcased in their clanbook, I'm not a big fan of it. I'm honestly not sure how you're supposed to integrate such a character into a coterie. The write up suggests using her as a mirror to show others how monstrous they are as vampires, and while that's great for an NPC, I don't think it works very well as a PC. Her abilities are completely unremarkable. Mostly a bunch of ones scattered across the character sheet, with three dots in Storytelling being the only major stand out. She also has two dots each in History, Science, Animal Ken (I suspect entirely for the Animalism), and Drive. One of those single dots is in Firearms. I'm not exactly sure why. There is an ember of a good character concept here: The idea of a normal nice and fairly sheltered innocent embraced into a clan of rugged individualists and survivors, having to make her way with the wits and courage she never knew she had, almost like an old fairy tale heroine, would be really cool, in my opinion. But this takes the &quot;innocent waif&quot; knob and turns it up to 11, drowning out anything interesting one might do with the character. <br /> <br /> <b>The Biologist</b><br /> Funnily enough, I think this is almost what the Gangrel Loner in the Players Guide should have been. A quiet loner type totally fascinated with the mysteries and wonders of nature and wildlife, and eager to study them every chance he gets. And because this is a Mentally focused character, which goes a bit against the initial stereotype, I find myself liking it. Science is his primary focus, with four dots, as well as three dots each in Medicine and Investigation, both of which make sense for the character as described. He rounds this out with two dots each in Firearms, Animal Ken, Drive, Repair, Survival, Occult, Linguistics, and Alertness, making for a pretty well rounded character. He's got an Intelligence of five, much like the third entry in Brujah, the Computer Hacker, and I can't help but think that the two of them would make interesting coterie mates. His Conscience is two, which seems a little weird when put next to the Archon in Training above. But other than that, there's nothing wrong with this character. He's a really solid, interesting and slightly unconventional concept with a well focused but diverse set of stats. The best of the bunch so far. <br /> <br /> <b>The Bold Urban Commando</b> <br /> Not all Anarchs are Brujah, and back in the early days, it was sort of expected that games would usually be Anarchs vs Camarilla (and probably the PCs as Anarchs). And here we have a hard core Marxist radical, complete with suburban background - though spiced up with some indications of domestic violence and running away from home as a teen - embraced after getting beat down during a counter protest against a bunch of skinheads lead by a Brujah ghoul (I can't help but wonder if this is a call back to the earlier template). Now she's a hard core Anarch out to over throw the elders. Her equipment list almost makes her sound like a relic left over from the early '70s, with a beat up VW bug, an Abbie Hoffman paperback, Molotov cocktails, and a .38 revolver. I can't help but wonder if that bug has an 8-track player in it. Ability wise, she's pretty solid, with three each in Brawl and Dodge, and two each in Intimidation, Firearms, Melee, Security, Stealth, and Politics. I'm a little surprised her Streetwise is only at a single dot. This isn't a bad character. The character art certainly has a lot of personality to it. I could easily see this character in the same coterie as the Anarch Terrorist or some of the other Brujah characters. The compare and contrast between the two character's early lives might make for some interesting role playing hooks. <br /> <br /> That's all for now. Next will be part two of Gangrel.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-14T05:36:04-07:00","id":1388303,"title":"","content":"This is awesome friend and I wish I'd started reading it earlier. Thank you for doing this.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-14T08:23:50-07:00","id":1388337,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Gangrel (part two)</b><br /> <br /> And so, picking up where we left off.<br /> <br /> <b>The Eco-Guerrilla </b><br /> So this is one of those early '90s archetypes, the environmental activist who prefers to take direct and often destructive action against the usual list of ecological despoilers. It's an idea I think fits fairly well with the Gangrel. I mentioned aspects of the Bold Urban Commando sounding a little like a throwback to the '70s (which admittedly was a thing in the '90s), and this one does also in some ways. It's the small town boy who went off to a war he didn't want any part of, only to come back and find that - shock - the rivers and woods he grew up around as a boy have been infected by modern development. It took me a reread to realize that the war it's referring to is probably the Gulf War and not Vietnam, because a lot if this feels like it's referring to the '70s. And so the character went down the environmentalist route, becoming increasingly disillusioned with the legal methods and getting more and more involved with terrorism. Until finally he gets embraced. And continues doing the same. At its heart, this is not a bad character. I really like the idea of a small town boy (or girl, it would've worked that way too, I think) who went off to war, and is desperately trying to hold on to this idealized image of the outdoors they experienced as a child as a way of recovering lost innocence, but really only falling deeper into a life of violent destruction, and I really wish more had been done with that angle. But as it, the character is a little one dimensional. The character's Appearance is a one, which really doesn't match up with anything in the write up, and especially not with the artwork. (I mentioned that Timbrook also did a lot of early Werewolf art, and this image would've been right at home in that line; The guy looks like your archetypical ruggedly handsome West Coast eco-grunge guy and if asked to rate his appearance, I'd have given him at least a two if not a three out of five.) His abilities are mostly a scattering of ones and twos, with the only exception being a three in Firearms. Given that he doesn't have a gun listed among his equipment, I think at least one or two of those dots could've been better spent elsewhere. Like on Survival, which he only has at one dot, and I find that too low for this concept. I also thing that single dot he has in Occult would've gone better toward Law, which is zero, given that you think he'd have some grasp of environmental and property laws from his earlier efforts to use the system. (He does have one dot in Bureaucracy.) His Virtues are all at four, and his Humanity at eight, which feels maybe one level too high. Overall, this isn't a bad template, and is clearly meant to be a hook for a segment of White Wolf's targeted fan base (including Werewolf fans who might be checking out Vampire), but I think it could've benefited from some fine tuning.<br /> <br /> <b>The Explorer</b><br /> It's Indiana Jones. It even has &quot;I'm making this up as I go along&quot; as the character quote. One bit of weirdness though is that the write up is specifically male, and an old one at that, having fought Nazi's in WW2 and not being embraced until really too old to keep going out in the field. However, the artwork is that of a young female. (Also odd is this huge mark across the face, which I honestly can't tell if it's meant to be massive scar that runs in a nonsensical pattern or just a stray pen mark that was never cleaned up.) I will note that this is at least three years before Tomb Raider debuted in 1996. The character's abilities are mostly ones and twos, with three each in Firearms and Survival. Notably missing for a character that is an archeologist and apparently a teacher, they have almost no abilities to reflect this. Science is at a single dot, as is Occult, and that's it. I've made my thoughts on Secondary Abilities clear, but this is only of those cases where I could see spending a few dots on Archeology (or Anthropology or History) would have been completely warranted. Also, a dot in Drive wouldn't have been out of place. This is one of those unfortunate cases where you take a fictional character who would be a perfect fit for a group and just try to import it whole rather than taking the core idea of that character and making it uniquely your own in the world of the game. To borrow an example from anime, in My Hero Academia, the character of All Might is very clearly meant to be a Superman pastiche, but while building off that core idea of &quot;the greatest hero who inspires every other hero&quot;, every other aspect of the character goes in their own directions that drive the world and plot in ways that Superman wouldn't. And I think that's the trick when doing a character template like this. It should be clear what the inspiration is, but still manage to stand on its own as a cool character you want to play. I really want to like this template, but I can't. This is not to say that I dislike it, but that I'm disappointed and wish it was better.<br /> <br /> <b>The Great White Hunter</b><br /> I think this may actually be my favorite template in the book (perhaps rivaled only by the Biologist). I have long had a habit of trying to find ways to make characters that seem to play against type but are at their heart perfectly in tune with the archetype of whatever group they're from. And this is one of those type of characters. He is your stereotypical upper class British person (no mention if he's a noble or not, but it feels like it would fit) who became a big game hunter out of a need to find something to do with his life. And he became really good at it. Eventually he went looking for a legendary beast in the great white north only to find it was his sire. Now he's a staunch and perfectly respectable member of the Camarilla who hunts their enemies. Everything you'd expect from a Gangrel - wilderness oriented, a deadly hunter - who just happens to be a throwback to a hundred years ago when that sort of description applied to upper class elites as much as rugged mountain men and so-called savages. His stats are pretty solid. I slightly question his having five dots in Dexterity, but I realize that's to take advantage of the rules for firearms combat. He has five dots in Firearms as well. He's also got three dots each in Alertness, Animal Ken, Stealth, and Survival, and two dots in Etiquette and Language. Resources are four, and he has a Retainer (the only one in the book to do so). I do question his having only a two in Conscience and six in Humanity, compared to other characters. But other than that, he's definitely my favorite.<br /> <br /> That's all for now. Next, the final three, and closing thoughts. Then the Malkavians.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-14-2020, 11:27 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-14T09:50:09-07:00","id":1388349,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Gangrel (part three)</b><br /> <br /> And the final three.<br /> <br /> <b>The Lupine Impersonator </b><br /> Oh dear. So, when Werewolf initially came out, it was inevitable that people would want crossovers and mixed PC groups with Vampire. And the Gangrel, being what they were, seemed like a natural outlet for that impulse. The clanbook devoted three pages to talking about the ins and outs - mechanics wise at least - of Gangrel interacting with Lupines, including a merit that lets them smell like they are still alive. Oddly, this character doesn't have that merit. So, what we have is a woman from &quot;the old country&quot; (I suspect this may in some ways also be meant as the The Rom character I was dreading earlier) who was fascinated by the stories of werewolves and decided to run off and find them. Instead, she ended up eventually meeting a Gangrel who embraced her, making it largely impossible for her to ever &quot;truly&quot; be what she wanted. So she still tries her best, constantly in fear of the truth being discovered. This isn't a terrible idea for a character template, and has the potential for some nice tragic role-play, but I don't think it's a great Vampire character. It's really meant more as part of a Werewolf game than a Vampire one, in my opinion. As far as stats go, it isn't too bad. I think her Physical attributes should be a 3/3/4 spread instead of the 2/4/4 one used. Brawl at four dots (her single highest attribute) feels too high. Survival, Dodge, and Animal Ken are all three dots. Subterfuge and Acting are both at two, and I fell that one or both could benefit from being a little higher. She has the secondary ability of Lupine Lore at two dots. I give credit for having it as a reasonable level and not at, say, four dots. (This is actually one of only three characters with secondary abilities; It's interesting to see how quickly these seemed to start to fall by the wayside.) Her Humanity is a nine, and I think this is really only because of the decision that Werewolf's Sense Wyrm gift only detected vampires with a Humanity lower than seven. So, all in all, it's OK, but not one that sets me on fire. <br /> <br /> <b>The Stunt Man</b><br /> For a character clearly built entirely around one of the clan's signature disciplines - Fortitude - this isn't a bad one. A life long daredevil, with some military experience, turned Hollywood stunt man (in the days before CGI when everything was still practical effects) only to botch a major stunt and get embraced by a conveniently nearby Gangrel who found him amusing. There's not a lot of depth here, but this character feels like he'd be more fun to play than, say, the Archon in Training, who is similarly shallow and focused on a single thing. I admit some of this may be influenced by the art work, with a guy who has that old school Hollywood feel and a certain amount of devil may care charm. Under attributes, he's got a Stamina of five dots, obviously. He only has a Stregth of two, though, which feels a little low. His Intelligence is one dot, which I think is a little disparaging to the profession he represents. Athletics are at four dots, with Brawl, Dodge, and Drive at three each. He's also got two dots in Finance, which seems out of place on some level. Naturally, he has four dots in Fortitude. This is one of those characters who is very good at what they do, but a little short in everything else. But it's not a bad concept, and as I said, feels like he'd be fun to play. <br /> <br /> <b>The Survivalist </b><br /> This is another of those '80s era stereotypes, and one that would eventually carry over into the '90s with the development of what late journalist Michael Kelly would dub &quot;fusion paranoia.&quot; (I'd link to the article, but it's behind the New Yorker's paywall.) Our character is convinced that The End of the World is nigh (and in the WoD, that's not being paranoid) and so had created the full set up of underground survival bunker, complete with supplies, guns, etc. Then one day he found a vampire in his bunker. (It's not said, but I presume it was the use of Protean to enter through to air intakes as mist.) Said vampire tells him about the Secret Vampire Overlords leading humanity to its doom, embraced him, and left. Now he's trying to figure out how to update his survival plans now that he needs other people to feed on. From a stat perspective, he's OK. A decent spread of ones and twos in abilities, plus four dots in Firearms. His mental attributes are primary. The main downside is that there's just not really any reason given for this character to come out of his hole and interact with other vampires on a regular basis, let alone join a coterie. I don't think that's an insurmountable problem, but will require some thinking on the parts of the player and the storyteller. So, one of those &quot;not bad, but could be a lot better with a few tweaks&quot; entries. <br /> <br /> And so that's the Gangrel. Overall, I think this collection was solid, but perhaps not as good as the Brujah. The two stand outs, to me at least, are the Biologist and the Great White Hunter, with the Stuntman getting honorable mention. The Bold Urban Commando is also a solid one. Unfortunately, I think the remaining six are just a little under par and would have benefited from some minor refinements (or perhaps more so with the Archon in Training and the Babe in the Woods). <br /> In terms of representation, we have four females and six males. However, one of those, the Explorer, is written as a male character and has a female image. None of them are sexualized, save kinda-sorta the Stuntman. And all of them are white. Except maybe the Bold Urban Commando, who may be Asian. At least none of them are The Rom, because I know if any of them were, they would've have been cringe inducing even by the standards of 20 years ago. <br /> I remember my thoughts about additional templates from way back when including that of a Private Detective, just because I thought that sort of tough streetwise survivor type would've worked really well for the clan, especially in a modern film noir type game. <br /> Regarding an overarching clan theme, I think it's Survival. And I think the best templates here are the ones that most play on that. The Biologist survives by exploring and analyzing the world around him. The Bold Urban Commando and Eco-Guerrilla through political activism and crusades. The Great White Hunter and Explorer by finding a purpose in life and whole heartedly embracing it. The Stuntman just from being the toughest SOB around. The weaker ones are the ones where this seems muddled. The Archon in Training, for example, fights not to survive but for the fun of beating the hell out of people. I think that would actually play better with the Brujah and their theme of passion, while a person who reluctantly became great at beating the hell out of people because he had to in order to survive would make a better Gangrel. <br /> <br /> Next, Clanbook: Malkavian.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-14T13:16:45-07:00","id":1388406,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Malkavian (a prelude)</b><br /> <br /> I feel that I should probably preface talking about the Malkavians with a bit about a pair of issues. One is the treatment of mental illness in roleplaying games. Mental illness is one of those things that, deep down, is very uncomfortable for a lot of people to think about. It is almost literally being betrayed by your own mind. And that is scary. So the natural human tendency is - when not trying to intellectualize and quantify it - to dismiss it, often with dark humor. The outward manifestations of some mental illnesses can be extremely absurd to someone who is fortunate enough to be rational. Be it the coping mechanisms employed to self-sooth OCD or anxiety, the unusual world views of schizophrenia, or the violent outbursts caused by other disorders, these behaviors will often end up being the basis for parody in order to deflect how disturbing they can be. &quot;Crazy&quot; is easier to deal with if it becomes &quot;Wacky&quot;. <br /> Unfortunately, a lot of RPGs back in the 80s and 90s tended to conflate the two. White Wolf, to their credit, tried to skew toward Derangements as a source of horror in the Vampire core books, but unfortunately, the Malkavians, as the &quot;Kook&quot; clan, tended to get presented as wacky. This is, quite frankly, not helped much by the original Clanbook. While Tim Bradstreet's cover image is certainly striking and somewhat disturbing, the same can not be said for a lot of the other art in the book. The back cover of each of the Clanbooks had a small cropped image of part of a white-skinned vampire's face, serving as kind of a snap shot of the clan's image. On the back of Malkavian is a close up of a nose with a finger stuck up it. Even if a drop of blood is coming out of the nose, that's not a promising start. The interior art includes a large amount of efforts to mimic childish crayon scribbles, including what I can only presume is meant to be a deformed Jesus having a bowel movement. Combined with a number of other elements, including various bad puns, and pages printed upside down or backwards, this results in a book that is more &quot;wacky&quot; insanity than horror insanity. <br /> And all of this together is part of what I think led to what are commonly called &quot;Fishmalks&quot;. Basically, these are - usually Malkavian - characters who use their insanity as an excuse to disrupt the game and generally be a pain to all of the other players. I consider this to be a violation of the gamer social contract; Everyone is there to have fun, and if you are deliberately engaged in behavior that is meant to keep other people from enjoying the game, then you are violating that contract. <br /> So those are two things that are going to be very much on my mind as I go back and look at the original Malkavian Clanbook's premade templates. Is the way they present their insanity something that fits into what's supposed to be a Gothic Punk setting about Personal Horror? (IE, is this meant to be scary/disturbing, or goofy/wacky?) And is this the sort of character that is likely to end up being a disruptive pest to everyone else in the game?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-14T14:45:13-07:00","id":1388439,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Malkavian (part one)</b><br /> <br /> So, this book came out in 1993, fairly quickly after Gangrel. The book was written by Daniel Greenberg, who was, IIRC, the brother of at the time Vampire Developer Andrew Greenberg. And the art is once again by Josh Timbrook. Which is a good thing, because if they'd all been the crayon style drawings, I probably would've stopped doing this thread for a while. Two things of note. First is that none of the characters say with their Derangement actually is. A few are easy to guess, but others not so much. Second is that, if you are only familiar with the clan from their presentation in Revised edition and later, this predates the introduction of the Dementation discipline, so they all have Dominate.<br /> <br /> <b>The Mesmeric Manipulator </b><br /> Right off the bat, we get a really strong one. This is the daughter and assistant of a Victorian Era spiritualist who crossed a rival who turned out to be a Malkavian. She became her sire's assistant for several decades, but now that he's disappeared, she's on her own. And I like this character a lot. The character image is very striking, and possibly my favorite in the entire book. (Really, any time I've thought of this book over the past 25+ years, this has been the first image to come to mind.) It's also a really good concept that plays well with the clan's signature disciplines as well as various concepts of charismatic &quot;madmen&quot; and mesmerists. Her stats are, perhaps, a little wonky. Subterfuge is only one dot, though Acting is at two and Empathy at three. Weirdly, she has Firearms at two dots, so is more adapt at shooting people than tricking them. After a near absence of them in Gangrel, we get a return of secondary abilities. She has Seduction, Ventriloquism, and Slight of Hand, but only at one dot for the first two and two dots for the last one. I think I've come to the conclusion that if one is going to spend points in a secondary ability, one should only do so if you're willing to get it at three dots or higher. Given her modus operandi, I think her Occult should be higher than two, and Finance higher than one. Also standing out is her Willpower of only four. After two entire books of characters with WP of 6+, this is almost shocking. As I said, we don't get a Derangement listed, and it's hard to pick one that fits. I could see Delusions of Grandeur or possibly believing her dead father still talks to her as being very fitting. Overall, I think this is a really good idea that just needs a little statistical tweaking.<br /> <br /> <b>The Freak</b><br /> I'm not really sure where to begin with this one. Born deformed, abandoned at birth, shunted from foster home to foster home before ending up homeless on the streets and turned into a vampire. Then he joined a traveling freak show (and lived happily ever after ....). He's got a lot of piercings and performs assorted disturbing acts while grinning like a happy idiot. His quote is &quot;Blea!&quot; The character image is of a balding hunchback holding a bloody axe. Charisma and Appearance are both at one, with Manipulation at four. Stamina is at five. His abilities are mostly ones and twos, save a three in Brawl, including one dot each in the secondary abilities of Carousing (?), Scrounging, and Pickpocket. His Willpower is four, seeming to continue a trend. He also has three dots in Obfuscate, which only reinforces my knee jerk reaction of &quot;what is this stereotypical Nosferatu doing in my Malkavian book?&quot; This is the first character in this series that I honestly do not like. In fact, I may actually actively hate this character. As potentially tragic as this character could be, he quite frankly exists for no other reason than engage in antisocial behavior and disruptive antics. He has no reason to interact with wider vampire society, and little to no motivation to do much of anything outside his existence in the freak show. It's just bad on so many levels.<br /> <br /> <b>The Conspiracy Theorist</b><br /> Another classic '90s archetype. It'd be amusing to see this character and the Gangrel Survivalist in the same coterie. This is a normal run of the mill middle class American kid, until her parents died in a car accident when she was in college. And this caused a mental breakdown. She believed the accident was her fault, and that some malevolent force wanted to punish her for getting too close to their secrets. She sees patterns that (probably?) aren't there, making connections between people and events that (probably?) don't exist, all in service to building up this massive conspiracy that only she can see but can't figure out in order to expose them. Her sire was one of the doctors at the hospital she was sent to, and wanted her to continue investigating and explaining this conspiracy for him. Then he was killed (obviously by Them). Now she's on the loose, still trying to uncover the secrets of Them, and has apparently acquired a ridiculous amount of firepower in the process. (Seriously, her equipment includes a submachine gun, grenades, and tear gas and she has three dots each in Firearms and Melee.) She has five dots in Perception and four dots in Intelligence, but only one dot in Wits. She's also got four dots in Computer. And for some reason has a few dots in the secondary abilities of Hypnotism and Camouflage. I really like this character. Aside from some stat quibbles, it's a really strong character with an interesting hook, and would work really well with any sort of investigative oriented or Anarch focused coterie. I especially like the fact that her initial mental break is something that conforms to the way these things will often happen in real life, with a major traumatic event stressing the mind past its breaking point and the resulting mental illness being an effort to cope with that event. Overall, I'd have to put this in my top ten of all the ones so far.<br /> <br /> <b>The Waking Dreamer</b><br /> This is an odd one, and I recall being confused by it even back in 1993. I'm not entirely sure I'm less confused now. The character is a waif from a very sheltered and wealthy upbringing, whose increasing anxiety and lack of self-confidence caused her to retreat more and more into her dreams, to the point of near narcoleptic catatonia. Exactly why this happened isn't really explained, but from my layman's knowledge of psychology, it's possibly a manifestation of schizophrenia or (more depressing) PTSD from possible abuse. Either one would be interesting to explore, I think. (Also, while this is two years before the first edition of Changeling, it's entirely possible she could be a changeling trying to undergo their awakening into their true form, only for it to be suppressed - and finally quashed - by attempts at psychiatric treatment.) Eventually, her parents brought her to a Malkavian doctor, who used probably a mix of Auspex and Dominate to forcefully rip her out of her dream world (and possibly keep her from dreaming anymore when she slept). And then a few years later came back, undid his fix and embraced her to be a channel into the dream world. And when he was done with whatever he was doing, he left her on her own. Now she's kind of a perpetual sleepwalker stuck in a twilight world between two realities. She has the Child demeanor, mainly, I suspect, as a way to try to get other people to take care of her and keep her from having to face the scary real world. Her biggest abilities are Empathy and Stealth at four dots each, and Animal Ken, Dodge, and Melee at three each. The first two I can easily get behind; the Melee not so much. She's got Auspex at four dots. I think having at least one dot in Obfuscate would've been extremely fitting. Curiously, given the Changeling thing I mentioned above, she's got a dot in Faerie Lore. Her Humanity is eight, which is one of the first time's I've thought that actually felt appropriate. Overall, this is a strange character, but I don't dislike it. She's got a some interesting aspects to play around with, though I'd likely have to really trust a player before agreeing to let them play this character in a game. If the Babe in the Woods character from Gangrel had had more of a dark fairy tale feel to it, I think these two would've made an interesting pairing.<br /> <br /> That concludes part one. Next will be part two of Malkavian.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-15-2020, 08:55 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-14T16:24:56-07:00","id":1388477,"title":"","content":"<b>Brujah</b><br /> <br /> I actually appreciated the <b>Streetwalker</b> character because I think it's a very easy way to get Blood and feeding. Mind you, you're going to deal with a lot of sleazebags but, as you say, Potence is something that can very much help with things. Also, that is going to be useful when you want to feed more deeply on assholes.<br /> <br /> I think the <b>Skinhead</b> character was a poor choice but understandable. White Supremacist Brujah are almost certainly a thing but I think they'd be definitely better regulated to the role of villains or NPCs. The <b>Swords of Heimdall</b> in Werewolf ended up having to be killed off in-universe because RL white supremacists were using them to justify their beliefs in-game and at events.<br /> <br /> I love the <b>Fake Rapper</b> character as well, especially since Victor from <i>LA by Night</i> is based on Suge Knight (albeit far less bloodthirsty oddly enough).<br /> But as you say, very stereotypical characters. Not necessarily bad but very common ideas.<br /> <br /> <b>Gangrel</b><br /> <br /> A big fan of your description of the <b>Bold Urban Commando</b> and agree that player characters were expected to be Anarchs against the Man.<br /> <br /> <b>The Explorer</b> is a bit fun and my headcanon is the fact that they're a transman from the 1940s.<br /> <br /> I actually played the <b>Great White Hunter</b> in a game. My ST and I clashed about what to do with him but he is an immensely entertaining archetype to do. Also because he's someone who doesn't really &quot;fit&quot; in with the upper class of yesterday, let alone today, and is eager to Embrace the Beast to be something different.<br /> <br /> <b>Lupine Impersonator</b> makes no damn sense is nonfunctional even if it were possible because you'd be hanging with WEREWOLVES rather than a coterie. However, I think you could easily rework it as a Kinfolk who is now a vampire and trying to apply their culture to a bunch of Wyrmspawn.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-15T05:49:01-07:00","id":1388656,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1388477#post1388477\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Brujah</b><br /> <br /> I actually appreciated the <b>Streetwalker</b> character because I think it's a very easy way to get Blood and feeding. Mind you, you're going to deal with a lot of sleazebags but, as you say, Potence is something that can very much help with things. Also, that is going to be useful when you want to feed more deeply on assholes.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think an interesting angle with the Streetwalker would've been if she'd decided to use her newfound powers, especially Potence and Presence, to start taking on more of a &quot;managerial&quot; role, taking other streetwalkers under her &quot;protection&quot; and using them as her own Herd (as well as a source for Retainers and Resources) and also renting them out to other vampires for feeding. Basically the exploited becoming the exploiter motivated by her own passion for self-interest, survival, and sense of power. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think the <b>Skinhead</b> character was a poor choice but understandable. White Supremacist Brujah are almost certainly a thing but I think they'd be definitely better regulated to the role of villains or NPCs. The <b>Swords of Heimdall</b> in Werewolf ended up having to be killed off in-universe because RL white supremacists were using them to justify their beliefs in-game and at events. </div> </div> </div>This sort of weird focus on white racism, especially the Neo-National Socialist ilk, thankfully seemed to have died out at White Wolf around 1993 or 1994, as we never got anything about it in early Mage or Wraith stuff. My own thoughts on the Sword of Heimdall - and I rather wish most of the Fenrir camps had different names - is that they were originally a Camp devoted to the tribe's Kinfolk and their protection, guidance, and nurturing, focused heavily on encouraging marriages and large families, respect for mates and offspring, tight household and community bonds, and pride in one's heritage combined with responsibility for future generations. And at some point in the 19th century, it went off the rails. Which is a shame, because I think the above is a fun idea that both ties in to the tribe's concept while also somewhat subverting expectations. <br /> As for the Skinhead template, as I mentioned, it's a general concept that has a lot of potential for good Storytelling and character growth (the two movies I cite being good examples), but instead it's just kind of there as &quot;this is a bad person, and also he is a bad person.&quot; I think one of the other things that annoys me about that template is that, had the exact same character with the same motivations and back story been race-flipped and done as a Black Panther or member of the Nation of Islam, he'd have been presented in an almost heroic light, complete with a high Conscience and Humanity simply because the writer and target audience would've found him more sympathetic. But I find racial supremism and separatism to be morally repugnant and irritating no matter who is doing it. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Lupine Impersonator</b> makes no damn sense is nonfunctional even if it were possible because you'd be hanging with WEREWOLVES rather than a coterie. However, I think you could easily rework it as a Kinfolk who is now a vampire and trying to apply their culture to a bunch of Wyrmspawn. </div> </div> </div>I think from a 1st ed Werewolf/very early 2nd ed Vampire perspective, the character probably works OK as an example of what to do if you want to have a vampire in your werewolf pack. But I'm not sure she works all that great in a Vampire game, so having it as one of the example characters in a Vampire book is, to me at least, a minor strike against it. But in fairness, unless you had a book entirely about doing Vampire/Werewolf crossovers (and I really think there should've been) that included a bunch of templates like Lupine Impersonator, Interspecies Diplomat, Ghouled/Embraced Kinfolk, and Ronin Hound, there's really no other place to put it. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-15T05:52:35-07:00","id":1388658,"title":"","content":"My oh my, someone also like to use templates in his games \ud83d\ude42. But you forgot about pregens from &quot;Alien hunger&quot; and IMHO you should also write a word or two about pregens from V:tM 2nd. edition core rulebook and Players Guide. I know these are only stats and concepts but I think your interpretation of them could be interesting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-15T07:07:58-07:00","id":1388670,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Malkavian (part two)</b><br /> <br /> And once more into the breach.<br /> <br /> <b>The Raving Lunatic</b><br /> Oh, for the love of .... Remember how I said I think I might actually hate the Freak? Well, I think I hate this character even more. I've also noticed that it seems that each Clanbook includes a template (the Rapper and the Biologist) that could be considered a refinement of that Clan's character from the Players Guide. Well this could be considered that, only refined in the worst way possible. What was this character like before the embrace? No one knows. We get one line that he had a happy and fulfilling life until he got attacked by a crazy woman in an alley. That's it. He flips back and forth between a hyperactive, gibbering, screaming loon and a broody, muttering, paranoid skulker. Usually for no rhyme or reason. (That's actually part of the suggested roleplaying hints.) He doesn't really have any motivation or goals. His equipment includes a straight jacket, steak knives, inflatable beach toys (WTF?), battered old taxi, lock picks, and autographed photos of TV stars (again, WTF?). He's got Brawl and Dodge at three dots, and Firearms and Melee at two each. Also Lockpicking and Escapology at one each. I repeat my original post comment about wondering why anyone would buy a single dot of Lockpicking instead of just getting Security. He's got Resources of one. There must be a bag of money in the trunk of that taxi cab or something. And he has a Conscience of five and Humanity of seven. I really, really dislike this character. Aside from being a walking stereotype of mental illness, it is almost 100% certain to disrupt and derail most games and player coteries. I'm not even sure this character would work well in an early edition Sabbat game. It's just bad all the way around.<br /> <br /> <b>The Crazed Monster</b><br /> A life-long victim of abuse, in and out of youth homes and eventually jail, only to be gunned down during a robbery gone bad and her dying body dragged away by a sire who would subject her to several days of torture before the embrace. Now she just wants to make the world burn and inflict her own pain on anyone she comes across. This is not a terrible idea for a concept. It's just lacking in depth and room for growth or evolution into something more meaningful. It's sole roleplaying hint is &quot;speak in morbid nursery rhymes.&quot; That's it. And not a lot to go on. The character image is interesting. It's got this early 90s morbid grunge-not-quite-goth feel to it that I don't dislike. Her Strength and Stamina are both at four dots, with Dexterity at two. Manipulation is at five dots, with the other social traits at one each. That feels off, maybe. I think a case could be made for a 1/3/2 split with an optional extra dot from freebies. Abilities are Alertness, Athletics, Intimitation, and Melee at three each, with Brawl, Firearms, Stealth, Investigation, and Occult at two each. Occult feels out of place for some reason. Given the character's focus, having those two dots in the secondary ability of Toxicology seems more fitting. Somehow the character has a Herd of three. I've no idea how she pulled this off. Conscience is at a one and Humanity a five, which feels fitting, if maybe hard to play. I find myself wanting to like this character, but it's flaws are holding me back. It's another of those that I think would be a lot stronger if a little more effort had been expended in the write up and the stats tweaked a little.<br /> <br /> <b>The Moody Loner</b><br /> Another of those period stereotypes (and again, kind of more from the '70s), largely owing to both certain political assassinations and - especially in this case - the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Texas_tower_shooting\" target=\"_blank\">1966 University of Texas shooting incident</a>. The art is clearly inspired by Lee Harvey Oswald, right down to a shirt referencing him. If this hadn't been several years before Columbine High School, we'd probably have been subjected to an image of a teen in a trench coat. This is a guy who never fit in, but wanted to be one of those cool and heroic types. Secret agent, soldier, PI, whatever. Instead, he's ended up as a pawn in the Jihad (or whatever we're calling it now). The character is walled off from others mainly out of a fear of rejection, and is desperate for praise and affirmation, becoming devotedly loyal to any authority figure that seems to appreciate him. I have to give credit for this actually conforming to certain assessments of Oswald. This is actually a pretty good concept for a character and one that has some interesting potential. I just wish they hadn't leaned so heavily into the &quot;water tower sniper&quot; schtick. Seriously, his equipment list includes &quot;map of local water towers&quot;. And his highest ability is a four in Firearms. But otherwise he's fairly competent as a character sent out to do work for his sire, as one of the Sheriff's flunkies, or as an Anarch agent. And like our previous entry, he's got two dots in Herd, as well as a one in Conscience and five in Humanity. I feel the first one needs a bit of an explanation, while the other two should be higher so as to differentiate him from just being yet another serial killer. This is another one of those that I don't dislike, but don't really love either. There's a solid idea here, but I think it needs to be better refined.<br /> <br /> That's all for this one. Next will be the final three Malkavians, and then Clanbook: Nosferatu.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-15-2020, 03:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-15T14:13:32-07:00","id":1388760,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Malkavian (part three)</b><br /> <br /> The final three.<br /> <br /> <b>The Detached Scientist</b><br /> This is hands down my absolute favorite concept of the book, and probably in my top ten of all Vampire templates. This wonderful character is effectively the first appearance of what would become <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Douglas_Netchurch\" target=\"_blank\">Dr. Netchurch</a>, the Malkavian who featured heavily in <i>Ghouls: Fatal Addiction</i> and <i>Time of Thin Blood</i>. He is one of my all time favorite Vampire characters and my favorite Malkavian (and I dearly wish he'd been the revised era signature character instead of Anatole), largely because he perfectly fits the clan's entire concept perfectly while also taking it as far away from &quot;crazy = wacky&quot; or raving lunatics as you can get. As for this initial incarnation, what we have is a man who is, at heart, deeply emotional and straight forward, but has struggled continuously to keep those emotions in check through logic, rationality, and later science. He's the stereotypical straight-laced, uptight, by the book, I-dotter and T-crosser intellectual. But he's also constantly teetering on a knife edge, about to completely lose it in a storm of unchecked emotion. And for a vampire, that probably means a blood-soaked Frenzy. It's almost terrifying to think about. Stat-wise, he has five dots in Intelligence, which doesn't really feel out of place here. I could easily see this as one of those young genius types who was too smart for his own good, his IQ rapidly dwarfing his EQ (emotional quotient). Strangely, his abilities all max out at three dots, including both Medicine and Science. However, he has one dot in Psychology, bought with freebies. I just fail to understand why you wouldn't just buy the fourth dot in Science and have a specialization in Psychology. He's also got three dots each in Firearms, Stealth and Security, which effectively qualifies him to be a professional hitman or secret agent. I also think this character could benefit from having a few dots in Herd, as being a doctor at a mental hospital gives one free reign to prey on the patients from time to time. That said, this is still my favorite of the bunch, and I think with a few points moved around, it'd be close to perfect.<br /> <br /> <b>The Reality Buster</b><br /> So, if Gangrel's Lupine Impersonator was an effort to include a crossover character for Werewolf, I'm pretty sure this one is an attempt to do the same for Mage (the first edition of which had come out around the same time as this book). This character believes that Reality is subjective, but has likely been locked down into its fixed form by some conspiracy or other dangerous force in order to steal or harness its power. Young, hip, and full of youthful energy and exuberance, her equipment is basically a list of assorted high tech gadgets, measuring devices, and puzzle toys. In other words, the sort of stuff a lot of modern Mages - especially Orphans or hip early 90s technomancers - might use as foci. This really really feels like a first edition Mage character that someone statted up as a Vampire by mistake. And I have mixed feelings about it. This isn't a bad character. I think it would fit in fairly well with a coterie that skewed toward investigation and research, or maybe an LA based Anarch coterie that is more about just being who they are in the Free States instead of being the vanguard of the revolution. But at the same time, I feel like more could be done with the idea. This was also around the same time that the first English translation of Kult came out, and I could see the basic &quot;reality is an illusion made to imprison us&quot; idea leaning heavily into the Gnostic themes of archons and the Rex Mundi for a much darker and edgier take. Likewise if it leaned in to things like chaos magic or psychonautics and altered states of consciousness. Ability-wise, she's fairly solid, with a scattering of ones and twos across the sheet. Humanity is only six, though, which feels low for this character. I'm a little surprised she doesn't have the Meditation secondary ability. This is also a character who Cryptography actually wouldn't feel out of place with. Like I said, this isn't a bad character by any stretch. It just feels perhaps a bit lacking or maybe out of place.<br /> <br /> <b>The Mad Prophet</b><br /> And another from the collection of Stock Character Crazy People. This is basically the guy who stands on the street corner raving to anyone who'll listen - or who can't get away fast enough - about whatever their fixation is, be it the end of the world, aliens among us, or the secret conspiratorial alliance of the dairy farmers and the homosexuals, and how listening to him will save us all. I don't really like it. But the fact that it's such a stereotype is only the lesser reason I dislike it. This guy was your average upper middle class success story, having earned success with his work, family, and community. Then an illness that hit him with a high fever for a week (it says 103, but looking up stuff online, it appears that's not actually high enough to result in brain damage), and something apparently just broke. He couldn't do his job or continue going through the motions of his mundane, mechanized former life. So he tried to commit suicide-by-auto-accident, only to be saved and embraced. Now he's trying to shake, startle, and shock other people out of the walking, waking death that is custom, habit, and routine. And that's actually an interesting idea for a Malkavian character. I just fail to see why he has to be a stereotypical disheveled street-ranter to do it. If this was some sort of motivational speaker/guru type, attracting a cult following while wearing a respectable casual suit and giving public talks, he'd be a really awesome character, and probably one of my favorites. Like some sort of weird cross between Dale Carnegie and Jim Jones. His starts are OK, but occasionally wonky. He's got five dots in Charisma, one dot in Manipulation, and four dots in Appearance. I'm wondering if those last two were put in backwards. Physicals are secondary and Mental tertiary, with a 3/2/1 spread. And a one in Wits feels too low for this character. His Empathy and Leadership are three dots each, and Firearms, Melee, and Stealth are all two each. I don't think that fits the concept all that well. All other abilities are one dot, including Public Speaking. He has five dots in Disciplines (Auspex 2, Dominate 3), meaning that's where all his freebies went. Over all, this is one of those ideas that needed a bit of fine tuning, both conceptually and statistically. There's a good potential character here, but it's lost in the clutter.<br /> <br /> <br /> So, those are the Malkavians. They are kind of a mixed bag, and in a way, indicative of the problems that have plagued the Clan for the entire run of the game. Three of them - the Mesmeric Manipulator, the Conspiracy Theorist, and the Detached Scientist - are very good, and a fourth (the Waking Dreamer) almost makes that grade. Three of them are fairly solid, but could use some improvement, and a fourth one (the Mad Prophet) could use a lot. And two of them - the Freak and the Raving Lunatic - I quite frankly dislike a lot.<br /> For representation, we have an even split of male and female, which is a first. For the most part, they are white. It's a little hard to tell with Timbrook's art style, but it's possible that the Conspiracy Theorist and Mad Prophet may be black, and the Reality Buster Asian (but this may just be an aspect of the way he tends to draw facial features). No one is really sexualized in any way, save maybe the Mesmeric Manipulator, who is more sort of that pre-1970s exotically attractive look than hyper sexualized.<br /> I think my original additional idea back in the day was some sort of obsessed stalker type, who serially fixated on a person and wanted to become them. Also the idea of a cult leader and the classic Cassandra oracle archetype (which I'm a little surprised wasn't one of the ones used here). And maybe a masked vigilante.<br /> I think if the Malkavians ever had an over arcing theme, it was that of Mystery. Many of the truths of the human brain and the psyche are potentially unknowable, including certain aspects of madness. The world itself is full of mysteries, and the World of Darkness only more so. And I think the better Malkavian concepts play into that sense of mystery. The four templates here that I like certainly do, as does the Reality Buster. And I think the Mad Prophet and Moody Loner sit on the edge of that, needing only some minor alterations to be pushed in the right direction. The Crazed Monster could be one of those also, once the details are more filled in. In contrast, the two I dislike are extremely shallow and one-dimensional, lacking any sense of mystery whatsoever. They are what they are and nothing more.<br /> And finally, I'll just note that this is the only one of the original seven Clanbooks to not have its character templates in alphabetical order.<br /> <br /> Next, everyone's favorite monsters, the Nosferatu.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-15-2020, 06:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-16T11:51:13-07:00","id":1389016,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Nosferatu (part one)</b><br /> <br /> Our fourth entry is also from 1993, so the clanbooks were coming at a fairly brisk pace to start with. We get a striking cover that is clearly an homage to Max Shrek's Count Orlok in FW Murnau's 1922 classic Nosferatu. This book was written by Robert Hatch, who would later go on to be Vampire: The Masquerade Developer during the later part of 2nd ed and the creation of the Revised edition. Our character template art is once again by Josh Timbrook. I'm glad to see this continue, as I think it helps give a certain level of continuity to the whole thing and ensures that these characters seem like they all come from the same world. <br /> <br /> <b>The Cleopatra</b><br /> Right away we start with an idea that plays against type - the Nosferatu with a social focus - while also being built off of the more common idea that the clan will often seem to embrace certain people purely out of a sense of spite. I generally like this idea, as it gives players a reason for playing the sort of character that doesn't seem to fit in with the clan and its image while not having to come up with an elaborate or even convoluted justification for why they are in that clan. In out case, we have an archetype of the time - perhaps of most times - with a woman who has always been able to go through life and get her way purely with her looks. And while this is a bit of a gender stereotype, I think this concept works just as well with your stereotypical hot guy hunk pretty boy. Prior to the embrace, this character was a corporate executive who'd made it a rung or three up the ladder. We don't really get any indication of what the character is doing after being embraced and her life ruined, other than to say that she's very bitter, but with her traits, I can easily see her managing to carve a place out for herself both continuing to make money (she has two dots each in Bureaucracy and Finance) and in the halls of Elysium, even as - dare I say it this early in the game's run when it was still a stereotypical Toreador role - a Harpy. Seriously, she's got five dots in Charisma and four in Manipulation and Wits, as well as four dots in Subterfuge and three each in Acting, Empathy, Etiquette, and Style. Her Willpower is a nine (!) and her Demeanor is Survivor. Her three dots in Obfuscate means she can still operate around mortals, and her backgrounds even include one dot in Status, which makes me think that she actually is meant to be a Harpy. If so, I rather wish that'd been spelled out more clearly in the write up. My only real quibbles about his character is that she has a Perception of one, which I think is too low, and a Conscience of one as well, which is more debatable. I think I would've liked to see her have a Retainer as well, again to play a bit against the clan stereotype (perhaps using the points spent on that single dot in the Body Alteration ability). But this is a very strong character and one that looks like it would be very interesting to play. I like it a lot. A very promising start for the book. <br /> <br /> <b>The Derelict </b><br /> This one plays very heavily into the clan stereotype, in this case, that of homeless bag lady. Out character was your average suburban working mom who got laid off from her job during a recession. Her depression, combined with the severe shift in the family's financial situation, caused her husband to leave her and take their daughter with him. She became an alcoholic and eventually homeless. Giving up, she tried to commit suicide in a back alley, only for a Nosferatu to feed on her and end up embracing her out of some twisted sense of guilt and remorse. She hates her existence even more now, to the point where it has at least deadened her previous sorrow and regret. Trait-wise, she's Mental primary with a 3/3/4 spread, and two dots each in Bureaucracy, Computer and Finance. Also the &quot;street survival&quot; abilities of Stealth and Survival at three dots and Alertness and Streetwise at two dots. Also twos in Drive, Etiquette and Repair. I'd really love to know where that Drive and Repair came from. Generally speaking, this is an interesting character background, and a character who seems to have the traits to go in more than a few directions. The problem is that, as it stands, she's just sort of there, kind of spinning her wheels and not going anywhere. She was homeless - and if I had to guess, may have had issues with depression and possible alcoholism even before losing her job - and is now a vampire. And I guess is just going to stay a bag lady? Out of some sense of bitter cynicism or just to wallow in her own misfortune, maybe? Which I guess works, at least for a little while. But with her abilities and the fact that the Nosferatu kind of have their own separate underground - sometimes literally - subculture even within the subculture that is the Kindred, it feels like there should be multiple opportunities for her to do more. A street agent for one of the elders, or one of the people who helps a warren make sense of and exploit all the paperwork and notes they scavenge from corporate and government trash bins. This isn't a bad character. I just feel like there should maybe be a little more here. <br /> <br /> <b>The Equalizer </b><br /> This is another of those late 80s/early 90s archetypes. Our character is a small town guy who grew up in the closet and was able to come out in college, especially when getting involved with various gay rights groups. Unfortunately, he and his boyfriend were beaten in an attack, the latter fatally. The character only survived because a Nosferatu embraced him. Now he's determined to use his newfound strength to protect the outcast and oppressed members of society, human and Kindred alike. He's actually a Mental primary character, which fits his background, with Physicals as secondary and augmented by an extra dot from freebies. Besides his mortal holdover abilities - Language at three and Politics at two, with a few ones scattered about - he's also got his newly acquired twos in Brawl, Dodge, and Melee. His Humanity is a nine, which I think is too high, but not too much as I think this is a character who could actually get away with having an eight. Strangely, he only has a single dot in each of the clan Disciplines. If any character was going to have a three in Potence, I'd have expected it to be this guy. I like this character a lot, and I recall him being one of my favorites even back when this book came out. Even beyond the desire to strike back against bullies - even if only vicariously through an RPG - the Nosferatu are at heart a bunch of outcasts, even in the eyes of the other Kindred. People see them as monsters purely because of their looks, and I can see how this can echo very strongly with the idea of people being treated as less than human simply because of their sexual orientation. This is a character that takes the Nosferatu and make them work as a direct metaphor for a social topic, and does so in a way that neither beats you over the head with it nor abandons anything about what makes the clan or the game itself what it is. Whether a vampire looks like a deformed monster or a beautiful angel, it's ultimately their Humanity and the actions related to it that define them, much in the way that how a human being treats others is a far better measure of character than their level of social acceptability. It's a theme I like, and it's a big part of why I love this character so much. <br /> <br /> That's all for part one. I'm going to try to get four of them in to the second part. <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-17T16:20:50-07:00","id":1389485,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Nosferatu (part two)</b><br /> <br /> More monsters.<br /> <br /> <b>The Gross Childe</b><br /> This is one of those names with a double meaning, being both a Childe in the Kindred sense and a child in the literal &quot;this is a kid turned into a vampire&quot; sense. By the time of the Revised era, there was a move away from the idea of children being embraced. It was already kind of frowned upon, but Revised went and spelled out a number of reasons why. It's a very serious potential Masquerade weakness, though that is perhaps not as big an issue with the Nosferatu, and is also seen as an act of cruelty, condemning someone to eternal unlike in a child's body. I'm not really sure this character really cares about that. This is your basic elementary school gross out artist and class clown who never really outgrew that phase in middle school. But where most would've been lucky enough to mature and turn this into a future career as a special effects makeup artist, horror film maker, or what have you, this kid was unfortunate enough to be embraced by a Nosferatu who found his antics entertaining. And like I said, he doesn't really care. In fact, the idea of being a gross looking monster for all eternity sounds down right fun. This is one of those characters I have mixed feelings about. One the one hand, I like the idea of something that plays into the idea that the the Nosferatu are gross and disturbing and will sometimes even revel in it in a &quot;shock the mundanes&quot; kind of way, and this character plays into that in a kind of odd and unique way. On the other hand, there's not a tremendous amount of depth here. That's not necessarily a bad thing, as the character is essentially a child. But given his focus, I can easily see this character being an outlet for certain problem players, effectively being the Nosferatu equivalent of the FishMalks I mentioned at the start of that book. Statistically speaking, he's pretty good. A Wits of five, a Manipulation and Dexterity of four each. Also a Charisma of one, which might be a little crippling in the long run. Three dots each in Stealth, Subterfuge, Dodge, and Toxicology (which is quite frankly disturbing, but in a good way). He's got a pet snake that has apparently gotten bigger after being fed his blood, making him the only character in the book with an animal Retainer. (The book spent a bit of space talking about Nosferatu spawning pits, so I'm a little surprised by that.) He has a Willpower of ten. I find that maybe too high, but I will admit it fits a little with certain aspects of the character's dogged commitment to his interests and lifestyle even in the face of all opposition. All in all, I like this character, but I'm very wary of it being abused by problem players. It's kind of like the Malkavian Waking Dreamer in that I'd have to really trust a player before letting them do this. I also kind of wish it had gone in a little bit more of a darker slant that the Toxicology ability seems to hint at. But it's still a good one.<br /> <br /> <b>The Leatherface</b><br /> Damn. This may be the most unsubtle in your face thing in the entire series of clanbooks. It is the huge hulking brute of a murder machine popularized by the 80s Slasher Movie genre. The artwork is strange, barely looking like anything recognizably human. I don't even think it's wearing clothes. The write up describes a childhood of unimaginable abuse from its parents, including some indication that the father may have actually been a more conventional real world type of serial killer. Naturally, this character had a total psychotic break, believing that the near by River was talking to it, and eventually murdering its parents. A Nosferatu found it before the authorities did. Now it exists only to inflict on others the pain it felt in life. And that's entirely what its traits revolve around. Fours in Strength, Stamina, Manipulation, and Perception, as well as Intimidation and Melee. Three each in Brawl and Stealth. And in spite of a total lack of education, one dot each in Police Procedure and Serial Killer Trivia. I'll have to go hunting for where the Trivia secondary ability first appeared, but this book leans into it strongly, with three characters having it for whatever subculture they're a part of. I'm really surprised it doesn't have a few dots in Taxidermy. This is the only character to have an out of clan Discipline, with a single dot in Fortitude, which does make sense on a certain level. Conscience is one, Humanity five, and Willpower at nine. This is one of those characters that is a cool idea, but is extremely difficult to integrate into a game and coterie. All it does is kill, in as brutal a fashion as possible. And, in addition to potential risk to the Masquerade, I don't see why such a character would associate with other vampires. At best, I can see it lurking among the edges of the Nosferatu's underground world near its beloved River, but not even they go near it. This is a really great NPC, but not so great of a player character, at least in my opinion. As much as I'm a fan of the 80s Slasher films, I I think this character would've benefited from hewing a little closer to the real world murderers like Ed Gein who initially inspired them and taking a slightly subtler route. Incidentally, I keep referring to this character as &quot;it&quot; because there is no indication of what its gender is/was. I've actually always wanted to see the slasher film Brute/Masked Murder Engine archetype done with a female character. (Like a lot of horror fans my age, I was disappointed there was never a follow up on the ending of Halloween 4 that clearly planned to go in that direction.) Overall, I have to classify this as a good idea that doesn't quite work right.<br /> <br /> <b>The Luchador Profesional </b><br /> I won't lie, this is my favorite one in the book. (The Equalizer comes close, but my love for professional wrestling makes this the winner.) This is your archetypical masked Mexican wrestler. And much in the way that the Detached Scientist birthed Dr. Netchurch, this one birthed <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/El_Diablo_Verde\" target=\"_blank\">El Diablo Verde</a>. At first glance, it might seem that a professional wrestler, even a masked one, is out of place in Vampire. However, Mexico has a long tradition through the 1950s, '60s and '70s of wrestling films in which masked heroes fought vampires, werewolves, mummies and other monsters (as well as gangsters and other enemies of the people), and I find this a fun nod to that. Our character was a champion selected to be part of a mass Sabbat embrace, only to be rescued by Camarilla vampires. Now he wrestles in the US, and I suspect still smashes the enemies of the Camarilla when necessary. He's got five dots in Strength and Charisma, and fours in Stamina and Brawl. I'm a little surprised his Athletics is only at a two. All his discipline points are in Potence. He's got Fame, Herd, and Resources all based around his career as a professional wrestler. (While it may seem odd for a Nosferatu, it's traditional for masked Mexican wresters to never remove their masks in public, and even in the presence of anyone.) As I said, I like this character a lot. He's a little over the top, but he's a really interesting subversion of the Nosferatu stereotype who can fill a number of roles in a chronicle or coterie.<br /> <br /> <b>The Morbid Artsy Type Vindictively Embraced</b><br /> (I've always felt those last two words were somehow meant to be in parenthesis for some reason.) So, what we have here is essentially a somewhat mean spirited stereotype of the '90s suburban Goth, ie, the presumed target audience for the Vampire RPG. When I was graduating high school when this book originally came out, I found this character fairly amusing. But now, over 25 years later, I'm not longer very impressed when I see creators disparage - or even express outright hatred towards - their customers for not conforming to their ideal of what's cool or in fashion at the moment. While this character has something in common with Brujah's Fake Rapper, it differs in that the Fake Rapper takes the time to put its character in an interesting situation (having to continuously play an invented role), gives him some useful skills, and potential for growth. The Morbid Artsy Type lacks that. His artistic ability and occult knowledge aren't as impressive as he believes them to be, and it strait out says he's not good for much of anything. It's a character that exists for no reason other than to be made fun of. But adding to this is that the stats on the character sheet don't exactly match up with this. He's got five dots in both Manipulation and Perception, which is really impressive. Acting, Alertness, and Subterfuge are all at three dots, and Etiquette and Stealth are at two (as is Occult and Literature). These stats are for someone who'd fit in pretty well at Elysium, and actually be kind of dangerous as he lurks about largely unnoticed, able to eavesdrop on others and drop in certain comments to drive things in a certain direction. I wish the write up had reflected or acknowledged this. Or possibly have sown the seeds of the embrace having a sort of galvanizing effect for him to eventually evolve into a classic Byronic (anti)hero. So, over all, this is an OK character, but with some work and less of a fixation on mocking a certain segment of the fanbase would've been a lot stronger and more interesting.<br /> <br /> That's it for part two. Next will be part three, with the final three templates and finishing thoughts.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-18-2020, 08:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-18T12:20:59-07:00","id":1389724,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Nosferatu (part three)</b><br /> <br /> And our final three.<br /> <br /> <b>The Shock Rocker </b><br /> This one is right up their with the Equalizer and the Luchador Professional as my favorites in the book, and again, this owes a little bit to my own interests and tastes. But it's also ties into the way it reflects the clan. What we have is the archetypical heavy metal rocker whose act is based more on spectacle than on musical genius. Effectively the likes of KISS, Alice Cooper, and certain stories about Ozzy Osborne taken in a heavy splatterpunk horror direction. This is just about the time Marylin Manson was first starting to become known outside of Florida, and that is probably why it plays more on late 80s metal tropes than 90s ones. The character was a young teen metalhead who dreamed of emulating his idols, but has a much better talent for eliciting shock reactions from people with his antics than in actually playing music. But he certainly put in the work to be the best shock spectacle he could be, and its always nice to see someone succeed at doing what they love. He attracted some non-human fans, and one of them decided to embrace him. And both he and his bandmates have taken the change pretty well. If anything, it's made him even better at his chosen career. His Charisma is maxed out at five dots, and his Manipulation isn't far behind at four. His two best abilities are Intimidation and Leadership. Music is only one dot, but Acting is two, which makes sense, but so are Melee and Drive for some reason. Everything else is a scattering of ones, including things like Finance, Politics, Style, and Body Alteration. (That last one is something that showed up in the Players Guide to the Sabbat - which I'll have to talk about soon - mainly as a skill to use with the Vicissitude discipline, but also slightly related to body piercing, tattooing, and plastic surgery.) He's got one dot in each clan Discipline, as well as an extra one in Presence, which makes complete sense even though it's usually the last power you'd expect a Nosferatu to cultivate. He's got a lot more background points than average, with threes each in. Allies, Contacts, and Herd, as well as two in Resources and one in Fame. Humanity is only six, which feels low. Willpower is five. As I said, I really like this character. He's fun in a way that is over the top, and while I might restrict this to a player I trusted not to FishMalk it up, it does at least come with a built in break in that it says if people start to ignore his antics, he tends to quietly sulk rather than up the ante. While I think a few trait adjustments would make some improvements, he's fairly solid, and I like the fact that he does not fall into the stereotype of Nosferatu being miserable at their appearance.<br /> <br /> <b>The Tunnel Crawler</b><br /> This was a life-long quiet and studious nerd. Eventually he became a fairly brilliant engineer and architect. Too brilliant, as a warren of Nosferatu decided they needed his skill and genius. Now instead of erecting skyscrapers, he's digging catacombs into the earth. And he seems to be fairly OK with this. His Intelligence and Perception are both at four dots, as is his Stamina. Alertness, Repair, Computer, Science, and Security are all at three dots, with the secondary ability of Architecture at five dots. He's got the one dot per clan Discipline thing again, and with an additional dot in Auspex that, while interesting, I don't think is really needed. Humanity is at eight, which feels fitting given how sheltered he was prior to the embrace. Generally, this character is OK. There's nothing noticeably bad about it. However, I think it's biggest flaw is that this character lives totally underground, and has no reason at all to go above ground or interact with any non-Nosferatu vampires. If you're doing an all Nosferatu chronicle, that's probably OK, but for anything else, not so much. It also leans heavily into the idea that the Nosferatu seem to exist entirely underground, and I wish it had played a little more with the idea of various urban buildings having secret passages and byways that Nosferatu have snuck into the blueprints and then erased from the records as a way to move around the cities and places of power without being seen or other vampires knowing about it. So while this is not a bad character, I feel it could've been a lot stronger and more interesting.<br /> <br /> <b>The Vet</b><br /> Another period archetype. And, once again, I'm pretty certain that this one is essentially just a refinement of the one presented in the Players Guide. The initial part of the prelude is a little off, as it describes the typical Vietnam era stereotype of the poor kid that couldn't go to college or get a draft deferment and ended up fighting in the jungles of ... Africa. Unless this guy was part of an air crew on one of the less than half a dozen transport planes that provided logistical support to Congolese troops during that nation's civil war - and the write up makes it clear that isn't the case - I'm left to conclude that either the history of the WoD is even more different from ours than I ever thought, or that Rob Hatch was sick the day they covered the difference between Asia and Africa in geography class. But that aside, this is the stereotypical Vietnam era vet coming back to a country that didn't really want to acknowledge his existence, could't really keep a job, and ended up homeless. And then a vampire. In spite of it probably being a decade or more since he saw combat, all of his abilities are geared around being a soldier. Alertness, Brawl, Dodge, Melee, Stealth, and Survival are all at three dots, with Firearms at four. Anything else, including Streetwise, is just a one dot afterthought. His backgrounds include a one dot Retainer, but no mention of who or what that is or how that came about. His equipment is an Army jacket, combat boots, aviator shades, a .45 revolver and an M-16. I'm not sure how a homeless guy walks around with an M-16 and no one notices. Or why this is more useful than the Rambo-style survival knife. On some level, I think maybe this character is supposed to be Rambo, at least as he was in the original <i>First Blood</i> film. And while that's not a terrible idea, I think that sort of thing would need some stat tweaking and more description of his personality in the write up. So, again, this isn't a bad character, but I think it could've been a little better with some more thought put into it.<br /> <br /> And those are our Nosferatu. Overall, a fairly strong showing, I think. Three of them I really enjoyed, and two others, the Cleopatra and Gross Childe, I like a lot. Four of them I think are good, but could've been great with just a little more work and focus. Overall, I think the weakest one is the Leatherface, as it is the hardest to incorporate into a coterie or chronicle, and is less of a character than it is a plot device. So that's a good collection.<br /> In terms of representation, only two of our characters are expressly female, and one of them was embraced to punish her for being a good looking but petty person. (The same could probably also be said for the Morbid Artsy Type.) However, as I mentioned, it's really impossible to guess what gender the Leatherface is/was. And by the same token, it's also impossible to guess what race or ethnicity any of the characters were in their living days, save for the Luchador Professional, who is Mexican. It would probably be a safe guess, though, that the Morbid Artsy Type and the Shock Rocker were probably white. And finally, the Equalizer is, to my knowledge, the first of our character templates to be explicitly LGBT+.<br /> Given how much space the clanbook devotes to certain aspects of Nosferatu culture - including their archetypical role as spies and collectors of information, as well as their monstrous spawning pools and certain artistic endeavors - I'm a little surprised that none of those characters really deal with any of that. There is no Lorekeeper, or Spy Master, or Professional Blackmailer, or Garbage Collector (similar to what The Penguin did in Tim Burton's <i>Batman Returns</i>). No Phantom of the Opera style singer with the face of a monster and the voice of an angel. And, though this is probably before people had heard of the hobby, no urban explorer either. I'm also a little surprised we don't have a character who takes full advantage of the clan's affinity for Animalism. I think one of those Crazy Cat Lady types who lurks alone in a creepy house with two dozen cats would be an interesting idea.<br /> I think the Nosferatu theme is basically that of the Outcast. They don't fit in. They are markedly different from other Kindred, and are kept apart from mundane humanity by their curse. And many of them were probably outcasts even before the embrace. Look at our collection of characters. The homeless, the unwanted veterans who reminded everyone of America's losing war, the homosexuals, the helpless victims of abuse, the socially awkward misfits and nerds, and those who are too self-involved to understand how isolated they are from others. Even El Diablo Verde has set himself apart from others the moment he decided to put on the mask and devote himself to the role of masked champion. And so do the alternate ideas I mentioned above. I think this sense of being an outcast and apart from others is part of what makes a great Nosferatu character, and all of these characters have that, even if some of the executions are flawed.<br /> <br /> And so that concludes Clanbook: Nosferatu. Next, I will be looking at something that isn't a clanbook, the <i>Players Guide to the Sabbat</i>, which also came out in 1993.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-18-2020, 08:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-18T16:20:02-07:00","id":1389774,"title":"","content":"<b>The Players Guide to the Sabbat (part one)</b><br /> <br /> So, 1993 also saw the release of our first look at the dreaded and feared Sabbat and made them playable. There's been debate in some quarters ever since if this was a good idea or not. This book was entirely written by Steven C. Brown, and the template art was once again by Josh Timbrook. I'm not sure how he got the gig as &quot;guy who does all the template art&quot;, but it's a nice bit of continuity.<br /> The Sabbat are a little different. For one, they encompass almost all the clans (meaning I'm going to be talking about a few of them before I really wanted to), including the all new in this book Lasombra and Tzimisce. In addition, the Gangrel are split in to two separate groups: the Country Gangrel, who are basically the same as normal Gangrel, and the City Gangrel, who get a different Discipline spread (Celerity and Obfuscate instead of Animalism and Fortitude) but keep the same weakness. The Venture also get Auspex in place of Presence, apparently with the reasoning that they're not allowed to be in charge because &quot;reasons.&quot; (Basically, because they are associated with the Camarilla and being &quot;The Man.&quot; This same sort of specious logic would also be behind the Ventrue being omitted from Veil of Night and Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom, on the basis of them somehow being so totally associated in the minds of the writers and developers with Europe that they just couldn't conceive of them in any other context, like say, Persia.) The Setites are the Serpents of Light, and are Caribbean instead of Egyptian, which somehow makes them more heroic or something. (I've honestly long wished that name had instead been used for a group that tied into the idea of the Serpent in the Garden and the Lightbringer to be some sort of Setite sect devoted to Luciferian Satanism. But I'm weird.) And we have the Panders, who are just Caitiff with a cool name and a sense of belonging.<br /> The Sabbat get no background dots, but get an extra dot in Disciplines, because they are more focused on being vampires than pretending to be mortal. I'm not sure why they didn't just keep the existing character rules (or knock them down to three) and insist that Sabbat characters try to limit their backgrounds to things like Generation, Sabbat Status, Pack Recognition, Black Hand Membership, and Mentor. Instead of Humanity, all characters follow one of the new Paths of Enlightenment, devoted to certain inhuman and esoteric values. And to reflect that, the Conscience and Self-Control virtues are replaces with Callousness and Instinct.<br /> <br /> <b>The Assamite <i>antitribu</i> Assassin</b> (aka Mercenary)<br /> This one is both very alliterative and very low hanging fruit. Basically the very first idea anyone ever thought of with the 1st and 2nd ed Assamites and with their antitribu in this book. A street thug and leg breaker who ran off to join the Foreign Legion to avoid a murder rap. Then he came back to Alabama of all places and tried to open a survivalist camp, only to have a vampire hire him to kill someone as a test of his skills before embracing him. He's basically your stock Black Hand recruit, skilled at what he does, but kind of arrogant about it and a bit of a bully. The concept write up says it's unusual that he's not Middle Eastern. (I'd really forgotten how much I disliked that aspect of the pre-Clayton Oliver Assamites.) Naturally, all of his Abilities revolve around being a KillNinja, with three dots each in Brawl, Dodge, Intimidation, Firearms, and Melee, and two dots in Stealth. Everything else is a scattering of ones. Physicals are primary. For some odd reason he has a 3/2/1 spread in Socials. I guess it's to make him more intimidating. He's got two dots in Celerity and one each in Obfuscate and Quietus. Again, KillNinja. Weirdly, while the Assamite <i>antitribu</i> write up claims almost every member follows the Path of Caine, with rare exceptions on the Path of Honorable Accord, this one follows the Path of Power and the Inner Voice. This was often one of the Paths singled out for being &quot;The Path of Whatever I Was Going To Do Anyway&quot;, which makes it oddly fitting for a KillNinja. I guess this character is OK. He's very one dimensional, and like I said, the most obvious character idea for this clan. Weirdly, when I started reading the write up, I kind of got the idea that he might be French for some reason, at least until it said he came back home to the States, and I don't know why but I find him more interesting as French. Generally, I just find this character kind of blah. (Although, if someone playing this character decided to go by the handle of M. Pierre KillNinja, I'd be highly amused.)<br /> <br /> <b>City Gangrel <i>antitribu</i> Animal Trainer </b><br /> This is a middle aged widow who took up raising animals to sell as pets and meat (I'm guessing this is mainly rabbits, but there's no telling). Some mysterious woman who was also interested in animals struck up a friendship with her, and eventually inducted her into the Sabbat. She holds herself very much aloof from her pack, still living her her old home apart from them, and resenting when she sends her animals to help the pack and they get killed. (I have to presume she also raises dogs, but I can't help but imagine a Sabbat pack being aided by a herd of angry, blood thirsty rabbits and chickens.) She is a total Loner, with that as both Nature and Demeanor. Her highest abilities are Alertness, Animal Ken, and Science at three dots each. Alertness, Brawl, Dodge, Streetwise, and Survival are all at two. She's on the Path of Harmony, which is kind of the stereotypical &quot;creature of nature and instinct&quot; one that Gangrel <i>antitribu</i> prefer. Her Disciplines are one in Celerity, and two each in Obfuscate and Protean. Now, before I mention it, can you spot what is missing here? This is a character built around the idea of raising a bunch of animals and training them to aid the pack, and she lacks any ability with Animalism. I have this suspicion that this character was supposed to be a Country Gangrel, with the usual Disciplines, but someone decided that they needed to showcase the new City Gangrel variant, and just changed it, forgetting this little detail. (I'd prefer to believe this, as sometimes these things happen to writers, but also because the alternative is that someone actually sat down and wrote a Vampire template entirely based around a Discipline the character doesn't have.) All of that said, I actually rather like this character. I just think that she would be a lot better as a regular Gangrel character in the Camarilla, Anarchs or just unaligned than part of the Sabbat. The Sabbat originally drew a lot of inspiration from the film <i>Near Dark</i>, and that film has always influenced how I tended to see them. The pack should be the center of a Sabbat's world, and the focus of their night to night existence. And it isn't for this one. While I could possibly see a scenario where this character's story arc focusing on her leaving the Sabbat and maybe regaining her Humanity, the write up doesn't really set that up. So this isn't a bad character. I just think it's in the wrong book.<br /> <br /> That's all for this time. Next I'll try to cover at least three of four more of the Sabbat.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-18-2020, 10:01 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-19T14:05:52-07:00","id":1390058,"title":"","content":"<b>The Players Guide to the Sabbat (part two)</b><br /> <br /> And continuing right along.<br /> <br /> <b>The Lasombra Manipulator</b> (aka Leader Wannabe)<br /> So, the Lasombra were presented as the Clan focused on leadership and intrigue, and this character leans into that idea pretty hard. Presented as a spoiled kid who grew into a manipulative corporate salesman, only to get embraced after taking advantage of what turned out to be a Sabbat pack. He's adapted fairly well, being a competent and brave leadership figure for his pack who takes the time to develop personal rapport with each member. The kind of guy you'd probably want to have in charge of your group, and his stats tend to back up this description. Which is why I'm a little puzzled at the character sheet listing his concept as &quot;Leader Wannabe&quot; instead of something like &quot;Smooth Talking Politico&quot; or the like. The term wannabe tends to imply that someone is trying to be something they clearly aren't. But that aside, his stats aren't bad. He's got a Manipulation of five, natural, with Charisma, Intelligence and Wits at three. Leadership, Subterfuge, and Finance (a hold over from his mortal life that still gets put to use from time to time) are all at three dots. Empathy, Drive, Streetwise, Etiquette, and Investigation are all at two dots, and there's a scattering of ones. His only combat related abilities are one dot each in Dodge and Firearms (and the write up spends a weird amount of detail on just what guns he carries and in which circumstances), which might be a bit of a disadvantage among the Sabbat. However, he does have that two in Drive, so I have this mental image of him being the driver when the Pack decides to do drive-by or other activities. He's got seven dots in backgrounds, which again raises my earlier question about why they didn't just give Sabbat characters limited background points, since everyone seems to end up having them. Willpower is at an eight, and he follows the Path of Power and the Inner Voice, which seems fitting. Overall, this is your textbook &quot;the Lasombra are leaders of the Sabbat so this Lasombra is a pack leader&quot; character, which isn't necessarily a bad thing. He is what he is and seems to do it fairly well. I would however, love to hear the story of how a young sales department type ended up looking like a member of a 90s Goth band wearing fishhooks as earrings. <br /> <br /> <b>The Malkavian <i>antitribu</i> Mime</b> (aka Lunatic)<br /> I approached this character with a great deal of trepidation. Anything involving Malkavians as clowns or clown-related, including mimes, is often a surefire sign of a FishMalk. Having the character sheet concept say &quot;Lunatic&quot; on top of that just makes it worse. So, our character was always &quot;strange&quot; and ended up in a home for wayward boys at the age of 10. Apparently having &quot;violent impulses beyond [his] control,&quot; he killed another kid and ended up in a mental hospital. I'm left to conclude that &quot;strange&quot; here is a euphemism for &quot;killed small animals and lit buildings on fires.&quot; Some Sabbat decided it would be amusing to break into the hospital and let all the patients out to roam free, and he took advantage of this to apparently murder several staff members. The Sabbat decided this meant he would make a perfect recruit. Most of the times, he's supposed to be fairly normal, kind and gentle, having insightful conversations with pack mates. And then every so often he gets a wild hair up his ass to dress up as a mime and brutally murder people. Apparently because a mime scared him as a child. Let that sink in for a minute. He's Mental primary and Physical secondary, and his abilities are about what you'd expect from a competent Sabbat pack member. Three dots each in Melee and Stealth, and twos in Brawl, Empathy, Intimidation, and Survival, with a few more ones to balance it out. And three dots in Acting for the Mime thing, I guess. Or maybe just to try to appear normal. His Disciplines are two each in Auspex and Demenetation, and one in Obfuscate. He's on the Path of Harmony, which generally fits his personality. Except for the mime thing, which violates several of its basic sins. I almost but not quite come close to hating this character. The mime thing is a blatant excuse to FishMalk. But I think what most irks me about this character is that, if you just ditched the stupid mime bit, this would actually be a pretty good character. Someone who has always been, in a sense, broken, and is now in a clan and a sect/cult that is only making him more broken, but who has ironically been thrown this lifeline in the form of the Path of Harmony. That path focuses on the idea that these violent impulses he's always struggled with aren't automatically &quot;bad&quot; or &quot;evil&quot;, only that they must be tempered and only unleashed when necessary. And if he's around a pack that mainly follows this path, he has a peer support group who understand him in a way that family and doctors and the like never would have. That would, in my opinion at least, have been a really cool angle and a way of turning some of the perceptions of the Sabbat on its head. But they had to go and screw it up with the whole &quot;look, a killer mime! LOL&quot; thing. And that's why I come so close to hating this character. If you dropped the mime thing and made him someone akin to, say, Norman Bates, who is usually normal and personable but occasionally has these psychotic breaks, and was trying to struggle with how these outbursts interfere with his following the Path of Harmony, I think you'd have a really strong and potentially tragic character. Instead, we get a mime. <br /> <br /> (I actually don't dislike the mime character art. If this was something other than a &quot;look how kooky the Malkavians are&quot; character, I might actually like it. I could see some sort of character who was just a street mime who worked in the parks, only to get snatched up as part of a mass embrace and is now part of the Sabbat without really wanting to be, perhaps using the mime make up as a way to disassociate themselves from the horrors they now commit. And I think such a thing would have made an interesting City Gangrel, Ravnos, Toreador, Ventrue or Pander concept.)<br /> <br /> Because of real world time constraints, that's all for this entry. Next will be more Sabbat.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-19T17:53:24-07:00","id":1390127,"title":"","content":"Solid continuation of your reviews, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar12890_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12890\" data-vbnamecard=\"12890\">No One of Consequence</a>!<br /> <br /> You've done a bang-up job here and I don't know what to add other than EL DIABLO VERDE IS THE BEST PONY.<br /> <br /> I will note that the early editions did have a lot of very obvious archetypes that I don't think were added to expand a player's imagination but simply say, &quot;Okay, you probably want to make this character so here is his stats already made up. Congratulations! We saved you some time.&quot;<br /> <br /> Leatherface remains my favorite because I am an enormous Slasher fan and even wrote a book about them.<br /> <br /> I think the Shock Rocker is underrated as well.<br /> <br /> I feel all of the Malkavian archetypes except Doctor Netchurch's kind of suck, though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-19T18:25:43-07:00","id":1390142,"title":"","content":"I honestly ended up liking several of the Malkavians a lot better than I thought I would, and more than I recall liking them 26 years ago. <br /> <br /> I think there is certainly a benefit to having what I've seen referred to as &quot;Brown Puppy&quot; archetypes (as opposed to highly specialized &quot;Pink Lizards&quot;) that are easy and basic pick-up-and-play tools. However, I think when you do one of those, you need to make it as strong and well thought out as you can. <br /> <br /> I really like the concept of the Leatherface Nosferatu. But I think the key thing is to give them depth and make sure they are able to work with other player characters, at least in terms of making a player character template.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-19T19:48:19-07:00","id":1390159,"title":"","content":"<b>The Players Guide to the Sabbat (part three)</b><br /> <br /> I'm not sure why, but for some reason I was thinking there were ten Sabbat templates, but there is actually only nine of them.<br /> <br /> <b>The Pander Anarchist</b> (aka Jack of All Trades)<br /> Basically a poor kid from a small town whose older brother had already been embraced into the Sabbat and decided to have his younger brother join them. And that's about it. He's not an anarchist, but dislikes authority and tends to question and oppose leadership as a knee jerk reaction. He's more of a devil's advocate than an anarchist. And that's reiterated more than once. He's got Stamina at five dots and three dots in each of his Mental traits. Brawl and Survival are at three dots each, and Athletics, Dodge, Streetwise, Drive, and Firearms are all at two. Then a broad collection of ones in a number of other abilities. So he does fit the jack of all trades descriptor. Panders have no in clan Disciplines, and he takes advantage of this fact to have one dot each in Auspex, Celerity, Dominate, Fortitude, and Vicissitude. He follows the Path of Cathari, which is partially derived from old Gnostic beliefs and focused on obtaining pleasure and power. It's another one that was often referred to as &quot;The Path of Doing Whatever I Want&quot;. This is not a bad character, just a very shallow one. This could easily be any faceless member of any random Sabbat pack. Which isn't a bad thing. I just find myself wishing that the write up focused more on the devil's advocate aspect, as well as the brief mention of his mischief making and carousing with pack mates. So I generally like this one more than I thought I would, but I can see some room for improvement.<br /> <br /> <b>The Serpent of Light Mystery Man</b> (aka Ongun)<br /> While not spelled out, it's implied that this character is probably Haitian, and his back story is that he was drafted into a voodoo cult's secret war against the Followers of Set at a young age, eventually being chosen to join the Serpents of Light. He's well versed in certain aspects of the occult and from all indications has adjusted pretty well to existence in the Sabbat. He often tries to appear mysterious and enigmatic just to look wiser than he probably is. He also enjoys collecting spiders. He's Mental primary and Social secondary. Other than Subterfuge and Occult at three dots each, his abilities are mostly twos in things that allow him to serve the pack socially and as a scout. He's on the Path of Death and the Soul, which is one of the ones favored by the Serpents of Light. Much like the Pander, this isn't a bad character. He's essentially the archetypical Serpent of Light, and is competently built as such. And I swear that the character art reminds me of a specific rap/hip hop artist from the very late 80s/very early 90s, I just can't for the life of me remember who. Also, his equipment entry seems strangely fixated on details about his gun. So, another of those &quot;out of central casting&quot; characters like the Pander and the Assamite. Which, again, isn't necessarily a bad thing, as those certainly have their place. I just once against find myself wishing he was fleshed out a little more in the write up, especially the idea that he's slightly faking his persona in an effort to live up to others' expectations.<br /> <br /> <b>The Toreador <i>antitribu</i> Sculptor</b> (aka Torture(d) Artist)<br /> I kind of like this one for some reason. I suspect part of that is she reminds me a bit of the character of Jane from MTV's old <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daria\" target=\"_blank\"><i>Daria</i></a> animated series, both in origin and how the character art looks (although this predates that show by a few years). She grew up in a family of fairly well off Bohemian artist types and was an extremely talented sculptor. But at some point, her art started becoming more and more morbid and macabre. (It's not said why this happened, but I can't help but wonder if this is some sort of reaction to turning 30 or 40, or else being diagnosed with some sort of terminal disease, either of which would be interesting, I think.) This attracted the attention of one of the Sabbat, and she was embraced. She's actually adapted to being in the sect fairly well, although still insists on keeping her own private haven and studio at what could very well be her family's mansion. Her Dexterity is at five dots, with threes in Stamina, Charisma, Manipulation, and Perception. However, she's got a one in Wits, which feels like it may be too low for some reason. Her main skills are Sculpting at four dots and Melee at three (which fits pretty well as she prefers using her art tools as weapons), with a bunch of twos in Drive, Etiquette, Stealth, Finance, Language, Medicine and Occult. I seem to be noticing a trend of characters who are very excellent drivers. I'm not sure why that is. She's another one with a lot of Background dots, including one in Fame and three in Resources. Her Disciplines are evenly spread, with one each in Auspex and Celerity, and two in Presence. She follows the Path of Death and the Soul, which kind of surprises me as I'd have figured her for a Path of Cathari. But over all I rather like this one. She's your archetypical Toreador artiste type but as a dark and twisted reflection of it, as the Sabbat <i>antitribu</i> are supposed to be to their clans (at least the better ones are). I think this is one of those characters that would work really well if you wanted to do a chronicle set in NYC back when the Sabbat ran it, and you're much more likely to have a pack where members aren't spending all their time on the road together and have had a chance to establish themselves in a location. All in all, I think this may end up being one of my favorites in the book.<br /> <br /> That's all for this post. Next will be the final two Sabbat templates, along with final thoughts about them as a whole.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-20-2020, 06:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-20T10:52:16-07:00","id":1390327,"title":"","content":"<b>The Players Guide to the Sabbat (part four)</b><br /> <br /> And finishing out the book.<br /> <br /> <b>The Tzimisce Priest</b> (aka Weird Lady)<br /> The Tzimisce were the other &quot;leader clan&quot; of the Sabbat, sort of like the Tremere to the Camarilla's Ventrue, in that they seem to take a more spiritual role than the political one of the Lasambra. This would change a little as the game went on, but that's the general impression their initial presentation left. This is also before the idea of flesh crafting freakishness and transhumanism became a thing with the clan, so this character is surprisingly low key and restrained for a Tzimisce, both in terms of concept and the artwork. And I find myself appreciating that. When every other Tzimisce is a Cenobite from <i>Hellraiser</i>, it's nice to see one that isn't. Our character was your typical middle class suburban housewife and mother who happened to get picked for the embrace. How she's sort of a mother hen type for her pack, offering leadership and spiritual guidance, while also occasionally reigning them in when necessary. Interestingly, she used to miss her family, but has apparently put that behind her. I say apparently, mainly just because I find that an interesting character angle of a Sabbat vampire who has never been able to fully divorce themselves from their previous life, and if playing this character, I'd probably have it be a reoccurring thing. Her Manipulation, Perception, and Intelligence are all at four dots. Her highest ability is a three in Occult, with twos in Politics, Investigation, Leadership, and Subterfuge. Everything else is only at one dot, including Dodge and Firearms, her only combat skills. Her Disciplines are one in Auspex and three in Vicissitude, making her the only character in the book to have a Discipline at higher than two dots. Oddly, given her focus on Vicissitude, she only has one dot in the Body Alteration secondary ability. If you remember me mentioning it when talking about Nosferatu, this ability primarily exists for use in Vicissitude power roles. While the first level power uses Intelligence + Body Alteration, meaning she can sort of &quot;brute force&quot; her way though it mentally, the second and third level powers use Dexterity and Strength, respectively, and she only has those at two. So the character is focused on a Discipline she's not actually that great at using. And this brings up something else I noticed when reading the character write up. It mentions her being taken from a Catholic Church by a man dressed as a Catholic priest, who used Dominate to make her come with him. The combination of Catholic tropes and the Dominate discipline sounds more like your archetypical Lasombra embrace. And when I went and looked at the rules for Obtenebration, I see that the first level power doesn't require roles, but the second and third level ones both use Manipulation + Occult. I can't help but wonder if this character was originally created to be the Lasombra template, but was changed to Tzimisce at the last minute. (Also, while the character has thirteen dots in Knowledges and five dots in Skills, she only has eight dots in Talents, and then the one dot of Body Alteration, which is supposed to be a Skill. Again, it feels like this character was remade at the last minute as a Tzimisce, and a Talent dot taken to make sure she had a token showing in Body Alteration.) Overall, I like this character. I think &quot;mother hen&quot; is an interesting role to have in a Sabbat pack, especially back during these early days when they were in the &quot;run wild and act crazy&quot; stage. But I think this character works better as a Lasombra. If I were to keep her as a Tzimisce, I'd probably shift her Discipline focus to Auspex and some Animalism, either a Auspex 2/Animalism 1/Vicissitude 1 set up, or 2/2 or 3/1 split between the first two. If I wasn't using Vicissitude, I'd take that Body Alternation dot and use it to raise her Alertness or Intimidation to two.<br /> <br /> <b>The Ventrue <i>antitribu</i> Know-It-All</b> (aka Whiner)<br /> I've always had sort of mixed feelings about the Ventrue <i>antitribu</i>. I think there was an issue in the early days of them coming across as Brujah wannabes. However, on rereading their entry in this book, I don't think that's entirely fair to them. I think there's a really cool idea here about individualism centered on the idea of reaching one's ultimate potential unfettered by shackles like tradition and politics, and it ties in really well with the Clan's overall themes. (And I think the Presence discipline honestly ties into that idea better than Auspex does, at least in my opinion.) But I think it gets a little muddled in the idea of them trying to be different than everyone else, especially the Camarilla Ventrue. And seeing &quot;Whiner&quot; listed under this character's concept certainly doesn't help their image. Also, it took me a minute to realize that the character pictured is wearing a trench coat of some sort rather than a Wiccan style robe or something. Anyway, this character is a natural born genius, always getting straight As and excelling academically, but lacking the charisma or ability to pick up on certain social cues to keep from coming across as pompous and unlikeable. She became a law student only for her to get taken for a mass embrace. (It mentions that she survived but her roommate, who was also taken, didn't. I kind of wish more had been done with that angle.) Now she's just an average member of the pack, save that she constantly annoys everyone by telling them what to do, pointing out when they're wrong, and saying &quot;I told you so&quot; when it turns out she was right all along, and being blissfully oblivious to just how irritating this make her to everyone else. I find this kind of amusing, and while I'd want to make sure it never became annoying to the point of making it un-fun for the players as opposed to the characters, it can make for a very interesting social dynamic in the pack. It says she complains when told what to do, but it feels like this is more one of those &quot;I already know what to do&quot; or &quot;that's a dumb thing to do&quot; type thing than actual whining. So I think the Concept should be different. (Like, maybe, the &quot;Know-It-All&quot; in the write up header.) Our character really is a genius, with an Intelligence of five. Weirdly, her Physicals are secondary, with a four in Stamina and three in Dexterity. (Also threes in Manipulation and Perception, with a one in Charisma.) I say this is weird because the write up goes out of the way to mention she's good looking, so I'm sort of surprised she doesn't have Socials as secondary in order to bump Appearance up higher. She's got three dots in Law, which makes sense because of her background, and then two each in Investigation, Language, Etiquette, and Drive. (There's that Drive skill again. What is it with Sabbat and cars? This is way, way before <i>Fast &amp; Furious</i> was ever a thing.) Everything else are ones scattered all over the place. Given her personality, I'm a little surprised her Dominate is only one dot. She follows the Path of Honorable Accord. Overall, I like this character a lot more than I thought I would when I started rereading, and also like the Ventrue <i>antitribu</i> a bit more than I remember doing so originally. She's generally a fairly fun personality, and a bit different from what you'd expect from the Sabbat.<br /> <br /> And so that's the character templates from The Players Guide to the Sabbat. Overall, they aren't that bad. I'm rather disappointed that we didn't get a Ravnos, Tremere, or Nosferatu. I actually think that the Leatherface concept from their clanbook would've worked a lot better here using the Sabbat rules. There are a few oddities here. As I've mentioned, I think the Animal Trainer would've worked a lot better as a normal non-Sabbat Gangrel, especially give the City Gangrel's lack of Animalism. I think the Tzimisce Priest would make more sense from several angles as a Lasombra. And the Malkavian would be so much better without the &amp;#!%ing mime thing. A third of our characters - the Assamite, Pander, and Serpent of Light - are very basic archetypes, the kind you'd expect to see in almost any pack. I think the Toreador and, strangely, the Ventrue, ended up being my favorites out of the bunch.<br /> As far as issues of representation go, four of the nine are female, although the Lasambra artwork has this androgynous quality to it and never really specifies the character being male or female. The Serpent of Light, obviously, is Afro-Caribbean, and I could actually see the Pander as being meant to be Native American or mixed-race. The Assamite looks like he could be read as Asian, if you wanted to, as could the Ventrue.<br /> While reading this, it occurred to me that, given how all Sabbat were automatically on a Path of Enlightenment at the time, and that we have six paths (not counting the Path of Evil Revelations, which was included in this book for some inexplicable reason), it would've been cool to see two templates for each Path, giving us twelve total, and allowing us to show almost all of the Sabbat clans. It also occurred to me that, in all honesty, the Sabbat should be kind of fractured along Path lines, with each pack all following the same Path of Enlightenment, lead by the pack priest, and being inducted into it as part of the creation rites. At least I think that would be an interesting idea to play around with.<br /> So, theme wise, the Sabbat are supposed to be monsters who've turned their backs on humanity and their old lives. Some of these - the Lasombra and the Tzimisce, especially - fit this idea really well. The Toreador and potentially the Pander also seem to have embraced this to a certain degree. Most of the others, not so much. As I mentioned in the Mime entry, I think it would've been really interesting to present this as a person who is potentially better off as a vampire. And the Gangrel Animal Trainer is the one who reflects this idea the least, which is probably why I keep thinking she'd be better as a regular Camarilla or unaligned Gangrel. So overall, I'd have to say that this is mostly successful, but not as big of a success as it could've been.<br /> <br /> Next, a brief interlude as I go back and look at the weird little character sheets that were part of each clan's entry in the second edition core book. And then <i>Clanbook: Toreador</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-20-2020, 07:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-20T17:28:59-07:00","id":1390422,"title":"","content":"Still posting awesome work! I have been actively looking forward to getting home from work to read them. (Well, and to get away from maskless people trying to kill me... but, that's another story...)<br /> <br /> Thank you, for providing a very welcome diversion.<br /> <br /> I'm rising in defense of the Ventrue knowitall.<br /> <br /> Ventrue antitribu from this edition are my favorite bloodline, bar none. Really.<br /> <br /> They are a distillation of the whole &quot;only we know how to run vampire society well, and we must bear the burden of doing so&quot; messiah complex of the Ventrue. Lacking Presence keeps them from looking cool while doing it. Without Presence, how deeply neurotic, desperate to please, and insecure they are gets to shine through. Auspex makes it so they actually sort of do know how to run things. They see the little details and flaws in the world around them, without filters or illusions. They believe they know how to fix most problems, if only somebody would listen to them for once.<br /> <br /> (I'm reminded of the identifying quote from forum regular <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar235_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"235\" data-vbnamecard=\"235\">Telgar</a> : &quot;I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!&quot;)<br /> <br /> Plus, early Sabbat in the US were closely tied to the culture of the northeastern US: New York, New Jersey, Detroit, Cleveland, Philadelphia, and, umm, Miami. The Ventrue antitribu played with the &quot;smart-ass, know-it-all Yankee&quot; stereotype. I saw the main Ventrue clan as old-money patricians, and waspy preps. The antitribu were the over-educated, liberal, unselfconsciously ethnic urbanite spoofed in Seinfeld, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ohw8jqyLPpU\" target=\"_blank\">Woody Allen </a><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=udoh1DZ7JKw\" target=\"_blank\">movies</a>, and the Billy Joel song &quot;<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Iyv905Q2omU\" target=\"_blank\">Pressure</a>&quot;.<br /> <br /> The Ventrue are the Mayflower, the antitribu are Ellis Island. The Ventrue are Brooks Brothers, the antitribu are Barney's. The Ventrue are Upper East Side, the Ventrue antitribu are Upper West Side. The Ventrue are the Wall Street Journal, the antitribu are the New York Times. The Ventrue are Tavern on the Green; the antitribu are Second Avenue Deli.<br /> <br /> The antitribu are smarter, yes, but never quite good enough.<br /> <br /> I miss them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11558-nosimplehiway","name":"Nosimplehiway","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11558_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1699"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-20T20:09:28-07:00","id":1390449,"title":"","content":"<b>Interlude: Back to the Core Book</b><br /> <br /> (Apologies ahead of time; This ended up being longer than I intended for a single post.)<br /> <br /> As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"13141\" data-vbnamecard=\"13141\">Nail Eater</a> mentioned, the second edition core book included tiny little filled in character sheets for each clan. These were, apparently, meant to be the characters illustrated in the artwork that accompanied each clan's two page write up. (This art is, btw, completely different from the art used in the first edition's similar section, and these images would go a huge way toward influencing people's perception of each clan for a lot of the second edition period. This two page spread template would likewise go on to be the standard for almost all of White Wolf's future books, both for the World of Darkness and later Exalted. It's actually a pretty good marketing tactic, as these things are usually almost the first thing people would look at back in the days of going into your local game store and flipping through something before buying it.)<br /> <br /> Artwork is, again, by Josh Timbrook. <br /> <br /> As with the Players Guide, we have seven of them. <br /> <br /> Out <b>Brujah</b> is, according to her concept, a <b>Revolutionary</b>. Her Nature is Cavalier, which isn't actually in the core book. Physicals are primary and Mental secondary, which does rather set the stage for how the clan would be presented in the clanbook. Abilities are spread fairly evenly, with three dots each in Brawl, Intimidation, and Firearms, making a character that's clearly expecting to see a lot of combat. Alertness, Leadership, Streetwise, Drive, Melee, and Stealth are all at two dots. Politics is only at one dot, which is a little ironic given how almost every character in the clanbook seemed to have it at three. She's also got one dot in Finance, which is interesting, if seeming a little random. Disciplines are spread evenly, with an extra one in Presence. Backgrounds are Allies, Contacts and Herd. Humanity is seven and Willpower is six. This all feels like it fits the character in the picture. She has this kind of female Billy Idol thing going on, which I don't dislike, and looks like she'd be the kind of character to have these stats, especially the Intimidation factor. (I think I want to repurpose her for use as a Redcap in Changeling.) A pretty striking image with good stats, even if maybe her Charisma could stand to be a little higher. I think this is partially why the Brujah were my favorite clan for the first few years of the game. <br /> <br /> Next we have our <b>Gangrel</b>. He - at least I think it's a he; there's a certain androgynous quality to it - has the listed concept of ... <b>Hippie</b>. (<sub>y</sub><sub>ou go to hell, game; you go to hell and die</sub>) Nature is Survivor; Demeanor is Loner, setting up the classic clan stereotype. Once again, Physicals are primary and Mental secondary. Though Charisma is a three at the expense of a Manipulation of one. We've got Alertness, Athletics, and Survival are at three dots, while we have twos in Brawl, Dodge, Empathy, Stealth, and Science. No Firearms or Melee, but with two dots in Brawl, I have to wonder if this is supposed to be one of those <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Billy_Jack\" target=\"_blank\">Billy Jack</a> type &quot;hippies.&quot; Disciplines are, again, spread evenly with an extra dot, this time in Protean. Backgrounds are Allies, Herd and Mentor. Humanity and Willpower are both at seven. This isn't a bad set of stats. However, they don't add up, as they appear to have spent four more freebie points than they should have. But I don't know where the hippie thing is coming from. Even the character image looks more like an extra from The Lost Boys or a member of a period Seattle grunge band. And that's not a bad look either. If I was to try to name this character's concept, I think I'd go more for something along the lines of &quot;Loner&quot; or &quot;Drifter&quot;. Really, this isn't bad. The name just bugs me to no end. <br /> <br /> Moving right along, we come to the <b>Malkavian</b>. This one is a <b>Neurotic Comedian</b> ... damn it. I haven't kept up with the stand up comedy scene for over a decade, but back in the 80s, there was a thing with &quot;wacky crazy&quot; or otherwise &quot;oddball&quot; comics. Robin Williams, especially when he was doing cocaine really heavily, and Bobcat Goldthwait, among others. And this is apparently what this character is going for. Nature is Jester and Demeanor is Deviant, because of course they are. Mental is primary and Social secondary, with most of that focused on the four in Manipulation. His best ability is Acting, at three dots. The rest are twos, including Alertness, Empathy, Intimidation, Subterfuge, Melee, Bureaucracy, and Linguistics, rounded out with several ones. Backgrounds are Herd, Influence, Resources, and Contacts. Disciplines are the same even spread, with an additional one in Dominate. Humanity and Willpower are both six. The character image is of a guy hanging upside down, hands on his head with fingers through disheveled hair, eyes wild and screaming. He's wearing a plaid shirt open to reveal a white t-shirt with &quot;Now I have a Machine Gun Ho Ho Ho&quot; written on it. (Yes, a <i>Die Hard</i> reference.) He also has a tie, that's just tied around his bare neck, because, you know, wacky. Just from a pure traits perspective (and again, these don't add up, apparently having spent two freebie points too many), this isn't a bad character. Fairly versatile, and I think it would work really well as, say, some sort of office drone or college student who just sort of snapped or was burning out only to get turned into a vampire. Maybe move the points in Acting a little, and change the Nature/Demeanor to something like Conformist, Director or Visionary. But I really dislike the art, and I suspect this largely torpedoed any chance of the Malkavians ever being taken seriously or seen as scary for much of the game's run. <br /> <br /> Next is the <b>Nosferatu</b> and, looking at his sheet I see that ... it is a reprint of the Malkavian one. Yep, a layout error. Thankfully, the actually sheet was put in the Malkavian clanbook (along with &quot;Page XX&quot;), under the section talking about their views on other clans. The art is of a Nosferatu in very nice pinstripe suit and bowler hat, holding a pocket watch. This is a total 180 from the stereotypical clan image, and I wish it was used more often, to be honest. The sheet lists his concept as <b>Corrupt Industrialist</b> (and really, is there any other kind in early WoD books?). Mental is primary, with fours in Intelligence and Wits, and Social secondary, with a five in Manipulation. (He has a one listed under Appearance, but I suspect this is just an artifact of the basic character sheet.) Dexterity is only a one, with Stamina a three. He's got a four in Finance (making him the first of the bunch to have more than three in an ability), and threes in Bureaucracy and Etiquette. Empathy, Intimidation, Leadership, Subterfuge, Investigation, and Linguistics are all at two dots. (He's also the first - and it turns out only - to have any skill with Computers, with only one dot.) He's only got one dot in each Discipline, which is a change. Backgrounds are Resources (at five), Influence, Retainers, and Generation (another first). Humanity is seven and Willpower is nine. Once again, the stats don't add up. But even if you clean them up - say, drop that single dot in Streetwise, the single dot of Generation, one dot worth of Retainer, and one dot of Willpower - you are left with a character that is very competent and potentially dangerous in the world of the Kindred. Which I think is awesome. And I really love the character art. He rocks that suit so well you almost don't notice the face. I kind of want to play this character, which I think makes it a big success. <br /> <br /> Next up is our <b>Toreador</b>. The character art is again kind of androgynous in that early 90s grunge way, but seems to be female (as is the clan stereotype). She's got some nice tattoo work, some of it pretty detailed, which is impressive. Her concept is <b>Performance Artist</b>, which is a little vague. I hope it involves more than doing cat's cradle like she is in the image. Nature is Architect and Demeanor Gallant, which is kind of stereotypical Toreador. Social are primary and Mental secondary, having a four in Manipulation and threes in Charisma, Appearance, Perception and Intelligence. Acting, Subterfuge, Etiquette, and Music are all at three dots. She also has twos in Politics and Firearms, giving our rose thorns, so to speak. And, strangely, there are four dots filled in down where Secondary Abilities would be, but no ability listed. I guess this was supposed to be some form of Expression (poetic, maybe?), but was deleted as it required something outside the core book. Disciplines are back to the even spread with additional dot, this time in Auspex. Backgrounds are Resources and Retainers. I'm a little surprised she doesn't have at least one dot of Fame. Humanity is seven, with Willpower at a seemingly low five. (Again, the points don't add up, being over by two.) This is a pretty good character. It feels like a solid out of the box Toreador character one could pic up and play, and the art is very striking. I have to give this one a win. <br /> <br /> And on to the <b>Tremere</b>. Here was have a little girl pulling a rabbit out of a hat. And she's a <b>Stage Magician</b>. I'm not sure why. I have to be honest, if asked to name the first clan to spring to mind at the mention of the concept of Stage Magician, it'd be the Toreador, hands down, and Ravnos at number two. However, I can see the idea of the world of professional magicians, with their cliquishness and secrecy from outsiders, as well as their dedication to learning and mastering a very difficult set of skills, mapping to that of the Tremere. I'm just not exactly sure it would've been my first choice to show off the clan in the core book. (Though, to be fair, the Nosferatu one is totally against stereotype and I love the hell out of that one.) I'm also not sure I'd have gone with a sleepy Wednesday Addams for the signature image either. Nature is Conniver and Demeanor is Child, which I guess is supposed to fit the art. Mental is primary and Social is secondary, which is both the Tremere archetype as smart people who are also kind of manipulative, and works for a stage magician. Then we get to abilities and things start to fall down a little. She has three dots each in Subterfuge, Investigation, and Occult. (A crime solving child vampire magician? It would've made a cool 80s TV show.) Alertness, Intimidation, Leadership, Etiquette, Bureaucracy, and Politics are at two each. There's also ones in Acting, Drive, Firearms, and Finance. Disciplines are again the even spread, with an additional dot in Thaumaturgy. Backgrounds are Herd, Mentor, and Retainer (and I can't help but picture the rabbit as the retainer). Humanity is eight, Willpower is seven. Once again, the stats don't add up, being over by at least four points. However, if you get rid of the extra dot in Self Control which bumped the Humanity up to eight, and the dot in Acting, it works. Which brings me to what I think happened here. These stats are for a pretty decent character, specifically for your sort of archetypical up and coming Tremere who is well skilled in the clan's strong suits of arcana and politics, and is being groomed for a position of leadership. Which is a really cool character and potentially a lot of fun to play. Then, I think the art came in, and for some strange reason, Josh Timbrook gave them a picture of Wednesday Addams pulling a rabbit out of a hat. (Which, by it self, isn't a bad idea for a Tremere. With some tweaks, I could see this character sheet easily turning into, say, &quot;Strange Little Girl&quot; or something.) It's a lot easier, faster, and cheaper to alter text than it is art. So I think someone changed the sheet's Demeanor to Child, the Concept to Stage Magician, and added that dot of Acting (and maybe the extra Self Control). So we're left with a sheet that doesn't really match the concept or art very well. Which is a shame, because like I said, the stats are for a pretty cool sounding occult investigator/up and coming politico type and the art looks like it might actually make a potentially interesting character in its own right. <br /> <br /> And, finally, the<b> Ventrue</b>. Out image is of a fairly cool looking guy in a nice, stylish suit, smoking a cigarette and looking pretty smug. Really the image of a dynamic up and coming young turk, as opposed to the idea of fossilized old tyrants or pompous blue blood elites the clan often gets stereotyped as. I rather like it. Our concept is <b>Ward Boss</b>. (This means he effectively runs the political party for a single ward or political unit of a city, pretty much picking the candidates for that ward's representative in the city council, as well as other politically appointed offices, and exercises a fair amount of influence within the party in the city, and possibly even on a state level.) He's Social primary, with four dots each in Charisma and Manipulation, and Mental secondary, with three dots each in Intelligence and Wits. Politics are at four dots, and Leadership and Subterfuge at three. This is rounded out with twos in Acting, Empathy, Drive, Etiquette, Bureaucracy, and Investigation, as well as a few ones including Finance, Law and Streetwise. Disciplines are one each. Backgrounds include Resources, Influence, and Contacts at three each, and a few more points in Allies and Herd. Humanity and Willpower are both at seven. Again, he's four points over. I'd probably drop one dot from Acting, the Allies background, and either one level of Contacts or Herd (or alternately one dot of WP). Over all, this is a really strong character, and I like it quite a bit. He's very archetypically Ventrue, but appears to be very competent and potentially dangerous in the more political realms of Kindred existence. Very much a win here. <br /> <br /> And so, that was, for second edition players new to the game, their first introduction to a filled in character sheet representing a somewhat typical member of that clan. And, overall, I think it did an OK job of it. The single biggest issue here is that most of the sheet's don't add up, points wise. Most of them you can fix without much trouble, but the sloppiness is a little off putting (said the person who has probably made at least a hundred typos in this thread alone). There's also the issue with the Tremere one clearly being out of sync, and the fact that the Malkavian one is fairly bad. And the very minor detail of the Gangrel having a dumb concept name. It might also be arguable that the Nosferatu isn't archetypical enough. (This may have been another instance where the artwork submitted totally mismatched the character sheet, only to have the Malkavian one end up there by mistake, and the one you see in the Malkavian clanbook a totally after the fact invention.) But I think the Brujah, Toreador, and Ventrue are extremely strong, as is the Nosferatu, and the Gangrel isn't bad. So overall, I have to call this a fairly solid and mostly successful effort, though one with a asterisk next to it in the record books.<br /> As far as representation goes, the Brujah, Toreador, and Tremere are all female, and the other four are male. (Though, as I mentioned, the Gangrel has a certain level of androgyny to it.) They seem to all be white, save possibly the Ventrue. <br /> <br /> I will note here that there are, IIRC, similar character sheets with each clan write up in the 2nd ed Players Guide. However, my copy of that book has some binding damage (and has had this damage since the late 90s) which makes me extremely reluctant to go looking through it to the extent it would take to review them. So I may not do those, unless I can find some other place to look them up. <br /> <br /> Next, <i>Clanbook: Toreador</i>. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-20T20:21:04-07:00","id":1390452,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nosimplehiway</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1390422#post1390422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm rising in defense of the Ventrue knowitall.<br /> <br /> Ventrue antitribu from this edition are my favorite bloodline, bar none. Really.</div> </div> </div> </div>Like I said, I ended up liking both the antitribu and this template a lot better than I remember from 25 years ago, which makes me glad I decided to start this project. I think the idea their write up talks about, with them all trying to reach the peak of their individual potential and help others do the same, just without some of the stuff they see as holding back the main part of the clan, to be pretty cool, and actually fits really well with the way the clan as a whole was presented in their revised clanbook. However, I think they should have Presence instead of Auspex, but that's just my personal opinion. I also think there's a place in the Sabbat for both this version of them and the monster hunters presented in the revised edition, and they may even compliment each other. One is moving forward toward a future of freedom, while the other is weirdly obsessed with trying to destroy what they see as chains from the past. <br /> But I also like playing Ventrue Anarchs, so I'm clearly weird. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-21T12:26:19-07:00","id":1390600,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Toreador (part one)</b><br /> <br /> So now we're moving in to 1994. The cover of Toreador is ... rather less interesting than its processors. Brujah and Malkavian were both very striking (even if I think the latter's might've been a little misguided), Nosferatu's was a nice homage to a classic vampire film, and even Gangrel, while pretty restrained, looked like some sort of urban predator. This cover image is very unremarkable. It's just a woman, hands together holding a cigarette, and looking at you with a mildly ... smug? ... look. I don't think it does much to convey anything about what's inside.<br /> This book was written by Steven C. Brown, who wrote the Players Guide to the Sabbat. Artwork is again by Josh Timbrook. As I've said before, I rather like the bit of continuity. I think it helps that you could easily take several of these images and photoshop them together into a single picture of your coterie or Elysium gathering and it'd look like it was done by one person.<br /> <br /> <b>The Burnout</b><br /> This appears to be the sort of person the Nosferatu Morbid Artsy Type was aspiring to be in life, complete with enough ennui to stun a large animal. The image is that of your archetypical vaguely Goth-like Bohemian artist type, with ruffled shirt sleeves, long hair and cigarette holder, and is so androgynous that the character's gender is anyone's guess (or choice). They were a top up and coming artist among the Toreador, and apparently managing to maintain certain ties to their mortal family (as Toreador are apparently want to do in this edition). The those evil Setites came along, killed the character's children and husband, and subjected them to torture. After escaping and going on a revenge spree, they found they couldn't create anymore. So they decided to try an unlife of hedonism, but that soon became dull. Now they are back in their home city, trying to start over, and aspiring to acquire a level of personal power that will ensure no one is ever able to hurt them again. Petty things like art, morality, or humans no longer concern the character; only power matters. They would rather continue to wallow in self-pity than actually try to heal the interior damage they suffered. I find myself rather liking this character, much more so than I remember from back in the day. It's very tragic in a way that seems appropriate for Vampire and its themes of personal horror. And anyone playing them has the option of either riding that bullet train to hell or actually trying to make that long hard crawl up from the darkness. Nature is Survivor, but I could maybe see Autist as a better fit. Socials are primary, with a five in Manipulation, offset by a two in Charisma. Artistic Expression is at a four, while Intimidation and Subterfuge are at three each. Politics, Etiquette, and Style are at two dots, as are Brawl, Firearms and Security, reflecting the character's backstory. Disciplines focus on Presence at two dots and Celerity at one. Given that the character seems to have certain traits in common with PTSD or mild paranoia, I'm a little surprised they don't have Auspex's Heightened Senses. Backgrounds focus on Resources at three, as well as some Generation, Retainer, Status and Fame. As the write up claims the character has &quot;many beautiful ghouls&quot; (and that the pet dogs are treated better), I'd have expected more than one dot in Retainer. Or maybe a dot or two in Herd. Humanity is at a very low five, representing how damaged the character is. Willpower is seven. Overall, I like it and think it does a good job of leaning into the whole &quot;tragically beautiful&quot; aspect of vampire fiction that the Toreador are there to represent.<br /> <br /> <b>The Comic Book Celebrity </b><br /> Remember what I said about the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1388093#post1388093\">Brujah Fake Rapper</a>? Well, this is the same thing. In this case, it's making fun of comic book artist <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rob_Liefeld\" target=\"_blank\">Rob Liefeld</a>. I have, over the years, come to appreciate Liefeld's enthusiasm for comics and for the superhero genre. But his art is and always has been terrible, with most of his characters clearly derivative of preexisting ones. (I honestly think he missed his true calling being an editor.) And back in the very early 90s, at the start of that period's speculator bubble, he - even more than some of the other founders of Image Comics - was big on self promotion. Most famously for the Levi's jeans commercial he was in. So, you can clearly see why, in a book that staunchly divided the Toreador between true Artistes and pompous Poseurs, they'd do a character like this. The write up background/bio is pretty much that of the character's inspiration with the serial numbers filed off, even down to the dig at his inability to meet deadlines now. He's generally uncomfortable around other Kindred and continuously dismisses any and all criticism of being due to jealousy. It's not a particularly deep character. But there is a slight glimmer of potential here in the offhand mention that his creativity has improved since the embrace and the new challenges are actually providing inspiration. This is a Social character, with the usual 4/3/3 split and Mental secondary (Intelligence is two, but Perception and Wits are three). Artistic Expression is only at two dots, along with the Masquerade ability to pass for the living. However, Finance is at four dots, which is an interesting twist but perhaps a little out of left field. He's also got two dots each in Computer, Investigation, Drive, Intimidation, and Subterfuge. He's also got some interesting one dot abilities, such as Security, Politics, Stealth and Leadership. Disciplines are two in Celerity and one in Presence. He's got a lot of Background points, including four dots in Resources, two in Fame, and three in Herd. Humanity is at eight and Willpower at seven. From a traits perspective, this is a very respectable character. If you shifted a dot or two into Bureaucracy, he's be a very competent manager of his comic company. As it is, I kind of have this amusing mental image of him being forced by circumstance to help investigate a murder or other mystery, like one of those old novels that would set a mystery at a SciFi or writers' convention. (Which would probably make an interesting one shot for a convention game.) So, much like the Fake Rapper, I find myself reexamining a character I dismissed as a joke 25 years ago and actually liking it and seeing a lot of potential for it as a character. All in all, not bad.<br /> <br /> That's all for this entry. Next will be part two of Toreador.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-21-2020, 04:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-21T18:54:27-07:00","id":1390678,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Toreador (part two)</b><br /> <br /> Continuing our look at the Clan of the Rose.<br /> <br /> <b>The Cult Leader</b><br /> I'm actually a little surprised it took us this long to get to one of these types. It's one of those concepts that works really well with a number of clans. Our character was one of those clean cut, good looking and easily charming people who had a mild interest in the occult and the sociological aspects of it. He was embraced after picking his sire up at a party after convincing her and another girl that he had psychic abilities and taking them back to his place. He got turned into a vampire; the other girl got turned into a snack. Now that he's been released and his sire gone on to do other things, he's decided to start his own cult as a means of feeding and material comfort. He's a little bit paranoid about other vampires harming his &quot;flock&quot; to harm him, so he's started teaching some of them how to actually hunt vampires. He's also pretty much addicted to the sense of power he has over the lives of his followers. He runs mostly off of Charisma, with five dots in that and only three in Manipulation. He's also got four dots in Wits, but is otherwise fairly average Attribute-wise. He's got four dots each in Acting, Empathy, and Leadership, and a scattering of twos and ones, including two dots in Occult, as well as in Drive and Firearms. (I'm not sure why, but Steven Brown seems to like making his characters excellent drivers. Only one character in this book lacks it, and everyone else has one or two dots in it, as well as all the ones with the skill in the Sabbat book.) I think this character might benefit from switching which of Skills and Knowledge is secondary. I think he could get by with two dots in Etiquette, and one each in Firearms, Security, and Stealth, then use the extra dots to get a few levels of Psychology and maybe a dot in Finance and/or Bureaucracy. His Disciplines focus entirely on Presence, which is fitting. (And I can't help but wonder how much more effective this guy will be as a cult leader when he starts getting a few dots in Auspex.) He's got four dots of Herd, three of Resources, two of Retainers and one of Mentor. Humanity and Willpower are both seven. Overall, I think this is a pretty good character. He's got a very strong vampire concept that I think both plays very strongly into the parallels between vampires and cult leaders as parasites preying on people and highlights his clan's tropes and strengths. The artwork is good and cuts a fairly striking image of what feels like a late 80s or early 90s West Coast cult leader. And, my above suggestion aside, the stats are pretty good. I like it.<br /> As a historical note, I'll mention that apparently some material about blood cults and how to run them/quantify them with backgrounds was apparently cut for space. This material ended up being printed in <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/3596/White-Wolf-Magazine-40?cPath=1_356\" target=\"_blank\">issue #40 of White Wolf Magazine</a>, which is available on drivethrurpg.com.<br /> <br /> <b>The Horror Writer</b><br /> This is a pretty straightforward idea. Your typical moderately successful writer of horror fiction whose work attracted the attention of another writer who happened to be a vampire. Interestingly, the character still lives with his wife and kids, and the wife knows he's a vampire. This concept is one meant to draw on two things the clanbook spends some time talking about. One is, naturally, the idea of being a vampire and famous at the same time. (I honestly wish the line had devoted an entire book to this subject, given what a big influence Anne Rice's <i>Queen of the Damned</i> novel was on the game and the fact that it's one of the biggest Masquerade-related questions there is.) The other is the idea that Toreador, more than any other clan, will often pretend to still be mortal, to the point of keeping mortal lovers/spouses and children. Revised would move away from this idea, but I actually find it kind of interesting. It ties in to a few aspects of Dark Ages' Road of Humanity, and makes a certain level of sense from the perspective of a clan devoted to art can't really carry out the human activity of creating art unless they actually spend time around the living. Out character is slightly alienated from Kindred society. Other vampires are wary they might end up in his stories, while he's a little worried his work - and even existence - might come to be seen as a Masquerade breach. On top of that, he's concerned by the fact that his work seems to be getting darker than before. I rather like that touch, as it plays into a number of Stephen King works, particularly <i>The Dark Half</i>. As far as traits go, for some reason his Physicals are secondary and Socials tertiary, resulting in threes in Strength, Stamina, and Charisma, but a one in Appearance, which doesn't match up with the art image of a fairly good looking young guy. His abilities include a four dots of Occult, which is pretty impressive. He's also got three dots each in Empathy and Poetic Expression. The rest is a collection of ones and two, including two in Drive. His Disciplines are all in Auspex. I'm a little surprised he doesn't have some Celerity, just as a subtle joke about Stephen King's voluminous level of output. He has four dots each of Resources and Fame. His Humanity is nine, which feels maybe a little high. Willpower is seven. Overall, this is a fairly solid character. I think if he has one weakness, he's probably more suited to a chronicle set in a small town (the sort seen in books by King or Bradbury), which isn't really a bad thing. I think it'd make an interesting idea for a Vampire chronicle.<br /> <br /> <b>The Idol of Millions</b><br /> And now one totally built around the earlier idea of famous vampires. Our character is a big time up and coming movie star who was embraced by a production assistant. One the plus side, she doesn't have to worry about getting old and aging out of the best roles. One the minus side, at some point she becomes a walking Masquerade breach. She's very personable and charming to the media and other people who's opinions she cares about, but cold and arrogant to most everyone else. So your average Hollywood celebrity. And there's not much more to her than that. The image is sort of what I'd imagine Marilyn Monroe would've looked like if she'd be a young star in the early 80s and late 90s, which fits this character pretty well. Obviously, this is a Social primary character. She has a five in Appearance, but only three in Charisma and two in Manipulation. I kind of feel like those could be higher. Physicals are secondary, with threes in Dexterity and Stamina. Abilities include three dots each in Etiquette, Masquerade, and Seduction. Acting is only two dots, as are Athletics, Dodge, Music, and Finance. This is rounded out with a number of ones. Disciplines are all focused on Presence. Backgrounds include five dots in Fame and four in Resources, as well as a few more in Contacts, Retainer, and Status. Humanity is seven and Willpower is six. This isn't a bad character. It's just a little shallow, and she's just kind of there. I feel like she needs a purpose or goal of some sort. Maybe becoming the next huge Hollywood mogul or something. But other than that, it's not a bad character. (I will mention a rather odd bit about both the equipment lists for both this character and the Horror Writer is they both have large caliber revolvers and shoulder holsters. I'm not sure it fits either character, especially the idea of walking around with shoulder rigs.)<br /> <br /> That's all for this time. Next will be part three of Toreador.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-22T15:15:25-07:00","id":1390860,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Toreador (part three)</b><br /> <br /> So, interestingly, our first five were all fairly conventional ideas of what most people would generally think about when you picture a clan that is built around a love of art and beauty and their amazing social prowess and attractiveness. Three are artists and the other two are based on their social abilities. The next five, in contrast, are not the archetypical artist/socialite types, and help expand a little on just what qualifies as &quot;art&quot; and what the clan ultimately is.<br /> <br /> <b>The Martial Artist</b><br /> I like this character as an idea. But I think the execution is lacking in a few areas. He's a martial arts prodigy, having grown up for several years in Hong Kong until his uncle's untimely death forced him to return to the States and his parents. He continued to perfect his art until he drew the eye of a Toreador and was embraced. Now he still tries to perfect his art and the idea of a union of mind, body, and spirit. Except that the fact that he no longer breaths is a minor snag. And also, other Kindred keep trying to use him as a tool or weapon and that's an annoying distraction. To the point he has some vague idea of trying to build his own power base to get them to leave him alone. He's Physical primary, with three each in Strength and Stamina and four dots in Dexterity and Wits. Brawl is, of course, at five dots, with threes in Alertness, Athletics, Dodge, Melee, and Stealth. And then a few ones and twos scattered about. (This is the only character without the Drive skill.) Disciplines are focused entirely on Celerity, and Backgrounds mainly on Generation. Humanity is nine, which feels a little high, but this is mainly the result of his Virtues all being nearly maxed out, with five in Self-Control and four each in the other two. This is a character built for combat, and almost nothing else. The character art even looks like your stereotypical &quot;young guy in a trench coat, band t-shirt and combat boots&quot; trope. Almost like the WoD equivalent of a D&amp;D Fighter, that shines in combat encounters but is effectively furniture anywhere else. This is a shame, because, as I said, I really like the idea of Toreador martial artist as a conceptual idea. There were a lot of ways this character could've gone that would've made him more interesting. The guy who's questing for Golconda through his marital arts studies. Someone who's become a martial arts teacher with his students as both Herd and Retainers. Someone embraced to be a weapon but rebelled and is now an Anarch. Even someone devoted to trying to master other traditional abilities as a way to further master himself and the Beast. All of these would've been really cool and fun to play ideas, I think. But this guy is your generic Lone Wolf McBadass. And while I appreciate the idea of showing people that, yes, you can do that character with a Toreador as well as Brujah or Gangrel, and the utility of going ahead and statting out such a character for anyone wanting to play it, I can't help but wish for more.<br /> <br /> <b>The Outcast</b><br /> This character was always one to buck authority. Juvenile delinquent, kicked out of the Marines, and becoming a tattoo artist, only to be embraced by a Toreador. Except that the embrace was apparently on a dare, and all the other Toreador turned their noses up at him. A gang of Anarchs got him to turn on his sire, only for force him to help commit diablerie on her, and threatened to tell everyone what he did if he didn't join them. (I can't help but wonder if they were really Sabbat pretending to be Anarchs.) So eventually he led a pack of lupines to the Anarchs and ran away as they were slaughtered. Now he's pretty much bitter and alone, with only his art and a desire to be accepted just as himself. His Charisma is a one, which feels maybe low, but I guess is supposed to represent his bad attitude. Streetwise, Artistic Expression, and Firearms are all at three dots. The rest are ones and a few twos in stuff that feels like a list of &quot;abilities most likely to come up in a street based game.&quot; Disciplines are spread evenly, with an extra dot in Presence. Humanity and Willpower are both seven. The artwork is that of an early 90s hip urban pretty boy (and actually looks more like Vanilla Ice than the Fake Rapper did), which fits the character. He's got a Desert Eagle hand cannon. There's nothing really wrong with this character. As an example of how shallow certain parts of the clan are in regards to who and what they consider worthy of their admiration, he works pretty well. I like the idea that he wants to belong somewhere but just can't because of his personality getting in the way. I think there's a lot to play with there. If his stats had a little more fine tuning, I'd probably like him better. But overall, he's not bad. I think he's probably make an interesting character in an Anarch game.<br /> <br /> <b>The Plastic Surgeon </b><br /> This is another of those characters I actually like quite a bit. From a wealthy Southern California family, top of her class in high school, college and med school, and with a successful practice catering to the rich and famous. A Toreador embraced her because she loved the work she did on several of her ghouls. (I could actually see this character being the Childe of the Idol of Millions character, which is kind of cool, I think.) Unfortunately, a lot of other Toreador don't see what she does as real art and look down on her. This infuriates her to no end, as she and her work have been fawned over her entire existence. But she's honestly too scared of them, especially the elders, to actually do anything about it. Meanwhile, she's fascinated by the rumors about Vicissitude and would do anything to learn it. The character art looks like a serious minded upper class medical professional, which I approve of. She's Mental primary, with also four dots in Dexterity. Medicine is at five dots (I'm a little surprised she doesn't have a specialty), and this is accompanied by two dots of Artistic Expression. She's also got three dots each in Subterfuge and Finance, and twos in Computer, Intimidation, Melee (mainly surgical tools), and Drive (again). This is rounded out with a scattering of ones. Disciplines are two dots in Auspex and one in Presence. I'm a little surprised it's not one each, as I could see her having Celerity very easily. Backgrounds include four in Resources, as well as ones in Allies, Fame, Generation and Status. I'm not exactly sure the Status fits what is said about her relation with other Toreador in the write up. Humanity is seven and Willpower is six. All in all, I like this one. It's a cool, atypical concept, but one that still ties into the clan's fixation on beauty and the idea of just what constitutes &quot;art&quot;. She's got a pretty strongly defined personality and a two clear goals. Probably one of my favorites in the books.<br /> <br /> That's all for this one. Next will be the final part of Toreador, with the last two templates and final thoughts. Then Tremere.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-23-2020, 12:08 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T03:01:55-07:00","id":1390939,"title":"","content":"Okay, I fell behind in my commentary but I'm editing one of my books and that's crunch-time for an author. As usual you've done a fantastic job.<br /> <br /> <b>Tzimisce Priest</b>: I've always felt the Tzimisce became Flanderized (see TVtropes.org) over time by the over-focus on Vicissitude. They should not just be <i>Necroscope</i> vampires but also have the elements of Dracula, dark occultism, pagan nature worship, and Eastern European nobility that makes them fascinating. Honestly, the flesh-crafting stuff seems kind of overplayed now and is probably the least interesting thing about them to me.<br /> <br /> <b>Ventrue Antitribu Know it All</b>: I admit, I really liked the take on them for Revised that the Ventrue Antitribu are descended from all the knight errants and heroic warriors of the Ventrue clan that were essentially exiled from the Clan when they switched from being feudal lords to merchants. It makes me think the entirety of the Road of Chivalry got screwed over because it was no longer profitable. The Know It All is a cute concept but doesn't feel very fun to play.<br /> <br /> <b>Malkavian Neurotic Comedian</b>: The Malkavians have always had a serious problem and that's the fact that they're some fundamentally incompatible ideas shoved together. The people who want to play serious mental illness don't get along with the people who just want to psychopathic serial killers as well as slashers while they don't get along with the people who want to play Tricksters as well as gadflies. Then there's the Mad Prophet people and religious ectastics. I feel like the Malkavians should have a bunch of sub-groups that include all of these but no one has ever really thought to do that. I find this archetype would go over like a lead balloon.<br /> <br /> <b>Tremere Stage Magician</b>: I always liked this one because I feel like stage magicians are a perfectly valid cover for Tremere. It allows them to act in an eccentric manner, potentially covers minor violations of the Masquerade, and you can set up places like the Magic Castle to cover your chantry. Mind you, I'm just biased because I've had several Stage Magician NPCs based on Zatanna and Zatara (who are both based on Mandrake).<br /> <br /> <b>Comic Book Celebrity</b>: Honestly, I feel like part of the fun of this thing is the fact Rob Liefeld is actually an artiste and the majority of Toreador would put him down for his <i>medium</i> rather than his art. &quot;Comic books aren't real art!&quot; However, Rob had the business savvy and media skill to really push forward not only independent comic creator rights but also his work. He may not be to everyone's tastes but that makes him more the stereotypical Toreador &quot;creator&quot; than the majority of their kind.<br /> <br /> After all the Toreador would rather have someone that 50-50 love/hate than someone who is loved by all. That would be Stan Lee the vampire.<br /> <br /> <b>The Horror Writer</b>: I wish they'd used Anne Rice as the basis rather than Stephen King. Mind you, I always felt Anne Rice stopping her vampire writing for decades is just begging to be stated to have been the result of Dominate by New Orleans' Prince. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> I also kind of wish that they'd mentioned that the Horror Writer has a motivation for adventuring. Perhaps the Embrace has caused his work to become dull and derivative. He needs actual adventure to get the blood flowing, so to speak. Maybe vampires being worried about being put in his books is accurate.<br /> <br /> <b>Martial Artist</b>: This is meant to be Bruce Lee meets Karate Kid and is a decent enough concept, albeit a bit stereotypical with the view of them seeking Golconda and still being involved in the spiritual side of things. I would much prefer if they'd based this one on Count Dante who is one of my top ten people I would suggest could be a vampire in real life. If anyone was a martial artist a Toreador would Embrace, it would be him.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Count_Dante\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b7/Count_Dante_%28John_Timothy_Keehan%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=index&amp;utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Count_Dante\" target=\"_blank\"> Count Dante - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Count_Dante\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Count_Dante </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 05-23-2020, 06:07 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T11:47:14-07:00","id":1391008,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Toreador (part four)</b><br /> <br /> And our final part. <br /> <br /> <b>The Speed Metal Guitarist </b><br /> This is another of those sort of period archetypes. While by 1994, heavy metal - especially late 80s hair metal - had gone out of fashion in favor of grunge and hip hop, it still had its aficionados, especially among 20-something gamers. Our character is a middle class suburban kid who formed a band in college, went off to LA and had some success only for the band to break up. A Toreador fan embraced him. (And I've noticed this seems to be a recurring origin story for many of the characters in this book. Which I guess is somewhat fitting for the clan, but it tends to make the atypical ones, like the Cult Leader, stand out.) He styles himself as a rebel, spreading a message of rebellion and intellectual and emotional freedom through his music. Apparently he rides that line between Anarch and not-Anarch/Camarilla because he's more focused on his music than actual politics, and also has this idea of trying to influence the powers that be within the clan of the worth of his art and ideals. I suspect this may be a doomed undertaking. His Dexterity is maxed out at five dots. Music is &quot;only&quot; four dots. He's got three dots in Drive, giving him the highest in the book. I presume this has something to do with him having a motorcycle listed among his equipment. (A Harley-Davidson no less.) Athletics is also at three dots. He's got two dots each in Brawl, Firearms and Occult, which feel a little random. He doesn't exactly strike me as the athletic punch-em-up type for some reason. The rest are single dots in things like Acting, Dodge, Etiquette, Melee, Repair, Security, Finance and Politics. I feel like this might could have benefited from some better focus. Disciplines include three dots in Celerity and one in Presence. He's got two dots in Fame. Humanity is eight and Willpower seven. Overall, this isn't a bad character. I do like the idea of someone torn between going full rebel and trying to convince the powers that be of his own worth. I think with some trait modifications, he'd be a little stronger of a character, but otherwise, I can't really complain about him. I actually think it'd be interesting to see him in a coterie with the Nosferatu Shock Rocker.<br /> <br /> <b>The Televangelist </b><br /> Here's an odd on. Again, this probably owes a lot to period cultural archetypes. If you're at all familiar with the likes of modern megachurches, I'll tell you that 80s televangelists were even weirder. And they kept getting caught up in financial and sex scandals. (The 1989 film <i>Fletch Lives</i> is a great send up of the entire thing, especially the shady financial ends of things, and makes a perfect basis for a Werewolf chronicle.) I will note that the character art is a little odd. It kind of looks like a televangelist as interpreted for a BL manga. Very bishonen. It's not bad, just a little strange. It is very visually striking though. As far as the character, he was never very religious, and still isn't, but somehow fell into being a revivalist preacher after high school. No hint as to how or why. He managed to gradually weed out the true believers and over a ten year period, managed to make a name for himself on TV. Eventually this grew into his own 24 hour cable station. And then a Toreador fan embraced him. OK, not really. It doesn't say anything about why he was embraced. And I think that's a shame. The idea of a character like this being embraced specifically for his influence among mortals, and especially for his ability to undermine an aspect of human society that the Kindred have always seen as a potential threat is kind of cool, and I wish this had spelled that out. Anyway, he doesn't know much about Kindred society, and continues on as before, which makes him a potential mark for any vampire looking for someone easy to manipulate. His Demeanor is listed as Fanatic, which I don't think fits. I honestly would've gone for Visionary, Traditionalist or even Judge as the false persona he was trying to project. Charisma and Manipulation are at four dots each, and Perfection and Intelligence at three. Acting and Leadership are at three dots, with Empathy, Intimidation, Subterfuge, Etiquette, Finance, Linguistics and Occult at two. That last one seems a little out of place. He also lacks any ability in Theology, even if just to fake it. Disciplines are two dots each in Auspex and Presence, which seems fitting. He's got four dots in Resources and two each in Fame and Allies, as well as one each in Contacts and Influence. I'm kind of surprised he has no Retainers. He'd also be the type of character I could see as having a high level of Influence. Humanity is six and Willpower seven. Overall, this is a pretty decent character. He plays well into the idea of style over substance, and in hindsight almost seems like a harbinger of the Dark Ages Toreador's interest in the church and Christianity as a focus of both art and adoration. I'd like to see him more integrated into Kindred society, but that's something that can easily happen during an initial chronicle. <br /> <br /> So that's our Toreador. Over all, I feel this was a fairly solid bunch. Several of them I ended up liking a lot more than I remember from 25 years ago. I think the Cult Leader and the Plastic Surgeon are my favorites of the group, but almost all of the others have a lot going for them as well, even if I think some of them could benefit from some fine tuning. If I had to pick a weakest one, I'd probably have to say the Martial Artist, just because he doesn't really have a lot beyond &quot;I'm min/maxed for combat&quot; going on. And I think that could've easily been fixed with just a few altered lines in the write up. So, overall a good collection. <br /> However, three things I will note. One, as I previously mentioned, is this recurring thing with the Drive skill. I suspect part of this is the way 1st and 2nd edition have the skill written up to make it sound like your character can't operate a motor vehicle without it. (At least not without a plus one to difficulty.) Revised would thankfully change/clarify that. The other is the strange fixation on so many of them carrying firearms. It feels like there was this assumption that characters were just going to be getting in gunfights on a nightly basis. And finally, several of the character sheets don't add up. Much like several of the ones from the core book, they've spent too many freebie points. I think what happened here is that when Werewolf came out, it had Willpower costing only one freebie point per dot, and Vampire just switched over to that, but I don't recall any sort of official notice about the change ever happening. If so, this does create an issue in that the pre-made characters from before this change are somewhat weaker than the ones after it. <br /> In terms of representation, we have two women - the Idol of Millions and the Plastic Surgeon - to seven men, and one totally androgynous character, the Burnout. Given that the Toreador always got kind of stereotyped as a feminine clan, I'm not sure if this is a plus or a negative. All of them are white, save the Martial Artist, who is implied to be at least half Chinese. The Idol is somewhat sexualized, but that's in keeping with the character. (If you like cute guys, the Televangelist is almost absurdly pretty.)<br /> As far as additional ones or what's missing, I think the only major one is that I'm a little surprised we don't have a Harpy character totally ingrained into the Kindred social scene. I also think a Gardener, fixated on perfect flowers and the like, and an Etiquette Maven who teaches others how to behave in polite society would've been interesting. A Fashion Designer also. <br /> If I were to give the Toreador a theme, I'd say it would revolve around the idea of perfection. The desire for it, the way master craftsmen and artists strive for it, and the way we are often awestruck by it. Toreador crave that perfection. The want to witness it, create it, even be it. It ultimately doesn't matter if it's a perfect angel or a perfect devil. It's the flame that draws the moth, and they want to be both the moth and the flame. I think most of these characters reflect that idea to some degree. Mostly in their art, but sometimes with a need for vengeance, wanting to be the center of attention, or just wanting to belong and be accepted. <br /> <br /> And that concludes Clanbook: Toreador. Next will be the Tremere.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T12:10:22-07:00","id":1391010,"title":"","content":"I fully believe Jerry Falwell Junior is just Jerry Falwell Senior using Obfuscate or Vicissitude.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T12:21:45-07:00","id":1391013,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1390939#post1390939\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Tzimisce Priest</b>: I've always felt the Tzimisce became Flanderized (see TVtropes.org) over time by the over-focus on Vicissitude. They should not just be <i>Necroscope</i> vampires but also have the elements of Dracula, dark occultism, pagan nature worship, and Eastern European nobility that makes them fascinating. Honestly, the flesh-crafting stuff seems kind of overplayed now and is probably the least interesting thing about them to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>I suspect that when I get to the Tzimisce clanbook, I'm going to find the origins of a lot of how that focus developed. <br /> I think it's fairly common for a clan or other group's hook - the thing that initially draws your attention to them and think &quot;that might be fun to play&quot; - with the actually core of them and what they are - or at least should - be about. With the Brujah, for example, the hook is the leather jackets and rebellion. The core, on the other hand, is that they're driven by - almost consumed by - their passions, be it politics, religion, rebellion, intellectual pursuits, or what have you. With the Tzimsce, the flesh crafting and mad scientist stuff is the hook, but I think a lot of times the shock value of that ended up overshadowing the core idea of a bunch of people trying to transcend the mundane. Sometimes this is a lot of biological mutative weirdness or extreme sadism. But it's just as often those nobles trying to transcend the common rabble, witches trying to transcend the material world for the spiritual, and even those trying to transcend vampirism for Golconda or godhood. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Ventrue Antitribu Know it All</b>: I admit, I really liked the take on them for Revised that the Ventrue Antitribu are descended from all the knight errants and heroic warriors of the Ventrue clan that were essentially exiled from the Clan when they switched from being feudal lords to merchants. It makes me think the entirety of the Road of Chivalry got screwed over because it was no longer profitable. The Know It All is a cute concept but doesn't feel very fun to play. </div> </div> </div>I think it's one of those things that depends on who is playing it and how, and a lot of times these things are entirely subjective. (People who hate professional wrestling or heavy metal music are unlikely to enjoy the two characters from Nosferatu as much as I did.) I also really liked the Ventrue antitribu in the revised Sabbat book, and its call back to the stuff from the Dark Ages. But at the same time, I ended up liking this version of the group a lot better than I remember from two decades ago, and I think there's room for both of them in the Sabbat. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Tremere Stage Magician</b>: I always liked this one because I feel like stage magicians are a perfectly valid cover for Tremere. It allows them to act in an eccentric manner, potentially covers minor violations of the Masquerade, and you can set up places like the Magic Castle to cover your chantry. Mind you, I'm just biased because I've had several Stage Magician NPCs based on Zatanna and Zatara (who are both based on Mandrake). </div> </div> </div>Stage Magician is actually one of my go-to concepts for Mage's Akashic Brotherhood. It's one of those very versatile character types that can work well with a bunch of different groups, including the Tremere. But I just suspect that that character wasn't actually meant to be a stage magician. I'd actually love to have a Tremere stage magician character based on Bill Bixby's The Magician character in a mid-70s Vampire game that was aiming for a &quot;Polyester Gothic&quot; Kolchak the Night Stalker/Blackula kind of feel. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Martial Artist</b>: This is meant to be Bruce Lee meets Karate Kid and is a decent enough concept, albeit a bit stereotypical with the view of them seeking Golconda and still being involved in the spiritual side of things. I would much prefer if they'd based this one on Count Dante who is one of my top ten people I would suggest could be a vampire in real life. If anyone was a martial artist a Toreador would Embrace, it would be him.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Count_Dante\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Count_Dante</a> </div> </div> </div>Count Dante would've made a very cool vampire, yes. Especially in my above Polyester Gothic campaign idea. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T12:33:38-07:00","id":1391018,"title":"","content":"The Drive skill's omnipresence, I believe, is an artifact of game mechanics and the publishing date.<br /> <br /> From First Edition through V20, the rules for the Drive skill described dot one as &quot;can drive an automatic transmission&quot;, and dot two as &quot;can drive a stick shift/five speed&quot;... or the rough equivalent of that phrasing. In addition, Drive was classified as a Skill, so driving untrained raised the difficulty by 1.<br /> <br /> Most STs handwaved it, or only asked for a roll when performing a difficult maneuver. However, this was a time when ttrpgs tended to have much crunchier rules. So, a strict ST could argue that without one dot you literally <i>could not drive a car at all</i>. (What you <i>could</i> drive with zero dots, but taking the +1 difficulty was never explained. A golf cart? A wheelchair?) <br /> <br /> This rule, arguably, made some small amount of sense in the early games. At the time, book standard pcs could be created with up to fifty years since their embrace. This means, technically, a pc could have been embraced in 1941. Automobile ownership was much less common then, and knowing how to drive was far from universal, especially among women and city dwellers. Plus, stick shifts were more common in the 90's, so not being able to drive one was less socially acceptable.<br /> <br /> (As a side note, the fifty-year rule may go a long way toward explaining why so many concepts make reference to the 1970's. It was roughly the median year of embrace for pcs.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11558-nosimplehiway","name":"Nosimplehiway","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11558_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1699"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T13:45:40-07:00","id":1391039,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1389485#post1389485\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Clanbook: Nosferatu (part two)</b><br /> <br /> More monsters.<br /> <br /> <b>The Gross Childe</b><br /> This is one of those names with a double meaning, being both a Childe in the Kindred sense and a child in the literal &quot;this is a kid turned into a vampire&quot; sense. By the time of the Revised era, there was a move away from the idea of children being embraced. It was already kind of frowned upon, but Revised went and spelled out a number of reasons why. It's a very serious potential Masquerade weakness, though that is perhaps not as big an issue with the Nosferatu, and is also seen as an act of cruelty, condemning someone to eternal unlike in a child's body. I'm not really sure this character really cares about that. This is your basic elementary school gross out artist and class clown who never really outgrew that phase in middle school. But where most would've been lucky enough to mature and turn this into a future career as a special effects makeup artist, horror film maker, or what have you, this kid was unfortunate enough to be embraced by a Nosferatu who found his antics entertaining. And like I said, he doesn't really care. In fact, the idea of being a gross looking monster for all eternity sounds down right fun. This is one of those characters I have mixed feelings about. One the one hand, I like the idea of something that plays into the idea that the the Nosferatu are gross and disturbing and will sometimes even revel in it in a &quot;shock the mundanes&quot; kind of way, and this character plays into that in a kind of odd and unique way. On the other hand, there's not a tremendous amount of depth here. That's not necessarily a bad thing, as the character is essentially a child. But given his focus, I can easily see this character being an outlet for certain problem players, effectively being the Nosferatu equivalent of the FishMalks I mentioned at the start of that book. Statistically speaking, he's pretty good. A Wits of five, a Manipulation and Dexterity of four each. Also a Charisma of one, which might be a little crippling in the long run. Three dots each in Stealth, Subterfuge, Dodge, and Toxicology (which is quite frankly disturbing, but in a good way). He's got a pet snake that has apparently gotten bigger after being fed his blood, making him the only character in the book with an animal Retainer. (The book spent a bit of space talking about Nosferatu spawning pits, so I'm a little surprised by that.) He has a Willpower of ten. I find that maybe too high, but I will admit it fits a little with certain aspects of the character's dogged commitment to his interests and lifestyle even in the face of all opposition. All in all, I like this character, but I'm very wary of it being abused by problem players. It's kind of like the Malkavian Waking Dreamer in that I'd have to really trust a player before letting them do this. I also kind of wish it had gone in a little bit more of a darker slant that the Toxicology ability seems to hint at. But it's still a good one.<br /> <br /> <b>The Luchador Profesional </b><br /> I won't lie, this is my favorite one in the book. (The Equalizer comes close, but my love for professional wrestling makes this the winner.) This is your archetypical masked Mexican wrestler. And much in the way that the Detached Scientist birthed Dr. Netchurch, this one birthed <a href=\"http://&quot;&#91;url\" target=\"_blank\">https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/El_Diablo_Verde&quot;]El</a> Diablo Verde[/URL]. At first glance, it might seem that a professional wrestler, even a masked one, is out of place in Vampire. However, Mexico has a long tradition through the 1950s, '60s and '70s of wrestling films in which masked heroes fought vampires, werewolves, mummies and other monsters (as well as gangsters and other enemies of the people), and I find this a fun nod to that. Our character was a champion selected to be part of a mass Sabbat embrace, only to be rescued by Camarilla vampires. Now he wrestles in the US, and I suspect still smashes the enemies of the Camarilla when necessary. He's got five dots in Strength and Charisma, and fours in Stamina and Brawl. I'm a little surprised his Athletics is only at a two. All his discipline points are in Potence. He's got Fame, Herd, and Resources all based around his career as a professional wrestler. (While it may seem odd for a Nosferatu, it's traditional for masked Mexican wresters to never remove their masks in public, and even in the presence of anyone.) As I said, I like this character a lot. He's a little over the top, but he's a really interesting subversion of the Nosferatu stereotype who can fill a number of roles in a chronicle or coterie.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fun facts about this two characters: they could work as a Werewolf/Vampire crossover. Gross Childe has Gooshy-Gooze (TM) in his equipment which is some kind of of jelly toy slime (I believe it was mentioned either in Book of Wyrm or Subsidiaries: Pentex) which comes alive at night, comes down thethe throat of the unfortunate kid and kills it (or makeit a fomori, this is the part that I have to check). And Luchador is later mentioned in Children of the Night (but his stats is largely modified), that he fights for Extreme Wrestling Warfare, a wrestling show sponsored and televised by Pentex where supernaturals of any kind could fight without regarding the Masquerade. And that's make this federation an ideal target for Garou.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":13141,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","user":"Nail Eater","date":". 05-23-2020, 05:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T14:01:21-07:00","id":1391041,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1389724#post1389724\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Clanbook: Nosferatu (part three)</b><br /> And by the same token, it's also impossible to guess what race or ethnicity any of the characters were in their living days, save for the Luchador Professional, who is Mexican. It would probably be a safe guess, though, that the Morbid Artsy Type and the Shock Rocker were probably white. And finally, the Equalizer is, to my knowledge, the first of our character templates to be explicitly LGBT+.</div> </div> </div> </div>I believe that Shock Rocker could be British by ethnicity (assumption based on popularity of grindcore, the kind of music he plays mentioned in description, in UK). Also Equalizer could be the first LGBT character which has that fact in the description. But Ragabash pregen from Rite of Passage has a earring in his right ear which is traditionally attribute to LGBT males. And personally I think that was a great idea, fact proven by my friend who was once playing it. He was accused of mating with other garou (female, which fell in love with him). The sept had guests, a large Get Pack. And, during his trial, he cried resentfully &quot;BUT I'M GAY!!!&quot;. How he gets away from Gets that's another story \ud83d\ude09.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T14:14:48-07:00","id":1391044,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1390159#post1390159\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>The Players Guide to the Sabbat (part three)</b> (...)with a bunch of twos in Drive, Etiquette, Stealth, Finance, Language, Medicine and Occult. I seem to be noticing a trend of characters who are very excellent drivers.</div> </div> </div> </div>As far as I know 2 dots in an Ability is not excelent but a standard level of a competent (read: learned average) human.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T14:30:12-07:00","id":1391049,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1390449#post1390449\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Interlude: Back to the Core Book</b><br /> As <span class=\"b-bbcode-user js-bbcode-user\">Guest</span> mentioned, the second edition core book included tiny little filled in character sheets for each clan. These were, apparently, meant to be the characters illustrated in the artwork that accompanied each clan's two page write up.</div> </div> </div> </div>So you've read my post. And what about characters from Alien Hunger \ud83d\ude42?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T14:38:13-07:00","id":1391051,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1390860#post1390860\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Clanbook: Toreador (part three)</b><br /> (I could actually see this character being the Childe of the Idol of Millions character, which is kind of cool, I think.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Except the Plastic Surgeon is one Generation lower than the Idol. As for Speed Metal Guitarist Brawl is useful in mosh pit, Occult for writing lyrics and Firearms is reasonable after what happened to Dimebag, Lennon and (almost) Lord.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":13141,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","user":"Nail Eater","date":". 05-23-2020, 05:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T15:20:33-07:00","id":1391060,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nail Eater</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1391049#post1391049\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So you've read my post. And what about characters from Alien Hunger \ud83d\ude42?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think I have that book, but if I do it is currently in a closet that has several boxes stacked in front of it from spring cleaning.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T16:35:51-07:00","id":1391068,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1391060#post1391060\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think I have that book, but if I do it is currently in a closet that has several boxes stacked in front of it from spring cleaning.</div> </div> </div> </div>So I see that you've got quite a collection there \ud83d\ude42.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T17:56:25-07:00","id":1391084,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Tremere (part the first)</b><br /> <br /> So, still in 1994, and now the sixth of the Clanbooks. Out cover is a bit strange. Much like the Toreador one before it, it kind of lacks anything that makes it stand up and say, &quot;this is what the Tremere are about&quot;, the way the first four books did. If anything, the guy kind of looks like he's standing in a corner pouting because the other people at the party made fun of his soul patch. Worse, there's an image on page 124 of the second edition core book of a guy with a cane who looks like more of an archetypical Tremere to me, and one much more sure of himself than this guy. Anyway, this book was written by Keith Herber, with additional material by Kerry Thornley. &quot;Additional material&quot; could mean nearly anything, so I've no idea which of them wrote the templates. Nor do I recognize either name off the top of my head, so I'm not sure what else they've done for Vampire or any other games. Artwork is once again by Josh Timbrook. <br /> <br /> <b>Child of the Tremere</b><br /> This is an interesting one. He's described as being from a family with a history of supernatural gifts. In the WoD, this could be any number of things. As this predates the introduction of the Tremere's own family of rogue Tzimisce reverents, the Ducheski, it's entirely possible he could be interpreted as one of them if you wanted to. He was taken away at age five and trained by the Tremere for 18 years, then embraced. He doesn't particularly like being a tool of the clan his entire existence, but he doesn't really have any alternative to compare it to either. Beyond that, he's kind of devoid of personality. It doesn't say anything about what he was taught or why, what he's actually interested in or hopes to achieve, or if he even knows how to fit in with mortal or Kindred society. His Nature is Child, which would imply that he's sheltered and used to having someone else make all his important decisions for him. So I guess you could play him as a sort of fish out of water type if you wanted. He's got five dots in Intelligence, while Socials have him with one dot in Charisma and three in Manipulation. I've mixed feelings about if that is slightly skewed or if it fits his weird upbringing. He's got three dots each in Linguistics, Investigation, Occult, Etiquette, and Subterfuge, and twos in Intimidation, Repair and Security. All other abilities - Melee, Music, Computer, Law, Medicine, and Politics, are only one dot. His disciplines are all one each, with an extra dot in Thaumaturgy. Backgrounds are spread between Generation, Herd, Mentor, Resources, and Retainer. Humanity and Willpower are both at seven. I like this idea as a concept. Unfortunately, that's all it is. It's a basic idea of a backstory and some traits, lacking any personality or motivation. Which is a shame, because I think if he had those things, as well as a better focus on spending his points that would come with it, he'd be a pretty cool character. But as he is, he's just sort of incomplete and fuzzy. <br /> <br /> <b>The Conspiracy Expert</b><br /> I suspect that it is intentional that this character image's clothes bring to mind Darren McGavin in Kolchak: The Night Stalker. This character is a life long conspiracy buff. And by that, I mean a fanatic. He spent his entire life searching for some sort of universal truth behind it all, only to end up embraced by a Tremere promising to give him answers. Now that he knows the truth - or at least thinks he does - he wants to research and investigate and interview every single facet of it he can. He's very enthusiastic about this, to the point I don't think he's aware of what a dangerous world he just stepped into. I feel like his Nature and Demeanor of Visionary and Fanatic may be backwards. He seems more like a fanatic that doesn't quite understand how obsessed he is than a guy with some greater ideal who is just acting as a fanatic. Somewhat oddly, his Socials are primary, with a four in Charisma and threes in Manipulation and Appearance. Mental is secondary, and with a three in Wits and a four in Intelligence, that leaves him with only one dot in Perception. While this character is a little out there, he doesn't come across as oblivious. He's got three dots each in Security, Stealth, Investigation, and Occult. Also twos in Alertness, Empathy, and Drive, then ones in Dodge, Streetwise, Animal Ken, Etiquette, Firearms, Repair, Survival, Computer, Linguistics, Medicine, and Science. These feel sort of all over the place. I'm a little surprised he doesn't have any secondary abilities like Kindred Lore, History, Cryptography, or the like. He's got three dots in Auspex and one dot in Thaumaturgy. Backgrounds are just Contacts at two and Resources at three. I feel like this is the kind of character who should really be maximizing his Contacts more. Humanity is eight and Willpower six. This is not a bad character, and I think he certainly fits with the whole idea of the Tremere - and in extension the Kindred - as this massive conspiratorial secret society. He's kind of an interesting counterpart to the Malkavian Conspiracy Theorist in that he wants to study and understand this thing while she feels she instinctively understands it and wants to destroy it. However, I think he would strongly benefit from having most of his stats reexamined and refocused. <br /> <br /> <b>The Cultist</b><br /> At first glance, this might seem to be a rehash of the Cult Leader in Toreador. However, it's actually quite a bit different. While the Toreador character never believed anything he was selling his followers, this guy is a true believer whose delved into the occult all his life in search of personal power. And finally he found what he was looking for when a Tremere showed up at a graveyard summoning and embraced him. He greatly enjoys the power of being a vampire, and is very likely soon to want even more. He's abandoned or otherwise betrayed almost every group he belonged to in life, and would likely try to sell out the Tremere at some point. He'd probably be voted Most Likely to Join the Sabbat or become an Infernalist. The character image is pretty good. He's got the whole &quot;Goth Satanist&quot; look down. His Charisma is at five dots and Manipulation at four. (Appearance is only two.) Abilities include four dots in Acting, and three each in Intimidation, Subterfuge, and Drive. (I guess this is so he can ride a motercycle.) Strangely, he's only got two dots in Occult. Disciplines are two dots in Dominate and one in Thaumaturgy. Backgrounds are spread evenly between Allies, Contacts, Mentor and Retainer. Humanity is seven, which feels maybe one too high for this guy. Willpower is eight. Overall, I kind of like this character. He's essentially an ass, as likely to throw his coterie-mates under a bus as he is to help them. But I think that plays pretty well into the Tremere clan concept. The only real problem is that I think his traits need the same reexamination and refocusing as the previous character. Other than that, he seem potentially fun. <br /> <br /> <b>The Explorer</b><br /> I kind of have mixed feelings about this character. Overall, I like it. But at the same time, I think it treads way too close to covering a lot of the same ground as the Conspiracy Expert. He's an archaeologist and Egyptologist who far preferred field work to university life. Another Egyptologist pointed him in the direction of a lost tomb in exchange for smuggling back some of the scrolls and artifacts he would find there. At this point, one of those &quot;Curse of the Mummy's Tomb&quot; scenarios unfolded (a nice touch which I greatly appreciate), with him as the final surviving member of the team. Then his Egyptologist &quot;friend&quot; embraced him, because his skills were too good to go to waste. Now he's somewhat stuck acting as a translator and scholar for the Tremere, which feels too much like the stuffy and bureaucratic university life he'd always avoided. He wants the excitement of being out in the field again. His Physicals are primary, which I find a little off for some reason. I appreciate the desire to want to do a physical Tremere, but I'm not sure this is the right one for it. This leaves his Mental as secondary, having a three in each of them. None of his abilities go above three either. Archaeology, Investigation and Linguistics - the things he was specifically embraced for - are each three dots. Science and Occult are only one dot each. He does have twos in Melee, Survival and Leadership. Meanwhile, he's got three dots in Auspex and one in Dominate. I honestly feel those seven freebie points could've been better spent on raising some of his abilities. His Backgrounds are spread between Allies, Contacts and Mentor, with none higher than two. (He feels like one of those characters were a level of Fame wouldn't be out of place.) Humanity is seven and Willpower is six. Overall, this isn't a bad character. He's got an interesting background and is fairly straightforward for a Tremere. But I think this one also suffers from the need to have his traits adjusted to better fit the concept. Also, for some reason I feel like there isn't anything specifically &quot;Tremere-like&quot; about this character. I think if the aspect that this was a guy perfectly willing to break the law and smuggle artifacts just in the quest for his own knowledge was played up a little more, it would solve that issue for me. But otherwise, I generally like this one. <br /> <br /> Thus concludes the first section of Clanbook:Tremere.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T18:09:27-07:00","id":1391086,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nail Eater</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1391068#post1391068\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So I see that you've got quite a collection there \ud83d\ude42.</div> </div> </div> </div>Almost everything from the original White Wolf lines, barring a few gaps from near the end when I was badly burned out, and a fair amount of the early CofD stuff. Plus a bunch of other stuff (Pathfinder, assorted Ars Magica books, Cyberpunk 2020, Kult, Nephilim, Over the Edge, TMNT, GURPS sourcebooks, other odds and ends). I sold all my Deadlands and Rifts books a decade ago, but I'm unlikely to ever get rid of the WoD/WWGS stuff.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T19:18:58-07:00","id":1391102,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Tremere (part the second)</b><br /> <br /> Continuing.<br /> <br /> <b>The Nexus</b><br /> So, in parapsychology and ghost lore, there are poltergeists - spirits that cause physical disturbances such as loud noises, moved or broken objects, and minor physical attacks. And some theories tie these instances to young adolescent or prepubescent girls, either as a fraud to get attention or some sort of psychic manifestation of rampant female emotions. If you've seen the 1982 film <i>Poltergeist</i> (and if you haven't, you really really should), then you are somewhat familiar with the concept. Stephen King's novel <i>Carrie</i> and its 1976 film adaption also tie into certain aspects of the latter theory to a degree. Our character is one of those young girls, and the parapsychologist her parents asked to investigate turned out to be a Tremere who was very interested in an apparently psychic child. One Amber Alert later, she's now a vampire, forever stuck in the age of apparently 14. (The image looks a little younger than this, and what I understand of poltergeist theories is that they start are supposed to start younger than that.) Apparently being a vampire isn't too bad for her, but she's resentful of having her teen years stollen from her. A lot of her mystical research involves trying to find a way to age herself. I could easily see her becoming very rebellious and acting out if this doesn't bear any fruit. Her Nature is Architect, but her Demeanor is Child, apparently because acting younger and more helpless than she really is is a useful strategy for manipulation and survival. She has four dots in Perception, Wits, and Charisma, and three each in Appearance and Dexterity. Strength is only one dot. Abilities include four dots in Masquerade and three in Empathy. Then two each in Acting, Alertness, Animal Ken, Computer, Investigation, and Occult. She's also got a couple of one dot abilities, including Mimicry for some reason. Her Disciplines are focused entirely on Thaumaturgy, I presume to make use of Movement of the Mind. Her Backgrounds are in Generation and Mentor. Humanity is at nine, as she has a Conscience of five (I guess no one wants to think about a cold blooded little girl) and Self-Control at four. Willpower is seven. I feel these may be too high. Over all, I rather like this concept. The problem with child vampires aside, I think it's interesting as both an example of how far the clan is willing to go in its quest for power, and a nice personal tragedy that echoes one of the story lines in Anne Rice's <i>Interview with the Vampire</i>. I do think it would be a bit stronger if it worked more with the idea of a character that is a mature and ambitious adult mind in an innocent looking child form, and if the traits were fine tuned to reflect that. But otherwise, I think it's a pretty good character.<br /> <br /> <b>The Politico</b><br /> In the world of politics, there are figureheads out front, and the real power players in the back. This character always wanted to be one of the power players. And he's really good at it too. A natural manipulator with a keen sense for politics and for other peoples weaknesses. He knows exactly how to build his own candidates up and tear the opposing candidates down using any means necessary. And his skill attracted a like minded Tremere from the old days who decided he'd make a perfect apprentice. He excels at the clan's political games, but plays the dangerous game of making himself look good at the expense of older vampires. I rather like the character art. He looks like one of those generic guys in expensive suits you see in the background of every upper class political event. (I'm a little embarrassed to admit that this is the first time in 25 years I noticed that he's holding a martini in his hand. I think I'd always thought it was a pen or something in front of a white pattern part of his suit.) His Manipulation is maxed out at five dots. Charisma, Perception and Wits are at three each. Politics, Bureaucracy, and Subterfuge are at four dots each, which is very fitting. Acting and Etiquette are three each and Intimidation and Leadership are at two. This is rounded out with some one dot abilities that are all useful to the concept and feel like they fit. His Disciplines are two in Dominate and one in Auspex. This actually makes him one of only two characters in the book without Thaumaturgy (the Explorer is the other) and I've very happy to see a Tremere character who feels extremely Tremere-like without actually needing blood magic. He's got three dots in Contacts and two in Influence. I feel these could be higher. Humanity is seven and Willpower is six. I like this character a lot. Besides my quibble about his Backgrounds, all of his stats perfectly fit the character. And I like the fact that he fits so perfectly in to the clan archetype of power broker and manipulator without having anything to do with magic or the occult. This is probably one of my favorite ones in the books.<br /> <br /> <b>The Professional Witch</b><br /> Some people practice magic for the belief in it. Others do it for power. And then there are those who do it for the money. That's this character. She's from a family of carneys who decided to settle down in New England so her mother could be a fortune telling witch for the tourists at Salem. When her dad left, it was just the two of them and her mother taught her everything she knew, and every so often, she might get an actual flash of something from other people. Eventually her reputation attracted the attention of a Tremere, who decided to embrace her. She's mostly adjusted fairly well, and continues to work as a professional &quot;witch&quot; and fortune teller. She's also trying to further develop the latent talents she possessed while alive. Charisma and Appearance are both at four dots, with Perception and Wits at three each. It might help if her Manipulation was higher than two, but that's probably debatable. She has the Fortune Telling secondary ability at four dots, and three each in Acting, Subterfuge, and Occult. This is rounded out with twos in Streetwise, Investigation, Linguistics and Sleight of Hand, and a few ones. Her Disciplines are two dots in Auspex and one in Thaumaturgy, which fits the character pretty well. I could see her getting extremely dangerous once she learns Dominate. Backgrounds are spread evenly between Allies, Contacts, Generation and Resources. She doesn't have Fame, which is a little odd given that the write up mentions her having a reputation. Humanity is eight and Willpower is seven. While I would prefer to see a few of these points shuffled around a little, I still like this character a lot. She seems intelligent, interesting and fun to play, and I like how she leans into the clan stereotype while at the same time subverting it. Another one that'll probably be my top three of the book.<br /> <br /> Thus ends part the second.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T19:47:47-07:00","id":1391107,"title":"","content":"I think it helped a lot that the early Vampire books had lots of art from Josh Timbrook, especially for the sample characters and NPC portraits. It provided a great deal of unity to the early game. It was cartoony, but every effective. And since anime influenced art hadn't exploded and become commonplace like it is now, it helped put the setting apart.<br /> <br /> I'll limit remarks on two characters from the Gangrel tribebook.<br /> <br /> I don't have the issues with the <b>Babe in the Woods</b> as you do. You say that you don't see how to integrate her into a coterie. I think it'd be the easiest thing imaginable. It's very much the kind of the character template you would give a brand new person to the game (or roleplaying, I know Vampire was the first RPG many people ever played that had zero interest in RPGs before). They don't need to have any starting knowledge. They learn about the game and its setting by playing. How the character develops is completely up to the specific player. It doesn't have a &quot;theme&quot; that all players would try to fill. I think it's great in that.<br /> <br /> I want to mention the <b>Lupine Impersonator</b> because I don't think it was a problem for the game to try to include elements of its entire gamelines. It is a world where vampires, werewolves, wizards, ghosts, and faeries exist. I am not a fan of the Monster Mash genre, but acknowledging that those elements exist and finding a good way to make reference to them isn't a turn off to me. I just don't want them to hijack the game. <br /> <br /> The problem with the Lupine Impersonator concept though is that as written, it can't work because werewolves have these other kinds of forms, and not being able to show them is a huge disadvantageous. But I don't think it is impossible to do so. First, it's not possible to be your typical Gaian Garou in a pack. That obviously fails. However, there are plenty of other concepts such a vampire could pretend to be - the kind of Garou known as Ronin. That is a social disadvantage, but better than getting killed. But it also limits how how much you can learn. It's also possible to play as a Lost Cub. that let's you get away with a lot - but also with the problem your new lupine friends want to bring you to the sept to educate you. You'd need a good story to stop that - but being adamant about rejecting Garou society could do it. But it depends very much on the kind of lupines you first meet. And in an urban game, there are lupines you can encounter that may not give a damn. Bone Gnawers actually have alliances with various kinds of vampires. And Glass Walkers have better things to do than kill vampires, so they like to keep the peace. But since this is a Gangrel template, we can expect it'd be actual wilderness lupines the impersonator runs against. Much more likely to get killed. But perhaps the impersonator ran into Children of Gaia, a lone Silent Strider, or a pack with Unicorm or Chimera totems. People less interested in killing others outright, and perhaps more respectful of letting someone find their own way.<br /> <br /> In other words, even in a Werewolf perspective, I think you can make it work. You just need an ST willing to craft it as an exception.<br /> <br /> I think your words about it feeling more like a Werewolf NPC (or crossover game where players come from different game lines) than a playable Vampire PC in a VtM game is more appropriate. But again, I think this is more of a template for someone to start with. A blank slate so the player can eventually do their own thing, but with one exception. This fits with a player who is attracted to the Gangrel because of their ties to nature. So they want to run in the woods like a wolf and do things like that. And if the coterie ever needed to have someone lead the group into the wilderness, but avoid the lupines, this is the perfect opportunity to allow them to do so. So I think this can be the basis for a starting PC.<br /> <br /> But they had to leave that life because recently they became spooked (they're asking too many questions!) and moved to the city where they ran into the other PCs. This background gives them, more or less, an alternate identity they can use if they run into lupines and present as a &quot;get out of jail free&quot; card. ST just needs to do a little work to allow that and write up how they were able to successfully impersonate a lupine, as that is something the ST needs to justify. A player simply can't do it and make it work because they don't know how to fit it into the setting in a way the ST would like. Plus it opens things for an ST to use this PC's background as a way to introduce future plots. Perhaps not even as a wilderness lupine, as some urban lupine in Central Park or whatever recognizes the &quot;ronin&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T19:51:59-07:00","id":1391109,"title":"","content":"I think you just need to ignore the Humanity ratings in early edition work in the same way that one ignores Rage in Werewolf. Issues like Humanity compared to their moral actions, and the Curse's affect of interactions with humans, just don't make sense for a lot of NPCs. In terms of NPCs, it's just better to revise the stats to reflect how they should be given the character background you've been given.<br /> <br /> Although I will say that in terms of starting character Templates that are supposed to reflect Character Creation rules, one reason for them is that the Player has to assign the points somewhere. And sometimes that leads to contradictions. You just need to deal with the fallout in game. Or work out a way to explain the obvious lack of remorse or guilt that must have accompanied their lifestyle with their Humanity ratings.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T20:10:10-07:00","id":1391112,"title":"","content":"Yes, especially in the early parts of 2nd edition, Humanity and Willpower are essentially just power ratings. I think that was one of the major improvements of Revised edition Vampire is that it actually sat down and thought about what it would actually mean to have a trait at each different level. <br /> <br /> I think my biggest issue with the Babe in the Woods is that I feel like it could make this really cool sort of modern day Grimm's Fairy Tales type character but doesn't. And that just bugs me on a creative level.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T20:29:53-07:00","id":1391122,"title":"","content":"A fairy tale heroine who survives by her wits and retains her virtue is certainly an option, but I don't think that's what most players would do with it. Certainly an interesting NPC concept as you suggested. But I don't think the lack of it ruins it as a starting player template. I think it's useful.<br /> <br /> And I will say that these templates weren't just something for players to use. I think another role was to give the ST an easy NPC to use when they needed to pull something out of her hat for the game while it was being played.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T21:13:21-07:00","id":1391124,"title":"","content":"Now that I've had a chance to read a lot of these template synopsis, it seems to me that a lot of these come from the &quot;spontaneous embrace&quot; school. The sire did not originally intend to embrace a new childe, but ended up doing so for whatever reason.<br /> <br /> I actually think this is a problem because for most vampires it takes a lot of effort to gain permission for the embrace. And if they're anarchs or whatever, an illegal embrace is grounds to get you killed when you otherwise might be tolerated.<br /> <br /> Of course there are runarounds the ST can employ. If you're running the Chicago city sourcebook, you can use Modius post-approval as an escape. Or if you're originally from the Anarch Free State where they might not care about that. Or the sire subsequently flees with their childe and ends up in a new city. Or if the sire is prestigious enough, maybe they have prior approval in the back pocket, or can get the Prince to approve post facto. But it takes effort.<br /> <br /> If there were as many spontaneous embraces in the setting as the templates indicate, you can see why Princes employ scourges. It really affects the setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-23T21:35:57-07:00","id":1391129,"title":"","content":"I'd noticed that as well. I think Toreador didn't lean as heavily into it, but after I finish Ventrue, I'm probably going to make some discussion of it as it applies to the Camarilla and Anarchs as a whole.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-24T00:29:21-07:00","id":1391139,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1391124#post1391124\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now that I've had a chance to read a lot of these template synopsis, it seems to me that a lot of these come from the &quot;spontaneous embrace&quot; school. The sire did not originally intend to embrace a new childe, but ended up doing so for whatever reason.<br /> <br /> I actually think this is a problem because for most vampires it takes a lot of effort to gain permission for the embrace. And if they're anarchs or whatever, an illegal embrace is grounds to get you killed when you otherwise might be tolerated.<br /> <br /> Of course there are runarounds the ST can employ. If you're running the Chicago city sourcebook, you can use Modius post-approval as an escape. Or if you're originally from the Anarch Free State where they might not care about that. Or the sire subsequently flees with their childe and ends up in a new city. Or if the sire is prestigious enough, maybe they have prior approval in the back pocket, or can get the Prince to approve post facto. But it takes effort.<br /> <br /> If there were as many spontaneous embraces in the setting as the templates indicate, you can see why Princes employ scourges. It really affects the setting.</div> </div> </div> </div>Admittedly, this is not just the templates but something that is apparently the favored method of game developers:<br /> <br /> * Vampire: The Masquerade: Redemption: Illegal Embrace<br /> * Vampire: The Masquerade: Bloodlines: Illegal Embrace<br /> * Coteries of New York: Illegal Embrace<br /> * Vampire: The Masquerade: Bloodlines 2: Illegal Embrace<br /> <br /> It's a very good and easy way to make sure your characters aren't inclined to the EstablishmentTM and more inclined to have an antagonistic relationship with the Prince.<br /> <br /> Which may be why it feels off to some people who were assuming the Camarilla was a group you were supposed to toe the line for.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 05-24-2020, 03:31 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-24T01:14:03-07:00","id":1391142,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1391139#post1391139\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Admittedly, this is not just the templates but something that is apparently the favored method of game developers: (...). It's a very good and easy way to make sure your characters aren't inclined to the EstablishmentTM and more inclined to have an antagonistic relationship with the Prince.</div> </div> </div> </div>Spoiler alert <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> True to the bone. It's a leitmotiv of almost every published story:<br /> <br /> Blood Nativity - characters Embraced against the will of Prince.<br /> Alien Hunger - characters Embraced against the will of Prince. <br /> Forged by Steel - characters Embraced against the will of Prince (technically, they traveled to Gary cause Lodin/their nominal Prince doesn't allow that).<br /> Plus in Blood Bond one of the NPCs forcibly Embrace a child. Also in one of the stories from Succubus Club (the one with a game of chess) to save the mortal thrown to them as a meal from asphyxation the characters have to Embrace them. Of course there are many NPCs forcibly Embraced. And on top of that there are Sabat Mass Embraces.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":13141,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","user":"Nail Eater","date":". 05-24-2020, 05:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-24T03:00:59-07:00","id":1391152,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1391139#post1391139\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Admittedly, this is not just the templates but something that is apparently the favored method of game developers:</div> </div> </div> </div>Good point. It's definitely an element to use when you want a game that is more aligned with the Anarchs. There's no problem with that - if that is your intention. However, I wonder how much of it is intended rather than lazy writing. You are really adding a lot more difficulty in the game for your character by going that route. As ST, I'd probably allow the PC to select an appropriate flaw to compensate.<br /> <br /> Personally, I don't remember any of my characters or the PCs of my friends ever being embraced on a whim. Every player wanted their pre-embrace mortal to be &quot;worthy&quot; of it in the sense their sire truly chose them. I could easily be misremembering that. I did have buddies who played Anarchs or undeclared Anarchs (sympathizers) though. But for the most part, the coterie was aligned with the Camarilla. In so far we were against the Prince, it was because we wanted someone to be Prince.<br /> <br /> However, in terms of character motivation, we also need to realize the impact on the sire. They've just painted a big &quot;kill me&quot; sign on it. Unless you're someone who already has a such a sign on the back, this is a huge risk you're taking. How many vampires would actually risk it?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1391139#post1391139\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which may be why it feels off to some people who were assuming the Camarilla was a group you were supposed to toe the line for.</div> </div> </div> </div>I always liked the Camarilla more than the Anarchs, but I never rooted for it in the sense I approved of them. I thought all vampires were monsters. It was just a more interesting society to roleplay in. The Anarchs in general always seemed more juvenile to me. The punk aesthetic (in real life and in the game) always seemed more thuggish and criminal rather than liberty seeking people of integrity. I knew the Camarilla elders and their lackeys were just as bad if not worse, but at least they had <i>style</i>.<br /> <br /> Maybe if the Los Angeles By Night book portrayed a more interesting Anarch society. I know we've discussed that book in the past. It should have really sold the Free State as an interesting place to be, but everyone I know was disappointed by the book.<br /> <br /> But I did think a lot of the canonical Golconda seeker NPCs were good blokes, even if I disliked how some players and storytellers presented the search for it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-24T15:22:00-07:00","id":1391285,"title":"","content":"re: Impulsive Embrace<br /> <br /> Remember, these templates are largely from the viewpoint of the pc being described. It's entirely possible the sire stalked them for months or years, and got all the appropriate approvals. The sire chose not to mention this very creepy fact to the human they just dragged out of the daylight world, and made into a vampire.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> example conversation:<br /> <br /> Alan mused, &quot;Is it normal you embraced me on a whim?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Oh, it was no whim, my boy. I'd followed your progress for years&quot;, said Bob as he leafed through the mail.<br /> <br /> &quot;For years?&quot;, asked an astonished Alan.<br /> <br /> &quot;Indeed, remember the time your computing machine vanished? That was I. Read the contents learning about you. Quite proud I was, too. How modern I had grown!&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;What? I almost got fired for that!&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Pish tosh. I would never have allowed that&quot;, huffed an insulted Bob, &quot;Without a few more years seasoning, what possible good would you have been?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Wait. So, last year when I woke up one morning to find the front door unlocked, and my dog dead?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;I observed your sleep,&quot; explained Bob, &quot;Stood in your chamber for a few hours. And, took notes. You wag your gums in your sleep, you know. Dangerous habit, that.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;You were in my room while I slept? For hours? And, the door?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;I forgot to lock the door on my way out. Surely you've forgotten to lock a door at one time or another.&quot; Bob held up an advertising circular. &quot;Oh, look, there's a sale on at...&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;And.. and... Mister Ruffles?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;I got hungry and his barking almost woke you. Proper restitutions were made. A new dog showed up on your porch the following week, yes?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Bastard.&quot; </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11558-nosimplehiway","name":"Nosimplehiway","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11558_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1699"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-24T19:05:42-07:00","id":1391385,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Tremere (part the third)</b><br /> <br /> And our final chapter.<br /> <br /> <b>The Psych Major </b><br /> A college psychology student motivated by a desire to help the mentally ill, only to be embraced by the Tremere in the belief that he may have some sort of latent prophetic ability. Except that he apparently doesn't. So while his mentor and other prod him to start having prophetic dreams again, he mainly acts as a clan liaison to the local Malkavians, taking advantage of his training and natural empathy. He's moderately successful at it, but really hates being a vampire and keeps contemplating self destruction. He's got four dots in Charisma and Appearance, and three dots each in Perception and Wits. I feel like these are maybe backwards, especially as the character image is of disheveled looking near-burn out. I'd see him more as having 4/2/2 in Social and 4/3/3 in Mental. He's got four dots in Science, I presume with a specialty in Psychology, but it doesn't say. (It also doesn't use any of the secondary abilities.) Medicine, Empathy, and Investigation are all three dots. The rest are single dot skills (including Brawl, Dodge, Melee and Firearms, somewhat oddly), save two dots each in Linguistics and Drive (I presume he drives stick shift.) He's got the usual one each in Disciplines, plus an extra dot in Auspex. I feel like this is one of those characters that didn't need to have Thaumaturgy. Backgrounds are Contacts, Generation, Mentor, and Resources. Humanity is eight and Willpower seven. Overall, I think this is an OK character. He could probably benefit from some point adjustments. If he has a major issue, it's that he just doesn't seem to have that &quot;Tremere&quot; feel to him. He seems to lack the sort of ambition, be it personal, political or whatever, that I tend to associate with the clan. It's not an insurmountable hurdle, just one that holds me back from liking this character as much as I think I could have. <br /> <br /> <b>The Pyramid Climber</b><br /> Another of those late 80s archetypes, but one that's in some ways eternal. A Wall Street executive and trader right out of the movies, with the full on &quot;Greed is Good! Now get out of the way of my Porsche, peasants!&quot; attitude. He wants it all, and he wants it now, but he's definitely ready to put in the time and effort to get it. While not above keeping an eye out for weaknesses to exploit in others, he much prefers the direct route of forcing his idea down other people's throats through sheer force of personality. As such, he's got four dots each in Charisma and Manipulation, with Mental secondary. Strangely, he's got three dots in Stamina at the expense of having only one in Dexterity. (Usually these are the kind of guys to be playing racketball at the office gym at least once a week.) He's only got twos in Bureaucracy, Finance and Politics, but three each in Etiquette, Intimidation, Leadership and Subterfuge. I think some of those abilities could stand a boost. Disciplines are again the three spread evenly, with an extra dot in Dominate. I really feel this is a character that could've done with just Dominate - maybe a single dot in Thaumaturgy if you wish - and spent those seven points on more abilities or backgrounds. His Backgrounds are just Generation five and Mentor three, which puzzles me to no end. This is a character that should have Resources, and possible Influence as well. Maybe even a Retainer and some Contacts. Humanity is seven and Willpower eight. Overall, this is another one of my favorites from the book. He has all that ambition I felt the last character was lacking and then some. I just think he needs some trait adjustments to better reflect the character. <br /> <br /> <b>The Underestimated</b><br /> This is a female character who, in spite of being pretty intelligent and well educated, has always been judged mainly by her appearance. This includes by the Tremere, who apparently embraced her to use as a &quot;honey trap&quot; or something for mortals they wanted to manipulate. (This seems like something a ghoul would be just as suitable for, really.) But she's always, both in life and undeath, wanted to be in the back rooms cutting deals and taking part in the real action of power and influence. Everyone, even vampires, have always dismissed her as just a pretty face, and she's out to take full advantage of this as she begins her climb to the top using the skills everyone forgets she has. Her Appearance is maxed out at five dots, with three in Charisma and Manipulation. Mental is secondary, with Perception and Wits at three each. Besides a three in Politics, all of her skills are just twos and ones, including Acting, Leadership, Subterfuge, Etiquette, Bureaucracy, Finance, and Investigation. So she's reasonably solid but doesn't stand out. Disciplines are one each, which seems fitting. Backgrounds are two each in Contacts, Generation and Resources. Humanity is eight and Willpower is nine. Even thought this character does lean on certain gender stereotypes, especially ones from a few decades ago, I rather like the idea of someone who isn't taken seriously for superficial reasons planning to prove everyone wrong. I think it ties well into the clan's overall idea of ambition, as well as certain aspects of the whole &quot;stodgy and hidebound traditionalists&quot; stereotype they tend(ed) to have. If I were to play this character, I'd probably shift a few trait points around, but other than that, I like it. <br /> <br /> And so that is the Tremere. Overall, I think this was a fairly solid effort. A lot of these are stronger characters than I remember them being, which is a good thing. The Politico, the Professional Witch, and the Pyramid Climber are my three favorites of the book. All three look fun to play and seem to exemplify what I think of when I look for a Tremere. The Cultist, Nexus, and Underestimated are also pretty good ones I think, and the Conspiracy Theorist and Explorer are both pretty solid ideas. I think the two weakest, at least from my perspective, at the Child of Tremere and the Psych Major. Both seem to lack any sense of ambition or goals, and the Child is especially lacking in personality. Which is a shame, as both are good ideas for a Tremere character. I just think they need some refinement. <br /> One recurring bit I've noticed, and I suspect that this is going to become even more of a thing when I get to the non-Camarilla clans, is that all but two characters have Thaumaturgy, and in a few cases it feels like they have it just because they're Tremere and it's available. This ties into something I mentioned earlier regarding the Tzimisce, about a clan's hook getting confused with the clan's focus. <br /> 25 years ago, I was still kind of hung up on the Tremere's hook, so a lot of my efforts at coming up with character concepts tended to fixate on the &quot;they're wizards&quot; trope. I think I remember being a little surprised at the lack of some sort of vampire druid type based off of the Order of the Naturists secret society presented in the book. I think in hindsight, I'm a little surprised there was no Frankenstein type. <br /> In terms of representation, we have three females, all of whom are in sort of &quot;traditional&quot; female roles - little girl, witch, and attractive eye candy. The Professional Witch and Underestimated are somewhat sexualized, but I don't think that's entirely outside the realm of what the characters are meant to represent. They all appear to be white, though I think the Explorer and the Underestimated could be interpreted as black if you wanted to. <br /> I've long considered the Tremere's overall theme to be that of ambition and the pursuit of power, and I think most of these characters fit into that fairly well. The only exceptions are the Child of Tremere and the Psych Major, and that's part of why I consider them the weakest of the group. The others all have some clearly defined goals, seeking personal power for their own advancement or just to ensure their own sense of individual freedom or understanding. And I think that's what makes a good Tremere and makes them stand out. The Ventrue want power and influence for some sort of noblesse oblige and the Lasombra just to dominate the world around them, but the Tremere want it because it's there, and they deserve it. <br /> <br /> Next, the Ventrue.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-24T19:08:52-07:00","id":1391388,"title":"","content":"Honestly, it may be an Occam's Razo