eonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-25T08:52:19-07:00","id":1391489,"title":"","content":"The Underestimated goes a long way to suggest that the idea the Tremere had an especially large problem with sexism among the Clans was a truth not a misconception by House Carna. Which is something that I'm glad we can settle with canon.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-25T09:36:14-07:00","id":1391493,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar12002_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12002\" data-vbnamecard=\"12002\">CTPhipps</a> I assume that you have read vastly more WOD material that me, but I do not think that settles it. It is a clear example where this impression of the Tremere as misogynist might come from. A point of evidence granted.<br /> <br /> That that does not prove that A. The entire clan is like that B. That is anymore misogynist than other clans or society at large.<br /> I guess it just clashes with my impression of the clan being comparably modern and pragmatic.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar12890_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12890\" data-vbnamecard=\"12890\">No One of Consequence</a> Thank you very much for your posts. I very much enjoy it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26030,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26030-mfalkenb","user":"mfalkenb","date":". 05-25-2020, 12:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26030-mfalkenb","name":"mfalkenb","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"49"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-25T09:53:16-07:00","id":1391498,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mfalkenb</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1391493#post1391493\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar12002_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12002\" data-vbnamecard=\"12002\">CTPhipps</a> I assume that you have read vastly more WOD material that me, but I do not think that settles it. It is a clear example where this impression of the Tremere as misogynist might come from. A point of evidence granted.<br /> <br /> That that does not prove that A. The entire clan is like that B. That is anymore misogynist than other clans or society at large.<br /> I guess it just clashes with my impression of the clan being comparably modern and pragmatic.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not to distract but I always had a somewhat bizarre idea of sexism in the Pyramid with the view that it existed primarily in the middle ranks than at the very top or the very bottom. The original Tremere Elders were part of the Order of Hermes and lived in a weird bubble different from the rest of the world with many female wizards working alongside men as equals (or closer to it). Modern Tremere are more used to it as well with Monica Chang (Anarchs Unbound) and other educated women. The Middle Tremere are those Tremere recruited from the 700 or so year period of the Clan's history where women struggled to get education in the sciences or academia.<br /> <br /> So, there's a huge number of Tremere embraced in times of great sexism that won't shake it off like in other Clans. Other Clans like the Toreador, Brujah, Nosferatu, and Ventrue are not going to Embrace exclusively from men because they're not Embracing purely for history in the (very-very sexist) academia and sciences where they'd be banned from among mortals.<br /> <br /> Even then, there would be women Embraced during these time periods by being recruited from either supernatural groups [witches, wizards, cults, psychics] or trained by the original Elder Tremere as apprentices outside of traditional misogynist European society. These would be, maybe, 1/4th of the Clan instead of 1/2 though or maybe even lesser. So in a weird way, yes, I can see the Tremere being objectively more sexist than other clans do to the Middle Management being recruited from Europe's male-only academic network.<br /> <br /> But at the end of the day, it's really just how you want to play the Tremere. My take on the subject is that there is a strain of sexism in the Clan but it's by profoundly petty and small minded men and not the movers and shakers of the Pyramid that are beyond such things [and have aged out of viewing human gender as mattering].","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 05-25-2020, 01:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-25T13:27:51-07:00","id":1391537,"title":"","content":"My take on the Tremere is focused on the idea of &quot;the Usurper&quot;. This term doesn't just imply that one is hungry for power, or has achieved power. It means that one has acquired (or attempted to acquire) power which was not theirs by natural right. But, what are the Tremere usurping? In one sense, their vampiric existence is a usurpation. More specifically, it is their seizing of rights and prerogatives usually preserved for High Clans.<br /> <br /> Each clan has socio-historical themes which inform their cultures.<br /> <br /> The Ventrue, Lasombra, and (especially Old Clan) Tzimisce hold power by right of inheritance, landed nobility, and working within the legal/social system into which they were born and later embraced. They are, at the bottom of it, children of privilege. If they manage to retain the glory of their ancestors, and perhaps extend it a few inches in one direction or another, they will be judged a success.<br /> <br /> Toreador and (traditional Idealist) Brujah hold power by virtue of their personal excellence. Intelligence, charm, bravery, talent, and, in some cases, refined tastes are the elements they combine to rule. Oh, they may have humble origins on paper, but at no time in their lives were they overlooked or thought of as being without potential.<br /> <br /> The Giovanni hold power by a mix of inheritance, nepotism, and cronyism. It doesn't matter how innately skilled they are. If they have the right last name and aren't total F***Ups, they may very well be ghouled or embraced.<br /> <br /> These are High Clans because no one is surprised when they attain power. Their success is barely noted, as it is the natural order of things.<br /> <br /> Then, on the other hand, there are the Tremere...<br /> <br /> The Tremere were never expected to succeed. They were outsiders. Commoners and craftsmen. Scribes and academics. Crass moneylenders and eccentric old crones. They dragged themselves up from obscurity and barged into the halls of power when no one... perhaps, least of all, they themselves... expected them to succeed.<br /> <br /> Tremere are identified with the rising bourgeois middle-class of the middle ages and the renaissance. They held no titles. No prerogatives. They had little more legal rights than a peasant. But, they, as a class, created the urban, intellectual, free-labor economy of Europe.<br /> <br /> Starting with craft guilds (and a heavy dose of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stadtluft_macht_frei\" target=\"_blank\">stadtluft macht-ing frei</a>), they brought in the best specimens of the humblest classes. Their (admittedly imperfect) meritocracy pushed the most ambitious and capable individuals to leadership, at a time when the aristocracy was so inbred some royals <a href=\"https://historycollection.co/16-royals-who-suffered-from-hereditary-mutations-and-defects-caused-by-inbreeding/2/\" target=\"_blank\">could barely eat food or form words</a>. The burghers invented trades, exploited loopholes in the noble's traditions, and amassed fortunes. Making themselves and their cities too profitable for the nobles to dismantle, they demanded and often received rights and privileges formerly held only by feudal lords.<br /> <br /> They eventually fomented revolutions, which <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bourgeois_revolution\" target=\"_blank\">remade society and politics for the benefit of the bourgeois</a>.<br /> <br /> In practical terms, this theme of talented but underestimated individuals coming together to increase their position is seen in all of the templates in the clanbook. These are not people anyone expected to succeed. They all pushed into realms they should have been barely aware of, and -by hook or by crook- made a place for themselves there.<br /> <br /> Now, they are being given the chance to do the same for Clan Tremere in kindred society.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11558-nosimplehiway","name":"Nosimplehiway","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11558_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1699"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-25T13:51:17-07:00","id":1391539,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nosimplehiway</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1391537#post1391537\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My take on the Tremere is focused on the idea of &quot;the Usurper&quot;. This term doesn't just imply that one is hungry for power, or has achieved power. It means that one has acquired (or attempted to acquire) power which was not theirs by natural right. But, what are the Tremere usurping? In one sense, their vampiric existence is a usurpation. More specifically, it is their seizing of rights and prerogatives usually preserved for High Clans.<br /> <br /> Each clan has socio-historical themes which inform their cultures.<br /> <br /> The Ventrue, Lasombra, and (especially Old Clan) Tzimisce hold power by right of inheritance, landed nobility, and working within the legal/social system into which they were born and later embraced. They are, at the bottom of it, children of privilege. If they manage to retain the glory of their ancestors, and perhaps extend it a few inches in one direction or another, they will be judged a success.<br /> <br /> Toreador and (traditional Idealist) Brujah hold power by virtue of their personal excellence. Intelligence, charm, bravery, talent, and, in some cases, refined tastes are the elements they combine to rule. Oh, they may have humble origins on paper, but at no time in their lives were they overlooked or thought of as being without potential.<br /> <br /> The Giovanni hold power by a mix of inheritance, nepotism, and cronyism. It doesn't matter how innately skilled they are. If they have the right last name and aren't total F***Ups, they may very well be ghouled or embraced.<br /> <br /> These are High Clans because no one is surprised when they attain power. Their success is barely noted, as it is the natural order of things.<br /> <br /> Then, on the other hand, there are the Tremere...<br /> <br /> The Tremere were never expected to succeed. They were outsiders. Commoners and craftsmen. Scribes and academics. Crass moneylenders and eccentric old crones. They dragged themselves up from obscurity and barged into the halls of power when no one... perhaps, least of all, they themselves... expected them to succeed.<br /> <br /> Tremere are identified with the rising bourgeois middle-class of the middle ages and the renaissance. They held no titles. No prerogatives. They had little more legal rights than a peasant. But, they, as a class, created the urban, intellectual, free-labor economy of Europe.<br /> <br /> Starting with craft guilds (and a heavy dose of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stadtluft_macht_frei\" target=\"_blank\">stadtluft macht-ing frei</a>), they brought in the best specimens of the humblest classes. Their (admittedly imperfect) meritocracy pushed the most ambitious and capable individuals to leadership, at a time when the aristocracy was so inbred some royals <a href=\"https://historycollection.co/16-royals-who-suffered-from-hereditary-mutations-and-defects-caused-by-inbreeding/2/\" target=\"_blank\">could barely eat food or form words</a>. The burghers invented trades, exploited loopholes in the noble's traditions, and amassed fortunes. Making themselves and their cities too profitable for the nobles to dismantle, they demanded and often received rights and privileges formerly held only by feudal lords.<br /> <br /> They eventually fomented revolutions, which <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bourgeois_revolution\" target=\"_blank\">remade society and politics for the benefit of the bourgeois</a>.<br /> <br /> In practical terms, this theme of talented but underestimated individuals coming together to increase their position is seen in all of the templates in the clanbook. These are not people anyone expected to succeed. They all pushed into realms they should have been barely aware of, and -by hook or by crook- made a place for themselves there.<br /> <br /> Now, they are being given the chance to do the same for Clan Tremere in kindred society.</div> </div> </div> </div>My take on the Tremere, by contrast, is exactly the reverse. The Tremere are beings who don't think of power the same way as the other clans. Power is not money, power is not fame, power is not titles, power is POWER.<br /> <br /> The Tremere venerate your ability to enforce your will on the world and respect it most when you are able to do it by direct command of others through magic, Dominating them into submission, sensing weaknesses, or the fear that people have of you because your powers.<br /> <br /> If I were to summarize my opinion of the Tremere, they are best defined as a <b>cult</b>. The cult is full of rituals, titles, ranks, and privileges wholly outside of the vampire race's normal secret society. They are a secret society within a secret society.<br /> <br /> The Tremere who founded the place were an aristocracy among aristocracies by being recruited form mages. Anyone could be Enlightened in those days. Nicolai, as we saw, was a turnip farmer's child when trained by his master. They seek psychics, witches, sensitives, and other people they mark for greatness with little regard for society's idea of it because the only people's approval that matters is the leader of their secret society.<br /> <br /> In a way, being a vampire diminished their off-brand oddity because they gained the ability to bestow &quot;greatness&quot; on anyone they wanted with the Embrace. Librarians, laywers like Abraham Dusable, scholars, and college administrators that would otherwise live mundane lives.<br /> <br /> Part of what I liked about <b>The Politico</b>, <b>The Professional Witch</b>, <b>The Psych Major</b>, and <b>The Conspiracy Theorist</b> is they're BAD Embraces. They're people the Tremere recruited and misjudged their supernatural arete. It's an irony that someone like Erichtho was Embraced for her beauty and to be Nicolai's consort (eww) but is probably one of the best Tremere they could have recruited.<br /> <br /> It's interesting to speculate, really, just how many Tremere kind of...suck...but survive because the Pyramid is about loyalty as well as subservience.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-25T14:05:44-07:00","id":1391545,"title":"","content":"Good reads so far! Vampire has always had interesting characters and sorta of introduces the splatbook to games.<br /> <br /> I also see the Tremere as usurpers. Taking the group from Ars Magica, which was a weaker house but played politics well, and then making them turn and become vampires, from a Mage: The Ascension perspective the Tremere are definitely usurpers. The Tremere freed themselves from Paradox and took Hermetic secrets with them to found another mystery cult, their clan. That clan embedded itself in vampire society.<br /> <br /> Not surprisingly, <i>Blood Treachery</i> in Mage: the Ascension has the Hermetics in the down times of Mage Revised go after the Tremere for their secrets. The Order of Hermes as a whole has trouble distinguishing vampires and the Tremere are able to survive in part because they built themselves enough into vampire societies that their fellow vampires will now fight the Order of Hermes. The Second Massassa War was a debacle for the Order of Hermes. Unless you go with metaplot weakening the Tremere, the Tremere went from stealing secrets of mages and being nobodies to very powerful beings in vampire society.<br /> <br /> In my Mage game, the only good Tremere is a dead Tremere, because the main NPCs are Order of Hermes. Other vampires are bad, but there's not the hatred for them.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Massasa_War\" target=\"_blank\">https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Massasa_War</a><br /> <br /> It would be interesting to see how different the example characters are between the original clanbooks and the Revised clanbooks in a side-by-side comparison.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-25T18:12:18-07:00","id":1391576,"title":"","content":"Ambition has always been at the heart of the Tremere, even in the original Ars Magica days, with their founder and the founder of House Tytalus having the same master and holding a lot of the same attitudes about the virtues of doing everything it takes to win. Their co-invention of Certamen and the encouragement of its use to settle disputes within the Order was motivated by the idea of giving them an edge in solidifying their overall influence in the group, and turning themselves into vampires was a way to hold on to power in a world were the traditional Hermetic arts were growing weaker. But once they because vampires, they couldn't hold on to the old Hermetic attitudes that mundane human society and its halls of power were largely irrelevant, nor to the idea that things like physical combat could be left solely to faceless Grogs. They had to start embracing people for reasons other than mystical aptitude, including merchants, diplomats, and their Wardens in order to build up a power base among the Kindred and defend themselves from the Tzimisce (and later the Order of Hermes). But the people they tended to embrace for these roles were ones who were ambitious and power hungry enough to think that being a vampire was a perfectly acceptable trade off for the chance at personal power and advancement. The Politico was embraced by one of those type of Tremere because he's exactly like the sort of political animals the clan has been embracing since the Dark Ages. The Conspiracy Expert's ambition is largely centered around his own desire to understand the secret masters of the world, and in stark contrast to the Malkavian Conspiracy Theorist who wants to destroy the conspiracy, he just wants to learn more about it and, in all honesty, finds being a part of it kind of cool. While the Professional Witch may have some latent psychic abilities - ones which Auspex have probably developed much further than she ever would have as a mortal - what ultimately makes her a perfect Tremere is that she was raised to view everyone else in the world as marks for her to take advantage of for her own benefit. (Honestly, I'd love to see her in a Boston based chronicle in the same coterie as the Brujah Elder Representative and possibly the Malkavian Mesmeric Manipulator as a rival or frenemy.)<br /> <br /> As far as the issue of sexism in the Tremere goes, there was kind of a theme running through the early WoD stuff of having a group of righteous underdogs vs what was essentially &quot;The Man&quot;, representing all the sexism, racism, and every other ism that cool GenXers were against. In Vampire's case, you've got the rebellious Brujah and outcast Nosferatu vs the snobbish Toreador, elitist Ventrue, and scheming Tremere, with the mad Malkvians and loner Gangrel sort of in the middle, but more often than not leaning toward the underdogs. This carried over into Werewolf, with the elitist Silver Fangs and scheming Shadow Lords as &quot;The Man&quot; compared to, say, the Bone Gnawers, Black Furies, or Wendigo. The Order of Hermes played &quot;The Man&quot; role in Mage, with the Celestial Chorus and sometimes Sons of Ether backing them up. Wraith just went for a full on Renegades vs Hierarchy thing. Thankfully this kind of faded away. Vampire: The Dark Ages even pointed out that certain gender attitudes were a little impossible to maintain when that frail and weak looking noblewoman could very well be a 200 year old Brujah with Potence 5 and able to tear your arms out of their sockets if you pissed her off. (This is one of those reasons I always wished there'd been an Ashen Lady book about women in the Dark Medieval world, as the reality was far more interesting than the stereotype and there's all these interesting areas were vampires, werewolves, mages, and the like don't have to and aren't going to follow &quot;the rules&quot; unless they damn well feel like it.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-25T18:39:00-07:00","id":1391580,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1391576#post1391576\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As far as the issue of sexism in the Tremere goes, there was kind of a theme running through the early WoD stuff of having a group of righteous underdogs vs what was essentially &quot;The Man&quot;, representing all the sexism, racism, and every other ism that cool GenXers were against. In Vampire's case, you've got the rebellious Brujah and outcast Nosferatu vs the snobbish Toreador, elitist Ventrue, and scheming Tremere, with the mad Malkvians and loner Gangrel sort of in the middle, but more often than not leaning toward the underdogs. This carried over into Werewolf, with the elitist Silver Fangs and scheming Shadow Lords as &quot;The Man&quot; compared to, say, the Bone Gnawers, Black Furies, or Wendigo.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a side note, but one of the things I've found interesting is how this was slowly subverted over time in Werewolf. In the original editions, the Silver Fangs were clearly screw ups, leading the Garou Nation into defeat, and acting increasingly tyrannical in order to shore up their political support. In first edition, some passages indicated that things were almost to the point of open revolt in many tribes. However, this was over turned in Revised, and I must say I've almost never seen such characters as PCs or NPCs in LARPs or Online play. I think the stereotype of the Good King was just too strong, and many players liked the idea of playing members of a royal family so they can be princes and princesses (although almost always with a common touch). Part of this is simply the player base wanting to play &quot;good guys&quot; in Werewolf, but I think a large part is that many players like the idea of being royalty. But even the Shadow Lords became good guys!<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-25T18:49:18-07:00","id":1391581,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1391580#post1391580\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This is a side note, but one of the things I've found interesting is how this was slowly subverted over time in Werewolf. In the original editions, the Silver Fangs were clearly screw ups, leading the Garou Nation into defeat, and acting increasingly tyrannical in order to shore up their political support. In first edition, some passages indicated that things were almost to the point of open revolt in many tribes. However, this was over turned in Revised, and I must say I've almost never seen such characters as PCs or NPCs in LARPs or Online play. I think the stereotype of the Good King was just too strong, and many players liked the idea of playing members of a royal family so they can be princes and princesses (although almost always with a common touch). Part of this is simply the player base wanting to play &quot;good guys&quot; in Werewolf, but I think a large part is that many players like the idea of being royalty. But even the Shadow Lords became good guys!</div> </div> </div> </div>Notably, Albretch was basically meant to be Connor Macleod and a renegade street level Silver Fang. Making him the King of All Werewolves changes things a wee bit.<br /> <br /> The Shadow Lords also had the Margrave who is, &quot;I dunno, maybe the Shadow Lords SHOULD be in charge if this is their guy.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-25T19:10:59-07:00","id":1391587,"title":"","content":"Yeah. My general impression of those characters is the same as when I had GMs include their PCs in the campaigns they ran as NPCs, and they were always much &quot;cooler&quot; and more powerful than they actually were as PCs in other games. This happened in tabletop a lot, but it was really bad in LARPs when members of the player base routinely spent time as one of the STs (or sometimes leave one LARP where they played their character, and then introduced them as a GM NPC in a new LARP that they ran!). The very few times I acted as ST in a LARP, I always introduced an excuse why my PC had to suddenly leave the game.<br /> <br /> I think Albrecht at least is a valid PC concept. But it should be noted that when the Morningkill family was introduced in first edition, there seemed to be a LOT more Silver Fang kings than the handful of houses we'd later get.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 05-25-2020, 10:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-25T19:41:33-07:00","id":1391595,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Ventrue (in prima parte)</b><br /> <br /> <i>Hear ye, hear ye. Let it be known to all that this missive shall instigate discussion and judgment upon that folio known to all as The Book of the Clan of Ventrue, and to those Lords thus demonstrated therein, as chronicled by Master Andrew of Greenberg in anno Domini Nineteen Hundred and Ninety Four and illuminated by Joshua Gabriel of Timbrook. </i><br /> <br /> And that's enough of that. This is the last of the original seven Camarilla clans, and somewhat curiously written by the gameline's Developer, which is sort of unusual. (Richard Dansky once told me that anytime he actually wrote any material for a line he was developing, he'd have to get one of the other developers in the office to look it over as it would be missing one of the usual steps of quality control and oversight otherwise. Usually if you see a Developer writing a book for their own line, it was because it was an emergency fill in - such as Ethan Skemp co-writing the original Silent Striders or Phil Brucato co-writing the original Euthanatos - or a passion project on a subject they really love - Phil Brucato doing the original Cult of Ecstasy book.) Our cover is, in my opinion, a lot better than the last two. Even with shaved head and dark sunglasses and leather jacket, this guy radiates a level of cocky self-assuredness that you'd expect from one of the Ventrue. It's dynamic and kind of hip in a way that isn't ostentatious. The back cover image is of a black man with a cigarette and a sort of world weary look in his eyes as if to say, &quot;what are you rebelling against <i>this week</i>?&quot; I like that. <br /> <br /> <b>The Anarch Wannabe</b><br /> Damn it. I rather dislike this character image. I've expressed my dislike for &quot;wannabe&quot; anything as a concept before, and the image of a slightly effeminate young man in designer leather jacket and pre-ripped jeans only adds to that. (I think the idea that the effeminate aspect is maybe supposed to make him look less serious or respectable only makes it bug me even more in hindsight.) And that is a shame, because otherwise, this isn't a bad character. The intelligent and well educated child of wealthy privilege, he's been forced to conform and live up to his parents' expectations and plans of a perfect coastal elite upbringing. The best pre-preschools to get on that track to the Ivy League at age 3, various tutoring and extracurriculars, elite prep schools, that sort of thing. Even after graduating at the top of his class from Yale and starting at a big time financial company, he was still under his parents' thumb. And then he got embraced, and suddenly what they want doesn't matter anymore. Even if he's still somewhat beholden to the clan, he relishes his new found sense of freedom and doesn't want to lose it. That's why he heavily sympathizes with the Anarchs, even if he isn't exactly a member of that sect. He doesn't mind shouldering the responsibilities that come with being a Ventrue, but he only wants to take on the responsibilities that he wants to rather than those other people try to force on him. And he's rather torn between his duties to his sire and his desires to go full Ararch. I really enjoy that sense of conflict and competing desires and responsibilities. He's Mental primary, with a 3/4/3 split, and Social secondary, with a 3/2/3, making him pretty smart and personable. Ability-wise, he's got three dots each in Etiquette and Finance, with twos in Bureaucracy, Investigation, Linguistics, Politics, Drive and Leadership, and then a couple of ones to round it out. Disciplines are the one dot in each with an extra dot in Fortitude set up. I really think he could've used those seven points more effectively for abilities and backgrounds, probably dropping the dot in Dominate or Presence, depending on how you wanted to play his personality. Contacts are spread evenly between Contacts, Generation, Herd and Resources. I'm a little surprised he doesn't have a Mentor. Humanity and Willpower are both at eight, neither of which bothers me over much. I like this character. He's got an interesting and sympathetic backstory that sets him up for a potentially dramatic conflict as the chronicle goes on, and he's got a fairly well rounded ability set. I just wish he was called an <i>Anarch Sympathizer</i> rather than a wannabe. <br /> <br /> <b>The Born Leader</b><br /> I like this one. The image is pretty good. Young, somewhat arrogant looking, very sure of himself. He could almost be the guy on the cover, just with hair. (And just from an artistic standpoint, I appreciate the use of very simple lines and minimal shading on an almost all white image. It's kind of a nice contrast to a lot of the darker colored artwork uses in a lot of Vampire art, especially with the leather jackets and the like. This sort of clean simplicity is something I actually miss a lot from the early 90s era of RPGs.) As far as the write up goes, this guy is a little bit more of a blank slate than the previous character. This is not necessarily a bad thing, as it allows more room for a player to fill in the spaces, but this does make him stand out a little less than other templates might. He's the sort of person who has always known the best way to do things - or at least thought he did - and could usually convince people to go along with his ideas. I think. It's actually a little contradictory about that, at first saying he had difficulty doing so at times, but later saying he was specifically embraced for his ability to sway others to his way of thinking. I wish this was a little more clear. But he rather enjoys being a vampire, especially the supernatural edge it gives him in convincing people that his way is the right way. He's very arrogant, and wants to be the center of attention, to the point that even some elders find him a little overbearing. Ironically, this apparently makes it easier for him to work with other clans, even Anarchs at times. Common enemies and all that. I kind of want to throw this guy into a room with the Tremere Pyramid Climber and see what happens. His Charisma is maxed out at five dots, and he's got three each in Manipulation, Perception and Wits. Abilities include three dots each in Leadership and Investigation, with twos in Acting, Empathy, Intimidation, Streetwise, Firearms, Bureaucracy, and Politics. (I kind of wish his Leadership was one higher, but his Charisma kind of makes up for it.) Disciplines include three dots in Presence and one in Dominate. I have mixed feelings about that. I could really see those seven points going a long way in boosting abilities and backgrounds; Alternately, I can see a good argument for having two each in Dominate and Presence. Backgrounds are all one or two each in Allies, Contacts, Herd, Influence, Resources, and Status. Humanity is eight and Willpower seven, which makes him a little less willful than the previous character. So, overall, I think this is a pretty good character and potentially fun to play. <br /> <br /> <b>The Capo</b><br /> A bit of a throwback to the 60s, 70s and 80s, but even now still has a certain place in certain cities. (And this character certainly works perfectly well as a different ethnicity, such as Russian or Japanese.) He grew up in the world of organized crime, thinking of them as his family in every way that mattered, and willing to do anything for the betterment of the family. Apparently even killing his uncle. One of the true powers behind the family admired his brutal ruthlessness and dedication, so made him a ghoul and eventually a vampire. (This is one of the handful of characters specifically mentioned as being a ghoul.) Now those attitudes he had for the family carry over to the Ventrue, and he'll do whatever it takes to advance both the interest of the clan and his own place in it. The image is of a pretty slim and wolfish looking classic mobster in pinstripe suit. I'm especially glad that he doesn't look like any of the characters from <i>Goodfellas</i>. (In fact, he looks like he would pistol whip Joe Pesci within an inch of his life the second he got out of line with that irritating schtick of his.) Physicals are primary and Socials secondary, but he does have an extra dot devoted to Wits. He's got most of the abilities you'd associate with a criminal enforcer, including three dots each in Brawl and Intimidation, and twos in Dodge, Streetwise, Subterfuge, Drive, Firearms, Melee, Stealth, and Security. Disciplines are one each. Backgrounds are ones and twos in Allies, Contacts, Fame, Influence, and Resources. I feel like a few of these could stand to be a little higher. Humanity and Willpower are both seven. (And if you've been keeping score, then this means that the murderous mobster has more Humanity than the Brujah Skinhead. For reasons, I guess.) Overall, I like this character. He's a little bit of a stereotype, but one that I think fits well with the clan, especially the idea of the parallels between the mob family and the Ventrue, and how this character approached both. So, continuing a pretty strong start. <br /> <br /> That's all for this entry. Next will be part two of Ventrue, hopefully managing to fit four of them in.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-25T19:50:03-07:00","id":1391599,"title":"","content":"Albrecht was always one of those characters I thought should've been reserved as a PC, so that your character could be the one to play Aragorn and do the Return of the King bit with the Silver Fangs (for whichever house you preferred) and be the beacon of hope right before the Apocalypse. <br /> <br /> I'm hoping to eventually get around to doing one of these for Werewolf. Once, as an intellectual exercise, I tried to come up with five famous fictional nobles who would work really well as Silver Fang auspice template names. I remember Mordred (the bastard Metis who is probably destined to overthrow his parent's rule of whatever sept they're from), Tarzan (the Ice Fang lupus &quot;noble savage&quot; archetype), and Hamlet (the Philodox who tends to over-intellectualize to the point of paralyzing himself), but I forget who the other two were.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T22:02:43-07:00","id":1391859,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Ventrue (in secunda parte)</b><br /> <br /> Continuing.<br /> <br /> <b>The Corporate Executive</b><br /> Essentially, this is a refinement of the original template from the Players Guide. A fast track ladder climber with an MBA (from Harvard, no less), on the way to being a CEO. However, the character was being groomed for all of this by their soon to be sire, and was embraced before that happened. Though somewhat regretful at the fact that they'll never get the public recognition of their talents they've always craved, the challenge and perks of being a vampire help make up for it. The clan, and to an extension the Camarilla, is like a huge shadowy corporation, and they can't help but imagine what the combined power and influence could accomplish if Kindred actually worked together. Especially under her leadership and guidance. While the write up doesn't specify (save for a mention of business dress under equipment), the character image is a woman. (The list of equipment also includes a portable FAX machine. How 90s.) She's Mental primary and Social secondary. However, she's got a two in Charisma and three in Appearance, and I can't help but feel like that's mainly just because she's female. (This is one of the reasons I'm glad CoD got rid of that trait.) Given she's described as the classic iron fist in a velvet glove personality, I'd think she'd have those two switched. She's got three each in Finance and Etiquette. (I think I made a quip about Brujah being issued three dots of Politics and it feels sometimes like Ventrue are done the same with Finance.) Bureaucracy, Investigation, Politics, Alertness, Empathy, and Leadership are all two each. I think there's an argument that one of those dots in Empathy could've been better spent raising her single dot of Intimidation or Subterfuge to two. Disciplines include two dots in Dominate and one in Presence, which feels very fitting. Backgrounds include four in Resources, and two each in Contacts, Generation, Herd and Mentor, and one each in Influence and Status. I'd probably move one of those Herd dots to Influence if I were playing this character. Humanity is eight and Willpower is seven. Overall, I think this is a pretty good character. It's a classic clan archetype, and done fairly well. While I have a few quibbles about some point placement, overall, it's very solid and great for one of those games involving a lot of politicking and intrigue.<br /> <br /> <b>The Counselor </b><br /> I remember back when the book first came out, it taking me a few rereads to actually get this one. The name is basically a play on the fact the it's used to refer to both therapists and lawyers, and while the image looks like that of a therapist, he's actually a lawyer. (Though, really, I think the idea of a Ventrue therapist/psychologist would be kind of fun.) He's always been a really good listener, and at telling people what they want to hear. This is something he does more for his own benefit and advancement than out of any sense of loyalty or altruism, which seems perfectly fitting for a vampire. He opted to go to law school instead of medicine or psychiatry. One of his clients apparently enjoyed his advice so much that he ended up embracing him. Now, he's somewhat terrified that other vampires might see through his act, but Kindred seem to love having their egos stroked even more than the living. He's Social primary, with four dots in Charisma, and Mental secondary with three each. He's got three dots each in Alertness and Empathy, and twos in Acting, Subterfuge, Etiquette, Bureaucracy, Investigation, and Politics. But wait, you might ask, I thought he was a lawyer? And yes, he only has one dot in Law, which feels like a major oversight. Even if he's not a very good lawyer, I'd have expected him to have at least two dots in the skill (and preferably more, as he's the only character in the book whose focus is law). Disciplines are two in Dominate and one in Presence. Backgrounds include a four in Resources, two in Allies and Herd, and one each in Retainer and Status. Humanity is nine and Willpower is six. Overall, I like this character, in spite of the Law ability issue. He's got an interesting hook of being a bit more &quot;weaselly&quot; than your typical Ventrue, and feels like he'd be fun to play in a game focused on intrigue and the Elysium.<br /> <br /> <b>The Lover of Unlife</b><br /> In some ways, the previous character could almost be seen as someone who blurred the lines between Ventrue and Tremere. Similarly, this one blurs the lines between Ventrue and Toreador. Born to extreme wealthy and privilege, she's never wanted for anything and has had every opportunity to hone her natural talents in whichever way she wished. She is perfect in every way, and will never let you forget that fact. Now that she's a vampire, she's pretty much the same, except possibly even more arrogant. She epitomizes the Ventrue in their role as pillars of culture and respectability, and I rather love her for that. The artwork is especially nice. Like the Born Leader, it uses a lot of strong lines and white space to great effect. She's Social primary, of course, with a 3/3/4 split. I don't really begrudge this one having Appearance as her highest trait, as it seems to fit the character. Physical is secondary, with 2/3/3. I found this a little odd until I saw she had two dots in Athletics and realized that those natural talents probably include one or more sports. I admit that I kind of like the idea of a Ventrue who tries to play golf, tennis or the like whenever she can. Abilities include threes in Acting, Etiquette and Music, and twos in Empathy, Leadership, Subterfuge, Drive, and Linguistics. (She has a single dot in Firearms, which I probably would've given her in Melee, just because Fencing seems like something she'd be into.) Disciplines are, again, two in Dominate and one in Presence. I feel like maybe that is backwards, although I also think this is a character where Fortitude wouldn't be at all out of place. Backgrounds include four dots each in Generation and Resources. (I'd have expected that last one to be five.) Also twos in Herd, Mentor, and Retainers, and one in Status. Humanity is seven and Willpower six. I rather enjoy this character, and recall doing so when the book first came out. I think my favorite bit is the comment that people feel inferior around her because, well, they are. This is another one that I think is a great choice for chronicles that focus on Vampire as a game about intrigue and social conflict.<br /> <br /> <b>The Military Man</b><br /> A somewhat older character. Went to military school, graduated West Point, served as an officer in Vietnam and later more covert work around the world, and finally Grenada. Then he was drafted into the Ventrue as part of their ongoing war against the enemies of the Camarilla. At first glance, this looks like a little bit of a one note stereotype. The image is clearly derived from a late 80s Arnold Schwartzenegger and the character quote is adapted from <i>Apocalypse Now</i>. However, the character does play into the clan's ideals of actually caring about the people serving under him, as well as trying to be fair and work with everyone toward the common goal. He's Physical primary and Social secondary. His abilities include three each in Drive, Firearms, Investigation, and Linguistics, with twos in Athletics, Brawl, Leadership, Stealth, and Medicine. This feels somewhat insufficient for a character described as &quot;knowing all the tricks of leadership&quot; and, well, generally being a military field commander. I feel his Leadership should be higher, and he could benefit from some Intimidation. And if any character was ever going to have the Military Science secondary ability from the second edition Players Guide, this would be the one. And it is at this point that I notice he's only got ten dots in Knowledges (and nine Skill and seven Talent). So I'm guessing he's actually supposed to have three dots in Military Science and they were left out. Oops. Disciplines are two in Fortitude and one in Presence. Backgrounds are evenly spread between Allies, Contacts, Generation, Resources, and Status. (One book from around this period, either Elysium or DSotBH, I forget which, introduced a Military Forces background, mainly for elders, but I could actually see it fitting with this character.) Humanity is seven and Willpower nine. As an effort to create a physically and martial focused Ventrue while still holding to the clan's modern day image, I think this is a good idea. However, I think the execution is lacking in terms of traits, or at least in regards to editing.<br /> <br /> That ends this entry. Next will be the third and final part of Ventrue.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-27-2020, 08:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-27T07:05:32-07:00","id":1391920,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1391859#post1391859\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, she's got a two in Charisma and three in Appearance, and I can't help but feel like that's mainly just because she's female. (This is one of the reasons I'm glad CoD got rid of that trait.) Given she's described as the classic iron fist in a velvet glove personality, I'd think she'd have those two switched.</div> </div> </div> </div>For her, a 3 Appearance makes sense.<br /> <br /> Appearance is about first impressions and forming superficial, momentary relationships. It doesn't need to represent classic, sex-oriented beauty. It can as easily represent a firm handshake, good eye contact, and an aura of competence. The Appearance is the velvet glove, as people who first meet her see her as highly polished, personable, and polite. Perfect for job interviews and giving presentations in business.<br /> <br /> Charisma 2 is what happens after people get to know her a little bit. She presents well on first meeting, yes, but she's not the sort of person you would follow into a hail of gunfire or write sonnets about. In her push to prepare, prepare, prepare for success, she may have let normal adolescent socialization slide a bit. Some people (her administrative assistant, her social acquaintances, her coterie) who have time to get to know her see the iron fist side of her personality more clearly.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>The Lover of Unlife... </b>Disciplines are, again, two in Dominate and one in Presence. I feel like maybe that is backwards, although I also think this is a character where Fortitude wouldn't be at all out of place. </div> </div> </div>The problem may have been Presence 2 itself. It is not a comfortable fit in the Presence powers.<br /> <br /> Several times in V20&amp;pre games I ran into characters for whom Presence 1, 3, 4, and 5 made total sense. However, Presence 2 involves baring your fangs and terrifying people so badly they can't do anything but cower. This is an entirely different feel from the other levels. It can be difficult to justify certain characters spending time learning the power except as a means to reach Presence 3. The Lover of Unlife may have just not gotten over the hump of eschewing her own image of herself as eternally graceful and adored long enough to learn it. This plants the seed for some early character growth.<br /> <br /> Conversely, Presence 2 is a useful power, and I have had characters for whom it made sense, but it was hard to justify Presence 1 on the way there. In one particular case, there was a player whose pc was a Nosferatu terrified of human contact. He would only interact with people through fear. He used Obfuscate <i>almost all the time</i>, and fed by terrifying humans into inaction. (In V5, twenty years later, this would be called an Alleycat.) He wanted a fear based-power, and I homebrewed Presence 1 into making him seem too scary to say &quot;no&quot; to. Basically, when he turned it on the room became an instant hostage situation.<br /> <br /> In another chronicle, I allowed Setites to incapacitate their enemies with Presence 2, described as overwhelming waves of pleasure and ecstacy. Same mechanical effect of taking targets out of the action, but a different flavor.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11558-nosimplehiway","name":"Nosimplehiway","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11558_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1699"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-27T09:18:16-07:00","id":1391963,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1391595#post1391595\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Clanbook: Ventrue (in prima parte)</b><br /> (And if you've been keeping score, then this means that the murderous mobster has more Humanity than the Brujah Skinhead. For reasons, I guess.)</div> </div> </div> </div>I believe it's because mobster is a ruthless killer but not a murderer. He kills only if he has to (too much killing would be bad for business). Also, as a capo he don't have to kill personally. He just orders it. I think it's easier to deal with the consequences than aktualny seeing a corpse, smeared brain on the wall or crying victim's family. <br /> On the other hand skinhead kills (if he really kills and not just beat it maim) personally. And he does so for ideology (which is, IMHO, a harder to explain and accept than business). <br /> That's why I think mobster has higher humanity. I think that he even could have a point or two lower &quot;some time ago&quot;, when he was just an enforcer (before spending freebies \ud83d\ude09).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-27T09:23:47-07:00","id":1391965,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1391599#post1391599\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Albrecht was always one of those characters I thought should've been reserved as a PC, so that your character could be the one to play Aragorn and do the Return of the King bit with the Silver Fangs (for whichever house you preferred) and be the beacon of hope right before the Apocalypse. <br /> <br /> I'm hoping to eventually get around to doing one of these for Werewolf. Once, as an intellectual exercise, I tried to come up with five famous fictional nobles who would work really well as Silver Fang auspice template names. I remember Mordred (the bastard Metis who is probably destined to overthrow his parent's rule of whatever sept they're from), Tarzan (the Ice Fang lupus &amp;quot;noble savage&amp;quot; archetype), and Hamlet (the Philodox who tends to over-intellectualize to the point of paralyzing himself), but I forget who the other two were.</div> </div> </div> </div>D'Artagnan (young and ambitious Ragabash as quick with his words as with his blade) and Achilles (Ahroun too proud to see that his actions could lead to his sept downfall)?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-27T09:35:19-07:00","id":1391970,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nosimplehiway</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1391920#post1391920\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> For her, a 3 Appearance makes sense.<br /> <br /> Appearance is about first impressions and forming superficial, momentary relationships. It doesn't need to represent classic, sex-oriented beauty. It can as easily represent a firm handshake, good eye contact, and an aura of competence. The Appearance is the velvet glove, as people who first meet her see her as highly polished, personable, and polite. Perfect for job interviews and giving presentations in business.<br /> <br /> Charisma 2 is what happens after people get to know her a little bit. She presents well on first meeting, yes, but she's not the sort of person you would follow into a hail of gunfire or write sonnets about. In her push to prepare, prepare, prepare for success, she may have let normal adolescent socialization slide a bit. Some people (her administrative assistant, her social acquaintances, her coterie) who have time to get to know her see the iron fist side of her personality more clearly.</div> </div> </div> </div>As with Dietrich description in Aliens book (I don't remember the quite exactly but it goes like this):<br /> <br /> &quot;Dietrich was considered the most beautiful in the platoon. She destroyed that reputation everytime she opened her mouth&quot;<br /> <br /> \ud83d\ude09","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-27T18:29:27-07:00","id":1392088,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Ventrue (in ultima parte)</b><br /> <br /> And finishing up. <br /> <br /> <b>The Operative</b><br /> This one is a little odd. In one sense, he's a little like the previous military character, just involving spooks and the intelligence community instead of military as a focus for a more physical action oriented Ventrue. This isn't really a bad idea. He worked himself up through the ranks of government surveillance and usually illegal work through a mix of competence and lack of morals, with the hope of eventually becoming one of the upper echelons of the Deep State, the secret masters who control the government regardless of trivial things like elections or laws. And he got his wish, only to find out that the ranks he was joining were the Ventrue and the Camarilla. Now he continues on as before, only with the minor problem that being a Ventrue means that a lot of other vampires automatically suspect him of being up to something. The character is Physical primary, and for some reason has his Dexterity maxed out to five, leaving him three Strength and two Stamina. This doesn't seem bad, just maybe a little odd or imbalanced. Likewise, all of his Social dots are dumped into Manipulation, leaving him with ones in Charisma and Appearance, which against feels odd and out of wack. Mental is more normal with a 3/2/3 split. He's got three dots each in Investigation and Subterfuge, and twos in Alertness, Brawl, Intimidation, Firearms, Stealth, Linguistics, and Politics. I find it rather odd that's he's only got a single dot in Security, and none at all in Computers. You'd think those would be vaguely important for someone who has specifically mentioned as having done electronic surveillance and black bag work. Disciplines are three in Dominate and one in Fortitude. I think this is another case where those seven points would be better served to other ends. I'd probably have gone with 2/1 or 1/2, leaving &quot;brainwashing mastermind&quot; to be something to work up to. He's got three dots in Contacts, two each in Influence and Resources, and one in Generation. This means he has more Influence than any other character in the book (anyone else with it only has the background at one dot), which feels wrong to me. Humanity is six and Willpower is seven. Overall, I like this concept and description, but I feel the stats need to be rethought and reworked a bit. <br /> <br /> <b>The Union Boss</b><br /> I suspect that this is, in someways, meant to be an adaption of Jimmy Hoffa. He's one of those old school trade union leaders from around the 60s or 70s, probably from a working class background (though it doesn't specify) who ended up making deals with less reputable types and eventually learned that some of them weren't human. He was going to testify to the government and the FBI-SAD only to end up embraced because he was too useful to kill but too dangerous to let be. However, his sire has recently decided that unions aren't such a useful power base anymore, and so has cut him loose. I find this a rather interesting origin for a Ventrue. Also interesting is that I could easily see this character interacting or even allying with certain types of Brujah or Anarchs. He's Social primary, with four each in Charisma and Manipulation. Abilities are mostly twos, including Brawl, Dodge, Empathy, Intimidation, Leadership, Melee, Bureaucracy, Finance, and Investigation, rounded out with a few ones. This results in a very well rounded character who'll be able to function in several different scenarios and chronicles. He's got one dot in each Discipline plus one extra in Dominate. This is probably another one where those seven points could've been better spent elsewhere. Backgrounds are spread evenly between Contacts, Fame, Resources, and Generation. I think he really should have a couple of dots in Influence. Humanity is eight and Willpower seven. Overall, I like this character (and again one of those I like better than I remember liking it 25 years ago), though I think he could benefit from a few point adjustments. <br /> <br /> <b>The Young Turk</b><br /> I think this character is maybe supposed to be a woman, but I honestly couldn't tell. The image is extremely androgynous and the write up is gender neutral. Something of a prodigy, they managed to master the fields of politics, business, and academia, but only in pursuit of the greater goal of the power that comes from behind the scenes of those arenas. At this point, they ended up hindering the manipulations of an elder. This probably would've spelled certain doom had not an ambitious ancilla decided to embrace them before that happened. Now they continue on as before, being highly aggressive and ambitious in the pursuit of power and influence, but being careful not to make unnecessary enemies on the way up (after all, you never know when various neonates of other clans might be useful in the future). In some ways, this is almost a similar idea to the first character, the Anarch Wannabe, just with the self confidence dialed up to 11 and no lingering parental independence issues. The image also makes an impression, but I'm not sure what kind. It really and truly looks like someone reinterpreted the leather-daddy biker from the Village People as a super-androgyne rather than a bear. Not that I find it bad. Hell, I wish I'd seen someone cosplay is at a convention. The character would certainly fit in at the Succubus Club. Anyway, they are Mental primary, with a 3/4/3 split, and Social secondary, with 3/2/3. I feel Manipulation might be more useful to this character than Appearance, but I'm kind of on the fence about it. They have three dots each in Computer, Finance, and Investigation (making them the most tech savvy character in the book), and twos in Bureaucracy, Etiquette, Drive, Firearms, and Subterfuge. This is rounded out with a number of ones, mostly in Talents. Disciplines are one dot each. Backgrounds are three in Resources, two each in Allies and Contacts, and one in Mentor. Humanity and Willpower are both seven. I think this is a pretty good character, if maybe a little vague in focus. Weirdly, I kind of want to try taking this one and the Anarch Wannabe and combining them into a single character that has the best of both. But, otherwise, this is a fairly solid entry. <br /> <br /> So, that's our Ventrue. Overall, I think this was a pretty good showing. My personal favorites ended up being the Lover of Unlife, the Anarch Wannabe, and, strangely, the Union Boss. But really, all of them are pretty solid conceptually. I think the recurring issue is certain places where traits are slightly out of wack, and where freebies spent on an extra Discipline dot could've gone to much better use with Abilities or Backgrounds. But those are generally easy fixes. <br /> Weirdly, I really can't recall any ideas I had for Ventrue character templates 25 years ago. In hindsight, I am a little surprised at a lack of any sort of Old World Nobleman type. And maybe a vampire/Noddist historian. <br /> In terms of representation, we have only two women - the Corporate Executive and Lover of Unlife - and one undetermined. One, the Councilor, is black. Everyone else appears to be white.<br /> I think the Ventrue's overall theme was best summed up by the revised Clanbook with the ancient Roman concept of Dignitas. The most literal translation is &quot;worth&quot;, and it's the origin of the modern term dignity, but it encompasses the idea of rank, position, and authority as well as worthiness and honor. It perfectly sums up the clan's attitude about themselves and their role in Kindred society. Members of the clan are expected to uphold that ideal, and I think all of these characters do a pretty good job of that. <br /> <br /> So that is the last of the original seven Camarilla clans. Next, I'll be taking a brief interlude to look at the two Caitiff templates from <i>World of Darkness: Outcasts</i>. After that, I'm debating between actually looking at the premade sheets in the 2nd ed Players Guide and the templates in Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand. (Though I'll be perfectly honest, I don't really like either set.) Then, depending on logistics, either the first of the final six clan books or (if I actually have it) Alien Hunger.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-28T20:14:03-07:00","id":1392402,"title":"","content":"<b>World of Darkness: Outcasts (Caitiff)</b><br /> <br /> Released in 1995, Outcasts was a book providing information on Vampire's clanless Caitiff, Werewolf's tribeless Ronin, and Mage's ... Hollow Ones. (Yes, instead of the completely factionless Orphans, for some reason. Also, it didn't cover anything about Wraith's mishmash Beggers' Legion, and Changeling really didn't have anything comparable.) Basically, it was about characters that didn't really fit in to their game's socio-political structure (at least, as far as the first two groups went) and how they may, sometimes, interact with each other. And it gave two premade character templates for each group. <br /> The entire book was written by James Moore. He was a fairly prolific writer for the WoD back then. I think he may have created the Samedi, but I'm not sure. He certainly liked using them a lot in his tie-in fiction. I can't really tell who did the artwork for the two Caitiff characters. A different artist was used for each game line's pair, and these two don't have a signature or initials the way the Werewolf or Mage ones do. And, quite honestly, the Caitiff images are some of the worst in the entire book. By and large, there's a lot of good artwork in this book, including a lot of the actual chapter about Caitiff. However, a few of the images in that chapter are sub-par to kind of bad, and these two character images in the back of the book are as bad as the worst of those. This is unfortunate, especially given how the two werewolves have this nice light greyscale pencil art kind of look, and the mages are these fairly striking and stylized heavy ink images that were very common in that game's 1st edition, making them feel very much a part of that game. In contrast, the two vampires are these sort of muddy and crude things making too much use of black ink and thick details lines. They just look ugly. I rather wish Joshua Timbrook had done these, but I don't think he was working with WWGS anymore at this time (for reasons I'll probably get into when I get to the Setite book). <br /> <br /> <b>The Questioner</b><br /> This character, drawing on certain period fiction, always &quot;wanted to believe&quot;, growing up with a strong interest in New Age mysticism, parapsychology, UFO lore, and the like. Rather than become an FBI agent, she became a journalist, using her job as an excuse to follow her true passion of running down clues about the existence of the supernatural. I somehow suspect she ended up doing a lot of work for tabloids. And true to form, one of her leads ended up embracing her. And then just leaving her on her own. Having little or no clue about Kindred society, she continues on as before, hoping to meet others like herself and learn as much as she can about this newly unveiled shadow world. Overall, I think this is a pretty good idea for a character, especially a Caitiff who is probably going to be interacting more with other Outcasts and various Twilight People (psychics, sorcerers, etc.) than vampires. She's Mental primary, with four dots each in Perception and Intelligence. Weirdly, she's Physical primary, with three dots in Strength and Dexterity. This leaves all her Social traits at two, which feels strange for a character based around the idea of going out and trying to interact with and even interview other supernatural creatures or witnesses. She's got three dots each in Investigation, Law, Occult, Firearms, and Security. Investigation and Occult I can understand. Maybe even Security. I'm not sure why she has Law that high and especially unclear on why Firearms is that big of a priority. She doesn't even have a gun listed among her equipment. She's also got two dots each in Politics, Intimidation, Streetwise, and Subterfuge. I have the same question about Politics as I do Law, and her write up and roleplaying hints don't really convey the idea of someone who tries to intimidate people. (The first thing it says is &quot;be friendly.&quot;) This feels like a character that should have some ability in Empathy and Computer, two traits she has no dots in. Likewise, she's only got a single dot each in Stealth and Science, which seem more fitting with the back story. Disciplines are one each in Potence, Thaumaturgy, and Presence. Potence I certainly get, as it's almost instinctive on some levels, though I wonder if Fortitude might work better. Thaumaturgy is also certainly in keeping with the character's background. Presence feel a little random. I'd think Auspex or Obfuscate would be more useful to a reporter. Backgrounds are four dots in Contacts and one in Retainer. Humanity and Willpower are both eight. As I said, I really like this character's concept and initial write up. I think the character sheet is kind of a disjointed mess though. I have this suspicion, based on what I remember of the fiction James Moore wrote back in the day, that he had a much more high-combat/heavy gunfire image of the WoD than some of the games, especially Vampire, tended to actually be going for. Not that there's anything wrong with that overall, but if a reporter needs to have the same level of Firearms as a typical soldier or SWAT officer, it feels off. So, I think if the character sheet was redone and the points shuffled around, this would be a really good character for a street level Vampire or mixed group chronicle. <br /> <br /> <b>The Angry Victim </b><br /> I'm not sure how to feel about this character. Basically, he's been picked on, bullied and made to feel like crap his entire life, and is convinced that all his failings in life are everyone else's fault. And now he's been turned into a vampire, is very high on his newly discovered sense of power, and out to get revenge on anyone who's ever done him wrong, including the kid that gave him a bloody nose in the fifth grade. While I can somewhat empathize with the character - God only knows what crazy ideas I'd have had if becoming a vampire or werewolf at the age of 19 or 20 - I can't help but feel that this character is here more to be laughed at than to be taken seriously or sympathized with. His attributes are ... exactly the same as the previous character. This must be some sort of editing error, as I'm not sure I see this guy as being that perceptive. Almost all of this abilities are twos, save a four in Stealth. I don't think that quite fits either. He doesn't sound like he's going for the lurking stalker archetype, especially as he doesn't have Obfuscate as a Discipline. Those twos include Acting (?), Alertness, Brawl, Dodge, Streetwise, Etiquette (?), Firearms, and Bureaucracy. I find it weird that he doesn't have two dots in Melee as well, given his revenge fixation. He actually doesn't have all of his ability points. He's got thirteen Talent dots, nine Skill dots but one of them is that forth dot in Stealth breaking the rules, and only four dots in Knowledge. And he's only spent eight of his freebie points. Disciplines are one in Auspex and two in Potence, which seem fitting. Backgrounds include three dots each in Generation and Contacts (again, no real context for this in the write up; he feels like a loner and even has that as his Nature), and two in &quot;Wealth&quot;. Humanity is seven and Willpower is eight. Damn. I feel like there's an idea of a potentially interesting character here, but it needs to be developed a little more in the writing. This is the sort of background that could be turned into an interesting coterie mate or associate of the Nosferatu Equalizer. Unfortunately, it falls a little flat, and the character sheet is a total mess. I'm disappointed on several levels.<br /> <br /> So, those were, besides the Sabbat's Pander Anarchist, our only examples of Caitiff PCs for a long long time. One is pretty good, the other not so much. One is female, one is male. (Curiously, this is the only female of the six in the book; both of the Werewolf and Mage ones are male.) The art makes it kind of hard to judge ethnicity. The Questioner could be interpreted as being Latina or possibly black or mixed race if you wanted. <br /> One of the characters I remember really enjoying in the original Hunters Hunted was the wealthy old man turned Caitiff recluse who now financed human vampire hunters out of a sense of revenge. I rather wish he'd been mentioned again among the &quot;famous&quot; Caitiff. But I really would've enjoyed seeing a Caitiff with a bunch of Resources, Influence and the like. Also, given how much the chapter spent on the idea of Caitiff as originators of their own new and unique Disciplines, I'm surprised we don't have one of those. The Gangrel Clanbook spent some time discussing vampires who, effectively being quasi-caitiff from a social standpoint and their lack of knowledge about Kindred society and truths, believe all the stuff from legends and movies (including weaknesses to garlic and crosses, dressing weird, and so forth). I rather wish that'd been touched on here as well, as a stereotypical Count Dracula or Elvira character would've been fun. <br /> The Caitiff don't really have much of a theme. But if one were to try to find one, I think I might make a case of it being the idea of failure. They represent a failure of their sire, of the power of Vitae, of Kindred society, and perhaps even of the world itself as it spins toward Gehenna and the Final Nights. Neither character really plays in to that so much, but I wouldn't really expect them to. <br /> <br /> Next will be <i>Clanbook: Assamite</i>. After that, I'm probably going to have to go look at Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand, as certain things about it are probably going to tie in to explaining certain things about Setite and why the final four clanbooks are so different from the previous ones in a number of ways.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-28T20:21:27-07:00","id":1392404,"title":"","content":"I actually feel like the Black Hand Templates were extremely well done. They were among the very few Elder Templates done and I remember them being surprisingly well-written.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-29T19:29:39-07:00","id":1392725,"title":"","content":"<b>Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand (part one)</b><br /> <br /> I'm not a big fan of this book, for a number of reasons. It was initially advertised and solicited as being about the Black Hand, the Sabbat's elite warrior society, only to be a bait and switch that instead was about a secret conspiracy/Gehenna cult of elders presented as ultimate puppet masters behind both the Sabbat and Camarilla and safeguarding humanity from alien parasites. (Something similar happened around the same time with the Legend of the 5 Rings RPG, when a book solicited as being about merchants and trade ended up being about a secret organization of organized crime ninja or something. Maybe this was the trend in game design that year.) <br /> Also, at some point around this period, something happened internally at WWGS that ultimately led to Vampire Developer Andrew Greenberg and Werewolf Developer Bill Bridges leaving the company. Apparently Steven Brown, the writer of this book, as well as both Sabbat books (the PG and the STH) also stopped working for WWGS, and it would appear that artist Joshua Timbrook did as well. Exactly what caused this is anyone's guess. <br /> Anyway, this book was written by Steven Brown. The character template art is by Lief Jones. I don't dislike Jones's work. I think he makes good use of lines and detail. However, everything he does has this somewhat distorted look to it (like a fisheye lens effect) that I tend to find a little off-putting. All of the templates in this book seem to have overly large heads. The characters in this book are all made using the elder creation rules from the earlier book Elysium. I'm honestly a bit surprised that Elysium didn't have any templates in it. I would've appreciated seeing some who weren't part of this cult, especially ones like Tremere or regular Brujah who are barred from this super special secret club house. <br /> <br /> <b>The Antiquarian (Gangrel)</b><br /> I have to give credit to this for being atypical for the clan, even if in some ways it is an extension of the Explorer from their clanbook. But if that one is Indiana Jones, this is more his rival Belloq, an Old World type in a nice casual suit. Unfortunately, this character is from the The Rom, which is a bit of a strike against it. But at least it only plays on some of the early material's complete lack of understanding of how vampires would be viewed by Roma culture rather than a bunch of the really awful stuff from WoD: Gypsies. Anyway, the character developed a passion for history, especially Kindred history and the relics relating to it. Eventually someone decided he would fit in with the cult and recruited him. Apparently, his &quot;love for nature&quot; makes him good at surviving expeditions, because &quot;Gangrel = nature&quot; or something. He seems fairly normal for an elder vampire, perhaps too normal. He's Mental primary, with both Intelligence and Stamina at five, and Perception and Wits at four. The rest are threes, save Appearance, which is two. Investigation is maxed out at five dots. The rest of his abilities only go up to three at the highest, including Linguistics, Occult, Politics, and ... Computer? This guy feels like he'd be too old school to have actually noticed computers by 1994. I can picture him having a desk that still includes an adding machine and ink blotter. He only has a single dot in Survival, which seems out of step with he character description. Likewise no dots in anything such as History, Archaeology, Kindred Lore or the like to reflect his interests. Disciplines are mostly three dots, including the clan ones and Auspex. Plus a single dot in Obfuscate. Backgrounds include Allies, Contacts, Resources and Retainers at five each, plus three in Herd, two in Age, and one in Generation. Humanity is five and Willpower is eight. Overall, I generally like this one. He fits the Gangrel fairly well (and I think in some ways he may be part of the inspiration for Beckett), and his interests seem like something an elder would occupy his existence with. I think some of the traits need to be adjusted, but other than that, it's pretty good. <br /> <br /> <b>The Corporate Conspirator (Lasombra antitribu)</b><br /> Basically your standard stereotypical elder. From a poor immigrant community who became a servant and later Childe of a vampire madam. He was a member of the Camarilla and Sabbat at the same time, playing them against each other for his own interests until he was caught by a member of the cult and brought into it. (Good thing he wasn't from one of those clans they don't like.) Now he just continues as before, pursuing his own interests while convincing most of the cult that it's for their overall benefit. So, your typical cookie cutter elder. (Oddly, he doesn't seem to be more than 100 to 150 years old, making him more an ancilla than an elder.) He's Social primary, with a 4/5/4 spread, and a 3/3/4 for Mental. Like our last character, he's got one skill maxed out to five, in this case Finance, and the rest three or less, including threes in Acting, Alertness, Etiquette, Bureaucracy, Investigation, and Politics. I'm a little surprised that his Law is only one. Disciplines are just his clan ones at threes and four. Backgrounds include Contacts and Resources at five, Influence at four, Allies and Retainers at three, Generation at two and Age at one. Humanity is five and Willpower eight. This is overall an OK character. He's just kind of generic and nothing really stands out about him. <br /> <br /> That's all for this one. Next will more Black Hand elders. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-30T02:09:43-07:00","id":1392774,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1392725#post1392725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, at some point around this period, something happened internally at WWGS that ultimately led to Vampire Developer Andrew Greenberg and Werewolf Developer Bill Bridges leaving the company. Apparently Steven Brown, the writer of this book, as well as both Sabbat books (the PG and the STH) also stopped working for WWGS, and it would appear that artist Joshua Timbrook did as well. Exactly what caused this is anyone's guess.</div> </div> </div> </div>I never knew this, but it is not surprising. It doesn't take much for small companies to have artistic differences or business interest clashes to blow up and have influential founders leave. I know Mark Rein Hagen himself left at some point. Eventually all the intrigues at TSR were revealed so I'm sure this too will be at some point.<br /> <br /> You can certainly how their departure lead to new Developers who had their own distinct case on the WoD.<br /> <br /> One reason I've disliked metaplot is that it implies a continuity in WoD that isn't really there. We've had at least four different World of Darknesses.<br /> <br /> First edition/early Second Edition<br /> Late Second Edition<br /> Revised Metaplot<br /> V5 era retconned Metaplot<br /> <br /> Each one had its different influences and style of play. I could probably come up with five core concepts of each version and explain how it different from the others.<br /> <br /> I like some of the sensibilities of all eras. But they were all built on different assumptions of play, and the Developers never bothered to preserve any continuity. They routinely dumped anything they didn't like from the previous regime, some often being outright insulting to certain predecessor ideas.<br /> <br /> I think for this reason any metaplot conceived lines will ultimately lead to diminishing returns. Rather than telling me what has happened to my game, it should be giving me toolkits to run the kind of game I want.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-30T19:56:01-07:00","id":1392986,"title":"","content":"<b>Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand (part two)</b><br /> <br /> Continuing our collection of elders.<br /> <br /> <b>The Coup Leader (Nosferatu <i>antitribue</i>)</b><br /> As odd as some aspects of Jones's artwork is, I will give credit here in that we have a Nosferatu who, although his face is heavy shadowed and hard to make out, isn't as distorted as most of those in the clanbook. If he's ugly, I get the sense that it's a restrained kind of ugly. I also like his suit. (I guess I'm just a sucker for a well dressed Nosferatu.) Anyway, our character was a simple English cobbler until turned into cannon fodder for the Sabbat. He survived, but after another attack where he was used as cannon fodder, he decided he'd had enough and left the Sabbat for the New World. He didn't really care much for what he saw of the Camarilla or Anarchs either, and ended up spending most of his time just derailing everyone else's grand plans and plots. Eventually he came to the attention of the cult and was recruited. Only now he wants to turn his efforts against the Black Hand as well. Apparently he really loves mortals and wants to defend the innocent from wicked vampires. While I don't object to this too much, it does kind of reflect one of my issues with the entire book overall, about how the Black Hand frequently seem to be set up as the WoD's secret heroes in a game that's meant to have protagonists and antagonists rather than heroes and villains. And as a general rule, I kind of expect my elder vampires to have grown apart from mortals on some level. (I believe this is part of the reasoning for why elder characters default to only five in Humanity.) But if you spend the points on extra humanity (five freebies per dot, IIRC), then it would fit. But there is a small problem here. This is the only character in this book who doesn't have Humanity. Instead, he's on a Path of Enlightenment, and in this case a new one from this book, The Path of the Scorched Heart. This path is designed to repress and do away with one's emotions, reflecting the True Brujah who created it. And while it has this whole thing about differentiating between good and evil, only feeding on &quot;evil&quot; mortals, and working for the greater good, it doesn't seem to match all that well with the character description. And, for a character whose concept is &quot;coup leader&quot;, he really doesn't seem to lead any coups. As far as stats go, he's Social primary, with five dots in Manipulation and four dots in Charisma and ... Appearance. Did I mention that this is a Nosferatu? Strength and Dexterity are also four, while Stamina, Perception and Wits are at three. Abilities include four dots each in Acting, Intimidation, Firearms, Melee, and Politics. He's also got threes in Brawl, Dodge, Alertness, Leadership, and Subterfuge. Disciplines see Obfuscate maxed out at five dots, with three in Animalism, two in Potence, and one each in Fortitude and Presence. Backgrounds include threes in Age, Military Force, and Retainers, and twos in Allies, Contacts, and Generation. His Path rating is five, and Willpower is nine. Generally speaking, this isn't a bad idea for a character. I kind of like the idea of an elder who has just developed this dislike for all the factions and actively works against all of them. But he seems to lack a certain ruthlessness or edge that I expect from my elder vampires. And the character sheet does raise a few questions. I think I'm going to have to consider this a really interesting idea that just misfired. <br /> <br /> <b>The Dotting Mentor (Nagaraja)</b><br /> So, this character was a poor Cajun who discovered his true talent for violence and killing after being drafted. (I guess he was drafted by the Confederacy during the Civil War, unless maybe it was WW1?) Apparently she just liked to kill and destroy things, and he turned on her when she killed a school bus full of high school students. A cult member happened along at just that time and helped destroy her, and then recruited him into the cult. Once again, this kind of sets off my &quot;we're the good guys!&quot; sense. Apparently, now he's like a super supportive and somewhat over protective godfather (or maybe helicopter parent) type for those he mentors and trains. I actually find that to be a kind of nifty idea for an elder character, and potentially fun to play. However, I'm not sure it - or this character in general - really represents the Nagaraja. They're described as preferring scholarly or mystical types who study death rather than people who cause it. I also kind of had the impression that they didn't really exist outside of the cult, so that one going rogue was a super rare occurrence among an already super rare bloodline. I kind of feel like this concept would work better with a Ventrue, Toreador, or Malkavian. Anyway, he's Physical primary, with five in Strength and fours in Dexterity and Stamina, as well as Perception and Wits. Abilities include four in Firearms, and threes in Brawl, Dodge, Etiquette, Melee, Security, Stealth and Thanatology. So, basically, this is a KillNinja with good manners. (He has two dots in Music, which makes me imagine he's a Zydeco KillNinja.) Disciplines include four dots each in Special Sauce #1 and Special Sauce #2, ie Necromancy and Nihilistics. Also two in Auspex and one each in Potence and Celerity. Backgrounds include four in Resources, three in Generation, two in Contacts, and one each in Age, Allies, and Retainer. He actually hasn't spent all of his Background points (or the bulk of his freebies either). Humanity and Willpower are both six. I have mixed feelings about this character. Like I said, I really enjoy the basic concept, although I feel it might work better with a different clan. On the other hand, from a pure trait perspective, this is a KillNinja, which also doesn't seem to fit what the Nagaraja are presented as being. If you were doing a Nagaraja clanbook that included multiple character templates and wanted to do one that showed them as warrior rather than scholar, I'd be all for it, but as the only one in this book that introduces them, I'm not sure that's the best idea. I fear this one is, probably not a misfire, but something that just missed the target. Which is a shame, as I think Helicopter Sire is a really fun idea for an elder. <br /> <br /> <b>The Former Chatterling (Toreador)</b><br /> So, Chatterlings are basically children who are kidnapped and raised by the cult, indoctrinated with their ideology and trained to be servants and ghouls, with the eye toward potential embrace at some point. Effectively &quot;more Black Hand than Black Hand&quot;, you'd expect. The book describes them as the cult's most insane and ruthless members. This character is ... none of those things. Kidnapped and raised by the cult, trained and eventually embraced, yes. Then he was presented to his city's Prince as a child of one of the Primogen (also a cult member), and acts like a normal Camarilla vampire and upholds the status quo. Although for some weird reason - never made clear - is that he pretends to be a Ventrue. And that's about it, really, as far as his duties to the cult go. Meanwhile, he sees all these other &quot;normal&quot; vampires and has very vaguely defined second thoughts about his upbringing and loyalty. Exactly how and why isn't mentioned. He's Physical primary, with a 4/5/4 spread, and four dots in Charisma. Manipulation and Appearance are three, as are Intelligence and Wits. He has four dots in Music, I guess because he's a Toreador. He has threes in Acting, Melee, Security, Stealth, and Occult. This is rounded out with twos in Alertness, Brawl, Dodge, Etiquette, Firearms, Repair, and Investigation, and ones in Empathy, Intimidation, Subterfuge, Drive, Survival, Linguistics, Medicine, and Science. These are not exactly the skills I'd pick for a deep cover operative. If anything, it feels like some of them were picked by throwing darts at a board. This is a character who really needs things like Subterfuge and Politics. Disciplines are Auspex at four, Celerity and Presence at three, and Fortitude at one. Backgrounds include four dots in Mentor, two in Generation, and one each in Allies, Contacts, Retainer, and Secret Identity. Again, he hasn't spent all his points. Humanity is five and Willpower seven. Given how they are raised, you would expect the entire point of Chatterlings is to totally indoctrinate them into the inhuman mindset of one of the Paths of Enlightenment, but apparently not here. Overall, this character just kind of feels like it was phoned in. The concept and write up seems to have an idea - or possibly two different ones; the Chatterling now questioning his upbringing and the vampire who masquerades as other vampires - but doesn't really take the time or effort to develop it. That character sheet feels disorganized and perhaps incomplete. I does not appear to have spent more than a third of the 50 freebie points it should have. (And looking back, I see that this appears to be the case for all of the characters, unless there's something in Elysium's elder rules I missed.) I am forced to judge this as a failure. <br /> <br /> And that's all for this entry. Next will be three more of our elder cultists, followed by the final two and final thoughts about them. Then a return to clanbooks.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-30T20:07:59-07:00","id":1392988,"title":"","content":"I wondered if the Nosferatu Antitribu was when Vicissitude could still &quot;fix&quot; the faces of Nosferatu. Mind you, that sort of thing would be doubly frowned on in the Black Hand.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-30T20:29:26-07:00","id":1392992,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I had the almost identical thought process. &quot;Is this a Vicissitude thing? Oh, wait, it can't be. Souleaters.&quot;<br /> <br /> I honestly wonder what you're supposed to do as an elder Nosferatu doing Social primary. You spent four on Charisma, four on Manipulation, and then ... two points wasted.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hope you're 7th generation or below. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 05-30-2020, 11:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-30T21:42:26-07:00","id":1392998,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1392725#post1392725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not a big fan of this book, for a number of reasons.</div> </div> </div> </div>The book was always the one pointed out to be a great cheese factor. And it has some weird ideas. (Some of which makes sense to me now, but didn't at the time. The Vicissitude as Umbral Disease seemed out of left field, but so did the Discipline when I first read it. It was probably another ten years before I read a synopsis of the Necroscope books online and suddenly realized that was the origin for both). I didn't like it either when I finally read it (which wasn't until 1998 or so).<br /> <br /> However, I actually like the book now in many ways. First, it does has some absolutely great ideas in it that I can use in my games. Second, it is the pinnacle of the game in terms of showing that the Jyhad has many, many levels. Third, it is a showcase telling you - your game should have mysteries in it, lots of it! And it didn't seem forced (even if some things were strange). I didn't think it was hamfisted like certain developments in Revised because the game was only showing me what was potentially out there as opposed to &quot;Sorry, now the Gangrel have left the Camarilla.&quot;<br /> <br /> The Greenberg era Vampire was excellent in showcasing how damn complex everything was. You go from a simple Anarch vs Camarilla perspective. Then you realize the Camarilla is divided between Prince and the Primogen. And then that the Princes actually defy the actual Camarilla organization in terms of not wanting Justicars and Archons to usurp their authority. Then you get the Sabbat vs Camarilla conflict. But you have Gehenna Cults with members in both organizations. And elder vampires having ties, pawns, and old friends in all these sects. And now we have the True Black Hand. You understand why at some point you become a Methusaleh and realize you can't keep track of everything so the smart play is to go into the Sleep of Ages and wait things out for clarity.<br /> <br /> As an <b><i>example</i></b> of how to do the Jyhad, it is great. Top notch. The problem is once the book is published, you've just ruined the mystery of the game. Instead of the PCs <i>learning</i> during the game that the Brujah are just a bloodline that eradicated the &quot;True&quot; Brujah; you just have players saying, &quot;Oh, I want to play True Brujah.&quot;<br /> <br /> Once I saw it less as &quot;canon&quot; but more as a tool teaching me what I could be doing as an ST, I became really enamored of it - even the totally gonzo things I once hated.<br /> <br /> The era that came afterwards eliminated much of the crazy gonzo-ness, but at the price of reducing things to a very simplistic Camarilla vs Sabbat plotline that culminated in the cities on the East Coast being besieged by monster Vozhd. Because, you know... that's so much less silly than the Chatterlings and Temporis.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-31T20:23:59-07:00","id":1393318,"title":"","content":"I think in hindsight, I rather wish that second edition Vampire had made a somewhat stronger effort to differentiate between your more localized/street-level/personal chronicles about neonates and younger ancilla and what Kindred existence is about for them and what they focus on vs more grand scale/political/strategic war of ages type stuff involving older ancilla and elder characters who no longer really interact with the mortal world in anything but a kind of abstract way. I think 2nd ed kind of got away from anything to do with starting level PCs in favor of elders. Revised slightly tried to correct this in some ways with books like The Gilded Cage and Dead Man's Party, but was overall too heavily bogged down in the elder-heavy meta plot. <br /> <br /> I think the game line as a whole really could've benefited from having early (like 93 or 94) releases that focused on how a Prince's court and all the city offices like Sheriff and whatnot worked and especially how core-book level PCs might interact with it and even became part of it, and a similar one for Elysiums that focused more on neonates and ancilla, saving the rules and methods of elders for their own stand alone book. Likewise some sort of book about just what goes into picking, developing and protecting a Haven (probably half havens and the other half extending that mindset to the larger scale of PCs establishing and maintaining their own domains), one about exactly what constitutes a city's Rack and how they work in a setting/chronicle and what PCs do there, a book about all the ins and outs of the sub-genre of vampires being famous celebrities and just how to handle the Fame background in terms of the Masquerade, a book about how vampires interact with human religions and some of their own cults, a book about money and just how vampires make it and hide it, and one about what the hell vampires do for regular fun and entertainment. ( I dub these last two Blood Money and Blood Sports.)<br /> Then for elders, a book about making elder characters, one about upper level political games and plots, a collection of weird elder conspiracies (like the Black Hand, the Inconnu, and, say, the Ordo Dracul or something) that elder PCs can be part of, a book about planning and running chronicles that span decades and centuries, and maybe even a book about Methuselahs were you don't even play them as regular characters but instead like nation states similar to TSR's Birthright setting or PC games like Civilization.<br /> <br /> One thing I think Requiem did really well was the way it divided it's New Orleans book into neonate, ancilla, and elder sections. I think it would've been cool to see something like, say, Chicago by Night do that take, where you see the city from a mortal/neonate perspective, then peel back a layer to see it from the Prince and ancilla level, and then once again for the games of the elders and secret masters.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-31T20:39:26-07:00","id":1393323,"title":"","content":"One advantage that Requiem and any later supplements had is that they had a decade or more of prior experience to build on. But I agree that having more toolkits to help PCs and STs figure things out early on would have been good.<br /> <br /> I think a lot of that information should have been in the Player's Guides or ST's Guides instead of the additional clans, bloodlines, and other add-ons to the games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-31T22:07:12-07:00","id":1393344,"title":"","content":"<b>Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand (part three)</b><br /> <br /> Once more into the breach.<br /> <br /> <b>The Globe Trotting Assassin (Malkavian)</b> <br /> This is another KillNinja. But I will give it credit for being a kind of interesting one. I do like the character image. Weirdly, he reminds me of former Vampire writer and Exalted developer Geoff Grabowski, were he a little thinner and his head about 50% too big for his body. He's a Malkavian, and I have to give a lot of credit here for him not being in anyway wacky or Fishmalky in any way. Instead, he's one of those types who seems relatively normal, at least until you get to know him and realize he's subtly mad in the way of certain fanatics and obsessives. In life, he was a piano prodigy. His sire embraced him for that reason at the age of 17 and then effectively kept him as a slave prisoner performing just for the sire. He absolutely hated his sire, and as methodically as planning out a recital, planned out his destruction. Now he believes that all vampires must be destroyed as they are evil. But the Black Hand found him and convinced him - mostly - that they were the good guys and now use him as a weapon to destroy &quot;the most evil of vampires who were evil.&quot; So now he walks the earth killing &quot;evil&quot; vampires, and apparently practicing the piano as much as he does martial arts. He's Physical primary and Mental secondary, as most KillNinja are, and naturally has the five in Dexterity. Intelligence - the least necessary for KillNinja - is a two so the other two traits can be fours. He's got a four in Firearms and threes in Brawl, Dodge, Melee, Stealth, Security, Linguistics, and Music. All the KillNinja abilities, plus piano. (I kind of wish that four had been in Music rather than Firearms. It feels like it would've fit the character better.) Every other is at one, save Athletics, Alertness, and Investigation at two. I think he has the most Disciplines of any character, with four in Obfuscate, threes in Auspex and Dominate, twos in Celerity and Quietus (of course), and ones in Potence and Fortitude. Backgrounds are just one in Age and three in Generation. (Apparently, these cost four background - and freebie - points per dot, which has been throwing off my counts on all the previous characters.) Humanity is six and Willpower seven. I don't dislike this character. In general, he's not a bad concept, especially for a Malkavian. But it again features the &quot;the Black Hand are the secret heroes and good guys of the WoD&quot; thing that kind of irks me. I kind of wish the music angle had been played up a little more, and perhaps that there had been hints that sometimes his definition of what qualified as &quot;evil&quot; was entirely random and obtuse. <br /> <br /> <b>The Government Leader (Ventrue)</b><br /> I think the name here might be a little misleading. Our man is a long time political operative, being a spymaster back in the days of King James I. In his role, he discovered the existence of vampires and was embraced into the Camarilla. And at some point, he discovered the existence of the Black Hand and was inducted into their ranks. These nights, he's deeply interwoven with the government (his quote makes it sound like he's in the US, but I honestly find him more interesting as some sort of secret master within the British government), using his influence within various government agencies to cover up the existence of Kindred, investigate other supernatural creatures and events, and gather intelligence for the cult (and I presume himself and the Camarilla at the same time). However, he's more than a bit paranoid, and believes that there may be even more secret conspiracies behind or beyond the Black Hand (which is an interesting idea that I wish this book had explored more). The character image is ... a little odd. He appears to be in the middle of wiping dog crap off his shoe or something. I'm also sort of amused that he bears more than a little resemblance to George H. W. Bush (though perhaps by way of Pruneface from Dick Tracy). His Perception, Charisma, and Manipulation are all maxed out at five each, as are Bureaucracy and Investigation. He's also got fours in Acting, Subterfuge, Security and Stealth. Weirdly, Politics is only three, while Leadership, Law and Linguistics are only one each. His Disciplines are all the Ventrue ones, including four dots in Dominate, plus two dots in Auspex. I'm a little surprised he doesn't have any Obfuscate. (Or Thaumaturgy, given it mentions he used to be a member of the Rosicrucians.) Backgrounds include five dots of Contacts, three of Resources and Status, and two of Age, Generation, and Secret Identity. I admit to being confused as to why this character has no Influence at all. If any character would have that at five, you'd think it would be this guy. Humanity is five and Willpower nine. Overall, I think this is not a bad character. I do like the effort put into the back story, which I think helps elevate this from &quot;typical elder&quot; to a more interesting character. I think it would benefit from a better name, and maybe some alterations to a few traits, but I like this one more than I thought I would going in to it. <br /> <br /> <b>The Jyhad Fanatic (Assamite <i>antitribu</i>)</b> <br /> #@%&amp; you, book. Seriously. ::sigh:: I was never really wild about the term &quot;Jyhad&quot;, even back then. It always struck me as a little weird that a stylized spelling of a term from an 8th century Middle Eastern religion would be the word various elders is Europe and the Americas used to refer to the eternal struggle between themselves and various secret masters. Surely there could've been some Latin or Greek word that imparted the same idea. (And then ten years later, that term suddenly takes on a totally new significance in the modern world that makes its use even more problematic.) I'm not really even sure where to start with this one. Should I mention that the write up specifically mentions him being willing to blow up the World Trade Center? This was in poor taste even in 1994, a year after the parking garage bombing, but in hindsight after 9/11, it looks even worse. He's the cookie cutter Assamite of 2nd ed, raised by the assassins just like his dad, and embraced into the Black Hand. (Specifically the Eastern branch of the Black Hand, unlike all of our earlier ones who are Westerners, just to further make this an alien &quot;other&quot;.) At some point, he encountered an ancient Methuselah who made him her tool and a tool of the Antediluvians, and he took this as some sort of religious revelation. Now he's even more devoted to the Antediluvians and super excited for the coming of Gehenna. As far as Stats go, he's a KillNinja. His Physicals are all four dots (and are Primary, meaning he's missing a dot). And like the Malkavian, his Mental is 4/2/4, because KillNinjas don't need Intelligence. Brawl, Dodge, Melee, and Firearms are all four dots, of course. Alertness, Athletics, Stealth, Survival, Security, Investigation, and Occult are all three. There's a random single dot in Music. Maybe the Malkavian gives music lessons to other KillNinja. Celerity and Quietus are at four, Obfuscate at three, and Auspex and Fortitude at two. Backgrounds are Age and Generation at three each, cuz KillNinja don't need no friends, connections, or money. Or, you know, a Secret Identity or anything. Humanity is five and Willpower ten. I have no idea why this character isn't on the Path of Blood or something similar. The character image is of essentially an almost stereotypical violently crazy looking North African/Arabic/South Asian man, with guns. While I can appreciate the need for a character that reflects the cult's weird reverence for the original Clan Founders/Antediluvians, and that weirdly enough the Assamites may be one of the ones best suited for this role, I just actively dislike this character on so many levels. It's like a perfect storm of stereotypes and bad ideas from the time, some of which look even worse in hindsight. I wouldn't go as far as to call this character racist, but I could certainly see where some people might. It's just bad all around. <br /> <br /> That's it for this time. Next will be the final two Black Hand and final thoughts, then we'll be doing Assamite ... <sub>damn it.</sub>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-31T22:31:40-07:00","id":1393347,"title":"","content":"Honestly, I have no problem with the belief that the True Hand thinks it's the good guys. Part of why I like the Web of Knives is the fact that they are passionately convinced they are the good guys and that they're the only vampires that are worth a damn--which they express by attempting to commit genocide on all other Kindred. The True Hand is explicitly a bunch of people who have set the Sabbat and Camarilla against one another with the intent of invoking a &quot;Forever War&quot; (see TV tropes) that consumes the vast majority of vampires between them. It coincidentally leaves no one to challenge their aims and destroys most of their rivals while leaving them in a position to profit.<br /> <br /> I also don't see any problem with &quot;protecting&quot; humanity because a vampire should definitely look after humanity.<br /> <br /> Humans can exist without vampires but vampires cannot exist without humans.<br /> <br /> So of course these guys would be against Global Warming and nuclear war. Humanity must be saved....so they can be eaten.<br /> <br /> The True Hand is at its best when you take the view you're aware they're a bunch of hypocrites and insane cultists. Sadly, the original book presents a lot of utterly batshit evil (we have a child-kidnapping ring that we use to raise children in HELL) but just kind of ignores how weird and fucked up it all is. The Malkavian insane pianist is basically just describing a typical Black Hand member's origins.<br /> <br /> Not to mention the insanity of waging a war over an Antediluvian's death millennia ago (True Brujah) plus the literal Bloodline of cannibal necromancers.<br /> <br /> Our &quot;good guys.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 06-01-2020, 02:00 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-31T22:58:50-07:00","id":1393357,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1393323#post1393323\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think a lot of that information should have been in the Player's Guides or ST's Guides instead of the additional clans, bloodlines, and other add-ons to the games.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think my ideal Players Guide template - with the idea that this would've been released early in the line - would have been:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Mind Body &amp; Soul, an over view of the psychology, physiology, and certain metaphysics of the game's characters. In the case of Vampire, this would be the place to answer all the questions about how vampire bodies work, trying to pass as human, how their emotions work, how the embrace impacts things like creativity, sensations, or emotions, general issues of alienation from mortals, some of the dehumanization vampires undergo and why/how that happens, can Necromancy effect destroyed vampires, etc. Also discussion of Humanity at its various levels, more about the Beast and how it works, things about Frenzy, Willpower as a reflection of personality, and anything else that might fall under the three general headings. All in all, aimed to give players a much better understanding of just what it is they are playing on a physical and mental level.</li> <li>Traits, an overview of Attributes, Abilities and Backgrounds, including what each of them means and represents, ways to interpret them, thinking about what each one means to the character and ties into their story/personality, discussion of specialties and what probably should or shouldn't count as one, and touching on what it means to have certain traits above five (I would probably save full rules for that for a future book about Elders, with this just giving basic guidelines), as well as probably optional character creation rules (doing Attributes and/or Abilities as just pools of 15/27 for example), and discussion of creating new Abilities when necessary (as opposed to a bunch of new ones that are mostly just hyper specialized versions of existing Abilities).</li> <li>Splats, in this case the Clans, giving an expansion on the two page spreads from the core book, probably four pages each, talking a little about their history, internal organization and factions (be it Malkavian mystery cults, Ravnos Jati, Tremere conspiracies, Toreador salons, Brujah political philosophies, etc.), certain Strains within each clan (I use Strain to differentiate between small-b bloodlines like Gangrel mariners vs big-B Bloodlines like Gargoyles or Salubri.), maybe a famous member or two, and whatever else might fit. I'd also like to see each one have a basic-but-interesting premade character template.</li> <li>Powers, in the case of Vampires, a look at the Disciplines, maybe with a few alternate powers for certain levels of a few of them, as well as the idea of Devotions/alloyed powers, where when a character has X level in two or more Disciplines, it's possible to learn new powers that build off of both effects (like say, something where if you have Auspex 2 or 3 and Obfuscate 3, it's possible to learn some sort of power where you appear as exactly who a person wants to or expects to see), and as with Traits, general guidelines for Disciplines above five dots, again just giving a basic idea rather than a bunch of new specific powers for each level. And, also some more stuff about Thaumaturgy and Necromancy, including the idea of types of Blood Magic outside of those two powers.</li> <li>Group Dynamics, which in Vampire's case is about the Coterie, with more about how and why they form, different types of them, what they do, how they function in Kindred society and its courts, handling inter-coterie conflict, thinking about the Coterie as a PC in its own right, and rules for Coterie members pooling their resources in order to create a joint Domain, including shared Backgrounds. This would also talk a little bit about characters thinking about their overall place in a city/domain, in a region, and even in the Camarilla (or other group) as a whole.</li> <li>Secondary Splats, ie Bloodlines. (I'm operating on the idea that the core book would've included all 13 clans.) I'd probably keep this kind of short. Just two page write ups for each, and rules for whatever their special Discipline is, although it might be worth it to have an extra page to spend time talking about how they fit in Kindred society and challenges of playing them. In my ideal world, this would be the two fallen, the Salubri and Cappadocians (with the idea that the Samedi are one of several surviving factions, including ones in Africa and the Middle East), two created, the Gargoyles and the Kiasyd (who'd be separate from the Sabbat), and two mysteries no one can explain the origins of, the Daughters of Cacophony (who may be harbingers of the end times) and the Tlacique (who are potentially a hint that there are Kindred lines in North American totally unconnected to the 13 Clans).</li> <li>Auxillery Character, meaning mortals and Ghouls, with character creation guidelines, and maybe a few paranormal abilities.</li> <li>Gear, a short appendix of character appropriate weapons, vehicles, clothing, services, and the like, including how much they cost or require in resources and how hard they may be to acquire under certain circumstances. I'd aim more for street level, civilian, and urban elite type stuff rather than military heavy weapons and the like. Maybe throw in a few arcane objects.</li> </ol>A lot of this would eventually be expanded on in the Clanbooks, an Elders Players Guide, a book about blood magic, a Ghouls book, World of Darkness: Armory, and the like. I'd have probably have done the Bloodlines as two in one books, The Fallen, the Created, and the Mysteries, each one getting a chapter, and then the last part discussing the idea of additional Bloodlines based on said theme. The Fallen's example would be the True Brujah (with a better name; because no one calls the Salubri the True Tremere), the Created's might be the Blood Brothers, Arhimane or Nagaraga, and the Mysteries could be anything. Such is my ideas, anyway. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-31T23:04:49-07:00","id":1393360,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1393347#post1393347\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The True Hand is at its best when you take the view you're aware they're a bunch of hypocrites and insane cultists.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, the book flat out says at the start that they are total hypocrites. I just wish that some of these character write ups reflected this a little more. There's actually a number of interesting and potentially fairly cool ideas in the book. It's just that they're caked over with annoying laters of Crossover Crud, Dumb Dust, Racial Rust, and Better Than Everyone Else Grime that need to be cleaned off first. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T19:35:50-07:00","id":1393726,"title":"","content":"<b>Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand (part four)</b><br /> <br /> Our last two, and, interestingly, both are from the new bloodlines presented in the book. <br /> <br /> (I just want to note that I consider the True Brujah to have one of the worst names in the entire Vampire line. If they actually are what they claim to be, it's the equivalent of the Salubri being called the &quot;True Tremere&quot; or the Cappadocians being called the &quot;True Giovanni&quot;. I think the only thing even comparable to this in terms of lack of effort was WoD:Gypsies having two of the &quot;families of power&quot; being called Ravnos and Lupine.)<br /> <br /> <b>The Seller of Secrets (True Brujah)</b><br /> This one is somewhat interesting. He's a bit of a crossover character, being a former member of the Arcanum. Normally, I'd find that a little off putting, but in this case it works OK and seems to tie into his overall concept pretty well. (I'm also a little bit more forgiving of the idea of embraced witch-hunters than I am of sorcerers, kinfolk, and what have you.) He's a Southerner educated at Harvard, which makes him akin to Woodrow Willson. However, the character image makes me think of HG Wells. As both were of a technocratic bent (I mean that in the real world sense rather than the Mage one), it makes me think that such a take might've been a more interesting concept for a True Brujah character, effectively making it a total antithesis of the Brujah stereotype. But I digress. He joined the Arcanum and became something of an expert on vampires, including legends and lore about the Antediluvians. Eventually he traded his findings to a member of the Black Hand in exchange for the embrace. Given how much Noddist vampires sometimes struggle for and treasure the lore they collect, it feels a little odd that a mortal might actually collect such impressive and unique lore as to make elder vampires take notice. Maybe they were just impressed with the fact that he'd been able to learn anything at all. Now he continues on as he did in life, collecting knowledge and lore and trading it to other vampires in exchange for favors. He's Mental primary with a 4/5/4 split. Social is secondary, and he has both Manipulation and Appearance at four. Really, I'd expect a character like this to be kind of unassuming, rather than super good looking. Maybe it's the &quot;bald is sexy&quot; thing that Patrick Steward had going on for him. None of his abilities top three, including Investigate, Linguistics, Occult and Science. He has no Kindred Lore skill either, which feels lacking for this sort of concept. He does have Athletics, Streetwise, and Etiquette at three though. (I'm kind of imagining him being on Harvard's rowing team and still doing it as a hobby in the middle of the night on the Charles River, which amuses me.) Disciplines are threes in Auspex, Potence, and Presence, two in Temporis, and ones in Animalism, Dominate, and Obfuscate. Backgrounds include Age, Contacts, Generation, Resources, Retainers, Secret Identity, and Status, mostly at two. His Humanity and Willpower are both five. The Willpower kind of sticks out in this book, where most everyone else has it from seven to nine. Generally, this isn't a bad character. I think his major weakness is that he's very similar in concept to the Antiquarian at the start of the set, and of the two the Antiquarian is the more interesting. I also don't think he's the best representation of his Bloodline. He really could have been just about any clan, and it wouldn't have made much of a difference. So as a True Brujah, he's not that interesting. But as a generic elder template, he's OK. <br /> <br /> <b>The Shadow Crusader (Old Clan Tzimisce)</b><br /> And in a total 180 from the previous character, this one's concept is entirely based on the bloodline's raison d'\u00eatre. She was the daughter of a 15th century blacksmith and had a Tzimisce lord fall in love with her and take her for his bride. Then the Tremere and/or Sabbat came and killed him and his vampiric family. A servant saved her and took her to a creepy Tzimisce elder who embraced her after telling her everything was the fault of the Souleaters. (I'm a little amused that this write up leaves a little bit of the impression that said elder was a raving paranoid and this entire Souleater thing is some sort of shared delusion.) So now she hunts Souleaters and their pawns (especially the Sabbat). And if anyone questions this crusade, then maybe they're one of Them too. I actually kind of like the back story here. It has a nice fairy-tail that went off the rails quality to it. In both character image and equipment list, this is a total 90s <a href=\"https://youtu.be/XaZem6lMEqw\" target=\"_blank\">edge lord</a>. Black clothes, black trench coat, black sunglasses, etc. All she's missing is a shotgun with Dragon's Breath rounds. Physical is primary, with a 4/5/4 split, and Mental secondary with 4/3/3. I'm a little surprised it isn't 4/2/4. Melee is maxed out at five dots, with Brawl and Firearms at four. Dodge is only three, as are Alertness, Athletics, Leadership, Stealth, and Occult. Disciplines include Auspex at four, Celerity at three, and Animalism, Dominate, Fortitude, and Obfuscate at two each. Backgrounds include Age and Generation at three, Contacts at two and Allies at one. Given her background and the Leadership ability, I'd have thought this would've been a good candidate for the Military Force background. Humanity is six and Willpower is nine. By and large, this is another KillNinja. But at least it's a KillNinja with a nice backstory. I kind of wish it had kept a little more of the broken fairly tail aesthetic, and made her feel a little more &quot;noble&quot; in dress/look instead of the &quot;clothing by <i>Highlander</i>&quot; thing. So it's OK, but not the best. <br /> <br /> So, that's the Black Hand. As I said, I'm not really a huge fan of the book. There are a number of interesting ideas and stuff that could have been better developed into potentially cool stuff. And that includes this collection of templates. At least four, if not five, of the characters are KillNinja, essentially designed for combat with anything else being an afterthought. However, the Malkavian and the Tzimisce both have pretty good back stories, and the Nagaraja is at least amusing enough in his overall concept that I can kind of overlook it. The Nosferatu has enough of an overall not-KillNinja concept that I can largely forgive his traits being designed as one. The Assamite on the other hand, I really dislike on a number of levels, and I can very easily see how some people might take it as being actively racist. (And that's not something I tend to say lightly.)<br /> Of the other five, they in many ways feel like general elder characters as opposed to members of the Black Hand. On the one hand, that's great, as we didn't get any templates in the Elysium book to go with its new elder creation rules, so it's nice to see something like that. One the other hand, it does leave a little bit of the impression that the Black Hand are just elders, with out a lot that makes them stand out besides the constant &quot;we're the secret heroes&quot; theme. (I kind of wish there'd been at least one who was clearly a &quot;hero&quot; in the same way, say, Stalin was, proclaiming himself the great champion of the masses while brutally slaughtering them by the millions.) I really do like the Government Leader and Antiquarian though. The Corporate Conspirator and Seller of Secrets are OK, but a little cookie cutter, especially when compared to the other two. The Former Chatterling is the one of the few that seems to be based specifically on the Black Hand and its overall culture and structure. Even if I do have some quibbles about it overall, that's certainly a plus in its favor. <br /> I really would've liked to see one or more templates that dealt with the cult and its takes on blood magic, as well as their whole interaction with the Underworld angle. Also maybe one fixated on Antediluvian worship who isn't a terrible stereotype from a clan already totally based around that idea. Maybe one or two based on the new Revenant linages introduced in the book. And more examples from the Eastern Hand would've been nice. <br /> <br /> So, that's the end of this section. Next will be Clanbook: Assamite. However, as I started back to work this week, these entries are likely to start coming at longer intervals. However, I am determined to at least finish the original run of clanbooks. At that point, if there is still interest, I will probably start examining the early Dark Ages books. (And if I actually do have a copy of Alien Hunger, I'll try to do those as well.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T19:52:47-07:00","id":1393728,"title":"","content":"Brujah is the name of the original Brujah Antediluvian. According to the True Brujah, the name of the False Brujah Antediluvian is Troile.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T20:19:16-07:00","id":1393730,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1393728#post1393728\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Brujah is the name of the original Brujah Antediluvian. According to the True Brujah, the name of the False Brujah Antediluvian is Troile.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know. I just still think it was creatively lazy. That or deliberately baiting the type of people who are attracted to wanting to be the &quot;True&quot; anything so that they can feel more elite and special than the common posers. I'd really have been less annoyed if they'd just said that Troile was the Antediluvian's name (and the name of the bloodline) and that the Brujah were just a bunch of rabble who destroyed him/her and usurped their place as a clan. The name just invites mockery, and I'm sure that somewhere lost in the Internet ether of the 90s is a Truer-than-True Brujah bloodline, claiming that they are the <i>real</i> Brujah and the True Brujah are fakes and wannabes. Probably while all sulking together in the corner of the Succubus Club. <br /> As a general concept - the idea of this group of Brujah who've taken the clan's infamous rage and suppressed it and swallowed it and intellectualized it to the point that they are these little bundles of icy fury who nurse even the tiniest grudge for centuries while acting as cold and emotionless and Spock-like as possible - they are really fun and neat. I've actually used that as a small-b bloodline/strain within the Brujah clan in my games before. I don't even mind their Special Sauce discipline that badly, though temporal magic feels like it would go better with a bunch of historians or something.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T21:11:37-07:00","id":1393736,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1393730#post1393730\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I know. I just still think it was creatively lazy. That or deliberately baiting the type of people who are attracted to wanting to be the &quot;True&quot; anything so that they can feel more elite and special than the common posers. I'd really have been less annoyed if they'd just said that Troile was the Antediluvian's name (and the name of the bloodline) and that the Brujah were just a bunch of rabble who destroyed him/her and usurped their place as a clan. The name just invites mockery, and I'm sure that somewhere lost in the Internet ether of the 90s is a Truer-than-True Brujah bloodline, claiming that they are the <i>real</i> Brujah and the True Brujah are fakes and wannabes. Probably while all sulking together in the corner of the Succubus Club.<br /> As a general concept - the idea of this group of Brujah who've taken the clan's infamous rage and suppressed it and swallowed it and intellectualized it to the point that they are these little bundles of icy fury who nurse even the tiniest grudge for centuries while acting as cold and emotionless and Spock-like as possible - they are really fun and neat. I've actually used that as a small-b bloodline/strain within the Brujah clan in my games before. I don't even mind their Special Sauce discipline that badly, though temporal magic feels like it would go better with a bunch of historians or something.</div> </div> </div> </div>It bothers me a bit that they're just the antithesis of the Brujah Clan. Also, you're right the &quot;True&quot; Brujah are ridiculous since Troile is still Brujah's descendant so the False Brujah are still the descendants of the original Brujah.<br /> <br /> I think it's also claimed in V20 that Troile was Brujah's only childe, so that means the True Brujah are all Troile's descendants as well--just pre-diablerie.<br /> <br /> One headcanon theory from BJD derived evidence was that the True Brujah were created in Carthage and are actually just a bunch of Troile descendants that bound their rage away magically after seeing their Antediluvian fall to the Baali.<br /> <br /> Possibly making up the whole story of Troile diablerizing Brujah.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T21:32:13-07:00","id":1393740,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1393736#post1393736\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One headcanon theory from BJD derived evidence was that the True Brujah were created in Carthage and are actually just a bunch of Troile descendants that bound their rage away magically after seeing their Antediluvian fall to the Baali.<br /> <br /> Possibly making up the whole story of Troile diablerizing Brujah.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is an interesting idea.<br /> <br /> At some point I'm going to have to study up on actually Carthage history and figure out how it might work as a setting beyond all the Brujah utopia weirdness the game invented. <br /> <br /> I kind of feel like the Antediluvians should more or less disappear from Kindred history after 1000 BC or so, turning into more mythical figures who only appear rarely, and by the time of the Ceasar, even 4th gen members of the same clan can't seem to agree on various details. And when four of them seem to be destroyed during that one four hundred year period, it should be both scarey and puzzling that they were even approached without their consent, let alone diablerized.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-02T22:32:58-07:00","id":1393742,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1393740#post1393740\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That is an interesting idea.<br /> <br /> At some point I'm going to have to study up on actually Carthage history and figure out how it might work as a setting beyond all the Brujah utopia weirdness the game invented.</div> </div> </div> </div>1. Carthagian history is an interesting place to have made the Brujah utopia given the fact that I'm of the historical side of things that it was one of the practicioners of regular child sacrifice. Rome actually used that as one of the evils that caused it to deserve to die (despite being no strangers to infanticide themselves).<br /> <br /> 2. The Gods of Carthage are the ones demonized by the Hebrew Bible into the Baali ones. Can't imagine why with the Moloch.<br /> <br /> 3. When I did up Carthage with the player characters, I actually had the view that it WAS a Utopia...by ancient city standards. In my view, the vampires ruled the city absolutely and fed from hundreds of willing slaves and human sacrifices. They provided the city wealth, prosperity, and protection with their vast magic. Yes, people died horribly but that was the price of doing business back then.<br /> <br /> What did you call a monster who demanded blood offerings, sexual slaves, and ruled absolutely?<br /> <br /> Answer: a god.<br /> <br /> Not exactly a great sell from a modern perspective, though. Especially if you're trying to use it as an example of humans and Kindred cohabitating peacefully.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I kind of feel like the Antediluvians should more or less disappear from Kindred history after 1000 BC or so, turning into more mythical figures who only appear rarely, and by the time of the Ceasar, even 4th gen members of the same clan can't seem to agree on various details. And when four of them seem to be destroyed during that one four hundred year period, it should be both scarey and puzzling that they were even approached without their consent, let alone diablerized. </div> </div> </div>I dunno, there's kind of an interesting angle to the idea of the Founders taking refuge in audacity by denying the Antediluvians exist to people who absolutely know for a fact they exist. Lasombra's castle, The Cathedral of Flesh, and other places where the Antediluvians have been up at various points in recent memory.<br /> <br /> It was one of the elements I liked about the Giovanni Chronicles.<br /> <br /> &quot;The Antediluvians are a myth. A fiction. Children's stories.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Cappadocias is in Italy. I saw him last week.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;How silly. Clearly you just met a Kindred who CLAIMED to be Cappadocias.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;He moved the moon for better light.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 06-03-2020, 01:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-03T17:17:10-07:00","id":1393885,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1393742#post1393742\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not exactly a great sell from a modern perspective, though. Especially if you're trying to use it as an example of humans and Kindred cohabitating peacefully.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah. I always thought that Carthage was such a weird choice of a hill for the Brujah to die on, so to speak. I really would've figured classical Greece would've been a more fitting choice. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;The Antediluvians are a myth. A fiction. Children's stories.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Cappadocias is in Italy. I saw him last week.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;How silly. Clearly you just met a Kindred who CLAIMED to be Cappadocias.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;He moved the moon for better light.&quot; </div> </div> </div>Heh. <br /> <br /> I sometimes wonder if a lot of the times Antediluvians interacted with lesser beings, including their own 4th generation offspring, they were just using mental projections or other cut outs of some sort. All the ones whose clans have Auspex as a discipline should be able to do mental projections, including ones that can use any and all of their other discipline powers as the projection (there's a high level Auspex power in revised edition that does this). Tzimisce can probably create homunculi duplicates of itself. Haquim might be able to do the same with blood created clones, Cappadocias out of ectoplasm, Lasombra out of shadows, etc. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-04T04:02:37-07:00","id":1394013,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1393726#post1393726\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand (part four)</b><br /> Given how much Noddist vampires sometimes struggle for and treasure the lore they collect, it feels a little odd that a mortal might actually collect such impressive and unique lore as to make elder vampires take notice. Maybe they were just impressed with the fact that he'd been able to learn anything at all.</div> </div> </div> </div>If we take seriously the introduction to &quot;The Book of Nod&quot; supplement, in which there's written that it is published in a few copies by Aristotle and send to his friends, maybe it was this template who, as a mortal, helped him complete it (that would explain the Age background) I know, that he's from different clan than Aristotle but he could find his True Brujah Sire who, in turn, send his findings to his ally de Laurent.<br /> <br /> My bad, I mistook the introduction to Encyclopedia Vampirica as an introduction to Book of Nod","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":13141,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","user":"Nail Eater","date":".  Critical error. 06-05-2020, 05:55 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-05T20:12:08-07:00","id":1394489,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Assamite (part one)</b><br /> <br /> So, the 2nd ed Assamite's had a number of ... issues. They were, essentially, the medieval Assassins cult turned into a vampire clan. The clanbook doubled down on this, basing their entire clan structure on said cult. In addition, they were all male, and all Middle Eastern or Indian. At least there were until, as this book claims, the 18th century when they started allowing women, and the 1900s when they started embracing white people. This book also introduces the Sorcerers and Scholars, but they're mostly just footnotes to the whole assassin thing. Every clan member follows the Path of Blood. And they don't interact with non-Assamites except on occasions when they pretend to be from another clan. This made it sometimes difficult to incorporate them into a chronicle that wasn't entirely Assamite focuses. <br /> (Ironically, the first edition Players Guide write up had them being about 50/50 gender wise.)<br /> This book was written by Graeme Davis, who I primarily recognize as a GURPS writer, especially on their books about the Middle Ages and Vikings, among other things. The interior art is, save for the chapter opening splash pages, entirely scans of real guns and melee weapons. The splash page and template art is by Robert Macneill, whose work is OK, but lacks some of the character and detail of Timbrook's. (This change in artwork from the Camarilla clanbooks has the probably unintentional effect of further &quot;othering&quot; the Assamites, which is unfortunate.)<br /> <br /> <b>The Playboy </b><br /> This is one of those &quot;pretends to be from another clan&quot; characters I mentioned above. In this case, he was born in some third world slum, and worked his way up from petty thief to self educated wealthy gambler who was able to pass himself off as a man of class and breeding. A long rivalry with an older gentleman over, of all things, backgammon led to his being selected for the clan. Now he continues as before, just adding another layer or two to his personal masquerade. He's Mental primary and Social secondary, which I kind of would've expected to be the other way around, but it makes sense this way as well. Though his Socials are 3/2/3, and I'd have figured him for a higher Manipulation. His abilities also seem a little lacking. He's got a three in Alertness, and everything else is at one or two dots. This includes just two in Subterfuge, Stealth, and Investigation, as well as Gambling, and ones in Acting and Etiquette. (He's got a lot of secondary abilities, contributing to this skill creep problem. In addition to Gambling, he's got single dots in Bribery, Carousing, Masquerade, and Seduction. Once again, I think that if you're going to get a secondary ability, it should be something an existing one doesn't already cover and you should really commit to it. I think if you got rid of everything but Gambling and raised that to a 3 or 4, it'd make a stronger character.) His Disciplines are one dot of each, plus an additional one in Quietus. I'm not sure why this character needs that second dot of Quietus. I think he'd work just fine with one each or having a 2/1 or 1/2 with Celerity and Obfuscate. And those seven points could've been better used elsewhere. Backgrounds include four in Resources, three in Generation, and one each in Fame and Status. (I've no idea if this is clan status or Camarilla/city status.) His Path rating is six and his Willpower is four. (He has the lowest WP of any template in the book.) I actually like this character. He's got a pretty cool backstory and feels like he'd fit in easily with any regular Vampire chronicle. He totally plays against the clan stereotype while still being a form of predator. I could even see this character fitting in just fine with the revised version of the clan. I'd definitely change him over to Humanity though. <br /> <br /> <b>The Holy Warrior</b><br /> So ... we've got an image of what looks like an extra from Lawrence of Arabia. The full press Saudi desert sheik with sword. And his backstory is basically the textbook 1980s Afghanistan/Kurd/etc. oppressed rebel. And at some point he was taken to the clan strong hold for more training and eventual embrace. And now he's back to doing the same as before. Roleplaying tips include mention that anyone who disagrees with his religious beliefs are just wrong and there's no point in discussion. As far as traits go, he's pretty much a KillNinja. Physicals primary with the four in Dexterity; Mental secondary with the two in Intelligence. Firearms at four dots;Melee, Brawl and Dodge at three (as is Survival). He's also got two dots in Demolition. Everything else is at a one, save Intimidation at two. Disciplines are again the one each with an extra dot, this time in Celerity. Backgrounds include three dots in Allies (Armed Group), one dot in Contacts (Black Market) and two dots in Generation. I will give credit for the added detail to the backgrounds. It's a nice touch that I wish more templates had. Path is five, Willpower is six. While I certainly don't have as strong a dislike for this character as I have had for, say, the Malkavian Freak, I don't really like it that much either. It is as if someone looked at the clan's ethnic stereotype and said, &quot;make that a character&quot;. I think it would be possible to do more with this character if a little more thought had been put in to it and the idea had been advanced a little more. Does his mortal faith clash in anyway with his clan indoctrination and the Path of Blood? Or has the Path of Blood completely consumed his mortal beliefs and now he's similar to the Jyhad Fanatic from the Black Hand book, waging a &quot;holy war&quot; for only Haquim? I think those sort of things might've made for a somewhat more interesting character. <br /> <br /> <b>The Psycho Killer</b><br /> OK then. This guy had a lifelong obsessive fascination with death, especially the moment of transition from living to dead. He quickly learned to keep this a secret - you know, as torturing and killing small animals is frowned upon in most places - and turned into the quiet, kind of creepy teenage loner, and once an adult became a serial killer. Eventually one of his would be victims turned out to be an Assamite who'd been watching him. So he got taken to the clan stronghold and embraced. And now he continues to kill people. He's Mental primary and Physical secondary. With the exception of three dots in Computer and Forensics, he has no skills above two in anything. He has two dots in First Aid, which is a bit redundant as he has two dots in Medicine. He also has two dots in Alertness, Stealth, Drive, and Science. Just ones in all the combat abilities, as well as Subterfuge, Streetwise, and Empathy. You'd think he might have a dot in Acting just to pass for normal. Disciplines are again the one each plus one, this time in Obfuscate. These mostly make sense, though I could see an argument for either cutting Celerity or moving that dot to Quietus or Obfuscate. Backgrounds are just three in Generation and two in Resources. Path and Willpower are both seven. This is not a terrible character. I'm just not sure it works very well as an Assamite. I think he'd actually make a pretty good Tzimisce or Giovanni (or Samedi). Maybe even a Ravnos. He's also work pretty well as a Sabbat, especially on the Path of Death and the Soul. (I'm not sure his obsessions fit that well with the Path of Blood's tenets.) His points need to be spent a little better, I think. But I'm not sure why he's in this clan other than the fact that he likes to kill people. <br /> <br /> And that's part one. Part two should be up this weekend.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-05T21:32:32-07:00","id":1394504,"title":"","content":"I've always had a bit of mixed feelings on the OG Assamites despite the fact they're every bit as much of a rip off of popular fiction as the Tzimisce. They're also a bit humorous given how utterly popular ASSASSINS CREED has gotten in recent years. They're both based on the Vladimir Bartol novel &quot;Alamut&quot; which pretty much made up a huge amount of the mythology that both the OG Assamites and the Ubisoft ones used.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alamut_(Bartol_novel)#Cultural_impact\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alamut...ultural_impact</a><br /> <br /> I've never actually had a problem with the OG Assamites to be honest but I've always felt like the game had a weird issue that they should be a bigger deal than just a crazy ass bloodline. If they were a demonically obsessed Blood Cult akin to the True Hand or (more aptly) Followers of Set, that's not a problem. It's just later books acted like the Assamites were the ONLY Clan in the Middle East and thus they added the Viziers and Sorcerers--turning the Assamites more into a sect than a Clan.<br /> <br /> I think the problem was only fixed when they made the Web of Knives into the sect that they were originally assumed all to be and also when they created the Ashirra. That way the Middle East wasn't reduced to &quot;Terrorist Land.&quot; Mind you, what was possibly an interesting oddball antagonist group in 1994 wasn't going to be anything but an excuse for racism post 9/11.<br /> Just my two cents.<br /> <br /> 1. The Assamites are actually still one of the most sexist of all clans according to canon as while this stated that they only started accepting women in the 18th century. It's still canon that Fatima al-Faqadi is the first woman Assamite. I think it's fine to assume they mean the first woman <i>Web of Knives Assamite</i> but they stuck with this a lot.<br /> <br /> 2. It's interesting to note almost all of your suggestions for the Holy Warrior formed into the basis for the Assamite Schism between Islamic sect members vs. Ur-Shulgi (as well as Jewish and Christian).<br /> <br /> 3. The Psycho Killer one warrants this:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://youtu.be/O52jAYa4Pm8\" data-code=\"O52jAYa4Pm8\" data-provider=\"youtube_share\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/O52jAYa4Pm8/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> I actually played this Template and the experience eventually inspired one of my books (albeit the characters are nothing alike).<br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://i.gr-assets.com/images/S/compressed.photo.goodreads.com/books/1590680855l/53573333._SY475_.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/><br /> <br /> The angle I took with my Assamite Psycho Killer was that he was someone who was brainwashed and blood-bonded into only going after &quot;evil&quot; people by his masters while being a natural killer. It amusingly predicted Dexter.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 06-06-2020, 12:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-06T20:58:27-07:00","id":1394714,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1394504#post1394504\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The angle I took with my Assamite Psycho Killer was that he was someone who was brainwashed and blood-bonded into only going after &quot;evil&quot; people by his masters while being a natural killer. It amusingly predicted Dexter.</div> </div> </div> </div>An interesting idea. I hadn't considered that. (At some point I should probably try to finish the book series, which I generally enjoyed more than the TV adaption.)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-06T21:20:43-07:00","id":1394717,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1394714#post1394714\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> An interesting idea. I hadn't considered that. (At some point I should probably try to finish the book series, which I generally enjoyed more than the TV adaption.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, I don't think the Web of Knives requires your participation to be voluntary. You are materials for their war. Maybe you'll die but that won't be your choice.<br /> <br /> And yes, I think the book's end was superior at least.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-06T22:13:17-07:00","id":1394721,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Assamite (part two)</b><br /> <br /> And away we go.<br /> <br /> <b>The Professional</b><br /> A professional wet-worker, and judging from the art, one at least somewhat inspired by the 1990 French film <i>La Femme Nikita</i> (which was remade as Hong Kong's 1991 <i>Black Cat</i> and America's 1993 <i>Point of No Return</i>, and inspired two TV series). While the write up is gender neutral, the character image makes this our first female Assamite character of the book. Basically ex-military sniper recruited by the CIA only to get burned, fake their death and go into business for themselves. Then recruited by one of the Assamites. They approach killing as just a particular business they happen to have a talent for. Mental is primary, with Perception at four dots. Physical is secondary with threes in Dexterity and Stamina. Alertness and Firearms are at four dots each, and Stealth at three. Everything else is twos or ones. Two of those twos are in the secondary abilities of Gunsmithing and Scan, both of which are fairly redundant, as the character's four in Alertness and two in Investigation should cover anything scan-related, and raising the Repair skill from one dot to two in conjunction with the high Firearms skill would seem to cover actually caring for one's weapons. Besides Firearms and a single dot in Dodge, the character has no combat skills. Disciplines are, again, the one dot each with an extra dot, this time in Quietus. I'm not really sure why. I don't think the extra dot is really needed, but if it was going to be there, Obfuscate feels like a better pick for the character. Backgrounds are two dots in Contacts (Espionage), two dots in Generation, and one dot in Resources. Again, I appreciate the extra detail with the Contacts. Path rating is seven, Willpower is eight. This isn't a terrible character, but it's not that great either. It's one of those taciturn lone-wolf types that are popular in certain circles, and is a bit of a one-trick pony. The character can shoot really well and ... that's about it. A lot of the skills you'd expect for a former soldier or former CIA agent - hand to hand combat, survival skills, subterfuge, pretending to be someone you aren't - are missing. As is much of a personality. They have no long term goals or aspirations, and apparently never did. It's less of a person than a tool that walks around on its own and makes the occasional facial expression. Maybe that works as a member of a giant murder cult, but feels a little lacking as a Vampire protagonist. <br /> <br /> <b>The Sportsman</b><br /> A long time Safari guide from Southern Africa. (It says he stowed away on a boat and came there at 15; I've no idea why as there've been plenty of white people being born in Southern Africa for well over a century or two at this point. And yes, the character image is of a white guy in the classic southern African bush ranger outfit.) He'd apparently been doing this for a fairly long time, and in the era of modern poaching decided to start hunting poachers. Which apparently drew the attention of an Assamite recruiter. Now he hunts even more dangerous game. Physicals are Primary, with Dexterity at four, and Mental at secondary. He's got threes in Alertness and Firearms. Everything else is ones or twos, including two in Survival, which feels too low for this character. He's also got no less than four secondary abilities, including a one in First Aid (why not just get a single dot of Medicine, which does the same thing?), and twos in Hunting and Tracking (which are redundant with Survival and in this case are actually taking away from points that could've raised that skill to four or even five), and that damn Scan ability again. Disciplines are, again, the one each plus one, again in Quietus. This doesn't bother me as much with this character. I can see a reason for him to want to learn all three abilities, and learning some of the more martial aspects of Quietus seems to fit. Backgrounds are three in Generation and two in Resources. Given his backstory, I'd have figured him for at least one dot of Fame. Probably some Contacts or Allies as well. Path and Willpower are both six. As a concept, I really like this character a lot. It takes the &quot;Assamites as hunters&quot; schtick in an interesting and offbeat direction fairly far from the ethnic stereotype. I just wish his stats were better, because I'm pretty sure that the Gangrel Great White Hunter would eat him for lunch. <br /> <br /> <b>The Mercenary</b><br /> Basically a life long solider, (probably of the Silent or Babyboomer generation from the sound of things) who semi-retired into a life of training soldiers in Third World nations and taking on private military contracts in the days before that was really a well known thing. Eventually one contract led to an Assamite stronghold. And now apparently he's one of them. Basically the stereotypical loud and larger than life type up until the shooting starts and training takes over. Physical is primary (slightly surprisingly with Stamina getting the four dots instead of Dexterity) and Mental secondary (naturally Intelligence is two). Four dots in Alertness and Firearms, and three in Melee and Linguistics (which is nice to see recognized as a useful skill for this type of character, though I can't help but feel that it may be because the writer couldn't think of anywhere else to put those dots; not that a mercenary might need things like Medicine, Finance or Bureaucracy). He has one dot in that Scan skill again. Also one in Demolitions and two in Heavy Weapons. Strangely, no Military Science. Disciplines follow the same drill, this time with the extra dot in Obfuscate. Backgrounds are three in Contacts (no specifications this time) and one each in Generation and Resources. Path is six, Willpower is eight. I'm not sure how I feel about the character. As a concept for an Assamite, it sounds ok. But there's something missing here that I can't quite put my finger on. Maybe it just feels a little phoned in, like it was included just for the sake of having a soldier character. Which is a shame, as I think there's a lot you could do with the idea of an old soldier past his prime given a new lease as a vampire. <br /> <br /> That's all for this entry. Next will be the final four.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-07T02:37:41-07:00","id":1394740,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1394721#post1394721\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Clanbook: Assamite (part two)</b><br /> A lot of the skills you'd expect for a former soldier or former CIA agent - hand to hand combat, survival skills, subterfuge, pretending to be someone you aren't - are missing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe not. If this character was a sniper (and I believe it does) it doesn't need hand to hand combat or survival. Subterfuge maybe. Even Alertness isn't neccesary because snipers works with the spotters. I believe though that Scan can be useful cause military snipers, besides shooting, reports many things to HQ: how big are enemy forces, their movements etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-07T02:48:15-07:00","id":1394742,"title":"","content":"I wonder where you would place those templates. I mean The Holy Warrior is of course from Middle East, The Sportsman is active in Africa, Tremere Professional Witch from New England but what about the others? Where you would put them (either in cities described in citybooks or somewhere else)?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-07T16:31:03-07:00","id":1394870,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nail Eater</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1394742#post1394742\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wonder where you would place those templates. I mean The Holy Warrior is of course from Middle East, The Sportsman is active in Africa, Tremere Professional Witch from New England but what about the others? Where you would put them (either in cities described in citybooks or somewhere else)?</div> </div> </div> </div>The Holy Warrior is deliberately vague about its origins, including the suggestion that it could just as well be a Contra as it could a Mujaheddin, though I think any sort of Central American Catholic (or Marxist) would be even more at odds with aspects of the Path of Blood than a Muslim. Most of these Assamite concepts are apparently conceived as being globe-trotting assassin types with no real home base outside of Alamut. <br /> <br /> Most of the ones from the original seven books were designed to be used in any Camarilla city, though a few are more specific, such as the Brujah Fake Rapper being specifically designed for the greater LA/Anarch Free States.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-07T18:41:38-07:00","id":1394927,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Assamite (part three)</b><br /> <br /> And again.<br /> <br /> <b>The Angel of Death</b><br /> Our second female character, and again, the write up seems to be somewhat gender neutral. (There are more than a few ballet references, but that doesn't really mean much, especially if you interpret the character as being Russian or Eastern European.) Anyway, this is a poor individual from the bad part of town who managed to work their way in with a gang, eventually moving pretty high up as an elite killer. And then got recruited by the Assamites. They view killing as some sort of pleasure high that they are really good at. Really, they feel like the main henchmen of the villain from a certain class of action crime-drama films. Mental is primary, Physical secondary, with Dexterity at four dots. Firearms and Melee are at three dots each, with Athletics, Dodge, Intimidation, Stealth, and Investigation at two. Disciplines are the same set up as all the others, with the extra dot in Quietus. I think having it in Celerity might've fit the concept better. Backgrounds are two dots in Generation and three in Resources. You'd think this character might have some Contacts or something. Maybe even a Retainer (a background that no character in this book has). Path is six, Willpower is eight. Overall, this isn't a bad character. I think the traits could stand some adjustment. Maybe make Physicals primary and Socials secondary. Buff up a few of the social/cultural abilities the write up mentions the character as having a strong interest in. As is, it's kind of one of those middle of the road things that doesn't stand out as being particularly good or bad, just there. <br /> <br /> <b>The Avenger</b><br /> Kind of a weird one. From the title and character image (a guy with a maniacal look on his face firing off twin Uzis), you'd think this was going to be <i>The Punisher</i> turned into a vampire, but no. He used to be a soldier, but where, when and for whom is left very vague, but from context, I'm going to guess probably from one of those countries that decided Israel was an affront to the honor of Islam and tried to wipe it out as soon as it came into existence in 1948. Anyway, he got recruited into the Assamites and apparently became fanatically fixated on the clan, especially on all the affronts to their honor from the Camarilla, especially the Tremere curse. And now he does the usual Assamite thing, but laughs maniacally about how members of the Camarilla pay him to avenge his clan upon them. Somewhat to my surprise, he's Mental primary and Physical secondary. Abilitywise, he's mostly a KillNinja. Alertness, Firearms, Melee and Stealth are three each, with Brawl, Dodge, Survival and Investigation at two each. He has five dots in Disciplines, with Celerity and Quietus at two each. (Of all the characters, I would've expected this to be one to go all in on the clan's Special Sauce power.) Backgrounds are four dots in Generation and one in Status. I presume that's Clan Status. Weirdly, for a guy so balls to the wall gung ho for the clan's ideology, his Path rating is only five, making him tied with the Holy Warrior as lowest. Willpower is six. As I said, this is a weird one. I can understand the idea of having a character totally devoted to avenging the clan's past losses, but this feels more like a motivation for a character rather than a complete character. Almost as if you had a Brujah totally obsessed with getting back at the Ventrue over Carthage, but was otherwise your bog standard cookie cutter street punk who does street punk things and little else. He's also a bit hampered by having all of his freebies spent on extra Discipline dots. So overall, I think I have to put this one pretty low on the list. <br /> <br /> <b>The Gamester</b><br /> I really love this character, and it's probably my favorite concept in the book. It takes the clan's stereotype and turns it on its head and inside out, into someone who approaches all of existence, including assassinations, as a series of puzzles to solve as part of an endless quest for mental stimulation. As a child, this involved various games and other pursuits before eventually discovering burglary and security systems. Eventually, they broke into a mansion that turned out to be owned by a vampire. This lead to being recruited by the Assamites, doing what they've always done best. The write up describes the character as a nerd type, and the artwork is youngish looking 90s geek girl (making our third female, and again with a gender neutral write up). And this is from before geek culture pretty much beat that concept to death. Mental is primary, naturally, with Wits at four. I'm a little surprised no freebies were spent to boost them even more. Investigation is maxed out to five dots, and Security at four. Stealth and Alertness are three each. Repair, Survival, and Computer are twos. I'm not sure Survival fits the character that much, and the other two feel a little low. Also, there are ones in Firearms and Melee, which more or less make sense, but none in Brawl even though it mentions having studied Aikido at some point. No Streetwise and Subterfuge at only one. And there's two dots in Game Playing, which I think could've been spent better elsewhere, even if might suit the character (in which case it should be higher, I think). And not all of the ability points have been spent. We have five in Talent, thirteen in Skill and only eight in Knowledge (two of those taking Investigation over three in violation of char gen rules). Plus two in Game Playing, which IIRC is a Skill. Disciplines are, again, the same deal as all the others, with the extra dot in Obfuscate. And again, I think those points could've been better spent elsewhere. Backgrounds are two in Generation, two in Resources, and one in Status. Path is at seven, Willpower at six. Like I said, I really enjoy this character a lot. I just wish the traits were done a lot better. <br /> <br /> <b>The Scholar</b><br /> So, this book introduced the Scholar and Sorcerer castes to the clan, but pretty much as foot notes, with both focused almost entirely on trying to break the clan curse. So this guy is a specialist in hematology. He was recruited to try to help find a cure for the clan's curse, and eventually embraced. And now he works in a lab at an Assamite strong hold doing research all night. And that's pretty much it. Mental is primary, with two extra dots to raise Intelligence to five. Wits is four and Perception three. I really think Wits could've been left at three to save a few points. Medicine and the secondary ability of Research are maxed out at five. Alertness is at three. I've no idea why. Maybe to make sure he knows when his lab machines' timers go off. Science is only at two, and then he has the totally redundant abilities of Biology and Chemistry on top of that. Seriously, just spend the points to raise his Science to four or five. He also has Kindred Lore two, which makes a certain level of sense, and Firearms at two, which doesn't. And again, some of his ability points are missing. He only has one dot in each clan discipline, making him the only character to not have spent freebies on it. He has two dots in Contacts, and one each in Generation, Resources, and Status. Path rating is eight, making him the most hardcore devotee in the book, and Willpower is five. Quite honestly, this is the worst character in the book. His entire existence is based around a single activity in a single location and he never leaves to do anything else. I could see maybe a handful of ways to get him out into the world, but most of them honestly feel like the sort of convoluted situations in a bad action movie plot. But as he is, this is kind of a waste of space. <br /> <br /> And so, that's the 2nd edition Assamites. Weirdly, I ended up liking these more than I thought I would. But even so, with the exception of the Playboy and the Scholar, these are basically eight variations on a theme. Effectively, eight different ways to do &quot;I kill people&quot; as a character concept. One of the reason's Clayton Oliver is my hero is that he was able to take such a one note clan and, like some sort of alchemist, turn them into a fleshed out and diverse bunch that ended up being one of my favorite revised era clans. I don't think its an accident that my favorites out of this book - the Playboy, the Sportsman, and the Gamester - all feel like they'd fit in perfectly with the revised version of the clan. (And per <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar12002_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12002\" data-vbnamecard=\"12002\">CTPhipps</a> even the Psycho Killer feels like he fits the Web of Knives OK, though I still think he'd make a better Sabbat.) But even so, most of these aren't terrible. If anything, they are just a little bit boring. Save the Scholar, who is almost useless as anything other than an NPC or MacGuffin. <br /> As far as representation goes, while three of the character images are female, all three write ups are gender neutral, so it could just be after the fact or totally accidental. It's a little hard to judge ethnicity. The Gamester, Sportsman and Scholar are all clearly white. The Holy Warrior image is clearly Arabic. The Playboy looks like he might be Latin American, as might the Angel of Death. The Professional might could be interpreted as black. <br /> I'm a little surprised that there was no Sorcerer, but given how the Scholar turned out that might be for the best. Also, as I long wondered, for a clan of contract killers, who handles their contract negotiations? Or their armories or other logistics? Surely that's not entirely left up to ghouls. (Especially seeing as none of these templates have the Retainers background.) As far as I could tell, all the ghouls are being trained as future contract killers. Some sort of Contract Lawyer or International Arms Dealer would've been a nice addition. <br /> As far as the second edition went, the Assamites were pretty much just predators. All of these characters are that, to some degree or another. <br /> <br /> Next, the Setites, who are evil and like-a da snakes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-07T21:00:22-07:00","id":1394985,"title":"","content":"One problem I have with both the Assamites and Setite clanbooks (and to a much lesser degree, the Tremere) is the idea that the embrace comes first, and then the clan indoctrination. In my own chronicles, those two clans ONLY recruit from mortals who have already been indoctrinated in some kind of cult or secret society controlled by the clan as a front. They work much harder to ensure future members are loyal and have already bought into much of the clan's culture. The character templates in these books don't reflect that at all. I think that was a major mistake and handicapped both clans as the game developed.<br /> <br /> The Tremere aren't as cult oriented, but they still see themselves as essentially a hermetic organization. They've had to make certain comprises as a vampire lineage seeking power within the greater vampire &quot;nation&quot; in general and the Camarilla in specific, but they still need people who can work within a very structured hierarchy and can think hermetically in order to develop their Thaumaturgy. So they too prefer front organizations (although these are much more broad than the fronts the other two clans employ) to recruit future potential fledglings, although they are open to exceptions - especially in terms of people who already seen to have occult powers.<br /> <br /> I've believed this since very early on, so after almost thirty years I'm fairly set on this.<br /> <br /> Looking forward to your comments on the Setites since I LOVE Howardiana and always enjoyed early editions' embrace of it (even if I don't like how certain things developed). Given your profile pic and other comments, I think you do too. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-08T10:34:31-07:00","id":1395105,"title":"","content":"The really weird thing about the 2nd ed Assamites - what became the Web of Knives - is that if you train someone as a human for seven years, then another seven as a ghoul, and then yet another seven as a vampire neonate before granting them the equivalent to the Camarilla's right of release, the end result is a level of near ludicrous over training that far outstrips what Vampire's basic character creation was set at. Probably even above what even the Shih rules from Demon Hunter X were going for. It's roughly the equivalent of elite special forces operators who are nearing retirement age. If I really wanted to play that kind of character, there's probably better games out there to do it with (Dark Champions springs to mind). <br /> <br /> The Tremere pyramid as presented in their clanbook has a similar problem. While it sounds good on paper, when the Camarilla LARP organization tried to actually implement it in reality, it didn't work. Much like the Web of Knives, I think it works better as a smaller organization within the clan who are heavily fixated on a certain facet of clan identity. (It's part of the reason I prefer to refer to the Setites as that instead of the Followers of Set; as the former sounds like a lineage while the latter sounds like a specific cult. I want my Setite characters - and my Assamites and Tremere and others - to be able to do their own thing under the umbrella of the clan's overall theme instead of having their activities and goals dictated to them from on high.)<br /> <br /> My own impression has been that maybe a third of embraces, even among Camarilla clans like the Brujah, Gangrel or Malkavians, are impulse ones (and a lot of those may end up being destroyed depending on circumstances). But the majority are done with some amount of care and foresight, including a certain degree of &quot;grooming&quot; and various tests before said Childe ever even encounters their sire in person. (I actually really liked the way the revised Followers of Set book laid out how potential embraces will have their lives slowly and methodically destroyed as part of a way of tearing down their preconceived ways of viewing the world and making them much more accepting of the Setite world view. I suspect that this happens to certain degrees among a lot of clans.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-08T12:25:23-07:00","id":1395127,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Setites (introduction)</b><br /> <br /> It's now 1995. This book lists two developers, Andrew Greenberg, whose departure I mentioned when talking about DSotBH, and Jennifer Hartshorn, who had, IIRC, been Wraith developer before being moved to take Greenberg's place. The book is written by Richard Watts, whose name I'm unfamiliar with from much else. Interior art is by Anthony Hightower, whose work has this sort of &quot;if watercolors were in black and white&quot; washed out feel to them. (It's not a bad look, but for some reason it strikes me as a style that would work better for Wraith than most other lines.) Joshua Timbrook is also listed, but it's just an opening page and the tiny bit on the back cover that I suspect were done long before. <br /> So, all of the Independent clans, as originally conceived, were ... I think &quot;problematic&quot; is the current in vogue term. I've long believed that, in spite of being in the Players Guide, they were all meant to be NPC antagonist factions rather than actual PCs. They were all essentially 1930s pulp fiction villain groups made into vampire clans. In the case of the Setites, it's the creepy Egyptian cultists, popular in a lot of old mummy movies, among other places, and seen in the proto-Egyptian kingdom of Stygia in Robert E. Howard's Conan stories and their later pastiches. And one of those pastiches is James Earl Jones as Thulsa Doom in the 1982 Conan the Barbarian movie. However, the character Jones plays is actually much closer to the character of Thoth-Amon, the greatest of all ancient Stygian sorcerers and worshiper of &quot;Father Set&quot;, who some have suggested may be one of Lovecraft's Great Old Ones. The character never meets Conan in any of Howard's works, instead being his enemy in a number of pastiche stories by later writers, especially in the Marvel Comics series from the 70s and 80s. This is probably where a lot of the early Setite influences come from.<br /> (In actual Egyptian mythology, Set was moderately heroic, or at least kind of an antihero, being the super badass warrior of the desert who defended Ra's sail barge from the chaos serpent Apep every night. He was the &quot;red god&quot; of the desert sands in contrast to his brother Osiris, the &quot;black god&quot; of the fertile river lands. Weirdly, a bunch of vampires who idolize Set sounds like it should be a gang of Gangrel who dig on being barbarians of the wastelands and don't have any time to waste with your wimpy civilization.)<br /> Added to the above, Werewolf had come along and introduced the whole Wyrm thing, and I guess someone decided that was now what the Setites were into, now casting Set as a worshiper of Apep and their entire bag being corruption. Why do they corrupt people and institutions and the like? For ... reasons. The clanbook doesn't really give much of a justification, other than an in character account of how totally awesome Set was in both life and after the embrace but none of the other 3rd generation were able to see how cool he was and made fun of him, and one day while he was out at Hot Topic, they killed the 2nd generation, including Set's sire who he totes adored, so he decided to start the Jyhad and use the other Antediluvians' corruption against them. (I'm kind of noticing how all the Independent clans have this &quot;our Founder was an awesome hero who never did anything wrong so we're the bestest clan&quot; thing going on. I think this may be part of why the Giovanni's &quot;no, we really are total assholes and go f--- yourself&quot; take ended up being so refreshing.) So, it's sort of that they're into corruption for corruption's sake, I guess. <br /> <br /> Also, each of these templates has a little Egyptian hieroglyph type image with it, I think meant to represent certain Egyptian gods. I can only really recognize a few of them, and I think they start to repeat at the end, so I've no idea if they were meant to carry any deeper meaning or not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-08T13:30:21-07:00","id":1395136,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Setites (part one)</b><br /> <br /> Our snake worshipers. BTW, the character sheets give an option for Humanity or Path, but none of them specify which Path. In second edition, the options were Typhon (from the 2nd ed Players Guide), Ecstasy, and Warrior (both in this book). I'll try to guess which one. <br /> <br /> <b>The Bent Narc </b><br /> A corrupt cop. Basically, he wanted to become a cop so no one would ever push him around. Instead, he gets to push everyone else around. And because he was perfectly fine with violating the law six ways from Sunday to make arrests, he got promoted to Narcotics, where he could abuse his authority even more. And eventually got embraced. Now he serves the clan by getting rid of criminal rivals and protecting their own drug importations. He doesn't really care about anything concerning the clan's deeper goals or philosophies, just as long as he gets to push people around and feed off of crackheads. He's Physical primary and Social secondary. His highest abilities are three dots each in Melee, Investigation, and Criminology. That last one feels a little redundant. I'm also not sure why his Melee is so high. All his other abilities are twos, including Brawl, Dodge, Firearms, Streetwise, Subterfuge, Survival, Drive, Security and Law. He's got ones in Stealth and Setite Lore. Given how little he seems to care about the clan and its goals, I'm not sure why he has that last one. Disciplines are one dot each in the clan powers. Backgrounds are two each in Allies and Influence, and one in Resources. You'd think drug money payoffs would rate higher. Given how he seems to like to feed off of crackheads, I'm sort of surprised he doesn't have any thing in Herd. I also feel like he might benefit from Contacts or maybe a Retainer/slave of some kind. His Path rating is five. Given the drug thing, and because it's mentioned under that Path, I'm guessing the Path of Ecstasy. Willpower is six. This isn't exactly a terrible character. The overall concept is fairly good, and the stats, while needing a little massaging, aren't that bad. I think I'd just prefer something a little closer to Denzel Washington in <i>Training Day</i> of Michael Chiklis in <i>The Shield</i>. The sort of character who is more proactive and goal oriented. As is, he feels a little more like a ghoul than a vampire. <br /> <br /> <b>The Closet Setite</b><br /> This is basically a Caitiff - this is back in the day when that term confusingly/annoyingly did double duty for both the real clan-less and those who had just been embraced and abandoned - who figured out she was a Setite when she developed the first dot of Serpentis. And now she doesn't know what to do, because everyone had told her that Setites are bad. And that's pretty much it. I'm not kidding. All we get of a backstory is that her living days were &quot;uneventful, ordinary, even boring.&quot; No, what's boring is this barely-an-idea of a character. There's just nothing here. And her character sheet is ... odd. Mental is primary and Social secondary, with an extra dot to bump Charisma up to four. Acting and Subterfuge are four dots each, and Empathy, Etiquette, Survival, and Occult are three each. Was she a Wiccan actress/con artist who moonlighted as a wilderness guide? She has one dot in each discipline. Backgrounds are three in Generation and two in Resources (Set only knows from what). Humanity is eight and Willpower five. This is just terrible. This isn't even a character, it's an idea for an aspect of a character. The character's abilities have no context, and honestly feel like someone just tried to max the ones that would involve pretending not to be a Setite. And I think what irritates me the most is that, when I say it's an idea for an aspect of a character, it's actually a pretty good angle. There just needs to be more to it than that. Maybe she was an ancient history/languages major, or a psychology student, or someone who's always made her way through school by cleverly cheating without getting caught, or the secret descendent of some cult priestess. Anything that would give me something to work with here. (Even better might've been a perfectly happy Setite who is just pretending not to be one for the purpose of shenanigans.)<br /> <br /> <b>The Corrupt Politician</b> <br /> If the Bent Narc was Corruption Archetype #1, then this is #2. The standard glib oily type straight out of central casting, worked his way up from local politics. And now he's a United States Senator. Present tense. As in he reached that office and then was embraced. I'm fairly certain that one of the top five ways to get your name on the Red List. And for said Senator to have a tragic plane crash. Anyway, whatever you imagine corrupt politicians doing, he does it. And much like the Bent Narc, he doesn't really care much about the clan's overall ideals and whatnot as long as he keeps getting to &quot;live&quot; the high life. Socials are primary, with Charisma and Manipulation at four each. Politics is at four also. Law, Bureaucracy, Finance, Etiquette, Leadership, and Subterfuge are threes. Disciplines are, again, one each. Backgrounds include Influence and Resources at four, and Fame, Allies and Contacts at two each. In spite of his equipment listing his staff (all of the ghouls), he has no Retainers. And I'd probably say his Resources and Influence are too low. Path is six. I'm again guessing it's the Path of Ecstasy. Willpower is four. I think if this had been a local politician, it might've been OK. A little cookie cutter, maybe, but still OK. But as a US Senator, no. How the hell do you work this character into a coterie? Or even a chronicle outside of the most specific of DC by Night ones? Sometimes the &quot;cute&quot; idea isn't the best choice. As I said, if you just ignore most of the write up and make him a local big wig, it's OK. <br /> <br /> And I think that's about all I can take for now. Next will be part two.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-08T13:33:35-07:00","id":1395137,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1395127#post1395127\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Added to the above, Werewolf had come along and introduced the whole Wyrm thing, and I guess someone decided that was now what the Setites were into, now casting Set as a worshiper of Apep and their entire bag being corruption.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think it was case that &quot;someone decided that was now...&quot; The Setites were always established as worshiping a &quot;dark god.&quot; They were published in the first edition Player's Guide in 1991 (as was the first edition Werewolf corebook) as you noted earlier. In 1992, the first edition Mummy supplement was published that explained in further detail the (narratively unreliable) background of Egypt in the World of Darkness including the Setites, and we learn there that the Setites &quot;serve Set as well as one of the guises of the Great Wyrm.&quot;<br /> <br /> I don't think this is the case of someone applying some retroactive continuity. I think a lot of things had been worked out in advance, and early White Wolf was slowly dribbling this information out. I think when the clan was created in 1991, they were intended to be servants of the Wyrm. Both Werewolf and Mummy, which utilize the Wyrm, must have also been in the planning stages while the Player's Guide was being written as well. I'm pretty sure when the World of Darkness was conceived as five different games, some thought had been given on the generic setting and how certain elements were going to play out in all of them before Vampire was even published. So this is not a new thing. It's been there from the beginning.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1395127#post1395127\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why do they corrupt people and institutions and the like? For ... reasons. The clanbook doesn't really give much of a justification...</div> </div> </div> </div>This was a problem in early World of Darkness. PENTEX and many other Werewolf antagonists associated with the Wyrm were equally ludicrous in their motivations. <br /> <br /> It's a problem with a lot of media in general in depicting evil. For example, the temptation of Luke Skywalker in Return of the Jedi never made much sense either. It's just a vague notion of the culture that if someone does something evil, then they go all out and become completely evil in everything. It's the same reason why in many movies when they show the antagonist, it's insufficient to simply make him a rival or personal enemy to the protagonist, they have to show him kicking puppies, using racist language, cheating at cards, and other offensive actions. It is lazy, but easy shorthand that &quot;this is the bad guy, don't root for him. boo! hiss! root for our hero instead, he loves puppies and would never kick one.&quot;<br /> <br /> I think a major reason for this is the desire to not glamorize or excuse evil. Many people feel that if you try to explain why someone did something morally wrong that you are justifying it. So things are often dumbed down for the target audience.<br /> <br /> The Wyrm wants to corrupt everything because that is how you destroy things. His followers do that because they gain power by favor of the Wyrm (in theory) if they do what it wants. In practice, the Setites need to have some justification that what they are doing is great because in reality most people need some level of justification for their actions for their own minds, as well as something to feed to outsiders. You'll always have sociopaths and thugs willing to do anything just because, but long term people need some kind of ideological justification. This takes more verbal gymnastics, but it's not particularly hard. Self-serving rhetoric has a very long history. You still have people today spouting Communist nonsense because they're unable to look past the propaganda line to what they actually did. (In Vampire this becomes even more important because the benefit of any Path is that it allows you to control the Beast so that you don't descend into an animal mindset).<br /> <br /> Eventually the game would begin to envision the various servants of the Wyrm having more reasonable motivations and more effective propaganda. But things were very crude early on.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1395127#post1395127\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is probably where a lot of the early Setite influences come from.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is very obvious that Robert E. Howard's work was an inspiration for certain parts of the World of Darkness. Besides the Set motif, the corruption of the Picts and the transformation of the White Howlers into the Black Spiral Dancers is obviously inspired by Howard's Bran Mak Morn stories. Even in Vampire, the hunter Sullivan Dane sounds very similar to Howard's Puritan witch hunter Solomon Kane. Somebody really liked their Howard. Don't know who, but I wouldn't be surprised if it was Mark Rein Hagen.<br /> <br /> Like you, I imagine the four independent clans as being more appropriate as NPC antagonists. That doesn't mean it isn't possible to run a game with PCs as characters of those clans. It's just that they are going to be played very differently than the &quot;intended&quot; clans.<br /> <br /> As for the contrast between Vampire Set and Mythological Set, the game did need to do a better job. As you stated, for a long time Set was an honored god among the Egyptians. And he performed various mythological acts that were heroic (like defend Ra from Apep when the solar barque entered the Underworld). The god didn't start being demoted in Egypt until after the Hyksos Invasion when foreigners ruled Egypt, and since Set was considered to be the god of foreigners, he became discredited. But that kind of knowledge isn't popularly known, and certainly wouldn't be expected of the game's main fanbase. But if one does know it, you have to start making up head canon as to why. And head canon can go into many different directions. Besides my own version, I've heard several other great explanations. But the problem is that we're all exclusionary to each other, so we start having very different conceptions of the clan and the kind of vampire Set is. The key is to remember the clan isn't about the actual Egyptian god named Set who isn't tied to snakes, it's about Howard's Set which is envisioned as a huge serpent. If one goes the Egyptian route (as opposed to it simply being a veneer), you're going to get the clan and &quot;Set&quot; wrong.<br /> <br /> The challenge for the first clan book was how well they could blend the various elements together. They needed to both be true to the Howardiana inspiration, show them clearly as a Wyrm cult, yet show how their propaganda both inside and outside the clan tries to conceal that. If I remember correctly, it fails at that though there are some useful things to be found.<br /> <br /> Presenting things from an IC point of view has its advantages. But I thought too many of the World of Darkness sourcebooks did that. I think it's better to have a combination of IC perspectives and OOC/Game perspective in order to make certain things clear to the reader. This is less important for the PC side of things than it is for the ST side. The problem with the clanbooks is that they are used by BOTH PCs and STs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-08T14:03:07-07:00","id":1395142,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1394870#post1394870\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Most of the ones from the original seven books were designed to be used in any Camarilla city, though a few are more specific, such as the Brujah Fake Rapper being specifically designed for the greater LA/Anarch Free States.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I know. I meant where to place them basing on their background and picture. That's why I placed Holy Warrior in Middle East. I was thinking like that: every city has its own atmosphere and Cainites. Some of the templates remember (or even have contact) with their Sires. Based on that and their Generation I tried to find where they come from but with little success. So I was wondering where they will fit. And p. ex. I don't agree with you that Fake Rapper is designed for Free States. I don't know why I would rather place him in the Vancouver area. But just by the hunch.<br /> <br /> I think that most abilities that you don't agree with can be explained. For example Assamite Scholar: Alertness 3 to spot changes of blood cells during the experiments, Medicine 5 to interpret it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":13141,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","user":"Nail Eater","date":". 06-08-2020, 05:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-08T14:19:29-07:00","id":1395149,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1394721#post1394721\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>The Professional</b><br /> A professional wet-worker, and judging from the art, one at least somewhat inspired by the 1990 French film <i>La Femme Nikita</i> (which was remade as Hong Kong's 1991 <i>Black Cat</i> and America's 1993 <i>Point of No Return</i>, and inspired two TV series).</div> </div> </div> </div>I recall thinking at the time the book came out that this template was almost certainly based on <a href=\"https://i.pinimg.com/originals/7e/cf/14/7ecf14bcbfc065e6e627279618bfd65d.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Lena Olin's hit woman character from <i>Romeo Is Bleeding</i></a>.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-08T14:48:44-07:00","id":1395154,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1395136#post1395136\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Clanbook: Setites (part one)</b><br /> <br /> How the hell do you work this character into a coterie? Or even a chronicle outside of the most specific of DC by Night ones?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's easy: make this coterie of him, Tremere Politico and Gangrel Lobbyst (from Clanbook: Gangrel Revised Edition) \ud83d\ude1c.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-09T20:33:10-07:00","id":1395514,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Setites (part two)</b><br /> <br /> More snakes. (Why did it have to be snakes?)<br /> <br /> <b>The Dilettante</b><br /> A big time celebrity sex symbol. I'm pretty sure this was inspired by Madonna as she was in the late 80s and early 90s, save that this is an actress who tried to break into music and bombed badly rather than the other way around. Anyway, she had the full hedonistic lifestyle, with drugs, assorted boy toys, jet setting, etc. A Setite apparently selected her for the embrace with plans to use her as the focus for a cross branded promotion of film, music, and related merchandizing in order to spread corruption and evil. What kind of corruption? The corrupting kind, apparently. Yes, it's very vague in the way that the entire book is, with corruption for corruption's sake. (I do kind of get the general idea, and in hindsight, I find it interesting that it predicts the '00's issues of media culture selling young girls on the idea of being hyper sexualized.) Socials are primary, with Charisma and Appearance at four each. Mental is secondary, with Intelligence and Wits at three. Abilities are three each in Etiquette and Music, and two each in Finance and Acting. And that's it. OK, not really. But all the rest are Secondary abilities. Carousing, Poetic Expression, Seduction, Singing, Style, and Setite Knowledge, all at three or higher except Poetic Expression. While I at least applaud going all in on the secondary abilities one picks, I think this is a bit much. I'm not really sure why she has Poetic Expression. In this case, I'm pretty sure it's meant for songwriting, and this is the sort of singer who would hire people for that. I'm not sure why her Setite Knowledge is at three dots either. She's far more into hedonism than anything about the clan's inner workings, and I'll point out that a later template is a total hardcore priest of Set who's rating in the ability is also three dots. I honestly think that you could drop the Carousing, Poetic Expression, and most if not all of the SK and use those dots for things like Intimidation, Subterfuge, or raising Acting. (You could probably drop Singing as well and raise Music to four and take Singing as a specialty.) Disciplines are three dots in Presence and one dot in Serpentis. I don't think she really needs Serpentis. But every single one of these templates has at least one dot in it, because apparently much like the Assamites, if the clan has a Special Sauce, everyone has to have it, just because. Backgrounds include two in Contacts, four in Fame, and five in Resources. This is one of the few times I've seen a five in any background, but it makes sense with this character. Given how the write up implies her current activities are part of some master plan on the part of her sire, I'm surprised she doesn't have Mentor. Or a Herd of groupies and entourage of Retainers. (Those seven dots that went toward that extra discipline dot could've covered all of that.) Path is six, and I'm guessing it's pretty obviously supposed to be the Path of Ecstasy. Willpower is four. In spite of its numerous trait problems and points that could've been better spent, I actually like the character concept quite a bit. While the generic corruption for corruption's sake angle is a bit weak, the over all idea is very sound, and even works really well with the revised presentation of the clan. So over all, I have to call this a win. <br /> <br /> <b>The Egyptologist</b><br /> So, I guess it would be inevitable that a clan so heavily tied to Egypt would have a template based on the scholarly study of that land's ancient history. This man was a devoted scholar and archaeologist, who apparently uncovered a lost tomb and was embraced by the Setite who was slumbering within. I confess to being somewhat amused by how much that feels like a classic mummy movie. (However, I should point out that this character is 13th generation. You'd sort of expect a concept and origin like that to result in at least a few dots in the Generation background.) He mainly serves the clan via helping move entorpored elders around in their sarcophagi by listing them as antiques. Meanwhile, he's really only interested in the clan and its lore in so far as what it can teach him about ancient Egypt, a subject he's fairly obsessed with. He's Mental primary and Physical secondary, with Stamina at four. His Manipulation is three, leaving his Charisma at one, which seems a little off. This is another character with a bunch of secondary abilities, and again going all in on them. Archaeology, Research, Search, and Setite Knowledge at three, and History at four dots. Linguistics and Survival are also three dots each, with Bureaucracy, Investigation, and Drive at two. I think this is another instance where you could trim some of the secondary ones, maybe by dropping Research and raising Investigation to four dots with the Research specialty, and dropping Search for a few dots in Alertness. Disciplines are one dot in Obfuscate and two in Serpentis. Backgrounds are three dots in Mentor, two each in Status and Allies, and one dot in Fame. As I mentioned, I think this concept could've benefited from several dots in Generation, because I'm not really sure how many 12th generation vampires were roaming around 1700 BC, but I somehow suspect those that were around were frequently referred to as &quot;snacks&quot;. Path rating is five, and I think this is probably our first Path of Typhon (what was the &quot;main&quot; Setite path back in 2nd ed). Willpower is also five. I understand why this character exists. I'd probably have been shocked if something like it hadn't been included. But as he is, he sort of feels more like the sort of character that should be a ghoul rather than a vampire. An Egyptology fanatic whose thirst for knowledge is being used to take advantage of his ability to move sleeping elders and other ancient treasures around the modern world. Maybe a more interesting take would be this character with the added angle that he's now bound and determined to &quot;correct&quot; the errors and heresies of the historical record and return the Setites and Set to their proper glory in the academic arena. <br /> <br /> <b>The Exotic Dancer</b><br /> And, again, I think this might've been inevitable. Curiously, it actually predates the film <i>From Dusk Til Dawn</i> by a year. So this is something along the lines of the Poor Little Rich Girl archetype, or at least probably Poor Little Upper Middle Class in this case. Apparently never wanting for anything, except for parental love and/or attention. So she started acting out at a young age, breaking things, hurting pets and other children, and even herself (I pick up a bit of a sociopath vibe here). As a teen, this turned to heavy drinking, drugs, casual sex, and the like, until she left home and became a prostitute. And then got embraced, apparently by a Setite who'd been following her long decline. Now she's a stripper, and not the classy kind at &quot;gentlemen's clubs&quot; either. More like sleazy dives, where she likes to seduce and &quot;corrupt&quot; (there's that word again) and eventually hurt the desperate and needy. She has a ghoul pet snake as part of her act, because of course she does. Her Nature is actually Masochist, so apparently she's still inherently self destructive. Social is primary, with Manipulation and Appearance both at 4. Physical is secondary, with Dexterity also at four at the expense of Strength being one. She's another with a bunch of secondary abilities. Athletics, Streetwise, and Stealth are all three dots, with Brawl, Dodge and Melee at twos. Dancing is at four dots. Given that Athletics are already so high, I don't know why you wouldn't just get the additional dot and take Dancing as a specialty. Seduction is three. Also at three is City Secrets. I never liked that ability, as it always felt like something that should be specific things a player should write down on their character sheet rather than an ability. She also has one dot in Camarilla Lore. I'm not sure why. Nothing about the write up says anything about her being a member or affiliated with them. (If anything, she feels like she'd fit in with a gang of Anarchs.) Disciplines are back to the one each plus one formula, this time with the extra dot in Presence. Backgrounds are three dots of Herd, and one each of Contacts and Fame. Path is five, and I'm guessing we're back to the Path of Ecstasy. Willpower is a whopping eight. I'm not sure how I feel about this character. On the one hand, it feels a little cheesy, in that sort of &quot;look how edgy and mature we are&quot; way I find a little cringe inducing (mainly I suspect because I remember how I used to indulge in it way back when). On the other hand, I do find the character's psychology and backstory somewhat interesting (if more than a little depressing), especially in light of what the revised version of this book had to say about how the clan will deliberately try to tear someone down prior to the embrace. It makes me wonder if this was some long term project the sire started when she was just a child, which is kind of disturbing. I think with some fine tuning, this could probably be a fairly strong character. <br /> <br /> <b>The Gangsta</b><br /> Sweet Christmas, where to start .... It's your stereotypical '90s black ghetto street thug. And that's it. I almost don't want to do this one. His quote is &quot;Mutha-fucker!&quot; He was a drug dealer. He tried to rip off a shipment that was owned by the Setites and got embraced for his efforts. (I guess it was a set up of some sort.) Now he's ... a drug dealer. It says he has contacts in DC, the CIA and the Mafia. He has no Contacts background. He used to do steroids and work out a lot. Roleplaying hints include &quot;quote Ice T and Body Count at every available opportunity.&quot; I'm going to take a wild guess here that Richard Watts is white and in 1995 knew little or nothing about gangsta rap or hip hop. I'm a little surprised the character's equipment includes an UZI and not a TEC-9 or AK-47. Also that it doesn't include some crack, a 40 of malt liquor, and some &quot;ho's&quot;. Nature and Demeanor are both Bravo, so he's exactly what he appears to be. Physicals are primary, with Strength maxed out to five dots. Socials are secondary, with Charisma and Manipulation both at two and Appearance at four. I guess he's the world's best looking crack dealer. Intelligence is one. I'm really not even sure what to say about that. Abilities include four dots in Streetwise, and another four in Area Knowledge, which feels redundant. He's got three dots each in Brawl, Intimidation, Firearms, and Melee. And two each in Alertness and Leadership. Disciplines are the one each plus one, this time in Presence. Backgrounds are three in Herd and two in Resources. I mentioned the Contacts thing already, but his equipment does list ghoul bodyguards, and he has no Retainers. No Allies that might be his gang either. Path rating is five, and I'm guessing he's supposed to be the Path of the Warrior. Willpower is also five. I don't really like this character on a number of levels. While I'm aware that this sort of individual exists in real life (I've actually met more than a few of them in my life), it feels like it's being presented as a racial stereotype, possibly just because the Setites are the clan most associate with Africa and black people. And if you compare and contrast with the Brujah Rapper, it definitely comes off as the worse of the two. Plus I just have a hard time buying the idea that a Setite drug gang looked at this guy and said, &quot;<i>this</i> is who we need to be our liaison with George Bush and his CIA cocaine importers.&quot;<br /> <br /> That's all for now. Next will be the final part of Setites.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-10T00:17:53-07:00","id":1395536,"title":"","content":"I've always liked the Followers of Set and they remain one of my all-time favorite clans along with the Old Clan Tzimisce, Brujah, and Malkavians. I've always felt that the original write-up kind of pigeon-holed them with the view that not only did they have to stick with being the &quot;obviously and deliberately evil&quot; ones but that they somehow managed to avoid any real breakaway sects over the millennia.<br /> <br /> I always felt the Followers of Set are one of the few groups that shoulds have vampires trying to be &quot;good&quot; because while they don't have to be, it's a clan built on people going out of their way to be evil by society's standards. It's why I feel like the Clanbooks failed to properly incorporate the Serpents of Light. The Serpents of Light are evil muthersuckers, don't get me wrong, but they believe themselves to be good despite being in the Sabbat. Because it takes a lot of mental gymnastics to adopt an ideology of war against reality.<br /> <br /> I also felt like the Children of Osiris were something that could be properly redeemed into mainstream vampire-lore by someone like <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/152-the-gentleman-gamer\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar152_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"152\" data-vbnamecard=\"152\">The Gentleman Gamer</a> just by the fact a vampire-hunting cult of religious fanatics is exactly the sort of group that might be produced by the Setites pushing their Neonates too hard. Mind you, I don't think this is spelled out in canon but I think it's also been hinted that the Children of Osiris were created by Kemintiri. They were her weapon against the mainstream Followers of Set and just had a good discipline for staving off the Beast.<br /> <br /> Part of why I like the Ministry update to the FoS is that it seems to be doing that in broader terms. Damballa isn't evil so the Followers of Set embracing a bunch of them and then trying to pass Set off as him resulted in a bunch of worshipers who want nothing to do with them. Worshipers of Eris may love Chaos but they're not going to subordinate their goddess to Egyptian mythology and so on. You can be evil and chaotic without believing that you should be EVIL in the name of CHAOS. The Church of Set in Cults of the Blood Gods amused me because I think Set would be less than pleased with an orthodox fundamentalist branch of his Chaos religion. Someone is doing something wrong.<br /> <br /> One thing I'd love to speculate on as a Medieval historian and student of comparative religion is whether the Church of Caine began as a Followers of Set blood cult then went off into its own thing. I could easily see the Followers of Set planning to corrupt Kindred wiht the whole, &quot;Caine is just a name for Set&quot; thing only to unwittingly tap into vampire mythology that went off on its own thing. After all, Setite Gnosticism is very similar to Church of Caine gnosticism with the exception of their messiahs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-10T00:30:54-07:00","id":1395539,"title":"","content":"1. <b>The Dilettante</b> is a character I think that would be more interesting on Humanity personally. I don't think the character as described would have the mental fortitude to do a Path of Enlightenment (also, the Path of Cathari and the Path of Ectasy seem redundant or related--I'm not sure which). I understand why the Setites use sex, drugs, and rock and roll to corrupt people. History is full of people being persecuted for &quot;fun&quot; things that are harmless. It's part of the Setites ideology that all religion and law are based on keeping your true potential down. However, those are the keys not the end goal. A true Path of Ectasy follower is going to be way-way-way beyond partying as a lifestyle.<br /> <br /> I think a more interesting way to play this character might also be Lindsay Lohan versus Madonna. The Setites embraced her, attempted to corrupt her spirit, and then kind of &quot;broke&quot; her. She's happy to keep spreading the message but instead of becoming an enlightened demiurge, is kind of just an addict and waste. I always like the idea of characters who aren't necessarily success stories for their clan.<br /> <br /> 2. <b>The Egyptologist</b> seems like a character that is being vastly underused by the Followers of Set. They of all people would probably have them trying to recover as many artifacts of the past and mainstream Egyptian mythology as anyone. As you say, moving around Elders is grunt work for a ghoul. I think this character would be a very amusing version of Belloq as they spend their days hunting down collectors of Setite relics and getting them back while also selling fine forgeries or inducting people into the cult. I don't think Setites necessarily mind people taking hold of their relics if they think it will convert them--they will take them back eventually after all. Also, I could easily see him as a guy who sells fraudulent relics while working to recover the real thing.<br /> Mind you, I was always a fan of Lovejoy and the fine art of &quot;antiqueing&quot; he occassionally engaged in.<br /> <br /> 3. <b>Exotic Dancer</b>: I dunno, sometimes a backstory's simplicity isn't a bad thing. A lot of these Archetypes don't actually fit into an existing gaming group without great difficulty. In Assamite Revised, there's a guy who spends all of his nights on the road traveling from city to city. That is not going to work with an existing coterie. A high-class Egyptian themed stripper isn't a bad concept by itself and can join any group without problem. Also, being plucked from a sleazy life of poverty and drug addiction before being made into a dark goddess isn't a bad premise. The Setites often have fanatical loyalty from their followers because quite a few went through hell and are grateful for the second chance.<br /> <br /> Satine Phoenix's character on LA by Night runs a brothel and is still one of my favorite characters of the game.<br /> <br /> 4. <b>The Gangsta</b>: Ugh, yes, I think this character is greatly lacking in irony. Sort of like how someone said, &quot;Theodore Dooley is how a black reader thinks a black character turned into a vampire would be, Theo Bell is how white people think how a black character turned into a vampire would be.&quot; Oddly, if I were to rewrite him, I would have the vampire originally be a lonely put upon nerd [perhaps with an interest in Egyptology] who once Embraced has started to live out his fantasies of being an incredible badass. Maybe even has High Obfuscate to appear to be big and intimidating. The Setites are not sure how to react. He's technically following the creed but so embarrassingly.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-10T19:15:53-07:00","id":1395723,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1395539#post1395539\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. <b>The Dilettante</b> is a character I think that would be more interesting on Humanity personally. I don't think the character as described would have the mental fortitude to do a Path of Enlightenment (also, the Path of Cathari and the Path of Ectasy seem redundant or related--I'm not sure which).</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, this was 2nd ed, when all it took to get on a Path was to get hit on the head with a shovel.<br /> <br /> I think that's one thing I preferred about Revised was that it made Paths into these seriously hardcore cults that required a lot of dedication and effort to devote oneself to.<br /> <br /> Although in reexamining the original Sabbat book, I do kind of like the (slightly modified) idea that every pack is, essentially, it's own little mini-cult who all follow the same Path under the spiritual direction of a pack priest who is the one with the highest Path rating. So everyone in Pack A follow the Path of Harmony and are a rather different manifestation of the Sabbat and its ideals than Pack B which all follow the Path of Caine and Pack C who all follow the Path of Death and the Soul.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3. <b>Exotic Dancer</b>: I dunno, sometimes a backstory's simplicity isn't a bad thing. A lot of these Archetypes don't actually fit into an existing gaming group without great difficulty. In Assamite Revised, there's a guy who spends all of his nights on the road traveling from city to city. That is not going to work with an existing coterie. </div> </div> </div>I actually like the character and her backstory works fine. I think my main issues is the one I have with the book overall: That they are all engaged in Corruption simply because. Or, if you buy the origin story in the history section, they do it because the other Antediluvians were big meanies and so Set turned their corruption against them to get back at them. I think that's why I much prefer the revised book, as it actually bothered to try to develop a theology for them as a sort of Dionysian (in the Nietzschean sense) antithesis to Aeonic morality. (It's something that in my own games, I'd worked out to involve not only original Egyptian concepts of Isfet vs Maat, but also Zoroastrianism concepts of druj/falsehood vs asha/truth, Luciferian Satanism, certain Greek philosophies, and Nietzsche's overman.) In the case of the Exotic Dancer, I think even the idea of her preferring to wallow in degradation is perfectly fine. I think there's just some small detail missing that would elevate the character from pretty good to great. I'm not entirely sure what that is, though.<br /> <br /> (I have some recollection of Clayton Oliver saying at the time that the courier character from Assamite Revised was specifically designed with the idea of being part of a nomadic coterie. I rather wish Masquerade had gotten a Nomads book akin to what Requiem had.)<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4. <b>The Gangsta</b>: Ugh, yes, I think this character is greatly lacking in irony. Sort of like how someone said, &quot;Theodore Dooley is how a black reader thinks a black character turned into a vampire would be, Theo Bell is how white people think how a black character turned into a vampire would be.&quot; Oddly, if I were to rewrite him, I would have the vampire originally be a lonely put upon nerd [perhaps with an interest in Egyptology] who once Embraced has started to live out his fantasies of being an incredible badass. Maybe even has High Obfuscate to appear to be big and intimidating. The Setites are not sure how to react. He's technically following the creed but so embarrassingly. </div> </div> </div>I think what I really would have liked to see here is the vampire equivalent to Wesley Snipes's character from New Jack City.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 06-10-2020, 10:19 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-10T19:34:58-07:00","id":1395731,"title":"","content":"Or Stringer Bell from the Wire.<br /> <br /> He strikes me as the kind of guy who the Setites would Embrace. Mind you, I believe that some developers have commented that's essentially who Kevin Jackson evolved into being.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-10T21:04:04-07:00","id":1395753,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Setites (part three)</b><br /> <br /> The final three.<br /> <br /> <b>The Priest of Darkness</b><br /> &quot;A mad eyed servant of absolute evil.&quot; That's literally the entire concept as written in the character write up. (I think this just made me realize what it is that irritates me so much about the whole &quot;We are EVIL - muhahaha&quot; presentation of the clan in this book: That's the fricking Baali. It makes one of the two groups redundant.) Anyway, we do at least get some backstory. A teen metalhead turned Satanist whose apparent dedication got him an invite to a Setite temple. Several years of ghouldom later, he graduated to full vampire and priest of the dark god. Now he spends his nights laughing insanely, rubbing his hands together, brooding, and waxing poetically about how awesome Set is. Seriously, that is what's listed under roleplaying hints. Socials are primary, with Manipulation maxed out to five, and Charisma at three. Mental is secondary, with Perception and Intelligence at three each. Intimidation, Leadership, Stealth, and Occult are all at three dots, as is Setite Knowledge. And he's got a few other secondary abilities. Camarilla Lore is at one. I don't know why he needs this. He also has one dot in Sense Deception. Again, I don't really see the need. And he has Public Speaking at two dots. I guess for leading temple services, though I'd think maybe Leadership could cover for that. I also find it a little odd that a priest of Set has no Subterfuge ability. You'd think that would be part of the basic curriculum. Disciplines are the one dot each, but this time with two extra dots, one in Presence and one in Serpentis. Just as a personal thing, I think you could drop the Obfuscate dot and either bump one of the others up to three or use the points for something else. Background is one in Generation, two in Herd and two in Retainers. I would've expected maybe at least one dot in Clan Status. Maybe Mentor as well. Path is five, and I'm pretty sure we can assume this is the Path of Typhon. Willpower is six. The concept of hardcore true believer Priest of Set is a perfectly solid one and potentially a good one. This does come anywhere near that potential. In fact it feels like it may have less thought put into it than the Gangsta. I would really enjoy seeing this concept done properly, because this interpretation of it is almost laughable. <br /> <br /> <b>Sister Sin</b> (The Sister of Sin?)<br /> A nun who apparently came from a family of career criminals, and who had a drinking problem. Apparently she kept stealing money from the poor box to cover her drinking. And this was enough to attract the attention of the Setites. I will pause here to note that this is one of those things were I think a little more effort or detail in the back story might strengthen things over all. Say, if her ruin was her sire's pet project from the moment she decided to take up holy orders. (I think this may be what I felt the Exotic Dancer was missing too; If, for example, the psychologist her parents sent her to as a child had ended up being her sire.) Anyway, now she's in charge of the orphanage, getting to corrupt all the little children. And no, not that way (thankfully). She's basically a modern day <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fagin\" target=\"_blank\">Fagin</a>, training the kids to steal and commit other crimes. Socials are primary, with four each in Charisma and Manipulation. Mental is secondary, with a 3/2/3 spread. And holy crap does she have a bunch of secondary abilities. At three dots each, we have Streetwise, Forgery, Lockpicking, Pickpocket, and Theology. At two each we have Empathy, Leadership, Security, Medicine, Instruction, and Bribery. I remember with the original Tremere template at the start saying that it felt like someone had just grabbed every &quot;magic&quot; ability on the list and given it to him. Well, this is the same with all the &quot;crime&quot; abilities. (I am so glad Dark Ages came up with the Larceny ability.) I honestly think Bribery, Forgery and Lockpicking can be done away with (and Security maybe raised to three or four, along with some points in Subterfuge). And as much as it's a stereotype, I'm a little surprised at the idea of a nun without Intimidation. Disciplines are, again, the one each plus one. This time it's Presence. If I had to drop one, it'd probably be the dot in Serpentis. Backgrounds include five dots in Retainers (the orphans, I presume), four in Herd (the same), and one in Allies. I feel like there should maybe be at least one dot of criminal Contacts. Maybe also Resources from all the illicit money her little crime ring brings in. She's actually a Humanity case, at five, with Willpower of seven. I like this character a lot. The modern day Fagin is a great vampire concept, and the idea of it being the nun in charge of an orphanage works really well as a method of undermining a traditional symbol of morality and virtue (and thankfully not in a way that involves sexual depravity). The problems with the point allocations are easily fixed. I think this is probably my favorite one of the book. <br /> <br /> <b>The Voodoo Houngan </b><br /> So, originally from Haiti - and this appears to draw on certain stuff about the country from the first edition <i>A World of Darkness</i> sourcebook, which I've not read in ages - and a junior initiate into the priesthood. Apparently, he was jilted or romantically betrayed by someone, and ended up going to one of the &quot;red&quot; sects in the hills for vengeance. The Setite who led the sect apparently decided he'd make a suitable Childe and embraced him. Eventually, he moved to Miami to run the clan's drug and corruption business there. So, apparently he's as much drug kingpin/crime lord as he is houngan. Socials are primary with Manipulation maxed out to five, and Charisma at three. Physicals are secondary, with Dexterity at four dots. I somehow feel Mental should be secondary or even primary with this character. Alertness, Leadership, Subterfuge, Survival, Occult and Politics are at three dots. Empathy, Streetwise, Stealth and Linguistics are two. Investigation is one. Curiously, he has no combat abilities. Disciplines are again one each, and this time with two extra going into Presence and Serpentis. I'm a little surprised one of them didn't go into Thaumaturgy. Backgrounds are two dots in Generation, three in Herd, and one in Contacts. Given the way he's described as being set up in Miami, I'm surprised at the lack of Retainers, Resources, Clan Status, and possibly Influence. He has Humanity at five, which again surprises me a little given how devoted he is said to be to Set and &quot;the Darkness&quot;. Willpower is also five. The character is not bad. I rather like the back story and the idea of one's broken/betrayed heart putting them on a path of vengeance and eventual damnation is a pretty interesting one. I kind of wish the drug angle had been left out, with more focus on him just being a figure of supernatural fear and terror within the immigrant neighborhood(s) he reigns over. And I think that seven points spent on one of the discipline dots could've been better spent elsewhere. But other than that, it's not bad. <br /> <br /> And that's it. This is kind of a mixed bag. The drug war thing is a little omnipresent, with three characters being involved in it (and two others heavy consumers, at least when alive). The Sister Sin nun is my favorite of the entire bunch, followed by the Dilettante and probably the Exotic Dancer and Voodoo Houngan (in spite of some of their flaws). Most of the others are OK, but kind of weak in various areas. The Gangsta and Priest of Darkness are probably the weakest of the lot, lacking much thought and being one dimensional stereotypes. And the Closet Setite is entirely based around the idea that the Setites are apparently supposed to be seen as the equivalent of the Baali or something. <br /> Representation wise, four of the ten are women. Three of them are clearly black - the Closet Setite, the Gangsta and the Voodoo Houngan - and the rest seem to be white. Given how the Setites were frequently presented as &quot;the African clan&quot;, that's certainly note worthy on some level. I'm a little surprised none of them appear to be Egyptian/Middle Eastern. <br /> I'm also a little surprised the nun is the only Christian religious figure. You'd have expected this parade of &quot;Gen X corruption's greatest hits&quot; to have included a televangelist. Also a little surprised that, given the whole snake angle, there's no snake handling holy roller type. No crooked accountant or Wall Street inside trader either. But I think the one I'm really kind of surprised not to see is some sort of sleazy porn producer along the lines of Larry Flint. <br /> The theme this book was going for was corruption, and all of the characters play in to that. Though personally I really prefer my Setites more along the lines of dangerous Dionysian rebels and Byronic antiheroes. But that's just me. <br /> <br /> Anyway, that's Clanbook: Setites. Next is, I believe, the Tzimisce. However, it'll probably be a few days before I'm able to get around to it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-10T22:33:47-07:00","id":1395766,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1395723#post1395723\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Although in reexamining the original Sabbat book, I do kind of like the (slightly modified) idea that every pack is, essentially, it's own little mini-cult who all follow the same Path under the spiritual direction of a pack priest who is the one with the highest Path rating. So everyone in Pack A follow the Path of Harmony and are a rather different manifestation of the Sabbat and its ideals than Pack B which all follow the Path of Caine and Pack C who all follow the Path of Death and the Soul.</div> </div> </div> </div>I had the same thought. But that tends to be undermined in games because the Paths of Enlightenment are a way to distinguish one's character, and therefore it was uncommon for a PC pack to actually be of the same Path of Enlightenment.<br /> <br /> However, since I did not run or play in any Sabbat chronicles (though I knew people who did), that was not a problem for me. So in the chronicles that I ST'd, this was a (background) element. The pack leader (ductus) lead the pack, and the pack priest was responsible for the pack's &quot;spiritual needs&quot;. However, since the pack priests reported to the episcopal hierarchy of the Sabbat (while pack leaders did not), pack priests were essentially Communist style military commissars who tried to control packs on behalf of their centralizing &quot;ultra-conservatives&quot; of the bishops and archbishops. So these two NPC roles were often at each other's throats as they struggled for power in many packs. This was more common in landed covens of bishop ruled cities than in nomadic packs which were more of the Loyalist (anarch) variety. This was because nomadic packs were much more endangered, so there was more importance placed on the leadership ability of the ductus, the tendency of people in danger to bond (thus disrupting the Communist like loyalty of the priests to the bishops and bonding with their packmates), and the not infrequent convenient destruction of pack priests who weren't loyal to their packmates.<br /> <br /> The comparison of pack priests to Soviet style commissars was first made to me by the old NYBN Vampire chronicle website by &quot;the Great Quail&quot;. It was one of the best vampire websites out there in the early days of the internet, but is unfortunately gone now.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-10T23:15:26-07:00","id":1395769,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1395723#post1395723\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think my main issues is the one I have with the book overall: That they are all engaged in Corruption simply because. Or, if you buy the origin story in the history section, they do it because the other Antediluvians were big meanies and so Set turned their corruption against them to get back at them. I think that's why I much prefer the revised book, as it actually bothered to try to develop a theology for them as a sort of Dionysian (in the Nietzschean sense) antithesis to Aeonic morality. (It's something that in my own games, I'd worked out to involve not only original Egyptian concepts of Isfet vs Maat, but also Zoroastrianism concepts of druj/falsehood vs asha/truth, Luciferian Satanism, certain Greek philosophies, and Nietzsche's overman.) In the case of the Exotic Dancer, I think even the idea of her preferring to wallow in degradation is perfectly fine. I think there's just some small detail missing that would elevate the character from pretty good to great. I'm not entirely sure what that is, though.</div> </div> </div> </div>I definitely agree that the Setites need some kind of ideological justification. Revised did put in more effort to do that which is good. However, I always thought that worked best as a cover story for the rubes. Setites spread &quot;corruption&quot; because that is how you destroy individuals and societies. They do it to weaken their enemies so they can seize power and ultimately take control. In essence, I view the Setites as being pimps and drug pushers. They are pimps and drug pushers with a grandiose sense of self. And as one works her way up the cult's hierarchy, they act less like low level pimps and drug pushers into the equivalent of caporegimes and godfathers. But as Don Corleone once said of a head of a rival crime family, &quot;Tattaglia's a pimp.&quot; So some things never change.<br /> <br /> I've been wary of the idea that since Setites push corruption on others, that it means their members need to be corrupt in those ways as well. After all, what crime boss wants their own dealers to be using their own product? It hurts the profit margins and leads to your underlings making dumb decisions. Ideally, the people you have selling the drugs should not be doing any drugs themselves.<br /> <br /> I've mentioned before that I essentially see the Setites as being a cult, and as such they only embrace mortal members of that cult - although of course their membership is in front organizations that reflect some of the Setite teachings as opposed to the &quot;real&quot; cult and its teachings which have to be hidden for obvious reasons. There are various layers of the onion, and only those &quot;members&quot; (and not all front organizations are obviously religiously inspired, there are many mundane ones like criminal gangs) found &quot;worthy&quot; are inducted into the next layer. And only that layer's &quot;worthies&quot; get inducted into the next layer and so on. Until they have a layer of mortals ready for potential embrace. I think this way because why would the Setites ever embrace someone not already part of their religion? Too much can go wrong by embracing someone who may have ideas on their own. And if so, you've wasted time, resources, and vitae on a bad investment. Even people you think fit into the clan's culture can go wrong (I like CTPhipps comment on the Dilettante that she works best as someone broken by the clan instead of being a success story), so it's best to minimize it.<br /> <br /> Setites need people who are part of that demimonde, twilight world of corruption so they have people who are capable of exploiting others. But not enough that they become corrupt themselves. It's a matter of distinguishing between the rubes (the people who buy the cult's BS - and that includes most of its own members among mortals), and the people who &quot;get&quot; it but go along with the exploitation because its part of their own path towards power. And the Setites can offer power to their members - first the embrace, then support from the clan's cultlike hierarchy, then by access to certain powers - especially clan specific blood magic, and lastly by that hoary vampire power source - knowledge. At high up in the clan, the elders likely have very powerful blood sorcery and other weird powers granted to them by the Wyrm, or whatever figurehead they use in place of it. That gives them things like demonic investments or ability to create fomori (like those &quot;vampire&quot; snakes in Berlin By Night).<br /> <br /> Eventually you can move up from a slaver to a warlord and finally to James Earl Jones' Thulsa Doom. So it's not a bad racket if you're good at it.<br /> <br /> Conflict within the clan comes from rival power groups within the cult (which in the case of Serpents of the Light caused a wholesale defection), and vampires who eventually become disappointed by what the cult offers - whether its because they didn't get the promotion, or because they realize how empty the &quot;enlightenment&quot; is of the Setite's teaching. &quot;Heresy&quot; of course is a convenient excuse to purge the clan of either group.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1395723#post1395723\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think this just made me realize what it is that irritates me so much about the whole &quot;We are EVIL - muhahaha&quot; presentation of the clan in this book: That's the fricking Baali. It makes one of the two groups redundant.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree that there is a conceptual error there. While it is possible to come up with distinguishing elements of the two, there is a big redundancy. Since the Revised era's attempt to make the clan more palatable, all it's done is push the Baali into a greater role of &quot;evil vampires&quot; that the Setites once slotted in. Of course at the time the first Setite clanbook was published, the Baali were a rarely used clan while the Setites were often used.<br /> <br /> I personally liked the Setites, as a concept, in that &quot;evil&quot; clan mode based heavily on Howardiana concepts. But I think the first clanbook botched it to a significant degree. It isn't useless - there are lots of usable ideas there, but it failed to do what it needed to do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1395723#post1395723\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The theme this book was going for was corruption, and all of the characters play in to that. Though personally I really prefer my Setites more along the lines of dangerous Dionysian rebels and Byronic antiheroes.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think there are plenty of Camarilla clans which fit the rolls of Dionysian rebels and Byronic antiheroes better. Brujah, Malkavian, and Toreador all fit that better in my head. I'm sure there are Setites who posture that way or see themselves as such, but I don't think that defines the clan.<br /> <br /> I think this now marks me as an outlier with much of the fanbase who went along with the various revisions and changes. But I'm comfortable with my interpretation. It works for me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-10T23:39:58-07:00","id":1395771,"title":"","content":"A couple of thoughts:<br /> <br /> 1. The Baali and Setites were both originally just the generic &quot;evil&quot; clans and also peculiarly divided. The Baali were never meant to be NPCs and basically just evil creatures at the heart of the Sabbat as embodied by Shaitan. [Editor's note: Interestingly enough, I am now adapting CHAOS FACTOR to my current Technocracy game.] The thing was that the Setites were not ACTUAL Satanists, though, just 90% Satanists and thus were available as a player character option. My view was the fact that this was perhaps a attempt to avoid being too controversial in what was already a controversial game.<br /> <br /> Now, 25 years later, the two clans have diverged enough that they are completely different. The Baali have the dubious honor of being a combination of Cthulhu Cultists and Banetenders [See the Uktena tribe]. It's their job to try to keep the Cthulhuoid abominations sealed and from waking up by relaxing them with the screams of babies and sacrifice of innocent maidens among other things much-much nastier. Then there's that small group of them that actively want to bring about the end of the world.<br /> <br /> The Followers of Set have since evolved into a kind of philosophical evil that I think is actually a better version of the Sabbat than the Sabbat generally are. The Sabbat claim to be philosophical warrior poets and &quot;beyond good and evil&quot; but are generally just douchebags with fangs. I think the Revised Setites are probably 90% people on Paths of Enlightenment and much better at being the inhuman intellectual evil. A large part of that would be the depiction of Hesha Ruhadze in SETITE where he was shown as being less &quot;muhahahhaa evil&quot; and more a guy who actually understands Nieztsche.<br /> <br /> Basically, whenever I mock the Sabbat [and I do, a lot, as much as I love them], I use this clip from A FISH CALLED WANDA.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://youtu.be/2j3adcbEwSM\" data-code=\"2j3adcbEwSM\" data-provider=\"youtube_share\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/2j3adcbEwSM/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> Which is why I think them joining the Anarchs is THE WORST THING. Because the Anarchs are a bunch of people dying for a unifying ideological, charismatic leaders, and thinkers rather than doers--which is just describing the Followers of Set Revised.<br /> <br /> 2. Which is actually why I love the <b>Priest of Darkness</b> in that I do think it'd be great to use this character as comic relief or basically as a card trick. The Priest of Darkness should be a 13th level Neonate who was Embraced solely to serve as a distraction to the other Kindred of the City. The character would then realize that he's nothing more than a obvious evil monster that other Kindred would use to justify ignoring the cult. The guy who recruits drug addicts, hookers, and bored soccer moms to his guilt while a much deeper set of mysteries was being denied them. The character is a joke and yet could be more than a joke--if he wants.<br /> <br /> But as you may have noted, I like &quot;failed&quot; Kindred.<br /> <br /> 3. <b>Sister Sin</b>: I feel more like a NPC than a PC, though, but that's just me. I agree a televangelist would be a perfect Follower of Set character. I think the Prosperity Gospel which is, essentially, inverted Christianity already would be something that the Followers of Set would be all over. I actually note the FoS have ocassionally maintained Jesus was a face of Set. I could easily view them as thinking that Set as the Anti-Christ would be an easy sell in the Bible Belt. You're damned anyway so might as well get on the winning side.<br /> <br /> 4. <b>The Voodoo Houngan</b>: I'd be interested in how this figure would react to the discovery that his Blood Cult is something of a scam. I mean if you were a mortal and met a Kindred, it'd be very easy to have them display supernatural powers and convince you of their divinity [or not]. However, as a vampire you might discover that they don't have any particular spiritual insights or power [though the Followers of Set would beg to differ]. I tend to think the Serpents of Light broke away after this point as they have room in their theology for Kindred as something other than demons but the Followers of Set are so atavistically evil that no sane person would follow them (but geniuses are not always sane).<br /> <br /> 5. One of my most beloved character was <b>Tet-Anubis</b> who was a Follower of Set/Serpent of Light/True Hand member. Yes, I broke a bunch of rules there but Tet-Anubis' thing was that he believed the Caine myth was a misunderstanding of the Osiris and Set myth (or vice versa). He was a follower of the Path of Caine and believed strongly in transcending the typical morality of humans to act like gods. The thing was that he believed the more traditional Egyptian interpretation of Set that he guarded the barge of Ra against Apophis. Thus he believed that Setites and vampires in general were meant to test human virtue and not actually be evil for the sake of evil. Also to protect them against supernatural evils like demons.<br /> <br /> He was a heretic many times over.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 06-11-2020, 02:44 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-13T18:33:16-07:00","id":1396267,"title":"","content":"I'll be doing Tzimisce tomorrow and Monday. At this point, they changed from having ten templates to only seven. (I remember being incredibly disappointed and annoyed at the change, and honestly still am to some degree.) We touched earlier on the question of when the clan went from &quot;creepy weirdo Draculas&quot; of the Sabbat PG to the &quot;super deformed freak show aliens&quot; version, and I think this is where it happened. The book had a ... curious choice of art style, especially for the templates (to say nothing of the back cover). <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-14T12:37:51-07:00","id":1396447,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Tzimisce (part one)</b><br /> <br /> Once again back to 1995. This book was written by Rob Hatch, who would go on to be Vampire developer for a while, and you may remember as the author of the Nosferatu book. Art is by John Cobb, who has a rather distinctive art style, in which I mean that almost none of it looks even remotely human or natural (and in some cases this includes even allegedly human looking characters seeming to have distorted features and/or anatomy). And almost every humanoid image in the book has these crazy bug eyes like they are rolling around in the creatures' heads. The only human looking image in this entire book is the cover image by Tim Bradstreet (which is pretty good, if totally at odds with the tone of the rest of the book; it kind of looks like a young version of Rob Zombie's wife). And I think the back cover image is the only one to rival that of the Malkavian nose-picker in the &quot;what were they thinking?&quot; category. I'm actually going to trigger warning this one.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">It's a face that's been remade into a vulva/vagina with teeth. The &quot;nose&quot; is a clitoris, and is pierced, because of course it is.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> This book was originally shipped in a plastic back with &quot;Adults Only&quot; printed on it, and a black square blocking said image on the back.<br /> This book was developed by Jennifer Hartshorn, who during her tenure at WWGS was developer for Wraith, Vampire: The Dark Ages and Vampire at various points.<br /> As I mentioned, this is clearly the point when the clan shifted from &quot;slightly respectable creepy weirdoes&quot; to &quot;all out freak show&quot;. This is not exactly helped by what I've come to notice as a trend of &quot;shock for shock's sake&quot; aspect of Hatch's work, especially when dealing with the Tzimisce. While I think grotesque certainly has its place in Vampire, I think this tends to over do it a bit. Some of it starts to feel like it's just &quot;look how edgy and adult I am for talking like this/about this subject&quot; adolescent posturing. And I can't help but roll my eyes at it when it pops up.<br /> These character use the Sabbat character sheet, with its paths and alternate virtues, but still have the standard character creation guidelines at the bottom. Unlike all of the previous ones, it doesn't have the clan Disciplines typed out on the sheet, instead having them written in. (This is weird as the four page sheet in the back actually has those.) Also, this is back when the Tzimisce and Lasombra lacked their own clan symbol, just having the Sabbat wax/blood seal with a T instead of an S. And all of these have a completely different concept written on the character sheet than what they are titled as. I find this a little annoying.<br /> <br /> <b>The Carnivorous Carny</b> (Anthropophagic Yokel)<br /> No, I'm not really sure why it was necessary to have an alternate concept on the character sheet using a super fancy term for &quot;cannibal&quot; and a slightly derogatory term for rural people. Anyway, this character was a member of the Zantosa revenant family. I'll note that this seems a little out of character for that family, as they are the wealthy hedonistic drug dealers/slavers known for their social graces, and this character is kind of the backwoods hillbilly <i>Texas Chainsaw Massacre</i> cannibal type one usually associates with the Bratovich. Apparently a Tzimisce being pursued by Camarilla archons stumbled upon the family compound, resulting in this guy saving him and managing to trap and stake the archons so said Tzimisce could eat them. This impressed the vampire so much that he embraced our character and took him off to join the Sabbat. Now he's the leader of a nomadic pack that apparently passes itself off as a traveling freak show, getting by mainly on his excess of rural charm and slick friendliness. All in all, I kind of like that as an idea for a Sabbat pack. Socials are primary, and for some odd reason he has Appearance at four (and the other two at three). I wouldn't find anything wrong with that if it weren't for the fact that the character image really doesn't reflect this. It looks like what you'd get if Rob Zombie tried to reimagine Grampa Munster. He barely looks human, let alone super attractive. Mental is secondary, and Physicals drop Stamina to one so Dexterity can be three. Abilities are ... literally all over the place. He has three dots in Subterfuge, and two dots in Acting and Leadership. The rest is just a lot of ones scattered all over the page. The write up says this is to reflect a lot of life (and unlife) experience, but instead it just feels like a total lack of focus. Disciplines are two each in Animalism and Vicissitude. Given that Zantosa dropped Animalism for Presence, this seems odd, even though I know embraced revenants don't exactly have to keep their living powers. Backgrounds are a lot, with two each in Allies, Contacts, and Resources, one in Status and three in Retainers. Path is Power and the Inner Voice at five, with Willpower at eight. In general, I like this character as a concept. It's an interesting idea for a Sabbat pack, and has a lot of potential for creepiness. However I wish both the art and the character sheet were a lot better. The character sheet doesn't add up. It's at least four points over with conventional rules and two points over with Sabbat ones. Rather than having dots just spread out all over, I think it would help to have just focused on the ones that best suit the concept. So, generally a good idea just hampered by bad implementation.<br /> <br /> <b>The Count</b> (Exiled Aristocrat)<br /> I supposed this was sort of inevitable. It's a picture of Bela Lugosi's Dracula. He's an Old Clan Tzimisce. The scion of an old and illustrious Romanian noble family that had to flee to the West after the Soviet's invaded, he grew up almost like some parody of a 19th century noble. Eventually his parents died and he found out the family was drowning in debt. Then an &quot;uncle&quot; from the old country arrived and embraced him into the &quot;true&quot; Tzimisce. And then the Sabbat came, ate his uncle, and gave him a &quot;join or die&quot; offer. So now he's an Old Clan in the Sabbat. He finds the sect interesting, even if it is overrun with annoying peasants. He lives in a creepy mansion and sleeps in a coffin. Socials are again primary, this time with Charisma at four. Mental are secondary. Abilities include three dots each in Intimidation, Leadership, Etiquette and Linguistics. Also in Balkan History. Then there are twos in Alertness and Dodge. The rest are a handful of ones. Disciplines are two each in Animalism and Auspex, and one in Dominate. Backgrounds include four dots in Retainers and two each in Generation and Resources. Path is Honorable Accord at five and Willpower at ... three. I think this may be the lowest of any template ever. This isn't a terrible character, but not a very good one either. In some ways it feels like an obligatory inclusion as a joke or something. The basic idea, especially that of a sort of stereotypical old world Tzimisce forced into the Sabbat against their will, is something that has a lot of potential. I think some sort of second draft refinement would've improved on this a lot.<br /> <br /> <b>The Fallen Surgeon</b> (Victim of Circumstance)<br /> A former top of her class surgeon from a family of doctors who fell into a life of drugs, gambling and debt. To pay off said debts, she found herself part of some sort of bizarre underground &quot;porn&quot; ring that used surgically modified freaks made from vagrants, junkies and runaways. Because grotesque. Anyway, apparently a bunch of Sabbat broke in and destroyed the place, but one Tzimisce was so impressed with our doctor's work that she embraced her. Now she still does odd research, mostly involving war ghouls, and tries to fool herself into believing that some sort of good might come out of her work. Mental is primary, with Perception maxed out to five and Intelligence at four, but Wits at one, which feels slightly wonky. Physicals are secondary, with Dexterity raised to four. Abilities have Medicine maxed out at five, which makes perfect sense. Melee and Body Alteration are at three each. Empathy, Etiquette, and Science are at two each. The rest are ones, including Torture. Disciplines have Vicissitude at three and Auspex at one. I'm actually happy to finally, after two clan books, see someone with a Discipline above two. Even more so in the clan's Special Sauce as this is a character that leans into that power and benefits from it. Backgrounds include Resources at three (I'm not sure from what), Retainers at two, and Sabbat Status and Herd at one each. Path of Death and the Soul is at eight, and I think the path fits the character. Willpower is only four. Overall, I like this character. I find the porn angle a little off putting, but that aside the character has a really good backstory, fits really well with the clan's overall image, and even has kind of an interesting if depressing personality. A few of the character points could maybe be improved, but otherwise I think this is a really good one.<br /> <br /> <b>The Seeker of the Forbidden</b> (Supernatural Investigator)<br /> And from the medical flesh freakery to the more scholarly side of the clan. A quiet bookish child who grew into an obsessive scholarly adult, her sense of wonder turning to cynicism and paranoia (if not borderline nihilism). Her encyclopedic grasp of paranormal and occult lore eventually attracted the attention of a Fiend that was so impressed that she embraced her so she'd have all eternity to continue her research. Now she travels, continuously looking for new secrets to unearth. Mental is primary, with Perception and Intelligence at four each. Physicals are secondary. Occult is at four dots, with Alertness, Investigation and Linguistics at three. Security and Stealth are at two each, and a couple of one dot abilities round things out. Among those are Lupine Lore, which I'm not really sure why she would have, and Cthulhu Mythos, which ... I guess I get what they're going for here, but it just seems redundant in the face of her high Occult score. Honestly, I'd much rather those two dots gone to something like Kindred Lore, which would feel a lot more fitting. Disciplines are four dots in Auspex and an extra dot in Thaumaturgy, which seems fitting. Also, after two books where almost every character had at least one dot in their clan's Special Sauce just because, it's nice to see one that doesn't do that. Backgrounds are a single dot each in Mentor and Resources. I'm a little surprised at the lack of Contacts. This also feels like a character where a single dot of Fame actually wouldn't feel out of place, which would be a novelty for a Sabbat character. Path of Caine is at five. Willpower is at seven. I really like this character. It plays to a side of the clan that I think often got overlooked with all the flesh crafting shenanigans, and feels like it'd be interesting to play or just include in a chronicle. So a win here.<br /> <br /> That's all for this entry. Next will be the last three Tzimisce. Then we will move on to Lasombra.<br /> <br /> ADDED: I forgot to mention the character art for the last two. They are, perhaps, the most human looking of any image in this book, save the cover. The Surgeon is fairly gaunt looking, which suits the character's backstory pretty well, and otherwise fairly restrained in appearance. Likewise the Seeker, who has this sort of blue-collar Goth look going on, and aside from what seems to be a third eye drawn or tattooed on the forehead, appears like a normal human being.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 06-15-2020, 02:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-14T13:18:39-07:00","id":1396463,"title":"","content":"The Vicissitude power really took over the clan. Signature Disciplines often did that because they were the most distinctive things about them. For me, one of the reasons I've had a very low interest in the Tzimisce is because of this. I found the concept of the Vicissitude Discipline to be completely inexplicable (and the statement in Dirty Secrets that it was also an umbral disease!). I wondered why this was in my Vampire game. It wasn't until a full decade later that I finally discovered it was because of the Necroscope series. A series none of my friends or I had ever read. But even after I learned that and understood why it was included, it wasn't an element I wanted in &quot;my&quot; Vampire. I thought it should have been an optional rule for STs to include it in chronicles if they wanted to go that route. I thought it was that off putting and genre-clashing.<br /> <br /> I know many other players ignorant of Necroscope as much as myself didn't mind and accepted it completely. But it was always head scratching for me.<br /> <br /> As a result, when I ST my own Vampire chronicles, I don't use Vicissitude at all. I replaced it with standard Protean twenty years ago. None of my friends minded. None of them liked that Discipline anyway. It did mean I had to throw out much of the canon established around the clan and reinvent archetypes. But I thought the basics of the clan being territorial (as a result of their clan weakness), being &quot;fiends&quot; in the sense they relished tormenting the living, and that had remnant ties to feudalism - whether as being tied to estates or having &quot;serfs&quot; among the Revenants - was more than enough to create a strong clan identity.<br /> <br /> There were some iconic elements in the Sabbat - the Blood Brothers and Vozhd - I thought had to be included, and they were somewhat dependent on Vicissitude. So my response was to make the Sabbat Tzimisce as possessing a unique Blood Magic like Bio-Thaumaturgy that allowed them to do that. Any kind of Dr Frankenstein concepts could work just as well with that instead and not &quot;pollute&quot; the rest of the setting.<br /> <br /> Strangely enough once I knew about the Necroscope concept, I had no problem taking that and applying it (and the Souleaters of Dirty Secrets) as a unique kind of fomori for the Garou to kill. But I always kept them limited in number and in isolated &quot;colonies&quot; because the Garou were effective at wiping out these umbral invaders.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-15T06:54:45-07:00","id":1396669,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1396463#post1396463\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I found the concept of the Vicissitude Discipline to be completely inexplicable (and the statement in Dirty Secrets that it was also an umbral disease!).</div> </div> </div> </div>Please try. Because it bothers me. I mainly run Werewolf but I've got a special place in my heart for Vampire (it was the first WoD setting I played back in the 90's). But the concept of Vicissitude escapes me. And I always been a curious guy. From what I know (besides the powers on certain levels of course) Vicissitude is a kind of thaumaturgical disease that, if you fail on a certain roll during raising it, will change your PC into an unplayable pool of clay. Is this &quot;umbral disease&quot; the same? Cause I had Dirty Secrets in my hands only for a few hours don't have time to read it all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-15T11:31:59-07:00","id":1396701,"title":"","content":"I think the revised clan book tried to explain the development of Vicissitude a little better, or at least I remember it attempting to do so. I actually found myself getting a better grasp of the discipline and the clan as a whole from reading Requiem's Ordo Dracul book of all things, as it derived from some of the same underlying ideas as the whole metamorphosist thing.<br /> My own take away is that most of the Antediluvians were, from human perspective, moderately insane or at least fairly alien in their thinking processes. Malkav and her broken perspective of reality after viewing some concept or truth that human minds can't comprehend is the most obvious example, but Cappadocius and his obsession with death, the dead and eventually diablarizing God, Saulot and his mercurial decisions of how best to interact with, indulge or control his Beast, and Absimilard and his hunter/predator fixations are other examples. And this extends to The Eldest, which seems to have had a pathological obsession with its own evolution/metamorphosis into something beyond even the nigh-godlike state of an Antediluvian. And because Antediluvians are already godlike, especially in their control over the arcane power of Vitae and what it can be used for, it was able to will itself the power to explore and work toward this goal of transformative evolution. <br /> The other thing to keep in mind is that when the Antediluvians created these Disciplines, they were essentially creating the 10th level powers that allow them do to nearly anything within the general idea of that Discipline. Lesser beings - which means any vampire less than 3rd generation - can only grasp parts of the true power, and those of the 8th and lower generations are even more limited. The first few levels of Vicissitude compared to what The Eldest truly created as its signature power are akin to basic math and algebra compared to a true understanding of theoretical physics. Unfortunately, this means a lot of younger or lower generation Tzimisce get hung up on what they can do rather than why they can do it, and fixated on the idea that they're above humanity rather than why they're supposed to be so (especially when, as Kindred, the Beast is constantly encouraging their worst impulses). <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-15T13:18:57-07:00","id":1396717,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Tzimisce (part two)</b><br /> <br /> More fiends. <br /> <br /> <b>The Monster Maker</b> (Demented Genius)<br /> It's a mad scientist. I really can't say much more than that. Maybe that he wears &quot;<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steve_Urkel\" target=\"_blank\">Urkel</a>&quot; pants and a bow tie? And a &quot;super villain cape.&quot; It kind of feels like someone looked at Mage's Sons of Ether and said, &quot;let's make fun of that.&quot; He's a genius and a geneticist, always mocked for being a nerd and crackpot, who took a trip to Eastern Europe and ended up getting embraced by a member of the Sabbat. Now he'll show them all. His Intelligence is maxed out to five. Social is tertiary with all points put in Manipulation, leaving the other two at ones. Physicals are mainly focused on Stamina. Abilities have Science maxed out at five, with Medicine and Body Alteration at three each. Alertness, Intimidation, Computer, Investigation and Occult are at two. the rest is a scattering of ones. Disciplines are just Vicissitude at four. Backgrounds is five dots in Retainers. He's on the Path of Caine at five, and that path really doesn't fit the character's motivations or behavior at all. Willpower is nine. I really don't like this character. And I say that as some one who loves the whole Frankenstein/Mad Scientist world in various games, genres and settings, including when done with the Tzimisce. (I've even played one who kept replacing human subject's brains with ghoul rats in hopes of creating an army of minions he could control with Animalism. Results were ... mixed.) And this character feels like it's mocking me for that. It's the sort of thing meant to be made fun of and sneered at rather than make you say &quot;that is neat and I want to play it&quot;. Even the Fake Rapper felt like something that would be cool to play. I want something that feels like Herbert West or BD Wong's character in Jurassic Park. In addition, given that the Fallen Surgeon also makes monstrous ghouls for the Sabbat, this character is a little redundant <br /> <br /> <b>The Tattoo Artist</b> (Artiste)<br /> This is another one of those that was probably inevitable, given the Body Modification skills tie to Vicissitude. She grew up in abject squaller, hating both the ugliness of the world and pretty world of normalcy that she desperately wanted but couldn't have. Experiments in self harm eventually led to running away from home and ending up as the apprentice of a tattoo artist. She became very very good at her craft, eventually attracting the attention of a Tzimisce who liked her work. She still runs her shop, and applies the clan's traditions about haven, sanctuary and hospitality to her workplace and its customers. (I find this last bit a nice touch.) The image is of a young woman, heavily tattooed, pierced and with likely other modifications. It fits the character pretty well. Physicals are primary, with a 3/4/3 split. I'm a little surprised it's not 2/5/3. Mental is secondary. Socials have Charisma at three at the expense of Appearance being one. I guess this is related to all her tattoos and piercings, but quite honestly that sort of thing is highly subjective in regards to looks. I've seen people with a lot of face work done where they still look attractive. Body Alteration is maxed out at five dots. Intimidation, Streetwise, Melee, and Brawl are all at three dots. Dodge is two and the rest are a scattering of single dots. Disciplines are Auspex and Vicissitude at two each. Backgrounds are Generation at five, Resources at one, and Mentor at two. She's on the Path of Cathari at four (one of the lowest ratings I can recall seeing), and Willpower is seven. I really like this character. It fits the clan and their image really well, but not in the usual &quot;monster&quot; way, and could easily be a character in any Vampire city. (I actually think you could make this character just as well using normal non-Sabbat rules.) Very much a win here. <br /> <br /> <b>The Torturer</b> (Sicko)<br /> So, a serial killer who grew up in an abusive and very dysfunctional home, with a bundle of psychosexual issues and a misogynistic streak a mile wide. And one of his victims was a Sabbat vampire who embraced him. And that's about it. There's no deeper motivation nor any specification of what he does in the Sabbat other than be a misogynistic creep and torture people for the hell of it. Manipulation is maxed out to five. Charisma, Strength and Stamina are threes. Intimidation and Torture are four dots. Subterfuge, Stealth, and Body Alteration are at three. Melee and Medicine are two. Then a few ones. Disciplines are one in Auspex and three in Vicissitude. Backgrounds include one in Generation, and one dot in Black Hand. There's no mention of this in the write up, and I'm not really sure why they'd want him. And he has four dots in Resources. There's no context for this and I'm not sure where or how he's getting this income. Path of Death and the Soul is six and Willpower is five. This character feels like a a bit of a mess. It's kind of a &quot;shock for the sake of shock&quot; thing. I don't really object to the idea of a torturer as a member of the Sabbat and especially of the Tzimisce, but I think it's the sort of thing that works much better with more context. Rather than the Black Hand, it's the sort of thing that should be, IMO at least, better served as part of the Sabbat's own Inquisition, torturing people nominally for &quot;the greater good&quot; of the sect but really for his own enjoyment. (Or, alternately, someone who was a professional interrogator in life, perhaps for the military or some Eastern European or Latin American police force, who didn't like it but was naturally good at it, and now finds himself in the position of his vampire nature making him enjoy it more than he wants to.) So, I have to say this was a decent idea kind of derailed by the author's &quot;look how edgy we are&quot; impulse. <br /> <br /> And, that's the Tzimisce. Basically split down the middle between the really good and the could've been better. The Fallen Surgeon, the Seeker of the Forbidden, and the Tattoo Artist I all really like, and I think that they represent aspects of the clan I'd like to see more of. The Carnivorous Carny, the Count, and the Torturer are all founded on pretty good ideas, but just kind of fail in implementation. And the Monster Maker is that turned up to 11. <br /> Representation wise, we have three of the seven being women (and those being the ones I liked best, oddly). The art work makes it difficult to guess ethnicity or anything, but they mostly appear to be white. <br /> I remember being disappointed that the Count was the closest thing we got to an old school Carpathian/Eastern European warlord. Back in the 90s, I would've enjoyed seeing some sort of modern urban version of that who ruled a housing project like his personal fiefdom. An Animalism focused concept would've been nice, and I've always wanted to see something that used Vicissitude as a shapeshifting/disguise method for spying. And possibly one that used it to achieve inhuman beauty (sharply contrasted by the monstrosity of the Horrid Form). <br /> If the Tzimisce have a theme, it's basically that of not being bound by limits - human, vampire, moral, physical, etc. - and most of these fit that to some degree. I think part of the reason I like the three characters I do is that they express that in more interesting ways than cruelty simply for the sake of being able to be cruel. <br /> <br /> Next, Lasombra.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-15T14:18:10-07:00","id":1396727,"title":"","content":"One aspect of the Sabbat clans is that their prime concept clashes to what the clanbook is trying to do. There are many contradictions within the Sabbat, and the writers needed to handle that. I don't think they ultimately succeeded in doing so.<br /> <br /> If the Camarilla's theme tends to be that the human was selected because there was something about their human life that caused the vampire to embrace them. That also ties into the theme of Humanity.<br /> <br /> But the Sabbat isn't like that. They're more like the monstrous vampires of old that simply selects their victim because it's what they have on hand. The original shovelhead theme went along with that. The vampires of the Sabbat didn't care what you were like as a human. It was utterly unimportant. What was important was what you did AFTER you became a vampire. That ties into the themes of the Paths of Enlightenment.<br /> <br /> As an example we can use Wendy Wade from Chicago By Night. She was simply a lawyer randomly abducted and embraced. Nothing about her in particular caused her embrace by the Sabbat. In first edition CbN no clan is listed (it just says Sabbat coven), but in second edition CbN it shows Tzimisce.<br /> <br /> That fits with the stereotypical vampire in fiction before Anne Rice or VtM. If a vampire shows up in your town - whether one of the Hammer film vampires, Count Yorga, Kurt Barlow, or Marie from Innocent Blood - you get randomly turned into a vampire because a vampire needs to feed, and feeds on whomever. That vampire may indeed become obsessed about an individual and specifically try to embrace that person, but most of the new vampires created were simply selected at random.<br /> <br /> My view was and is that the Sabbat was an attempt to emulate that style while making it work within the mechanics and setting created for the World of Darkness.<br /> <br /> So in my mind, all the templates in the Lasombra and Tzimisce books are essentially wrong at a categorical level. They are more appropriate for a Camarilla type clan instead of the Sabbat. Sabbat templates should heavily include random embraces that worked out in the end (as opposed to those who never became True Sabbat, or met Final Death beforehand), and how they became something appropriate for the Sabbat. As opposed to people who already fit the archetype.<br /> <br /> This was a problem I had in general with these clanbooks. They did well in describing the clan in pre-Sabbat terms, but failed to show what actually changed during the Anarch Revolt. Just like we saw differences between the anti-tribu and the main clans, we should be able to see the difference in the clans pre-Sabbat (and reflecting the Lasombra antitribu and Old Clan Tzimisce) and post-Sabbat. I don't think that was successfully done. There just seemed too much continuity between them.<br /> <br /> Eventually the writers acknowledged this, and began the process of eroding those stylistic differences between the Camarilla and Sabbat. We're later told that shovelhead vampires aren't the norm, and that in fact the Sabbat vampires act much like the Camarilla only &quot;more evil&quot;. Boring. The Sabbat just became another Camarilla as opposed to a different kind of sect that could be used to emulate a different style of vampire (the more traditional monster kind that dominated before Anne Rice). This was a categorical error, and it would lead to more bad decisions later on.<br /> <br /> When I create NPC Sabbat packs for my chronicles (I do occasionally use them as antagonists in Vampire, but I usually use them as the de facto vampires in my Werewolf and other WoD games), my concepts for their human lives tend to be things like Farmer, Mailman, Runaway, and Housewife in order to reflect the random nature of their embrace by the Sabbat. Eventually those Sabbat vampires that live (not dying at the hands of the sect's enemies, or by other Sabbat!) get molded into a &quot;proper&quot; Sabbat Lasombra and Sabbat Tzimisce. Which is close to their antitribu and Old Clan versions, but not identical because after all the Anarch Revolt had some effect on those clans' cultures.<br /> <br /> Since in my head canon history, many Lasombra and Tzimisce elders did join the Sabbat, there is a faction of Sabbat vampires who do keep to the old ways - they're the &quot;Ultraconservatives&quot;. And the tension between them and the &quot;real&quot; Sabbat (in the Loyalist movement) is what lead to the sect's civil wars. So I do think some templates of the clanbook are acceptable Sabbat templates. But I thought the clanbooks should have made that distinction, and showed us examples of good archetypical T and L vampires that became that way after their embrace, but did not fit that before while giving us some of the templates they actually used.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 06-15-2020, 05:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-15T14:32:49-07:00","id":1396733,"title":"","content":"I think in perfect hindsight, it might've been better to have everyone be a member of the Camarilla - at least nominally - and the Sabbat be made up entirely of antitribu - including the Lasombra and Tzimisce - who've largely rejected clan for the Sabbat's shared ideology and the Paths of Enlightenment playing a larger role in how the Sabbat divides itself than clan does.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-15T15:33:42-07:00","id":1396740,"title":"","content":"One thing I always thought is that in-character, both the Sabbat Lasombra and Camarilla Lasombra should think the other one is the &quot;anti-tribu&quot;. I know for players, this has to be made explicit, or it gets too confusing to known what is being talked about. And it makes sense for the larger group to be the &quot;real&quot; clan.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-15T16:32:26-07:00","id":1396745,"title":"","content":"To be fair, even when making shovel heads, the Clan basically looks for people that have some resemblance to their stated preference. The Tzimisce seek out people like Wendy Wade who are upper class, intelligent looking, or cultured. It can lead to some serious mistakes but the idea is that if they do survive that they are going to be eventually brought into the fold of the Clan Culture.<br /> <br /> If you had a template for a Shovel Head, it would probably be <b>Shovel Head</b> and they would be described as someone who is being strongly encouraged to shed their human existence and brainwashed by the Vaulderie as well as the cult-like nature of Sabbat existence to become &quot;More Tzimisce than Tzimisce.&quot; <b>Wendy Wade</b> is, for example, a perfectly excellent example of the Tzimisce: ambitious, refined, and utterly amoral.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We something similar with <b>Sierra Burace</b> who is a perfectly fine Lasombra but also someone who has nothing really in common with her clan's esoteric interests. </div> </div> </div>It's only if you think of the Tzimsice as a bunch of Cenobites that engage in nothing but flesh-related horrors that the shovel-heads feel like an afterthoughts. I got into a bit tiff about this in the Lasombra thread but I've always felt the <b>Flesh-Monkeys</b> (my slang for these) were a significant but fundamentally minority group in the clan--same as the Kolduns. Powerful, influential, and &quot;famous&quot; as Tzimisce go but not the majority.<br /> <br /> I admit I've always been a fan of the <b>Old Clan Tzimisce</b> far more so than the mainstream clan. I tend to prefer them because I'm all about the honor, oaths, hospitality, creepy castles, and Castlevania elements. The Revenant Families are much more important to my view of the Tzimisce than Vicissitude. The Basarab are my favorite Revenant families but each of them basically means the Tzimisce have their own sorta-Bloodlines.<br /> <br /> So <b>The Count</b> is my favorite of these by far.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-15T17:01:24-07:00","id":1396751,"title":"","content":"Is there a full definition for &quot;Flesh Monkeys&quot;?<br /> <br /> One thing I've tried to do in my own games over the years is present clans as being like these really weird extended families. (And while I do try to hew to certain overarching themes for each clan - passion for the Brujah, survival for the Gangrel, etc. - that sort of thing can manifest in a vast multitude of ways.) There are usually some wide spread traditions and customs among each clan, such as the Tzimisce's customs of hospitality and domain sanctity, and assorted blood ties and common traits, but also a bunch of internal factions and subcultures. In the Fiends' case, this includes the old school lords (basically the Old Clan, just without the name), the various revenant families, the Koldun witches, the metamorphosists, the Knights of the Dragon, the would be gods, the Balkan Muslims who exist on the fringes of the Ashirra, and others, as well as the various members who are just doing their own thing. And so on with the other clans.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-15T17:07:20-07:00","id":1396755,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1396751#post1396751\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is there a full definition for &quot;Flesh Monkeys&quot;?<br /> <br /> One thing I've tried to do in my own games over the years is present clans as being like these really weird extended families. (And while I do try to hew to certain overarching themes for each clan - passion for the Brujah, survival for the Gangrel, etc. - that sort of thing can manifest in a vast multitude of ways.) There are usually some wide spread traditions and customs among each clan, such as the Tzimisce's customs of hospitality and domain sanctity, and assorted blood ties and common traits, but also a bunch of internal factions and subcultures. In the Fiends' case, this includes the old school lords (basically the Old Clan, just without the name), the various revenant families, the Koldun witches, the metamorphosists, the Knights of the Dragon, the would be gods, the Balkan Muslims who exist on the fringes of the Ashirra, and others, as well as the various members who are just doing their own thing. And so on with the other clans.</div> </div> </div> </div>Flesh monkeys is basically the Tzimisce version of Fishmalk where there's not really an ideology for all the gross-out horror stuff. Your character is a inhuman Nosferatu-looking monster who warps flesh of other vampires as well as humans more or less just for the hell of it. You have cathedrals of flesh, fleshy decorations, living furniture, and so on because VICISSITUDE IS SCARY. This is reflective of certain fans but also the fact sometimes we get the Tzimisce doing nightmarish horrible things (like the headcrabs in Bloodlines) just because.<br /> <br /> 1. Create Human Centipede<br /> 2. ???<br /> 3. World Domination!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-15T17:14:10-07:00","id":1396757,"title":"","content":"Ah, yes. Those guys. <br /> <br /> I think every clan actually has some version of the FishMalk. I think I remember Dierdre Brooks saying that the Ravnos version was the person who stole the Elysium silverware and lit the Prince's curtains on fire before jumping out the window because &quot;Crime!&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-15T17:19:23-07:00","id":1396758,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1396757#post1396757\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ah, yes. Those guys.<br /> <br /> I think every clan actually has some version of the FishMalk. I think I remember Dierdre Brooks saying that the Ravnos version was the person who stole the Elysium silverware and lit the Prince's curtains on fire before jumping out the window because &quot;Crime!&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>100% agreed.<br /> <br /> One of the ironies is that Vicissitude is always used to be horribly evil when it seems like almost every High Humanity healer character would want it. Fuck Obeah, Vicissitude is where it's at if you want to cure all cancer.<br /> <br /> But I've NEVER seen that even HINTED at in canon.<br /> <br /> Only cosmetic surgery.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 06-15-2020, 08:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-15T17:47:45-07:00","id":1396763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-character-templates?p=1396745#post1396745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To be fair, even when making shovel heads, the Clan basically looks for people that have some resemblance to their stated preference.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is exactly what I disagree with. I understand that at this point, I am arguing against 25 years or so of continuity so it's a hopeless argument. I can't win by appealing to &quot;canon&quot;. I'm not going to change the game. But the idea that the Sabbat is selective about who it turns really goes against what the sect should be. I'm fine with the occasional embrace being done because the vampire feels she must have that individual. Sabbat can be passionate and have an impulsive embrace as well. But it shouldn't be standard for the Sabbat.<br /> <br /> When Kurt Barlow is turning the entire town of Salem's Lot into vampires, he isn't very particular about who he takes. That's the same for most other classic vampire movies when the movie often ends with the lone hero having to hunt down and destroy his friends before finally catching up the master vampire who was the cause of everything. That the World of Darkness vampires don't emulate those kinds of vampires is OK (and needed because Stephen King's story is all about taking the idea seriously that being bitten by a vampire automatically makes you a vampire would mean humanity is quickly wiped out is a serious objection to that scenario; it is why Bram Stoker made sure to show that something else needs to be done to turn someone). But it's the <i>effect </i>we're trying to duplicate, and the <i>terror</i> of that effect on us. That we could be eaten and turned into bloodthirsty monsters because we were in the wrong place at the wrong time. The Sabbat is more than just an emulation of classic vampire stories and movies. It has many other inspirations, but I feel this is a major inspiration and the game needs to reflect it. The shovelhead mass embrace is important in creating that. (I think that is also why we saw a lot of Infernalism in the early Sabbat books. They had infernalists because in the Hammer Horror movies, Satan worship was a very big element.) But we lost that early on because of these clanbooks.<br /> <br /> And in that type of scenario the vampire doesn't pick most of his victims. Nor does it make sense that Sabbat doing a mass embrace of humans in order to get replacements are going to be selective. No one would say, &quot;Before we start abducting people at random to feed them our blood, whack them on the head with a shovel, and dump them in a mass grave to see who comes out, it's very important we all try to find people we think would be good picks for our clans. That way if they happen to crawl out, and if they happen not to be Caitiff, that'd be really cool.&quot; I find that to be completely ludicrous.<br /> <br /> Wendy Wade wasn't picked. She was turned by pure chance. &quot;She made the mistake of walking home late from a neighborhood fern bar.&quot; She wasn't being stalked or targeted. She just had the misfortune to be out and alone late at night when a van of Sabbat were looking for random people to dump into a pit. If she had never went to that bar that night, or came home early, or was picked up by an attractive man and went back to his place, she would never have become a vampire.<br /> <br /> And that it was a Tzimisce who turned her was also random chance. It could have been any member of that particular Sabbat pack. She could have been a Lasombra or one of the anti-tribu. In fact, &quot;ambitious, refined, and utterly amoral&quot; seems to work better for a Lasombra than the Tzimisce based on the templates we just saw.<br /> <br /> I don't see the random turning of many of the Sabbat to be a flaw. It really distinguishes the sect from the Camarilla and Independents who do pay much more attention to their &quot;candidates&quot;. Not all Sabbat are turned like this. Certain mortals will attract a Sabbat who wants that one specific person to be turned, but that is a different dynamic, and I think it weakens the sect as a concept if done too much. In fact, I think there should be a distinction in the Sabbat between Sabbat who were randomly picked, survived, and became True Sabbat versus those vampires who were &quot;groomed.&quot; Wouldn't there be resentment? Wouldn't those who proved themselves consider themselves better? Wouldn't it make yet another point of contention between the elders and neonates or ultra-conservatives and loyalists within the sect? I think it would. And I think it'd be an interesting conflict for the sect.<br /> <br /> One of the reasons the Sabbat sect ended up being changed as we went into Revised is because of this. People wrote the Sabbat clanbooks as if they were the Camarilla clanbooks, and didn't take the unique aspects of the Sabbat into consideration. So in wrestling with these contradictions, later writers simply decided to dump many of the original conceptions of the Sabbat and make everything more like the Camarilla, but with a different kind of hierarchy and more gore. That's when we started getting excuses that Sabbat pick their embrace just like Camarilla vampires as well as doing shovelheads, and then like your comment that even the shovelheads get picked.<br /> <br /> The game developed in a different direction. I understand that. I just feel it was a mistake.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-16T00:47:02-07:00","id":1396831,"title":"","content":"I loved the templates of the clanbooks too, No One of Consequence. Although I prefer the templates of the third edition and, in general, I prefer these clanbooks. In second edition, I printed the templates of the Giovanni book and give them to the players for a one-shoot game, they matched very well and represented the different archetypes of the Clan Giovanni with success. They have fun playing these characters. I also have used templates for Mage the Ascension newbies in one-shots, because the character creation and the introduction to the game requires lot of time and is easier than to explain a character. In consequence, I have studied almost all the templates from the tradition and convention books.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24202-justycar","name":"Justycar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24202_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-18T15:13:02-07:00","id":1397391,"title":"","content":"<b>Clanbook: Lasombra (part one)</b><br /> <br /> So, now it's 1996. This book was developed by Rob Hatch, so he's taken over as Vampire developer now. (IIRC, Jennifer Hartshorn was Vampire: the Dark Ages developer at this point.) Writing is by Richard Dansky (who would later go on to be developer for Wraith, Vampire: The Dark Ages, and KotE, as well as a lot of writing), and Elizabeth Ditchburn, who I think mainly wrote for Wraith. Interior art is by a lot of people, including Joshua Timbrook, and he appears to have done the template art as well. I've no idea how or why that happened, but it is a nice return to original form, especially after the deformed weirdness of Tzimisce. As a stylistic choice, this book is almost entirely black background with white type, because, shadows. This can make it hard for some people to read (and I recall Bruce Baugh mentioning it when talking about writing the revised version). This book is also notable for having one of the silliest covers in the entire clanbook line. Bald guy with a Black Hand logo on his head and his tongue sticking out doesn't exactly imply &quot;highly competent political leaders of the Sabbat.&quot; This image became somewhat derisively known as &quot;Scary Black Hand Face&quot;. <br /> <br /> Characters are all made using the Sabbat rules, even though the regular rules are at the bottom of each sheet. <br /> <br /> <b><i>Antitribu</i> Pirate</b><br /> A guy who grew up loving the sea, and once financially stable enough at his day job to afford it, bought a boat and enjoyed sailing (presumably in the Gulf of Mexico/Caribbean). And one night he ran across a very old pirate ship and its captain embraced him. Now he's a modern day pirate, helping attack ships at night and stealing various cargo ranging from drugs and gold to electronics and computer chips. Overall, he enjoys being a vampire pirate, and sometimes his enthusiasm gets the better of him and he acts like an Errol Flynn character. Physicals are primary, with a 4/3/3 split, and Socials as secondary, with 3/2/3. There's also an extra dot in Wits from freebies. Abilities include Firearms, Melee, and Boating at three each, then Brawl, Intimidation, Streetwise, and Finance at two each. Also a handful of one dot abilities, including Navigation. Disciplines are one dot each, plus another in Obtenebration. Backgrounds are Generation at five dots, and two each in Mentor and Resources. I'm noticing a lack of Allies or Retainers to represent the rest of the pirate crew. Path of Harmony rating is four, and Willpower is five. (The bulk of the freebies got spent on backgrounds and that dot of Wits. I'm starting to notice that the background thing is maybe a detriment to the Sabbat characters.) I like this character quite a bit. He's fun and part of a modern genre I wish Vampire had explored a little more (it did to a degree with the revised Lasombra book and <i>WoD: Blood-Dimmed Tides</i>). If it has a down side, it's that I think this character might've been a little stronger if made under standard character creation rules, loosing either the dot in Dominate or the second one in Obtenebration to boost his backgrounds, and maybe fine tune the abilities a little. <br /> <br /> <b>The Cadet</b><br /> A top student at one of the military academies embraced just before graduation after being scouted by his sire for a place in the Black Hand (the military branch, not the cult). Now he's the head of a Black Hand pack, putting his training and leadership to good use in the existing world of the Jyhad and ultimately aiming for a top spot in the Black Hand and the Sabbat. Physicals are primary, with a 3/4/3 split, and Socials secondary, with four dots in Charisma. Wits once again has an extra dot from freebies. I'm kind of surprised it doesn't just have Mental as secondary and use the extra dot in Charisma. Athletics and Firearms are at three dots each. Alertness, Brawl, Dodge, Leadership, Streetwise, Drive, and Melee are at two. Also ones in Security, Stealth, and a handful of suitable Knowledges. I'm a little disappointed by the lack of the Military Science secondary ability, as it seems like it would've been appropriate. Disciplines are the one each plus one, this time in Potence. Backgrounds are one each in Black Hand, Resources, and Sabbat Status. Path of Honorable Accord is six and Willpower is seven. Once again, this is a pretty good character. He's fairly well suited to the job he was created for, and would make a pretty good center to build a Black Hand pack around. As a matter of personal amusement, the character art looks like a young Clayton Oliver, who was an intern at WWGS back in the late 90s before becoming a writer, and I'm wondering how much of the character was inspired by him. <br /> <br /> <b>The Cat Burglar</b><br /> Someone with a life long habit of going where they weren't supposed to and poking through other people's stuff just for the fun of it, eventually becoming a very skilled classic cat burglar. So skilled that they drew the attention of a Lasombra, who plucked them off the side of a building in the middle of a job to undergo the creation rites. Now they're an even better thief, frequently stealing from members of the Camarilla. Physicals are primary, with Dexterity maxed out to five and Strength at three. Mental is secondary. Athletics, Stealth and Security are all at four dots, and boy has it been a minute since I've seen a character with more than one ability above three. Alertness, Dodge, Streetwise and Finance are at three each. Disciplines are Obtenebration at three and Potence at one. Backgrounds are four in Resources and one in Contacts. Path of Cathari is five and Willpower six. I like this character a lot. It seems like it'd be fun to play, and its nice to see one focused on necessary skills instead of just all over the place. Incidentally, I keep referring to this character as &quot;they&quot; because the image is pretty androgynous and it's impossibly to tell if it's male or female. <br /> <br /> That's all for this one. Next will be the rest of the clan.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-19T07:00:28-07:00","id":1397500,"title":"","content":"I found the obsession with Lasombra pirates to be weird because while modern piracy exists, it's very different from how the clanbook wanted it to be. So it threw me. I understand the &quot;logic&quot; that Lasombra = Obtenebration = Abyss = the Deep Ocean = Ships = Pirates. But it was something that seemed artificial to me and did not emulate a &quot;natural&quot; WoD arising from gothic horror elements, but a quirk.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-19T09:22:17-07:00","id":1397531,"title":"","content":"I attribute some of the anachronism to the fact that a bunch of the elders who maintain the ships and fleets being from the Golden Age of Piracy or Seven Years War/American Revolution/Napoleonic Wars privateers. But when they roll up on another ship, I presume it looks more like John Carpenter's The Fog, where darkness engulfs the ship and soon most of the people on board are dead. Then it's stripped of anything valuable and scuttled, becoming another ship that just vanished.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-19T10:05:42-07:00","id":1397541,"title":"","content":"I cand understand the sea focus with the concept of &quot;mediterranean clan&quot;, the Lasombra were the Peoples of the Sea that invade Greece and reduced this ancient and proud culture to ruins, the Castell de l\u00b4Ombro is the main fortress of the clan and it is in Sicily, they are also the explorers of Castilla and the Conquistadores of Las Am\u00e9ricas, Spain was the first world naval empire. But, while I understand the focus in the sea, the deeps and the exploration, I do not understand the piracy theme. Probably, as the Tzimisce are based in the eastern Europe stereotypes, the Lasombra are strongly influenced with the latino stereotypes. And for the americans, we are commonly see as rogues, bandits and lovers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24202,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24202-justycar","user":"Justycar","date":". 06-19-2020, 01:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24202-justycar","name":"Justycar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24202_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-19T14:09:22-07:00","id":1397576,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1387573-wir-pre-made-vampire-cha